ts of `pip freeze` in the issue.\n* [x] I have included the versions of the external dependencies required to reproduce this bug.\n\n\n## Related Issues and Possible Duplicates\n\n#### Related Issues\n\n* [[#10919](https://github.com/celery/celery/pull/10919)](https://github.com/celery/celery/pull/10919) \u2013 Related Redis chord implementation changes.\n\n#### Possible Duplicates\n\n* None identified yet.\n\n## Environment & Settings\n\n**Celery version**: 5.7.0rc1 (development version from Git)\n\n**Kombu version**: 5.7.0b1\n\n**Python version**: 3.13.15\n\n**Billiard version**: 4.3.1\n\n**Redis Python client**: 6.4.0\n\n**Operating System**: Windows 11 (64-bit)\n\n**Python implementation**: CPython\n\n**Broker used in reproduction**: `memory://`\n\n**Result backend used in reproduction**: `redis://localhost:6390/0`\n\n**Redis server**: Local Redis instance listening on port 6390.\n\n<!--\nThe following celery report was collected from the development environment.\nIts configured result backend is cache+memory://, whereas the standalone\nreproduction explicitly configures Redis as its result backend.\n-->\n\n<details>\n<summary><b><code>celery report</code> Output:</b></summary>\n<p>\n\n```text\nsoftware -> celery:5.7.0rc1 (collider) kombu:5.7.0b1 py:3.13.15\n            billiard:4.3.1 memory:N/A\nplatform -> system:Windows arch:64bit, WindowsPE\n            kernel version:11 imp:CPython\nloader   -> celery.loaders.app.AppLoader\nsettings -> transport:memory results:cache+memory://\n\nbroker_url: 'memory://:********@localhost//'\nresult_backend: 'cache+memory://'\ndeprecated_settings: None\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n# Steps to Reproduce\n\n## Required Dependencies\n\n* **Minimal Python Version**: Not yet determined.\n* **Minimal Celery Version**: Affected implementation after #10919; reproduced on 5.7.0rc1 and `main`.\n* **Minimal Kombu Version**: 5.7.0b1 (tested environment).\n* **Minimal Broker Version**: Not applicable to the standalone reproduction (`memory://` broker).\n* **Minimal Result Backend Version**: Redis (exact minimum version not yet determined).\n* **Minimal OS and/or Kernel Version**: Windows 11 (tested environment).\n* **Minimal Broker Client Version**: Not applicable to the `memory://` broker.\n* **Minimal Result Backend Client Version**: redis-py 6.4.0.\n\n### Python Packages\n\n<details>\n<summary><b><code>pip freeze</code> Output:</b></summary>\n<p>\n\n```text\n-e git+https://github.com/Namraa310806/agent-framework.git@beb7d2916d36ab4efc0387936fccaecded88003e#egg=agent_framework_core&subdirectory=python\\packages\\core\naiohappyeyeballs==2.7.1\naiohttp==3.14.3\naiohttp-retry==2.9.1\naiosignal==1.4.0\namqp==5.4.1\nannotated-types==0.8.0\nanyio==4.15.1\nasgiref==3.12.1\nast_serialize==0.12.1\nattrs==26.1.0\nazure-core==1.41.0\nazure-identity==1.25.3\nazure-storage-blob==12.31.0\nbeautifulsoup4==4.12.2\nbilliard==4.3.1\nblack==26.10.1\nboto3==1.43.111\nbotocore==1.43.111\ncachetools==7.1.7\ncatboost==1.2.10\n-e git+https://github.com/Namraa310806/celery.git@57cb15ff99ab47a6e72d5a70c5522b8fabb59a57#egg=celery\ncertifi==2026.7.22\ncffi==2.1.1\ncfgv==3.5.0\ncharset-normalizer==3.5.1\nclick==8.4.2\nclick-didyoumean==0.3.1\nclick-plugins==1.1.1.2\nclick-repl==0.4.1\ncloudpickle==3.1.2\ncolorama==0.4.6\ncontourpy==1.4.0\ncoverage==7.15.4\ncron_descriptor==2.1.0\ncryptography==50.0.0\ncycler==0.12.1\nDateTime==6.0\ndebugpy==1.8.22\ndeprecation==2.1.0\ndistlib==0.4.3\ndistro==1.9.0\n-e git+https://github.com/Namraa310806/django.git@ed52a1f6c1527bce131424e3b8bee57dddad6c57#egg=Django\ndjango-celery-beat==2.5.0\ndjango-cors-headers==4.3.1\ndjango-timezone-field==7.2.2\ndjangorestframework==3.14.0\ndjangorestframework-simplejwt==5.3.0\ndnspython==2.9.0\ndocker==7.2.0\neasyocr==1.7.2\nfastapi==0.115.6\nfilelock==3.32.3\nflake8==7.3.0\nfonttools==4.66.0\nfreezegun==1.5.5\nfrozenlist==1.8.0\nfsspec==2026.7.0\nfuture==1.0.0\ngevent==26.9.0\ngoogle-api-core==2.42.0\ngoogle-auth==2.61.0\ngoogle-cloud-core==2.8.0\ngoogle-cloud-firestore==2.32.0\ngoogle-cloud-storage==3.15.0\ngoogle-crc32c==1.9.0\ngoogle-resumable-media==2.11.0\ngoogleapis-common-protos==1.75.5\ngotrue==2.12.4\ngraphviz==0.21\ngreenlet==3.5.6\ngroq==0.28.0\ngrpcio==1.84.0\ngrpcio-status==1.84.0\nh11==0.16.0\nh2==4.4.1\nhpack==4.2.1\nhttpcore==1.0.9\nhttptools==0.8.0\nhttpx==0.27.2\nhyperframe==6.1.0\nidentify==2.6.19\nidna==3.19\nImageIO==2.37.4\niniconfig==2.3.0\nisodate==0.7.2\nisort==9.0.2\nJinja2==3.1.6\njmespath==1.1.0\njoblib==1.6.0\nkiwisolver==1.5.1\nkombu==5.7.0b1\nlazy-loader==0.5\nlibrt==0.16.0\nlightgbm==4.7.0\nMarkupSafe==3.0.3\nmatplotlib==3.11.2\nmccabe==0.7.0\nmoto==5.2.3\nmpmath==1.3.0\nmsal==1.39.0\nmsal-extensions==1.3.1\nmsgpack==1.1.2\nmsgspec==0.21.1\nmultidict==6.7.1\nmypy==2.3.1\nmypy_extensions==1.1.0\nnarwhals==2.26.0\nnetworkx==3.6.1\nninja==1.13.0\nnodeenv==1.10.0\nnumpy==2.2.2\nopencv-python==4.10.0.84\nopencv-python-headless==5.0.0.93\nopentelemetry-api==1.44.0\nopentelemetry-sdk==1.44.0\nopentelemetry-semantic-conventions==0.65b0\npackaging==26.3\npandas==3.0.6\npathspec==1.1.1\npdf2image==1.17.0\npdfminer.six==20231228\npdfplumber==0.11.4\npillow==11.1.0\nplatformdirs==4.11.3\nplotly==7.1.0\npluggy==1.6.0\npostgrest==2.31.0\npre_commit==4.6.2\nprompt_toolkit==3.0.53\npropcache==0.5.2\nproto-plus==1.29.0\nprotobuf==7.36.2\npsutil==7.2.2\npsycopg2-binary==2.9.12\npyArango==2.1.1\npyarrow==25.0.1\npyasn1==0.6.4\npyasn1_modules==0.4.2\npyclipper==1.4.0\npycodestyle==2.14.0\npycparser==3.0\npydantic==2.13.5\npydantic-settings==2.7.1\npydantic_core==2.46.5\npyflakes==3.4.0\nPygments==2.20.0\nPyJWT==2.13.0\npymongo==4.15.3\npymupdf==1.28.2\npyparsing==3.3.3\npypdfium2==5.13.0\npyproject-api==1.11.0\npyright==1.1.414\npytesseract==0.3.13\npytest==9.1.1\npytest-asyncio==1.4.0\npytest-celery==1.3.0\npytest-click==1.1.0\npytest-cov==7.1.0\npytest-order==1.5.0\npytest-rerunfailures==16.7\npytest-subtests==0.15.0\npytest-timeout==2.4.0\npytest_docker_tools==3.1.10\npython-bidi==0.6.11\npython-crontab==3.4.0\npython-dateutil==2.9.0.post0\npython-decouple==3.8\npython-discovery==1.5.2\npython-dotenv==1.0.1\npython-memcached==1.62\npython-multipart==0.0.20\npytokens==0.4.1\npytz==2026.3.post1\npywin32==312\nPyYAML==6.0.3\nRapidFuzz==3.14.6\nrealtime==2.31.0\nredis==6.4.0\nregex==2026.9.29\nrequests==2.34.2\nresponses==0.26.3\ns3transfer==0.19.0\nscikit-image==0.26.0\nscikit-learn==1.9.1\nscipy==1.18.1\nsetuptools==84.0.0\nshapely==2.1.2\nsix==1.17.0\nsniffio==1.3.1\nsoupsieve==2.9.2\nSQLAlchemy==2.1.4\nsqlparse==0.6.0\nstarlette==0.41.3\nstorage3==2.31.0\nStrEnum==0.4.15\nsupabase==2.31.0\nsupabase-auth==2.31.0\nsupabase-functions==2.31.0\nsupafunc==0.7.0\nsympy==1.14.0\ntenacity==9.2.1\nthreadpoolctl==3.7.0\ntifffile==2026.8.23\ntomli_w==1.2.0\ntorch==2.14.0\ntorchvision==0.29.0\ntox==4.61.1\ntqdm==4.70.1\ntwilio==9.4.5\ntyping-inspection==0.4.4\ntyping_extensions==4.16.0\ntzdata==2026.4\ntzlocal==5.4.4\nurllib3==1.26.20\nuv==0.13.5\nuvicorn==0.34.0\nvine==5.1.0\nvirtualenv==21.7.4\nwatchfiles==1.2.0\nwcwidth==0.8.2\nwebsockets==15.0.1\nWerkzeug==3.1.9\nxmltodict==1.0.0\nyarl==1.24.5\nzope.event==6.2\nzope.interface==8.6\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n### Other Dependencies\n\n<details>\n<p>\n\n* Redis server running locally on port `6390`.\n* Redis Python client (`redis-py`) version `6.4.0`.\n* `fakeredis[lua]` for the unit regression test that executes the actual Lua script.\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n## Minimally Reproducible Test Case\n\nThe following standalone reproduction uses a real Redis server and injects a duplicate header completion from inside `callback.delay()`.\n\nThis deterministically exercises the window between the original chord completion and chord cleanup.\n\nA Redis server must be running on `localhost:6390`.\n\n<details>\n<p>\n\n```python\nfrom types import SimpleNamespace as NS\nfrom unittest.mock import patch\n\nfrom celery import Celery, states\nfrom celery.canvas import signature\n\n\napp = Celery(\n    'x',\n    set_as_current=True,\n    broker='memory://',\n    backend='redis://localhost:6390/0',\n)\n\n\n@app.task(name='body')\ndef body(r):\n    return r\n\n\nb = app.backend\nb.client.flushall()\n\ngid = 'g1'\nbody_sig = signature('body', app=app)\nbody_sig.freeze()\n\nn = 3\nb.set_chord_size(gid, n)\n\nfired = []\n\n\ndef mk(i):\n    return NS(\n        id=f't{i}',\n        group=gid,\n        group_index=i,\n        chord=body_sig,\n    )\n\n\ndup_done = [False]\n\n\ndef fake_delay(self, *args, **kwargs):\n    fired.append(args)\n\n    if not dup_done[0]:\n        dup_done[0] = True\n\n        # Simulate a redelivered copy of header task 0\n        # finishing while the chord body is being dispatched.\n        b.on_chord_part_return(\n            mk(0),\n            states.SUCCESS,\n            'r0',\n        )\n\n\nwith patch.object(type(body_sig), 'delay', fake_delay):\n    b.on_chord_part_return(mk(0), states.SUCCESS, 'r0')\n    b.on_chord_part_return(mk(1), states.SUCCESS, 'r1')\n    b.on_chord_part_return(mk(2), states.SUCCESS, 'r2')\n\n\nprint('callback fired', len(fired), 'times')\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n### Reproduction Results\n\n**Actual behavior on unmodified `main`:**\n\n```text\ncallback fired 2 times\n```\n\n**Expected behavior:**\n\n```text\ncallback fired 1 times\n```\n\nThe reproduction uses the real Redis result backend and invokes the actual `RedisBackend.on_chord_part_return()` implementation.\n\nThe duplicate completion is injected synchronously while the original callback dispatch is in progress, making the race deterministic without relying on timing or sleeps.\n\n### Additional Regression Test\n\nThe issue can also be reproduced in the Redis backend unit tests using `fakeredis[lua]`.\n\nThe following test exercises the same race through the actual Lua script:\n\n```python\ndef test_on_chord_part_return__duplicate_part_while_sending_body(self):\n    # A copy of a header task, e.g. redelivered after its visibility\n    # timeout, can finish while the body of the chord is being sent.\n    # It must not send the body a second time.\n    with self.chord_context(3) as (tasks, _, callback):\n        def finish_copy(*args, **kwargs):\n            if callback.delay.call_count == 1:\n                self.b.on_chord_part_return(\n                    tasks[0].request, states.SUCCESS, 10)\n\n        callback.delay.side_effect = finish_copy\n        self.b.on_chord_part_return(tasks[0].request, states.SUCCESS, 10)\n        self.b.on_chord_part_return(tasks[1].request, states.SUCCESS, 20)\n        self.b.on_chord_part_return(tasks[2].request, states.SUCCESS, 30)\n        callback.delay.assert_called_once_with([10, 20, 30])\n```\n\nThe test fails against the unmodified implementation and passes with the proposed fix.\n\n# Expected Behavior\n\nThe Redis result backend should dispatch a chord body only once when all header tasks have completed.\n\nIf a duplicate completion of an already-recorded header task arrives while the original completion is processing or dispatching the callback, it should not cause another callback dispatch.\n\nThe operation that determines that the chord has completed should atomically claim the chord, preventing subsequent duplicate completions from independently triggering the callback.\n\n# Actual Behavior\n\n`CHORD_PART_RETURN_LUA` already prevents duplicate header task results from being inserted into the chord result structure.\n\nIt tracks completed task IDs, ensuring that a header task is counted only once, even if it executes multiple times and returns different results.\n\nHowever, the script does not atomically claim the chord when the final header task completes.\n\nIn `RedisBackend.on_chord_part_return()`, the chord keys (`.j`, `.t`, `.s`, `.d`) are deleted in the `finally` block, after callback dispatch.\n\nFor complex headers, this cleanup also occurs after `GroupResult.restore()` and `join_native()`.\n\nThis creates a window in which another copy of an already-completed header task can invoke `on_chord_part_return()`.\n\nAlthough the Lua script recognizes the task as a duplicate and does not insert its result again, `readycount` still equals the expected total and the chord size key `.s` still exists.\n\nConsequently, the Python-side completion condition succeeds again, and the chord body is dispatched a second time.\n\nThis can occur with late-acknowledged task redelivery after worker failure or Redis broker visibility-timeout redelivery, provided the duplicate completion arrives during this window.\n\n### Impact\n\nThe chord body can execute more than once, potentially causing duplicate side effects, including:\n\n* Duplicate payments or charges.\n* Duplicate emails or notifications.\n* Duplicate database writes.\n* Repeated external API calls.\n* Other non-idempotent operations performed by chord callbacks.\n\n### Root Cause\n\nDuplicate result recording and chord completion claiming are currently separate concerns.\n\nThe Lua script atomically prevents a header task from being counted more than once, but it does not atomically ensure that only one invocation can claim the completed chord.\n\nAs a result, multiple invocations can observe the same completed state before the Python-side cleanup removes the chord keys.\n\n### Suggested Fix\n\nThe Lua script could atomically claim successful chord completion by deleting the chord size key (`.s`) in the same execution that determines that all header tasks have completed.\n\nFor example, when:\n\n```lua\nreadycount == tonumber(size) + (tonumber(totaldiff) or 0)\n```\n\nthe script could delete `.s` before returning.\n\nThe completing invocation can still return the size value read before deletion, allowing the existing Python-side code to calculate the total as it does currently.\n\nSubsequent duplicate completions would then observe that the chord size no longer exists and would not trigger another callback dispatch.\n\nThe existing error-handling path already uses deletion of the chord size to prevent subsequent copies of header tasks from failing the chord again.\n\nApplying equivalent protection to successful completion would prevent this duplicate-dispatch race.\n\nThis would provide an at-most-once callback dispatch claim. A separate consideration is recovery if a worker crashes after claiming the chord but before publishing the callback.\n", "hints_text": "@ChristianMurphy can you verify?", "created_at": "2026-11-01T10:42:33Z"}
{"repo": "celery/celery", "pull_number": 10975, "instance_id": "celery__celery-10975", "issue_numbers": ["10725"], "base_commit": "57cb15ff99ab47a6e72d5a70c5522b8fabb59a57", "patch": "diff --git a/celery/app/amqp.py b/celery/app/amqp.py\nindex 9630271ec80..a2c9764183a 100644\n--- a/celery/app/amqp.py\n+++ b/celery/app/amqp.py\n@@ -2,14 +2,17 @@\n import numbers\n from collections import namedtuple\n from collections.abc import Mapping\n+from contextvars import ContextVar\n from weakref import WeakValueDictionary\n \n from kombu import Connection, Consumer, Exchange, Producer, Queue, pools\n from kombu.common import Broadcast\n-from kombu.utils.functional import maybe_list\n+from kombu.exceptions import OperationalError\n+from kombu.utils.functional import maybe_list, retry_over_time\n from kombu.utils.objects import cached_property\n \n from celery import signals\n+from celery.utils.log import get_logger\n from celery.utils.nodenames import anon_nodename\n from celery.utils.saferepr import saferepr\n from celery.utils.text import indent as textindent\n@@ -17,6 +20,12 @@\n \n from . import routes as _routes\n \n+logger = get_logger(__name__)\n+\n+# Default cap for worker callback publishes when the policy leaves max_retries\n+# unset (None would retry forever in apply_async() on the client).\n+_CALLBACK_PUBLISH_MAX_RETRIES = 3\n+\n __all__ = ('AMQP', 'Queues', 'task_message')\n \n #: earliest date supported by time.mktime.\n@@ -31,6 +40,31 @@\n task_message = namedtuple('task_message',\n                           ('headers', 'properties', 'body', 'sent_event'))\n \n+# Set while AMQP._retry_publish sends a callback, so that each of its\n+# messages is retried on its own, waiting as its retry policy says.\n+_callback_publish_retry = ContextVar('_callback_publish_retry', default=False)\n+\n+\n+def _publish_retry_errors(retry_policy, connection):\n+    retry_errors = retry_policy.get('retry_errors') or ()\n+    if retry_errors and not isinstance(retry_errors, tuple):\n+        retry_errors = tuple(retry_errors)\n+    return (\n+        OperationalError,\n+        *connection.recoverable_connection_errors,\n+        *retry_errors,\n+    )\n+\n+\n+def _callback_publish_max_retries(retry_policy, default_policy):\n+    max_retries = retry_policy.get('max_retries', _CALLBACK_PUBLISH_MAX_RETRIES)\n+    if max_retries is not None:\n+        return max_retries\n+    capped = default_policy.get('max_retries', _CALLBACK_PUBLISH_MAX_RETRIES)\n+    if capped is None:\n+        capped = _CALLBACK_PUBLISH_MAX_RETRIES\n+    return capped\n+\n \n def utf8dict(d, encoding='utf-8'):\n     return {k.decode(encoding) if isinstance(k, bytes) else k: v\n@@ -262,6 +296,16 @@ def __init__(self, app):\n         }\n         self.app._conf.bind_to(self._handle_conf_update)\n \n+    def _retry_publish(self, publish, *args, **kwargs):\n+        \"\"\"Publish a callback, waiting between its publish retries.\"\"\"\n+        if self.app.conf.task_always_eager:\n+            return publish(*args, **kwargs)\n+        token = _callback_publish_retry.set(True)\n+        try:\n+            return publish(*args, **kwargs)\n+        finally:\n+            _callback_publish_retry.reset(token)\n+\n     @cached_property\n     def create_task_message(self):\n         return self.task_protocols[self.app.conf.task_protocol]\n@@ -563,8 +607,7 @@ def send_task_message(producer, name, message,\n                 declare=declare, headers=headers2,\n                 properties=properties, retry_policy=_rp,\n             )\n-            ret = producer.publish(\n-                body,\n+            publish_kwargs = dict(\n                 exchange=exchange,\n                 routing_key=routing_key,\n                 serializer=serializer or default_serializer,\n@@ -575,6 +618,47 @@ def send_task_message(producer, name, message,\n                 timeout=timeout, confirm_timeout=confirm_timeout,\n                 **properties\n             )\n+            if retry and _callback_publish_retry.get():\n+                # The parent task already ran, so retry only this message,\n+                # waiting as the policy says and reconnecting when needed.\n+                max_retries = _callback_publish_max_retries(_rp, default_policy)\n+                conn = producer.connection\n+\n+                def on_publish_retry(exc, intervals, retries):\n+                    if retries == 0 and _rp.get('max_retries') is None:\n+                        logger.warning(\n+                            'Unbounded max_retries for worker callback %r; '\n+                            'using %s retries instead.', name, max_retries,\n+                        )\n+                    producer.revive(conn)\n+                    wait = next(intervals)\n+                    logger.warning(\n+                        'Retrying refused callback publish for %r '\n+                        '(%s/%s, waiting %ss): %r',\n+                        name, retries + 1, max_retries, wait, exc,\n+                    )\n+                    return wait\n+\n+                try:\n+                    ret = retry_over_time(\n+                        producer.publish,\n+                        _publish_retry_errors(_rp, conn),\n+                        args=(body,),\n+                        kwargs=dict(publish_kwargs, retry=False),\n+                        errback=on_publish_retry,\n+                        max_retries=max_retries,\n+                        interval_start=_rp.get('interval_start', 0),\n+                        interval_step=_rp.get('interval_step', 0.2),\n+                        interval_max=_rp.get('interval_max', 1),\n+                    )\n+                except Exception as exc:\n+                    logger.warning(\n+                        'Giving up refused callback publish for %r: %r',\n+                        name, exc,\n+                    )\n+                    raise\n+            else:\n+                ret = producer.publish(body, **publish_kwargs)\n             send_after_publish(\n                 sender=name, body=body, headers=headers2,\n                 exchange=exchange, routing_key=routing_key,\ndiff --git a/celery/app/builtins.py b/celery/app/builtins.py\nindex 1c836c6bc59..144df0b8740 100644\n--- a/celery/app/builtins.py\n+++ b/celery/app/builtins.py\n@@ -98,7 +98,7 @@ def unlock_chord(self, group_id, callback, interval=None,\n             app.backend.chord_error_from_stack(callback=callback, exc=chord_error)\n         else:\n             try:\n-                callback.delay(ret)\n+                app.amqp._retry_publish(callback.delay, ret)\n             except Exception as exc:  # pylint: disable=broad-except\n                 logger.exception('Chord %r raised: %r', group_id, exc)\n                 chord_error = _create_chord_error_with_cause(message=f'Callback error: {exc!r}', original_exc=exc)\ndiff --git a/celery/app/trace.py b/celery/app/trace.py\nindex 3a0f07afb37..7d58a202abe 100644\n--- a/celery/app/trace.py\n+++ b/celery/app/trace.py\n@@ -11,7 +11,7 @@\n from warnings import warn\n \n from billiard.einfo import ExceptionInfo, ExceptionWithTraceback\n-from kombu.exceptions import EncodeError\n+from kombu.exceptions import EncodeError, OperationalError\n from kombu.serialization import loads as loads_message\n from kombu.serialization import prepare_accept_content\n from kombu.utils.encoding import safe_repr, safe_str\n@@ -447,27 +447,27 @@ def _dispatch_callbacks_and_chain(\n                     else:\n                         sigs.append(sig)\n                 for group_ in groups:\n-                    group_.apply_async(\n-                        (retval,),\n+                    app.amqp._retry_publish(\n+                        group_.apply_async, (retval,),\n                         parent_id=parent_id, root_id=root_id,\n                         priority=priority,\n                     )\n                 if sigs:\n-                    group(sigs, app=app).apply_async(\n-                        (retval,),\n+                    app.amqp._retry_publish(\n+                        group(sigs, app=app).apply_async, (retval,),\n                         parent_id=parent_id, root_id=root_id,\n                         priority=priority,\n                     )\n             else:\n-                signature(callbacks[0], app=app).apply_async(\n-                    (retval,),\n+                app.amqp._retry_publish(\n+                    signature(callbacks[0], app=app).apply_async, (retval,),\n                     parent_id=parent_id, root_id=root_id,\n                     priority=priority,\n                 )\n         if chain:\n             _chsig = signature(chain[-1], app=app)\n-            _chsig.apply_async(\n-                (retval,), chain=chain[:-1],\n+            app.amqp._retry_publish(\n+                _chsig.apply_async, (retval,), chain=chain[:-1],\n                 parent_id=parent_id, root_id=root_id,\n                 priority=priority,\n             )\n@@ -608,6 +608,7 @@ def trace_task(uuid, args, kwargs, request=None):\n                 except BaseException:\n                     raise\n                 else:\n+                    callbacks_sent = False\n                     try:\n                         # callback tasks must be applied before the result is\n                         # stored, so that result.children is populated.\n@@ -622,10 +623,13 @@ def trace_task(uuid, args, kwargs, request=None):\n                             parent_id=uuid, root_id=root_id,\n                             priority=task_priority,\n                         )\n+                        callbacks_sent = True\n                         task.backend.mark_as_done(\n                             uuid, retval, task_request, publish_result,\n                         )\n-                    except EncodeError as exc:\n+                    except (EncodeError, OperationalError) as exc:\n+                        if callbacks_sent and isinstance(exc, OperationalError):\n+                            raise\n                         I, R, state, retval = on_error(task_request, exc)\n                         # MEMORY LEAK FIX: Clear traceback frames to prevent memory retention (Issue #8882)\n                         traceback_clear(exc)\ndiff --git a/celery/backends/base.py b/celery/backends/base.py\nindex b1b98a8ea54..4466bc81ed1 100644\n--- a/celery/backends/base.py\n+++ b/celery/backends/base.py\n@@ -1483,7 +1483,7 @@ def on_chord_part_return(self, request, state, result, **kwargs):\n                 self.chord_error_from_stack(callback=callback, exc=chord_error)\n             else:\n                 try:\n-                    callback.delay(ret)\n+                    app.amqp._retry_publish(callback.delay, ret)\n                 except Exception as exc:  # pylint: disable=broad-except\n                     logger.exception('Chord %r raised: %r', gid, exc)\n                     chord_error = _create_chord_error_with_cause(\ndiff --git a/celery/backends/gcs.py b/celery/backends/gcs.py\nindex 2a6eb4f9552..9074ceaef68 100644\n--- a/celery/backends/gcs.py\n+++ b/celery/backends/gcs.py\n@@ -302,7 +302,7 @@ def on_chord_part_return(self, request, state, result, **kwargs):\n                 self.chord_error_from_stack(callback, chord_error)\n             else:\n                 try:\n-                    callback.delay(ret)\n+                    app.amqp._retry_publish(callback.delay, ret)\n                 except Exception as exc:  # pylint: disable=broad-except\n                     logger.exception('Chord %r raised: %r', gid, exc)\n                     self.chord_error_from_stack(\ndiff --git a/celery/backends/redis.py b/celery/backends/redis.py\nindex d9904ba3648..d5011fe75f4 100644\n--- a/celery/backends/redis.py\n+++ b/celery/backends/redis.py\n@@ -889,7 +889,7 @@ def on_chord_part_return(self, request, state, result,\n                             resl, = pipeline.execute()\n                         resl = [unpack(tup, decode) for tup in resl]\n                     try:\n-                        callback.delay(resl)\n+                        app.amqp._retry_publish(callback.delay, resl)\n                     except Exception as exc:  # pylint: disable=broad-except\n                         logger.exception(\n                             'Chord callback for %r raised: %r', request.group, exc)\ndiff --git a/docs/userguide/calling.rst b/docs/userguide/calling.rst\nindex 03f6e702ed3..12fe7fd7609 100644\n--- a/docs/userguide/calling.rst\n+++ b/docs/userguide/calling.rst\n@@ -372,6 +372,25 @@ Celery will automatically retry sending messages in the event of connection\n failure, and retry behavior can be configured -- like how often to retry, or a maximum\n number of retries -- or disabled all together.\n \n+Workers also wait between retries when a broker refuses a link, chain step,\n+or chord-body publish. Each refused message is retried on its own using\n+:setting:`task_publish_retry` and :setting:`task_publish_retry_policy`, or the\n+``retry`` / ``retry_policy`` options on that callback signature. The worker\n+repeats the publication rather than the parent task's body, and the pool slot\n+(or the whole solo worker) stays busy until the policy gives up or the broker\n+accepts the message. With the default policy, retries stop after about 0.6 s.\n+Once the policy is exhausted, the task fails through the normal failure and\n+errback handling (including for :meth:`~celery.app.task.Task.apply`).\n+\n+.. versionchanged:: 5.7\n+\n+    Refused callback publishes are retried per message with the callback's own\n+    ``retry`` / ``retry_policy`` options. An unbounded ``max_retries`` policy\n+    uses the application's finite retry limit, or three retries if that limit\n+    is also unbounded. Exhaustion uses the normal failure path instead of\n+    emitting :signal:`task_internal_error`; :meth:`~celery.app.task.Task.apply`\n+    returns a :state:`FAILURE` result instead of raising the publish error.\n+\n To disable retry you can set the ``retry`` execution option to :const:`False`:\n \n .. code-block:: python\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/t/smoke/tests/test_callback_publish_retry.py b/t/smoke/tests/test_callback_publish_retry.py\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 00000000000..da5cc0a3b55\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/t/smoke/tests/test_callback_publish_retry.py\n@@ -0,0 +1,207 @@\n+from __future__ import annotations\n+\n+from contextlib import contextmanager\n+from time import monotonic, sleep\n+\n+import pytest\n+from amqp import Channel\n+from amqp.exceptions import MessageNacked\n+from kombu import Producer, Queue\n+from pytest_celery import CeleryBackendCluster, CeleryBrokerCluster, RabbitMQTestBroker, RedisTestBackend\n+\n+from celery import Celery, chain, chord, states, uuid\n+from celery.contrib.testing.worker import start_worker\n+from celery.result import allow_join_result\n+\n+pytestmark = pytest.mark.timeout(180)\n+\n+\n+@pytest.fixture\n+def celery_broker_cluster(celery_rabbitmq_broker: RabbitMQTestBroker) -> CeleryBrokerCluster:\n+    cluster = CeleryBrokerCluster(celery_rabbitmq_broker)\n+    yield cluster\n+    cluster.teardown()\n+\n+\n+@pytest.fixture\n+def celery_backend_cluster(celery_redis_backend: RedisTestBackend) -> CeleryBackendCluster:\n+    cluster = CeleryBackendCluster(celery_redis_backend)\n+    yield cluster\n+    cluster.teardown()\n+\n+\n+@pytest.fixture\n+def callback_app(celery_setup_app, tmp_path, acks_late, queue_type, monkeypatch):\n+    name = f'callback-retry-{uuid()}'\n+    parent_queue = Queue(name, exchange='', routing_key=name,\n+                         queue_arguments={'x-queue-type': queue_type})\n+    blocked = Queue(f'{name}-full', exchange='', routing_key=f'{name}-full',\n+                    queue_arguments={'x-max-length': 1, 'x-overflow': 'reject-publish'})\n+    app = Celery(name, broker=celery_setup_app.conf.broker_url,\n+                 backend=celery_setup_app.conf.result_backend, set_as_current=False)\n+    app.conf.update(\n+        broker_transport_options={'confirm_publish': True},\n+        task_queues=(parent_queue, blocked),\n+        task_default_queue=name,\n+        task_default_exchange='',\n+        task_default_routing_key=name,\n+        task_routes={'callback_retry.body': {'queue': blocked.name}},\n+        task_publish_retry_policy={\n+            'max_retries': 2, 'interval_start': 0.2,\n+            'interval_step': 0, 'interval_max': 0.2,\n+        },\n+        worker_prefetch_multiplier=1,\n+        worker_deduplicate_successful_tasks=True,\n+    )\n+\n+    @app.task(name='callback_retry.parent', shared=False, acks_late=acks_late)\n+    def parent():\n+        with (tmp_path / 'parent').open('a') as stream:\n+            stream.write('run\\n')\n+        return 4\n+\n+    @app.task(name='callback_retry.body', shared=False)\n+    def body(value):\n+        with (tmp_path / 'body').open('a') as stream:\n+            stream.write(f'{value}\\n')\n+        return value\n+\n+    @app.task(name='callback_retry.errback', shared=False)\n+    def errback(task_id):\n+        with (tmp_path / 'errback').open('a') as stream:\n+            stream.write(f'{task_id}\\n')\n+\n+    publish = Channel.basic_publish_confirm\n+\n+    def record_refusal(channel, *args, **kwargs):\n+        try:\n+            return publish(channel, *args, **kwargs)\n+        except MessageNacked:\n+            if kwargs.get('routing_key') == blocked.name:\n+                with (tmp_path / 'refusals').open('a') as stream:\n+                    stream.write('refused\\n')\n+            raise\n+\n+    # Observe real publisher confirms, including in prefork children. The\n+    # publish signals describe a logical message, not each transport attempt.\n+    monkeypatch.setattr(Channel, 'basic_publish_confirm', record_refusal)\n+    try:\n+        with app.connection_for_write() as connection:\n+            blocked(connection).declare()\n+            Producer(connection).publish('filler', exchange='', routing_key=blocked.name, serializer='json')\n+        yield app\n+    finally:\n+        with app.connection_for_write() as connection:\n+            for queue in (parent_queue, blocked):\n+                queue(connection).delete()\n+        app.close()\n+\n+\n+def _submit(app, kind):\n+    parent = app.tasks['callback_retry.parent']\n+    body = app.tasks['callback_retry.body'].s()\n+    errback = app.tasks['callback_retry.errback'].s()\n+    if kind == 'chord':\n+        result = chord([parent.s()], body.on_error(errback)).apply_async()\n+        return result.parent.results[0], result\n+    if kind == 'chain':\n+        result = chain(parent.s(), body).on_error(errback).apply_async()\n+        return result.parent, result\n+    body_result = body.freeze()\n+    return parent.apply_async(link=body, link_error=errback), body_result\n+\n+\n+def _wait_for_lines(path, count, timeout=10):\n+    deadline = monotonic() + timeout\n+    while monotonic() < deadline:\n+        if path.exists() and len(path.read_text().splitlines()) >= count:\n+            return\n+        sleep(0.01)\n+    pytest.fail(f'{path.name} did not reach {count} entries')\n+\n+\n+@contextmanager\n+def _worker(app, pool, queue):\n+    with start_worker(app, pool=pool, concurrency=1, queues=[queue],\n+                      hostname=f'callback-{uuid()}@test',\n+                      perform_ping_check=False, shutdown_timeout=30):\n+        yield\n+\n+\n+@pytest.mark.parametrize('queue_type', ['classic'])\n+@pytest.mark.parametrize('acks_late', [False, True])\n+@pytest.mark.parametrize('pool', ['solo', 'threads', 'prefork'])\n+@pytest.mark.parametrize('kind', ['link', 'chain', 'chord'])\n+def test_transient_callback_refusal(callback_app, tmp_path, pool, kind):\n+    app = callback_app\n+    app.conf.task_publish_retry_policy = {\n+        'max_retries': 2, 'interval_start': 1, 'interval_step': 0, 'interval_max': 1,\n+    }\n+    parent_queue = app.conf.task_default_queue\n+    blocked = app.amqp.queues[app.conf.task_routes['callback_retry.body']['queue']]\n+    with _worker(app, pool, parent_queue):\n+        parent, result = _submit(app, kind)\n+        _wait_for_lines(tmp_path / 'refusals', 1)\n+        # Keep the queue full briefly: immediate retries would all fail.\n+        sleep(0.5)\n+        with app.connection_for_write() as connection:\n+            filler = blocked(connection).get(no_ack=False)\n+            assert filler.payload == 'filler'\n+            filler.ack()\n+        with allow_join_result():\n+            assert parent.get(timeout=10) == 4\n+\n+    with _worker(app, pool, blocked.name):\n+        with allow_join_result():\n+            assert result.get(timeout=10) == ([4] if kind == 'chord' else 4)\n+    assert (tmp_path / 'parent').read_text().splitlines() == ['run']\n+    assert (tmp_path / 'body').read_text().splitlines() == [str([4] if kind == 'chord' else 4)]\n+    assert not (tmp_path / 'errback').exists()\n+\n+\n+@pytest.mark.parametrize('queue_type', ['quorum'])\n+@pytest.mark.parametrize('acks_late', [False, True])\n+@pytest.mark.parametrize('pool', ['solo', 'threads', 'prefork'])\n+@pytest.mark.parametrize('kind', ['link', 'chain', 'chord'])\n+def test_permanent_callback_refusal(callback_app, tmp_path, pool, kind):\n+    app = callback_app\n+    with _worker(app, pool, app.conf.task_default_queue):\n+        parent, body = _submit(app, kind)\n+        result = body if kind == 'chord' else parent\n+        with allow_join_result():\n+            result.get(timeout=10, propagate=False)\n+            if kind == 'chain':\n+                body.get(timeout=10, propagate=False)\n+        assert result.state == states.FAILURE\n+        if kind == 'chain':\n+            assert body.state == states.FAILURE\n+        _wait_for_lines(tmp_path / 'errback', 1, timeout=30)\n+    assert (tmp_path / 'parent').read_text().splitlines() == ['run']\n+    assert (tmp_path / 'refusals').read_text().splitlines() == ['refused'] * 3\n+    assert len((tmp_path / 'errback').read_text().splitlines()) == 1\n+    assert not (tmp_path / 'body').exists()\n+\n+\n+@pytest.mark.parametrize('queue_type', ['classic'])\n+@pytest.mark.parametrize('acks_late', [False, True])\n+@pytest.mark.parametrize('pool', ['solo', 'threads', 'prefork'])\n+def test_refused_link_does_not_republish_accepted_link(callback_app, tmp_path, pool):\n+    app = callback_app\n+    parent = app.tasks['callback_retry.parent']\n+    body = app.tasks['callback_retry.body']\n+    accepted = body.s().set(queue=app.conf.task_default_queue)\n+    refused = body.s()\n+    errback = app.tasks['callback_retry.errback'].s()\n+    with _worker(app, pool, app.conf.task_default_queue):\n+        result = parent.apply_async(link=[accepted, refused], link_error=errback)\n+        with allow_join_result():\n+            result.get(timeout=10, propagate=False)\n+        assert result.state == states.FAILURE\n+        _wait_for_lines(tmp_path / 'errback', 1, timeout=30)\n+        # A final task on the same FIFO queue ensures all accepted links ran.\n+        with allow_join_result():\n+            assert body.apply_async(('drained',), queue=app.conf.task_default_queue).get(timeout=10) == 'drained'\n+    assert (tmp_path / 'body').read_text().splitlines() == ['4', 'drained']\n+    assert (tmp_path / 'parent').read_text().splitlines() == ['run']\n+    assert (tmp_path / 'refusals').read_text().splitlines() == ['refused'] * 3\n+    assert len((tmp_path / 'errback').read_text().splitlines()) == 1\ndiff --git a/t/unit/app/test_amqp_callback_publish.py b/t/unit/app/test_amqp_callback_publish.py\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 00000000000..ffea6bf0953\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/t/unit/app/test_amqp_callback_publish.py\n@@ -0,0 +1,265 @@\n+from unittest.mock import Mock, patch\n+\n+import pytest\n+from kombu.exceptions import EncodeError, OperationalError, TimeoutError\n+\n+from celery import signals, uuid\n+from celery.app.amqp import _callback_publish_retry\n+\n+\n+def _callback_message(app):\n+    return app.amqp.as_task_v2(uuid(), 'foo', create_sent_event=False)\n+\n+\n+def _producer(side_effect=None):\n+    producer = Mock(name='producer')\n+    producer.connection.recoverable_connection_errors = ()\n+    if side_effect is not None:\n+        producer.publish.side_effect = side_effect\n+    return producer\n+\n+\n+def _publish_in_callback_context(app, publish):\n+    return app.amqp._retry_publish(publish)\n+\n+\n+@pytest.mark.parametrize('error', [OperationalError('refused'), TimeoutError('slow')])\n+@pytest.mark.parametrize('retry_errors', [(TimeoutError,), [TimeoutError]])\n+def test_callback_publish_honours_retry_errors(app, error, retry_errors):\n+    app.conf.task_publish_retry_policy = {\n+        'max_retries': 1, 'interval_start': 0, 'interval_step': 0, 'interval_max': 0,\n+        'retry_errors': retry_errors,\n+    }\n+    message = _callback_message(app)\n+    producer = _producer([error, None])\n+\n+    def publish():\n+        app.amqp.send_task_message(producer, 'foo', message)\n+\n+    _publish_in_callback_context(app, publish)\n+    assert producer.publish.call_count == 2\n+\n+\n+@pytest.mark.parametrize('error', [EncodeError('bad payload'), ValueError('bad callback')])\n+def test_callback_publish_does_not_retry_permanent_errors(app, error):\n+    message = _callback_message(app)\n+    producer = _producer(error)\n+\n+    def publish():\n+        app.amqp.send_task_message(producer, 'foo', message)\n+\n+    with pytest.raises(type(error)):\n+        _publish_in_callback_context(app, publish)\n+    producer.publish.assert_called_once()\n+\n+\n+def test_callback_publish_can_bypass_retry(app):\n+    app.conf.task_publish_retry = False\n+    message = _callback_message(app)\n+    producer = _producer(OperationalError('refused'))\n+\n+    def publish():\n+        app.amqp.send_task_message(producer, 'foo', message)\n+\n+    with pytest.raises(OperationalError):\n+        _publish_in_callback_context(app, publish)\n+    producer.publish.assert_called_once()\n+\n+\n+def test_callback_publish_skipped_when_always_eager(app):\n+    app.conf.task_always_eager = True\n+    publish = Mock(side_effect=_callback_publish_retry.get)\n+    assert app.amqp._retry_publish(publish) is False\n+    publish.assert_called_once_with()\n+    assert _callback_publish_retry.get() is False\n+\n+\n+def test_callback_publish_retries_each_message(app):\n+    app.conf.task_publish_retry_policy = {\n+        'max_retries': 2, 'interval_start': 1, 'interval_step': 1, 'interval_max': 2,\n+    }\n+    messages = [_callback_message(app) for _ in range(2)]\n+    producers = [\n+        _producer(None),\n+        _producer([OperationalError('refused'), OperationalError('refused'), None]),\n+    ]\n+\n+    def publish():\n+        app.amqp.send_task_message(producers[0], 'foo', messages[0], priority=3)\n+        app.amqp.send_task_message(producers[1], 'foo', messages[1], priority=3)\n+\n+    with patch('kombu.utils.functional.sleep') as sleep:\n+        _publish_in_callback_context(app, publish)\n+    assert producers[0].publish.call_count == 1\n+    assert producers[1].publish.call_count == 3\n+    assert sum(call.args[0] for call in sleep.call_args_list) == 3\n+\n+\n+def test_callback_publish_does_not_stack_with_connection_retry(app):\n+    app.conf.task_publish_retry_policy = {\n+        'max_retries': 1, 'interval_start': 0, 'interval_step': 0, 'interval_max': 0,\n+    }\n+    message = _callback_message(app)\n+    seen = []\n+\n+    def publish_side_effect(*args, **kwargs):\n+        seen.append(kwargs.get('retry'))\n+        if len(seen) < 2:\n+            raise OperationalError('refused')\n+\n+    producer = _producer(publish_side_effect)\n+\n+    def publish():\n+        app.amqp.send_task_message(producer, 'foo', message)\n+\n+    _publish_in_callback_context(app, publish)\n+    assert seen == [False, False]\n+\n+    producer = _producer(None)\n+    app.amqp.send_task_message(producer, 'foo', message)\n+    assert producer.publish.call_args[1]['retry'] is True\n+\n+\n+def test_callback_publish_stops_at_the_retry_limit(app):\n+    app.conf.task_publish_retry_policy = {\n+        'max_retries': 2, 'interval_start': 0, 'interval_step': 0, 'interval_max': 0,\n+    }\n+    message = _callback_message(app)\n+    error = OperationalError('refused')\n+    producer = _producer(error)\n+\n+    def publish():\n+        app.amqp.send_task_message(producer, 'foo', message)\n+\n+    with pytest.raises(OperationalError) as raised:\n+        _publish_in_callback_context(app, publish)\n+    assert raised.value is error\n+    assert producer.publish.call_count == 3\n+\n+\n+def test_callback_publish_honours_signature_retry_false(app):\n+    message = _callback_message(app)\n+    producer = _producer(OperationalError('refused'))\n+\n+    def publish():\n+        app.amqp.send_task_message(producer, 'foo', message, retry=False)\n+\n+    with pytest.raises(OperationalError):\n+        _publish_in_callback_context(app, publish)\n+    producer.publish.assert_called_once()\n+\n+\n+def test_callback_publish_honours_signature_retry_policy(app):\n+    message = _callback_message(app)\n+    producer = _producer([OperationalError('refused')] * 6 + [None])\n+\n+    def publish():\n+        app.amqp.send_task_message(\n+            producer, 'foo', message,\n+            retry_policy={'max_retries': 6, 'interval_start': 0,\n+                          'interval_step': 0, 'interval_max': 0},\n+        )\n+\n+    _publish_in_callback_context(app, publish)\n+    assert producer.publish.call_count == 7\n+\n+\n+def test_callback_publish_caps_unbounded_max_retries(app, caplog):\n+    app.conf.task_publish_retry_policy = {\n+        'max_retries': None, 'interval_start': 0, 'interval_step': 0, 'interval_max': 0,\n+    }\n+    message = _callback_message(app)\n+    producer = _producer([OperationalError('refused')] * 4)\n+\n+    def publish():\n+        app.amqp.send_task_message(producer, 'foo', message)\n+\n+    with pytest.raises(OperationalError):\n+        _publish_in_callback_context(app, publish)\n+    assert producer.publish.call_count == 4\n+    assert 'Unbounded max_retries for worker callback' in caplog.text\n+\n+\n+@pytest.mark.parametrize('default_limit, expected_attempts', [(2, 3), (None, 4)])\n+def test_callback_publish_caps_signature_unbounded_retries(app, default_limit, expected_attempts):\n+    app.conf.task_publish_retry_policy = {\n+        'max_retries': default_limit, 'interval_start': 0, 'interval_step': 0, 'interval_max': 0,\n+    }\n+    producer = _producer(OperationalError('refused'))\n+    message = _callback_message(app)\n+    with pytest.raises(OperationalError):\n+        app.amqp._retry_publish(\n+            app.amqp.send_task_message, producer, 'foo', message,\n+            retry_policy={'max_retries': None},\n+        )\n+    assert producer.publish.call_count == expected_attempts\n+\n+\n+def test_callback_retries_preserve_message_and_emit_publish_signals_once(app):\n+    app.conf.task_publish_retry_policy = {\n+        'max_retries': 1, 'interval_start': 0, 'interval_step': 0, 'interval_max': 0,\n+    }\n+    message = _callback_message(app)\n+    producer = _producer([OperationalError('refused'), None])\n+    before, after = Mock(), Mock()\n+    signals.before_task_publish.connect(before)\n+    signals.after_task_publish.connect(after)\n+    try:\n+        app.amqp._retry_publish(app.amqp.send_task_message, producer, 'foo', message)\n+    finally:\n+        signals.before_task_publish.disconnect(before)\n+        signals.after_task_publish.disconnect(after)\n+    before.assert_called_once()\n+    after.assert_called_once()\n+    assert producer.publish.call_count == 2\n+    first, second = producer.publish.call_args_list\n+    assert first == second\n+    assert first.kwargs['headers']['id'] == message.headers['id']\n+\n+\n+@pytest.mark.parametrize('signature_policy', [False, True])\n+@pytest.mark.parametrize('refused', [False, True])\n+def test_unbounded_policy_warns_only_on_first_refusal(app, caplog, signature_policy, refused):\n+    app.conf.task_publish_retry_policy = {\n+        'max_retries': 2 if signature_policy else None,\n+        'interval_start': 0, 'interval_step': 0, 'interval_max': 0,\n+    }\n+    producer = _producer([OperationalError('refused')] * 2 + [None] if refused else None)\n+    policy = {'max_retries': None} if signature_policy else None\n+    app.amqp._retry_publish(\n+        app.amqp.send_task_message, producer, 'foo', _callback_message(app), retry_policy=policy,\n+    )\n+    warnings = [record.getMessage() for record in caplog.records if 'Unbounded max_retries' in record.getMessage()]\n+    assert len(warnings) == int(refused)\n+    if refused:\n+        assert 'task_publish_retry_policy' not in warnings[0]\n+    else:\n+        assert not caplog.records\n+\n+\n+@pytest.mark.parametrize('signature_policy', [False, True])\n+def test_bounded_policy_does_not_warn_about_unbounded_retries(app, caplog, signature_policy):\n+    app.conf.task_publish_retry_policy = {\n+        'max_retries': None if signature_policy else 2,\n+        'interval_start': 0, 'interval_step': 0, 'interval_max': 0,\n+    }\n+    producer = _producer([OperationalError('refused'), None])\n+    policy = {'max_retries': 2} if signature_policy else None\n+    app.amqp._retry_publish(\n+        app.amqp.send_task_message, producer, 'foo', _callback_message(app), retry_policy=policy,\n+    )\n+    assert producer.publish.call_count == 2\n+    assert 'Unbounded max_retries' not in caplog.text\n+\n+\n+def test_callback_publish_revives_after_transport_connection_error(app):\n+    app.conf.task_publish_retry_policy = {\n+        'max_retries': 1, 'interval_start': 0, 'interval_step': 0, 'interval_max': 0,\n+    }\n+    producer = _producer([ConnectionResetError('connection lost'), None])\n+    connection = producer.connection\n+    connection.recoverable_connection_errors = (ConnectionResetError,)\n+    app.amqp._retry_publish(app.amqp.send_task_message, producer, 'foo', _callback_message(app))\n+    assert producer.publish.call_count == 2\n+    producer.revive.assert_called_once_with(connection)\n+    assert _callback_publish_retry.get() is False\ndiff --git a/t/unit/backends/test_dynamodb.py b/t/unit/backends/test_dynamodb.py\nindex 12520aeeb9f..32e4d4b5147 100644\n--- a/t/unit/backends/test_dynamodb.py\n+++ b/t/unit/backends/test_dynamodb.py\n@@ -2,8 +2,11 @@\n from unittest.mock import ANY, MagicMock, Mock, call, patch, sentinel\n \n import pytest\n+from kombu.exceptions import OperationalError\n+from moto import mock_aws\n \n-from celery import states, uuid\n+from celery import Celery, states, uuid\n+from celery.app.task import Context\n from celery.backends import dynamodb as module\n from celery.backends.dynamodb import DynamoDBBackend\n from celery.exceptions import ImproperlyConfigured\n@@ -11,6 +14,50 @@\n pytest.importorskip('boto3')\n \n \n+def test_chord_publish_retry_does_not_increment_dynamodb_again():\n+    with mock_aws(), Celery('chord-retry', backend='dynamodb://@us-east-1',\n+                            set_as_current=False) as app:\n+        app.conf.task_publish_retry_policy = {\n+            'max_retries': 2, 'interval_start': 0, 'interval_step': 0, 'interval_max': 0,\n+        }\n+\n+        @app.task(name='chord_retry.body', shared=False)\n+        def body(value):\n+            return value\n+\n+        app.finalize()\n+        backend = app.backend\n+        group_id = uuid()\n+        header = app.AsyncResult(uuid())\n+        backend.store_result(header.id, 4, states.SUCCESS)\n+        app.GroupResult(group_id, [header]).save(backend=backend)\n+        key = backend.get_key_for_chord(group_id)\n+        backend.client.put_item(**backend._prepare_init_count_request(str(key)))\n+        producer = Mock(name='producer')\n+        producer.connection.recoverable_connection_errors = ()\n+        producer.publish.side_effect = [OperationalError('refused'), None]\n+        callback = body.s()\n+        request = Context(group=group_id, chord={'chord_size': 1})\n+        from contextlib import contextmanager\n+\n+        @contextmanager\n+        def reraise_as_library_errors():\n+            yield\n+\n+        acquire = Mock(name='producer_or_acquire')\n+        acquire.return_value.__enter__ = Mock(return_value=producer)\n+        acquire.return_value.__exit__ = Mock(return_value=False)\n+        producer.connection._reraise_as_library_errors = reraise_as_library_errors\n+        with patch('celery.backends.base.maybe_signature', return_value=callback), \\\n+                patch.object(app, 'producer_or_acquire', acquire), \\\n+                patch.object(backend, 'incr', wraps=backend.incr) as incr:\n+            backend.on_chord_part_return(request, states.SUCCESS, 4)\n+        assert incr.call_count == 1\n+        assert producer.publish.call_count == 2\n+        assert 'Item' not in backend.client.get_item(**backend._prepare_get_request(str(key)))\n+        assert header.state == states.SUCCESS\n+\n+\n class test_DynamoDBBackend:\n     def setup_method(self):\n         self._static_timestamp = Decimal(1483425566.52)\ndiff --git a/t/unit/backends/test_gcs.py b/t/unit/backends/test_gcs.py\nindex baf7e18511b..b542d790ba5 100644\n--- a/t/unit/backends/test_gcs.py\n+++ b/t/unit/backends/test_gcs.py\n@@ -3,7 +3,10 @@\n \n import pytest\n from google.cloud.exceptions import NotFound\n+from kombu.exceptions import OperationalError\n \n+from celery import uuid\n+from celery.app.amqp import _callback_publish_retry\n from celery.backends.gcs import GCSBackend\n from celery.exceptions import ImproperlyConfigured\n \n@@ -371,6 +374,7 @@ def test_incr(self, mock_firestore_ttl, mock_firestore_document):\n     @patch.object(GCSBackend, '_delete_chord_key')\n     @patch('celery.backends.gcs.allow_join_result')\n     @patch.object(GCSBackend, '_is_firestore_ttl_policy_enabled')\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize('refused', [False, True])\n     def test_on_chord_part_return(\n         self,\n         mock_firestore_ttl,\n@@ -379,6 +383,7 @@ def test_on_chord_part_return(\n         mock_restore_deps,\n         mock_incr,\n         mock_maybe_signature,\n+        refused,\n     ):\n         request = MagicMock()\n         request.group = 'group_id'\n@@ -393,6 +398,18 @@ def test_on_chord_part_return(\n             'result2',\n         ]\n         mock_maybe_signature.return_value = MagicMock()\n+        mock_maybe_signature.return_value.id = 'callback-id'\n+        if refused:\n+            message = self.app.amqp.as_task_v2(uuid(), 'callback', create_sent_event=False)\n+            producer = Mock(name='producer')\n+            producer.connection.recoverable_connection_errors = ()\n+            producer.publish.side_effect = [OperationalError('refused'), None]\n+\n+            def delay(ret):\n+                assert _callback_publish_retry.get() is True\n+                self.app.amqp.send_task_message(producer, 'callback', message)\n+\n+            mock_maybe_signature.return_value.delay.side_effect = delay\n \n         b = GCSBackend(app=self.app)\n         b.on_chord_part_return(request, state, result)\n@@ -407,7 +424,8 @@ def test_on_chord_part_return(\n             timeout=self.app.conf.result_chord_join_timeout,\n             propagate=True,\n         )\n-        mock_maybe_signature.return_value.delay.assert_called_once_with(\n+        assert mock_maybe_signature.return_value.delay.call_count == 1\n+        assert mock_maybe_signature.return_value.delay.call_args == call(\n             ['result1', 'result2']\n         )\n         mock_delete_chord_key.assert_called_once_with(group_key)\ndiff --git a/t/unit/backends/test_redis.py b/t/unit/backends/test_redis.py\nindex c7648b458e6..54a43204d47 100644\n--- a/t/unit/backends/test_redis.py\n+++ b/t/unit/backends/test_redis.py\n@@ -18,9 +18,11 @@\n     exceptions = None\n     CredentialProvider = None\n \n+from kombu.exceptions import OperationalError\n from kombu.utils.encoding import ensure_bytes\n \n from celery import signature, states, uuid\n+from celery.app.amqp import _callback_publish_retry\n from celery.backends.base import COMPRESSED_PAYLOAD_MAGIC\n from celery.canvas import Signature\n from celery.contrib.testing.mocks import ContextMock\n@@ -2383,6 +2385,28 @@ def test_on_chord_part_return__success__ordered(self):\n             self.b.on_chord_part_return(tasks[1].request, states.SUCCESS, 20)\n             callback.delay.assert_called_with([10, 20])\n \n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize('ordered', [False, True])\n+    def test_on_chord_part_return_retries_only_the_body_publish(self, ordered):\n+        self.app.conf.result_backend_transport_options = {'result_chord_ordered': ordered}\n+        self.app.conf.task_publish_retry_policy = {\n+            'max_retries': 2, 'interval_start': 0, 'interval_step': 0, 'interval_max': 0,\n+        }\n+        with self.chord_context(1) as (_, request, callback):\n+            callback.task = 'foobarbaz'\n+            message = self.app.amqp.as_task_v2(uuid(), 'foobarbaz', create_sent_event=False)\n+            producer = Mock(name='producer')\n+            producer.connection.recoverable_connection_errors = ()\n+            producer.publish.side_effect = [OperationalError('refused'), None]\n+\n+            def delay(ret):\n+                assert _callback_publish_retry.get() is True\n+                self.app.amqp.send_task_message(producer, 'foobarbaz', message)\n+\n+            callback.delay = Mock(side_effect=delay)\n+            self.b.on_chord_part_return(request, states.SUCCESS, 10)\n+            assert producer.publish.call_count == 2\n+            assert self.b.get_key_for_group(request.group, '.s') not in self.b.client.keyspace\n+\n     def test_on_chord_part_return__callback_raises(self):\n         with self.chord_context(1) as (_, request, callback):\n             callback.delay.side_effect = KeyError(10)\ndiff --git a/t/unit/tasks/test_chord.py b/t/unit/tasks/test_chord.py\nindex ec511723a8c..eb3ab4b003a 100644\n--- a/t/unit/tasks/test_chord.py\n+++ b/t/unit/tasks/test_chord.py\n@@ -2,6 +2,7 @@\n from unittest.mock import Mock, PropertyMock, patch, sentinel\n \n import pytest\n+from kombu.exceptions import OperationalError\n \n from celery import canvas, group, result, uuid\n from celery.canvas import Signature\n@@ -117,20 +118,55 @@ def test_deps_ready_fails(self):\n             unlock_chord('groupid', Mock(), result=[Mock()],\n                          GroupResult=GroupResult, result_from_tuple=Mock())\n \n-    def test_callback_fails(self):\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize('error', [IOError, OperationalError])\n+    def test_callback_fails(self, error):\n+        self.app.conf.task_publish_retry_policy = {\n+            'max_retries': 2, 'interval_start': 0, 'interval_step': 0, 'interval_max': 0,\n+        }\n \n         class AlwaysReady(TSR):\n             is_ready = True\n             value = [2, 4, 8, 6]\n \n         def setup(callback):\n-            callback.apply_async.side_effect = IOError()\n+            callback.apply_async.side_effect = error()\n \n         with self._chord_context(AlwaysReady, setup) as (cb, retry, fail):\n             fail.assert_called()\n             assert fail.call_args[0][0] == cb.id\n             assert isinstance(fail.call_args[1]['exc'], ChordError)\n \n+    def test_callback_publish_retry_keeps_the_ready_header(self):\n+        from contextlib import contextmanager\n+\n+        @contextmanager\n+        def reraise_as_library_errors():\n+            yield\n+\n+        class AlwaysReady(TSR):\n+            value = [2, 4]\n+\n+        from celery.app.task import Task\n+\n+        producer = Mock(name='producer')\n+        producer.connection.recoverable_connection_errors = ()\n+        producer.publish.side_effect = [OperationalError('refused'), None]\n+        producer.connection._reraise_as_library_errors = reraise_as_library_errors\n+\n+        def setup(callback):\n+            callback.apply_async = Task.apply_async.__get__(\n+                callback, callback.__class__,\n+            )\n+\n+        acquire = Mock(name='producer_or_acquire')\n+        acquire.return_value.__enter__ = Mock(return_value=producer)\n+        acquire.return_value.__exit__ = Mock(return_value=False)\n+        with patch.object(self.app, 'producer_or_acquire', acquire):\n+            with self._chord_context(AlwaysReady, setup) as (cb, retry, fail):\n+                assert producer.publish.call_count == 2\n+                retry.assert_not_called()\n+                fail.assert_not_called()\n+\n     def test_unlock_ready_failed(self):\n \n         class Failed(TSR):\ndiff --git a/t/unit/tasks/test_trace.py b/t/unit/tasks/test_trace.py\nindex cac5cb96013..efa1d227e93 100644\n--- a/t/unit/tasks/test_trace.py\n+++ b/t/unit/tasks/test_trace.py\n@@ -3,9 +3,10 @@\n \n import pytest\n from billiard.einfo import ExceptionInfo\n-from kombu.exceptions import EncodeError\n+from kombu.exceptions import EncodeError, OperationalError\n \n from celery import group, signals, states, uuid\n+from celery.app.amqp import _callback_publish_retry\n from celery.app.task import Context\n from celery.app.trace import (TraceInfo, build_tracer, fast_trace_task, get_log_policy, get_task_name,\n                               log_policy_expected, log_policy_ignore, log_policy_internal, log_policy_reject,\n@@ -54,6 +55,138 @@ def trace(self, *args, **kwargs):\n \n \n class test_trace(TraceCase):\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize('group_as_link', [False, True])\n+    def test_grouped_callbacks_publish_in_retry_context(self, group_as_link):\n+        @self.app.task(shared=False)\n+        def link(value, extra):\n+            return value + extra\n+\n+        callbacks = [link.s(1), link.s(2)]\n+        if group_as_link:\n+            callbacks = [group(callbacks, app=self.app), link.s(3)]\n+        contexts = []\n+\n+        def publish(*args, **kwargs):\n+            contexts.append(_callback_publish_retry.get())\n+\n+        producer, acquire = self._producer_for_publish(publish)\n+        with acquire:\n+            result, info, _ = self.trace(\n+                self.add, (2, 2), {}, request={'callbacks': callbacks}, eager=False,\n+            )\n+        assert result == 4\n+        assert info is None\n+        assert producer.publish.call_count == len(contexts)\n+        assert contexts == [True] * (3 if group_as_link else 2)\n+        assert _callback_publish_retry.get() is False\n+\n+    def _producer_for_publish(self, side_effect):\n+        from contextlib import contextmanager\n+\n+        @contextmanager\n+        def reraise_as_library_errors():\n+            yield\n+\n+        producer = Mock(name='producer')\n+        producer.connection.recoverable_connection_errors = ()\n+        producer.publish.side_effect = side_effect\n+        producer.connection._reraise_as_library_errors = reraise_as_library_errors\n+        acquire = Mock(name='producer_or_acquire')\n+        acquire.return_value.__enter__ = Mock(return_value=producer)\n+        acquire.return_value.__exit__ = Mock(return_value=False)\n+        return producer, patch.object(self.app, 'producer_or_acquire', acquire)\n+\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize('acks_late', [False, True])\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize('dispatch', ['callbacks', 'chain'])\n+    @patch('celery.canvas.maybe_signature')\n+    def test_refused_publish_retries_without_rerunning_body(\n+        self, maybe_signature, dispatch, acks_late,\n+    ):\n+        self.app.conf.task_publish_retry_policy = {\n+            'max_retries': 2, 'interval_start': 0, 'interval_step': 0, 'interval_max': 0,\n+        }\n+        self.add.acks_late = acks_late\n+\n+        @self.app.task(shared=False)\n+        def link(x, y):\n+            return x + y\n+\n+        sig = link.s(2)\n+        maybe_signature.return_value = sig\n+        request = {dispatch: [sig], 'root_id': 'root'}\n+        producer, acquire = self._producer_for_publish(\n+            [OperationalError('refused'), None],\n+        )\n+        with acquire, patch.object(self.add, 'run', wraps=self.add.run) as run:\n+            result, info, _ = self.trace(\n+                self.add, (2, 2), {}, request=request, eager=False,\n+            )\n+        assert result == 4\n+        assert info is None\n+        assert producer.publish.call_count == 2\n+        run.assert_called_once_with(2, 2)\n+        assert self.add.backend.get_state('id-1') == states.SUCCESS\n+\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize('acks_late', [False, True])\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize('dispatch', ['callbacks', 'chain'])\n+    def test_refused_publish_exhaustion_is_a_task_failure(self, dispatch, acks_late):\n+        self.app.conf.task_publish_retry_policy = {\n+            'max_retries': 2, 'interval_start': 0, 'interval_step': 0, 'interval_max': 0,\n+        }\n+        self.add.acks_late = acks_late\n+        task_id = uuid()\n+\n+        @self.app.task(shared=False)\n+        def link(x, y):\n+            return x + y\n+\n+        sig = link.s(2).set(task_id=uuid())\n+        producer, acquire = self._producer_for_publish(OperationalError('refused'))\n+        with acquire, patch.object(self.add, 'run', wraps=self.add.run) as run, \\\n+                patch('celery.app.trace.signals.task_internal_error.send') as internal_error:\n+            _, info, _ = self.trace(\n+                self.add, (2, 2), {}, request={'id': task_id, dispatch: [sig]},\n+                task_id=task_id, eager=False,\n+            )\n+        assert info.state == states.FAILURE\n+        assert producer.publish.call_count == 3\n+        run.assert_called_once_with(2, 2)\n+        internal_error.assert_not_called()\n+        assert self.add.backend.get_state(task_id) == states.FAILURE\n+        if dispatch == 'chain':\n+            assert self.add.backend.get_state(sig.id) == states.FAILURE\n+\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize('dispatch', ['callbacks', 'chain'])\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize('error', [EncodeError, OperationalError])\n+    def test_result_write_error_after_callbacks_preserves_error_handling(self, dispatch, error):\n+        @self.app.task(shared=False, after_return=Mock())\n+        def add(x, y):\n+            return x + y\n+\n+        @self.app.task(shared=False)\n+        def link(value):\n+            return value\n+\n+        task_id, sig = uuid(), link.s()\n+        with patch.object(sig, 'apply_async') as apply_async, \\\n+                patch.object(add.backend, 'mark_as_done', side_effect=error('store failed')), \\\n+                patch.object(add, 'run', wraps=add.run) as run, \\\n+                patch('celery.app.trace.signals.task_internal_error.send') as internal_error:\n+            _, info, _ = self.trace(\n+                add, (2, 2), {}, request={'id': task_id, dispatch: [sig]},\n+                task_id=task_id, eager=False,\n+            )\n+        apply_async.assert_called_once()\n+        run.assert_called_once_with(2, 2)\n+        assert info.state == states.FAILURE\n+        assert add.backend.get_state(task_id) == states.FAILURE\n+        if error is OperationalError:\n+            internal_error.assert_called_once()\n+            add.after_return.assert_not_called()\n+        else:\n+            internal_error.assert_not_called()\n+            add.after_return.assert_called_once_with(states.FAILURE, ANY, task_id, (2, 2), {}, None)\n+\n     def test_trace_successful(self):\n         retval, info, _ = self.trace(self.add, (2, 2), {})\n         assert info is None\n", "problem_statement": "A link the broker refuses after the task body ran records FAILURE and acks the message, so the link is never sent\n# Checklist\n\n- [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `main` branch of Celery.\n- [ ] This has already been asked to the [discussions forum](https://github.com/celery/celery/discussions) first.\n- [x] I have read the relevant section in the\n  [contribution guide](https://docs.celeryq.dev/en/main/contributing.html#other-bugs)\n  on reporting bugs.\n- [x] I have checked the [issues list](https://github.com/celery/celery/issues?q=is%3Aissue+label%3A%22Issue+Type%3A+Bug+Report%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)\n  for similar or identical bug reports.\n- [x] I have checked the [pull requests list](https://github.com/celery/celery/pulls?q=is%3Apr+label%3A%22PR+Type%3A+Bugfix%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)\n  for existing proposed fixes.\n- [x] I have checked the [commit log](https://github.com/celery/celery/commits/main)\n  to find out if the bug was already fixed in the main branch.\n- [x] I have included all related issues and possible duplicate issues\n  in this issue (If there are none, check this box anyway).\n- [ ] I have tried to reproduce the issue with [pytest-celery](https://docs.celeryq.dev/projects/pytest-celery/en/latest/userguide/celery-bug-report.html) and added the reproduction script below.\n\n## Mandatory Debugging Information\n\n- [x] I have included the output of ``celery -A proj report`` in the issue.\n- [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `main` branch of Celery.\n- [x] I have included the contents of ``pip freeze`` in the issue (the packages that matter; the environment also has `requirements/test.txt`).\n- [x] I have included all the versions of all the external dependencies required\n  to reproduce this bug.\n\n## Optional Debugging Information\n\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one Python version\n  and/or implementation.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one message broker and/or\n  result backend.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one version of the message\n  broker and/or result backend.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one operating system.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one workers pool.\n- [x] I have tried reproducing the issue with autoscaling, retries,\n  ETA/Countdown & rate limits disabled.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue after downgrading\n  and/or upgrading Celery and its dependencies.\n\n## Related Issues and Possible Duplicates\n\n#### Related Issues\n\n- #6631: the rest of a chain is lost after a retried task. A different trigger, the same outcome for the chain.\n- #3456: task cleanup on worker failures.\n\n#### Possible Duplicates\n\n- None\n\n## Environment & Settings\n\n**Celery version**: 5.7.0a1 (recovery), `main` at f0b132007\n\n<details>\n<summary><b><code>celery report</code> Output:</b></summary>\n<p>\n\n```\nsoftware -> celery:5.7.0a1 (recovery) kombu:5.7.0a1 py:3.12.12\n            billiard:4.3.0 redis:8.1.0\nplatform -> system:Linux arch:64bit, ELF\n            kernel version:6.18.33.2-microsoft-standard-WSL2 imp:CPython\nloader   -> celery.loaders.app.AppLoader\nsettings -> transport:redis results:redis://localhost:6379/11\n\nbroker_url: 'redis://:********@localhost:6379/10'\nresult_backend: 'redis://localhost:6379/11'\ndeprecated_settings: None\ntask_acks_late: True\nworker_prefetch_multiplier: 1\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n# Steps to Reproduce\n\n## Required Dependencies\n\n- **Minimal Python Version**: N/A or Unknown (reproduced on 3.12.12)\n- **Minimal Celery Version**: N/A or Unknown (reproduced on `main`, f0b132007)\n- **Minimal Kombu Version**: N/A or Unknown (5.7.0a1)\n- **Minimal Broker Version**: N/A or Unknown (Redis 7.4.11)\n- **Minimal Result Backend Version**: N/A or Unknown (Redis 7.4.11)\n- **Minimal OS and/or Kernel Version**: N/A or Unknown (Linux, Docker)\n- **Minimal Broker Client Version**: N/A or Unknown (redis-py 8.1.0)\n- **Minimal Result Backend Client Version**: N/A or Unknown (redis-py 8.1.0)\n\n### Python Packages\n\n<details>\n<summary><b><code>pip freeze</code> Output:</b></summary>\n<p>\n\n```\namqp==5.4.0\nbilliard==4.3.0\ncelery @ file:///src (main, f0b132007)\nclick==8.5.0\nkombu==5.7.0a1\nredis==8.1.0\nvine==5.1.0\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n### Other Dependencies\n\n<details>\n<p>\nRedis 7.4.11 as broker and result backend.\n</p>\n</details>\n\n## Minimally Reproducible Test Case\n\nWith a real worker: one task (`send_message`) with a link (`announce`) and an error callback (`report_failure`), `task_acks_late=True`, `celery worker --pool=prefork --concurrency=1`, Redis as broker and backend. In the worker, the first `Signature.apply_async` the task's execution makes (the link) raises kombu's `OperationalError(\"[Errno 111] Connection refused\")`, as a broker that is down, or at `maxmemory` with noeviction, would. Then the worker is started again:\n\n<details>\n<p>\n\n```\n                                      state     message sent   what the callbacks announced   broker\nnormal operation                      SUCCESS   1              sent                           queue 0, unacked 0\nthe broker refused the task's link    FAILURE   1              failed: OperationalError       queue 0, unacked 0\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\nThe contract that produces this, `t/due_work/test_worker_contract.py`, is in the PR that follows.\n\n# Expected Behavior\n\nThe task's body ran and its effect happened. Either the link is published later, or the message stays unacknowledged so the task is delivered again. In both cases the chain continues, and the task isn't recorded as a failure it didn't have.\n\n# Actual Behavior\n\nThe side effect happened, the result says FAILURE, the error callback fires, and the message is acknowledged even with `acks_late`. So the link is never sent, and a chain stops there for good.\n\n[`_dispatch_callbacks_and_chain`](https://github.com/celery/celery/blob/f0b132007/celery/app/trace.py#L619) runs before `mark_as_done`, and only `EncodeError` is handled around it ([L628](https://github.com/celery/celery/blob/f0b132007/celery/app/trace.py#L628)). A broker error reaches the outer handler, which records FAILURE and fires the errbacks. `Request.on_failure` then acknowledges, because `task_acks_on_failure_or_timeout` defaults to True. Dispatch isn't atomic either: with several links, the ones before the refused one are already sent.\n\nIt also happens with a broker that really refuses the write, not only an injected error: with the Redis broker full (`maxmemory 1`, `maxmemory-policy noeviction`) when the link is published, Redis rejects the publish with `OutOfMemoryError`, kombu raises `OperationalError`, and the task ends FAILURE with its error callback fired, the message acknowledged (queue 0, unacked 0) and the link never sent, even after the worker restarts. A broker that only pauses writes (`CLIENT PAUSE ... WRITE`) does not trigger it: the publish waits and succeeds. The script is in [this comment on #10726](https://github.com/celery/celery/pull/10726#issuecomment-6395714642).\n\n> **Aside: how this was found.** This came from [pytest-obligation](https://github.com/gigaverse-app/pytest-obligation), an open-source pytest plugin for background work that must not be lost, run twice or recorded wrongly. Given one task and how to see what it did, it generates the failure cases on a real `celery worker`: it kills the pool child at each of Celery's stages, makes a hook raise, refuses a publish, restarts the worker, and compares every outcome with a normal run. In Celery it produced #10724 (fixed by #10752 and #10759), #10725 and #10954. Each stays pinned as a strict xfail, so a fix shows up as a change, and so does a regression; when the two #10724 fixes landed, the unchanged contract flagged exactly those two cases. It runs nightly against Celery's `main` on [gigaverse-app/celery](https://github.com/gigaverse-app/celery), and #10726 shows what adopting it in Celery's own CI would look like.\n\n*Written with Claude Code at Aviad Rozenhek's direction; every result above was produced by running the code shown.*\n\n", "hints_text": "> I'm Claude Opus 5.5, AI can make mistakes. The next step fell off the chain.\n\n### ELI5: what can break\n\nA chain is a relay race: each task hands the baton to the next one.\n\n```\n  task A (runs the body)                  broker                        task B (the link)\n  \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500                  \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500                        \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n  does its work: payment captured  \u2714\n  hands the baton to B \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u25ba   \u2718 \"connection refused\"\n                                          (restarting, or full:\n                                           maxmemory + noeviction)\n  \"that failed\" \u2500\u25ba records A = FAILURE    (A's work happened!)\n  error callback: \"failed\"\n  acks A's message \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u25ba   A's message deleted        never starts\n```\n\nWith `acks_late` you'd expect the message to stay unacknowledged, so A is delivered again and hands the baton over next time. Instead the refused hand-off is recorded as A's own failure, and A's message is acknowledged (`task_acks_on_failure_or_timeout` defaults to True). Nothing is left to deliver.\n\n- **The work happened, but the record says FAILURE.** Anyone who re-runs failed tasks captures the payment again.\n- **The chain stops for good.** B, and everything after it, never runs.\n- **With several links**, the ones before the refused one were already sent.\n\n#10726 adds the due-work-harness contract that found this by refusing the task's link as one of Celery's failure stages.\n\nChords on the Redis result backend fail the same way. If the last header finishes while the broker link is down, Celery's one attempt to send the body fails. It then marks the whole chord as failed, and nothing tries again. I see this on 5.7.0rc1, main (`d6d754e`), and every older release I tried, back to 4.4.7.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Script. It needs Redis on <code>localhost:6379</code>.</summary>\n\n```python\nfrom unittest.mock import patch\n\nfrom kombu.exceptions import OperationalError\n\nfrom celery import Celery, signature, states, uuid\nfrom celery.app.task import Context\n\napp = Celery(broker=\"memory://\", backend=\"redis://localhost:6379/0\")\nbackend = app.backend\ngroup_id, header_ids = uuid(), [uuid(), uuid()]\nbody = signature(\"body\", app=app).freeze()  # gives the body a task id\nbackend.set_chord_size(group_id, 2)\n\n\ndef header_done(index, value):\n    request = Context(id=header_ids[index], group=group_id, group_index=index,\n                      chord=signature(\"body\", app=app, task_id=body.id))\n    backend.on_chord_part_return(request, states.SUCCESS, value)\n\n\nheader_done(0, \"A\")\nwith patch(\"celery.canvas.Signature.delay\", side_effect=OperationalError(\"broker link reset\")):\n    header_done(1, \"B\")  # the last header finishes while the broker link is down\nprint(\"body state:\", backend.get_task_meta(body.id, cache=False)[\"status\"])\n\nwith patch(\"celery.canvas.Signature.delay\") as body_delay:\n    header_done(1, \"B\")  # the same header runs again once the broker is back\nprint(\"body sent on retry:\", body_delay.called)\n```\n\nCelery 4.4.7 and 5.0.5 have no `set_chord_size()`. On those, I passed the size as `chord_size` on the body signature and got the same output.\n\n</details>\n\n```\nbody state: FAILURE\nbody sent on retry: False\n```\n\nCelery stores FAILURE for the body, with `ChordError(\"Callback error: ...\")`, and deletes the chord's keys. When the header runs again, there is no chord left to finish. Nothing ever sends the body, and no task ever reads the header results. In a fault test on 5.6.3 with real workers and short network drops between one worker and RabbitMQ, this happened to 1 of 60 chords.", "created_at": "2026-10-27T05:18:24Z"}
{"repo": "celery/celery", "pull_number": 10960, "instance_id": "celery__celery-10960", "issue_numbers": ["10957"], "base_commit": "d819916f5613ad2216aaad1107c077103998d04b", "patch": "diff --git a/celery/backends/base.py b/celery/backends/base.py\nindex 15aafe865b1..b1b98a8ea54 100644\n--- a/celery/backends/base.py\n+++ b/celery/backends/base.py\n@@ -824,19 +824,20 @@ def _get_result_meta(self, result,\n     def _sleep(self, amount):\n         time.sleep(amount)\n \n-    def _ensure_retryable(self, func, *args, fallback_exc=None, fallback_msg=None, **kwargs):\n+    def _ensure_retryable(self, func, *args, fallback_exc=None, fallback_msg=None, max_retries=None, **kwargs):\n         \"\"\"Helper to execute a function with the backend's retry policy.\"\"\"\n         retries = 0\n+        limit = self.max_retries if max_retries is None else max_retries\n         while True:\n             try:\n                 return func(*args, **kwargs)\n             except Exception as exc:\n                 if self.always_retry and self.exception_safe_to_retry(exc):\n-                    if retries < self.max_retries:\n+                    if retries < limit:\n                         retries += 1\n                         logger.warning(\n                             'Failed operation %s. Retrying %s more times.',\n-                            getattr(func, '__name__', repr(func)), self.max_retries - retries,\n+                            getattr(func, '__name__', repr(func)), limit - retries,\n                             exc_info=True)\n                         try:\n                             self.on_backend_retryable_error(exc)\ndiff --git a/celery/worker/request.py b/celery/worker/request.py\nindex 7e8d2a8fe32..d3afc52610f 100644\n--- a/celery/worker/request.py\n+++ b/celery/worker/request.py\n@@ -18,8 +18,8 @@\n from celery.app.task import Context\n from celery.app.trace import fast_trace_task, task_has_custom, trace_task, trace_task_ret\n from celery.concurrency.base import BasePool\n-from celery.exceptions import (Ignore, InvalidTaskError, Reject, Retry, TaskRevokedError, Terminated,\n-                               TimeLimitExceeded, WorkerLostError)\n+from celery.exceptions import (BackendGetMetaError, Ignore, InvalidTaskError, Reject, Retry, TaskRevokedError,\n+                               Terminated, TimeLimitExceeded, WorkerLostError)\n from celery.platforms import signals as _signals\n from celery.utils.functional import maybe, maybe_list, noop\n from celery.utils.log import get_logger\n@@ -707,7 +707,13 @@ def on_failure(self, exc_info, send_failed_event=True, return_ok=False):\n             return self.on_retry(exc_info)\n \n         is_worker_lost = isinstance(exc, WorkerLostError)\n-        if is_worker_lost and self._success_was_stored():\n+\n+        # _success_was_stored() returns the meta dictionary if the task\n+        # already stored SUCCESS, or False otherwise. This allows us to reuse\n+        # the fetched metadata below without a second backend round-trip.\n+        success_meta = self._success_was_stored() if is_worker_lost else False\n+\n+        if success_meta:\n             # The pool child stored SUCCESS and was lost afterwards, e.g. in\n             # on_success, a task_postrun receiver or process_cleanup.\n             # Recording a failure here would fire the errbacks and count a\n@@ -716,11 +722,12 @@ def on_failure(self, exc_info, send_failed_event=True, return_ok=False):\n                  self.name, self.id, exc)\n             if self.task.acks_late:\n                 self.acknowledge()\n+\n             # Emit the success event so Flower/events.State do not stay STARTED.\n-            try:\n-                result = self.task.backend.get_result(self.id)\n-            except Exception:  # pylint: disable=broad-except\n-                result = None\n+            # Reuse the metadata we just fetched instead of hitting the backend\n+            # again from the worker's main process (which can be slow during\n+            # an outage).\n+            result = success_meta.get('result')\n             self.send_event(\n                 'task-succeeded',\n                 result=saferepr(result, self.task.resultrepr_maxsize),\n@@ -843,8 +850,32 @@ def _success_was_stored(self):\n         as_uri = getattr(backend, 'as_uri', None)\n         if callable(as_uri) and as_uri() == 'rpc://':\n             return False\n+\n+        # Bounded retry: keep the retry for transient blips, but cap it at\n+        # 3 attempts so a down backend can't block the worker's main process\n+        # indefinitely. Respect a lower user-configured\n+        # result_backend_max_retries via min(). Pass max_retries explicitly\n+        # instead of mutating backend.max_retries, which is shared across\n+        # threads when result_backend_thread_safe=True. The isinstance guard\n+        # is only around the min() computation to tolerate Mock backends in\n+        # tests (a non-numeric max_retries falls back to the 3-attempt cap).\n+        configured_max_retries = backend.max_retries\n+        if isinstance(configured_max_retries, (int, float)):\n+            bounded_max_retries = min(configured_max_retries, 3)\n+        else:\n+            bounded_max_retries = 3\n+\n         try:\n-            return backend.get_state(self.id) == states.SUCCESS\n+            meta = backend._ensure_retryable(\n+                backend._get_task_meta_for,\n+                fallback_exc=BackendGetMetaError,\n+                fallback_msg=\"failed to get meta\",\n+                task_id=self.id,\n+                max_retries=bounded_max_retries,\n+            )\n+            if meta['status'] == states.SUCCESS:\n+                return meta\n+            return False\n         except Exception:  # pylint: disable=broad-except\n             # No backend, or it can't be reached: keep the failure handling.\n             return False\ndiff --git a/t/smoke/tasks.py b/t/smoke/tasks.py\nindex c2f240c6131..ccceab02633 100644\n--- a/t/smoke/tasks.py\n+++ b/t/smoke/tasks.py\n@@ -112,3 +112,12 @@ def self_termination_exhaust_memory():\n     mem = []\n     while True:\n         mem.append(\" \" * 10**6)\n+\n+\n+@shared_task\n+def kill_child_process_once(marker=\"/tmp/kill_child_process_once\"):\n+    \"\"\"Exit the pool child on the first delivery only, as if it were OOM killed.\"\"\"\n+    if not os.path.exists(marker):\n+        open(marker, \"w\").close()\n+        os._exit(1)\n+    return \"survived\"\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/t/integration/test_request_backend_down.py b/t/integration/test_request_backend_down.py\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 00000000000..be11ee79036\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/t/integration/test_request_backend_down.py\n@@ -0,0 +1,80 @@\n+import time\n+from unittest.mock import Mock\n+\n+import pytest\n+from billiard.einfo import ExceptionInfo\n+\n+from celery import Celery\n+from celery.exceptions import WorkerLostError\n+from celery.worker.request import Request\n+\n+\n+@pytest.fixture\n+def failing_app():\n+    # Point at a closed port so connection attempts fail immediately.\n+    app = Celery(\n+        broker='memory://',\n+        backend='redis://localhost:1/0'  # Port 1 is almost always closed\n+    )\n+    app.conf.result_backend_always_retry = True\n+    app.conf.result_backend_max_retries = float('inf')\n+    app.conf.result_backend_base_sleep_between_retries_ms = 10\n+    app.conf.result_backend_max_sleep_between_retries_ms = 10\n+    return app\n+\n+\n+@pytest.mark.timeout(30)\n+def test_on_failure_returns_fast_with_unreachable_backend(failing_app):\n+    \"\"\"\n+    Integration test: a real backend connection failure should not block\n+    on_failure() indefinitely. With the bounded retry, it should fail fast\n+    and requeue the task.\n+    \"\"\"\n+    @failing_app.task(bind=True, acks_late=True, reject_on_worker_lost=True)\n+    def dummy_task(self):\n+        pass\n+\n+    try:\n+        raise WorkerLostError(\"Worker exited prematurely\")\n+    except WorkerLostError:\n+        einfo = ExceptionInfo(internal=True)\n+\n+    # Create a mock message object that mimics Kombu's Message\n+    message = Mock()\n+    message.headers = {'id': 'test-id', 'task': dummy_task.name}\n+    message.body = b'[]'\n+    message.content_type = 'application/json'\n+    message.content_encoding = 'utf-8'\n+    message.payload = ([], {}, {})\n+    message.delivery_info = {}\n+    message.properties = {}\n+\n+    # Construct a minimal Request. We hold a reference to on_reject\n+    # to assert on it after the failure is processed.\n+    on_reject_mock = Mock()\n+    req = Request(\n+        message=message,\n+        app=failing_app,\n+        task=dummy_task,\n+        on_ack=Mock(),\n+        on_reject=on_reject_mock,\n+    )\n+\n+    start_time = time.monotonic()\n+    req.on_failure(einfo, send_failed_event=False)\n+    elapsed = time.monotonic() - start_time\n+\n+    # On main this hangs indefinitely (in _success_was_stored);\n+    # here it should fail fast and requeue.\n+    assert elapsed < 2.0, (\n+        f\"on_failure took {elapsed:.2f}s. It should fail fast \"\n+        f\"instead of retrying indefinitely with max_retries=inf.\"\n+    )\n+\n+    # Ensure the task was actually requeued, not silently dropped.\n+    # The on_reject callback receives (logger, connection_errors, requeue).\n+    on_reject_mock.assert_called_once()\n+    assert on_reject_mock.call_args.args[2] is True, (\n+        \"The task should be requeued (requeue=True) when the worker is lost \"\n+        \"and reject_on_worker_lost is enabled.\"\n+    )\ndiff --git a/t/smoke/tests/test_worker.py b/t/smoke/tests/test_worker.py\nindex a988848b634..f935a21b79b 100644\n--- a/t/smoke/tests/test_worker.py\n+++ b/t/smoke/tests/test_worker.py\n@@ -684,3 +684,35 @@ def test_apply_async_succeeds_with_missing_queue_creation_disabled(\n         app.conf.task_queues = {\"non_default_queue\": {}}\n         result = add.apply_async((1, 2), queue=\"non_default_queue\")\n         assert result.get(timeout=RESULT_TIMEOUT) == 3\n+\n+\n+class test_unreachable_result_backend(SuiteOperations):\n+    @pytest.fixture\n+    def default_worker_env(self, default_worker_env: dict) -> dict:\n+        # The worker takes its result backend from this variable, not from\n+        # app.conf, so point it at a port with nothing listening.\n+        return {**default_worker_env, \"CELERY_RESULT_BACKEND\": \"redis://localhost:9999/0\"}\n+\n+    @pytest.fixture\n+    def default_worker_app(self, default_worker_app: Celery) -> Celery:\n+        app = default_worker_app\n+        app.conf.result_backend_always_retry = True\n+        app.conf.result_backend_max_retries = 9999999\n+        app.conf.result_backend_base_sleep_between_retries_ms = 10\n+        app.conf.result_backend_max_sleep_between_retries_ms = 10\n+        app.conf.task_acks_late = True\n+        app.conf.task_reject_on_worker_lost = True\n+        return app\n+\n+    def test_worker_survives_child_death_with_unreachable_backend(self, celery_setup: CeleryTestSetup):\n+        from t.smoke.tasks import kill_child_process_once\n+        queue = celery_setup.worker.worker_queue\n+\n+        kill_child_process_once.si().set(queue=queue).delay()\n+        celery_setup.worker.assert_log_exists(\"Worker exited prematurely\", timeout=RESULT_TIMEOUT)\n+\n+        # On main, the main process now retries the read without end, so it\n+        # never receives another task. The worker can't store results, so\n+        # check its log instead of a result.\n+        alive = identity.si(\"alive\").set(queue=queue).delay()\n+        celery_setup.worker.assert_log_exists(f\"{alive.id}] received\", timeout=RESULT_TIMEOUT)\ndiff --git a/t/unit/worker/test_request.py b/t/unit/worker/test_request.py\nindex 092fdf1780c..6090249f646 100644\n--- a/t/unit/worker/test_request.py\n+++ b/t/unit/worker/test_request.py\n@@ -1240,8 +1240,12 @@ def test_on_failure_WorkerLostError_after_success_was_stored_acks_late(self):\n         req.task.acks_late = True\n         req.task.reject_on_worker_lost = True\n         req.task.backend = Mock()\n-        req.task.backend.get_state.return_value = states.SUCCESS\n-        req.task.backend.get_result.return_value = 42\n+        req.task.backend._ensure_retryable.return_value = {\n+            'status': states.SUCCESS, 'result': 42\n+        }\n+        req.task.backend._get_task_meta_for.return_value = {\n+            'status': states.SUCCESS, 'result': 42\n+        }\n         req.task.backend.as_uri.return_value = 'redis://localhost'\n \n         req.on_failure(einfo)\n@@ -1269,8 +1273,15 @@ def test_on_failure_WorkerLostError_after_success_skips_retry_on_worker_lost(sel\n         req.task.retry_on_worker_lost = True\n         req.task.max_retries = 3\n         req.task.backend = Mock()\n-        req.task.backend.get_state.return_value = states.SUCCESS\n-        req.task.backend.get_result.return_value = 7\n+        # _success_was_stored calls backend._ensure_retryable(...) directly\n+        # with max_retries=3 (no more get_task_meta), so mock that instead.\n+        req.task.backend._ensure_retryable.return_value = {\n+            'status': states.SUCCESS, 'result': 7\n+        }\n+        # The task-succeeded event block still reads _get_task_meta_for.\n+        req.task.backend._get_task_meta_for.return_value = {\n+            'status': states.SUCCESS, 'result': 7\n+        }\n         req.task.backend.as_uri.return_value = 'redis://localhost'\n \n         with patch('celery.canvas.Signature.apply_async') as apply_async:\n@@ -2160,6 +2171,171 @@ def test_group_index(self):\n         job = self.xRequest(id=uuid(), group_index=group_index)\n         assert job.group_index == group_index\n \n+    # Tests for the blocking issue in PR #10752.\n+    # These tests verify that `_success_was_stored` uses a bounded retry so\n+    # that a down backend cannot block the worker's main process, and that\n+    # the `task-succeeded` event block bypasses `_ensure_retryable` via\n+    # `_get_task_meta_for`.\n+    def _setup_blocking_backend(self, req):\n+        \"\"\"Configure req.task.backend to raise ConnectionError on every read.\n+\n+        Returns (read_calls, sleep_calls) so the test can assert on retries.\n+        \"\"\"\n+        backend = req.task.backend\n+        backend.always_retry = True\n+        backend.max_retries = 3\n+        backend.base_sleep_between_retries_ms = 50\n+        backend.max_sleep_between_retries_ms = 50\n+        backend.url = 'cache+memory://'\n+        backend._cache.clear()\n+\n+        read_calls = []\n+        sleep_calls = []\n+\n+        def failing_meta(task_id):\n+            read_calls.append(task_id)\n+            raise ConnectionError(\"backend down\")\n+\n+        backend._get_task_meta_for = failing_meta\n+        backend._sleep = lambda amount: sleep_calls.append(amount)\n+        backend.exception_safe_to_retry = lambda exc: True\n+        backend.on_backend_retryable_error = lambda exc: None\n+        return read_calls, sleep_calls\n+\n+    def test_repro_10957_event_block_does_not_retry_on_backend_down(self):\n+        \"\"\"\n+        The `task-succeeded` event block must not go through `_ensure_retryable`.\n+        Expected: 0 retries.\n+        \"\"\"\n+        req = self.get_request(self.add.s(2, 2))\n+        req._ignore_result = False\n+\n+        backend = req.task.backend\n+        backend.always_retry = True\n+        backend.max_retries = 3\n+        backend.base_sleep_between_retries_ms = 50\n+        backend.max_sleep_between_retries_ms = 50\n+        backend.url = 'cache+memory://'\n+        backend._cache.clear()\n+\n+        sleep_calls = []\n+\n+        def failing_meta(task_id):\n+            raise ConnectionError(\"backend down\")\n+\n+        backend._get_task_meta_for = failing_meta\n+        backend._sleep = lambda amount: sleep_calls.append(amount)\n+        backend.exception_safe_to_retry = lambda exc: True\n+        backend.on_backend_retryable_error = lambda exc: None\n+\n+        try:\n+            raise WorkerLostError()\n+        except WorkerLostError:\n+            einfo = ExceptionInfo()\n+\n+        # Force `_success_was_stored` to return True so we enter the event block.\n+        with patch.object(req, '_success_was_stored', return_value={'status': states.SUCCESS, 'result': '42'}):\n+            req.on_failure(einfo, send_failed_event=False)\n+\n+        assert len(sleep_calls) == 0, (\n+            f\"Expected 0 retries in event block, got {len(sleep_calls)} sleeps.\"\n+        )\n+\n+    def test_repro_10957_requeue_with_failing_backend(self):\n+        \"\"\"\n+        With acks_late and reject_on_worker_lost, on_failure should requeue\n+        the task without blocking on the backend write, using a bounded retry.\n+        \"\"\"\n+        try:\n+            raise WorkerLostError()\n+        except WorkerLostError:\n+            einfo = ExceptionInfo(internal=True)\n+\n+        req = self.get_request(self.add.s(2, 2))\n+        req.task.acks_late = True\n+        req.task.reject_on_worker_lost = True\n+\n+        backend = req.task.backend\n+        backend.always_retry = True\n+        backend.max_retries = float('inf')  # Default\n+        backend.exception_safe_to_retry = lambda exc: True\n+        backend.on_backend_retryable_error = lambda exc: None\n+\n+        backend._get_task_meta_for = Mock(side_effect=ConnectionError(\"backend down\"))\n+        backend._store_result = Mock(side_effect=ConnectionError(\"backend down\"))\n+\n+        sleep_calls = [0]\n+\n+        def counting_sleep(amount):\n+            sleep_calls[0] += 1\n+            if sleep_calls[0] >= 10:\n+                raise RuntimeError(\"Stop retrying!\")\n+\n+        backend._sleep = counting_sleep\n+\n+        req.on_failure(einfo)\n+\n+        req.on_reject.assert_called_with(req_logger, req.connection_errors, True)\n+        backend._store_result.assert_not_called()\n+        # On main: reaches 10. On this branch: stops at 3.\n+        assert sleep_calls[0] <= 3\n+\n+    def test_repro_10957_does_not_hang_on_inf_retries(self):\n+        \"\"\"\n+        On main, _success_was_stored uses get_state() -> _ensure_retryable\n+        with max_retries=inf, so it retries until _sleep raises (10 times).\n+        On this branch, the retry is bounded to 3, so it returns False fast.\n+        \"\"\"\n+        req = self.get_request(self.add.s(2, 2))\n+        req._ignore_result = False\n+\n+        backend = req.task.backend\n+        backend.always_retry = True\n+        backend.max_retries = float('inf')\n+        backend.exception_safe_to_retry = lambda exc: True\n+        backend.on_backend_retryable_error = lambda exc: None\n+\n+        backend._get_task_meta_for = Mock(side_effect=ConnectionError(\"backend down\"))\n+\n+        sleep_calls = [0]\n+\n+        def counting_sleep(amount):\n+            sleep_calls[0] += 1\n+            if sleep_calls[0] >= 10:\n+                raise RuntimeError(\"Stop retrying!\")\n+\n+        backend._sleep = counting_sleep\n+\n+        result = req._success_was_stored()\n+        assert result is False\n+        # On main: reaches 10 (RuntimeError). On this branch: stops at 3.\n+        assert sleep_calls[0] <= 3\n+\n+    def test_success_was_stored_keeps_a_lower_max_retries(self):\n+        req = self.get_request(self.add.s(2, 2))\n+        backend = req.task.backend\n+        backend.always_retry = True\n+        backend.max_retries = 1\n+        backend.exception_safe_to_retry = lambda exc: True\n+        backend.on_backend_retryable_error = lambda exc: None\n+        backend._get_task_meta_for = Mock(side_effect=ConnectionError(\"backend down\"))\n+        backend._sleep = Mock()\n+\n+        assert req._success_was_stored() is False\n+        assert backend._sleep.call_count == 1\n+\n+    def test_on_failure_reads_the_backend_only_when_the_worker_is_lost(self):\n+        req = self.get_request(self.add.s(2, 2))\n+        req._success_was_stored = Mock(return_value=False)\n+        try:\n+            raise KeyError('boom')\n+        except KeyError:\n+            einfo = ExceptionInfo()\n+\n+        req.on_failure(einfo)\n+\n+        req._success_was_stored.assert_not_called()\n+\n \n class test_create_request_class(RequestCase):\n \n", "problem_statement": "Worker stalls on _success_was_stored when result_backend_always_retry=True and the result backend is down\n## What's wrong\n\n#10752 fixed `self.ignore_result` and added `saferepr` to the `task-succeeded` event, but it didn't address the `_ensure_retryable` note from @MehrazRumman. `_success_was_stored` still calls `backend.get_state()`, and the event block still calls `backend.get_result()`. Both go through `get_task_meta()` \u2192 `_ensure_retryable()`.\n\n`on_failure` runs in the worker's main process, so a blocked read there stalls the whole worker, not just one task.\n\n## Environment\n\n- Celery: `5.7.0rc1` on `main @ e3cc7cc53` (editable install)\n- Python: 3.14.4, CPython\n- OS: Linux, 64-bit\n- Backend used in tests: `cache+memory://` with a mocked `_get_task_meta_for`\n- Broker: `memory://` (not relevant to this bug)\n\n<details>\n<summary>celery report</summary>\n\n```text\nsoftware -> celery:5.7.0rc1 (collider) kombu:5.7.0rc1 py:3.14.4\n            billiard:4.3.1 memory:N/A\nplatform -> system:Linux arch:64bit, ELF\n            kernel version:7.0.0-38-generic imp:CPython\nloader   -> celery.loaders.app.AppLoader\nsettings -> transport:memory results:cache+memory://\n\nbroker_url: 'memory://'\nresult_backend: 'cache+memory://'\ndeprecated_settings: None\n```\n\n</details>\n\n## Where\n\nAt `e3cc7cc53`:\n\n- `celery/worker/request.py:844` \u2192 `backend.get_state(self.id)`\n- `celery/worker/request.py:718` \u2192 `self.task.backend.get_result(self.id)`\n\n## Reproduced\n\nMocked `_get_task_meta_for` to raise `ConnectionError`, `always_retry=True`, `exception_safe_to_retry=True` (the same predicate Redis and DB backends use).\n\n`_success_was_stored`:\n\n```text\n[REPRO-1] reads=4, sleeps=3\nWARNING Failed operation failing_meta. Retrying 2 more times.\nWARNING Failed operation failing_meta. Retrying 1 more times.\nWARNING Failed operation failing_meta. Retrying 0 more times.\n```\n\nThe `except Exception` only runs **after** `_ensure_retryable` gives up. With `max_retries=3` that's 3 retries. With the default `max_retries=inf` (from `result_backend_max_retries`) it never gets there. @MehrazRumman measured 50+ sleeps (~200s) in their review.\n\nEvent block in `on_failure`:\n\n```text\n[REPRO-2] event path sleeps=3\n```\n\nEven if `_success_was_stored` is fixed, the event block has the same problem for the same reason.\n\nI wrote a minimal unit test locally (alongside the existing `WorkerLostError` tests in `t/unit/worker/test_request.py`) using a mocked backend whose `_get_task_meta_for` raises `ConnectionError`. I can submit it as a PR with `strict xfail` markers if that's the preferred approach.\n\n## Fix\n\nUse `_get_task_meta_for` instead of `get_state`/`get_result`, which skips `_ensure_retryable`:\n\n```python\ntry:\n    return backend._get_task_meta_for(self.id)['status'] == states.SUCCESS\nexcept Exception:\n    return False\n```\n\n```python\ntry:\n    meta = self.task.backend._get_task_meta_for(self.id)\n    result = meta.get('result')\nexcept Exception:\n    result = None\nself.send_event('task-succeeded',\n                result=saferepr(result, self.task.resultrepr_maxsize),\n                runtime=None)\n```\n\nA down backend then falls back right away, which is what the comment in `_success_was_stored` says the intent is.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Only tested with `cache+memory://` and a mocked backend. Worth double-checking on real Redis/DB with the backend actually unreachable \u2014 the retry path is the same, but a real-network failure could behave differently.\n\ncc @MehrazRumman @auvipy @ChristianMurphy\n\n\n", "hints_text": "@majidkhazaei  You already suggested a fix . Why don\u2019t you open a PR, push your code and check against existing test. Then figure out what went wrong from my side, that will be pretty cool . I will be very happy to see your solution and review your PR.", "created_at": "2026-10-25T18:27:45Z"}
{"repo": "celery/celery", "pull_number": 10956, "instance_id": "celery__celery-10956", "issue_numbers": ["4925"], "base_commit": "36596d094be360591f4a7d8d3ca1a7fe9eae0589", "patch": "diff --git a/celery/backends/asynchronous.py b/celery/backends/asynchronous.py\nindex 1f6507e4667..9f883a3111d 100644\n--- a/celery/backends/asynchronous.py\n+++ b/celery/backends/asynchronous.py\n@@ -8,7 +8,7 @@\n from contextlib import contextmanager\n from queue import Empty\n from time import sleep\n-from weakref import WeakKeyDictionary\n+from weakref import WeakKeyDictionary, WeakValueDictionary\n \n from kombu.utils.compat import detect_environment\n \n@@ -32,6 +32,17 @@\n )\n \n \n+def _waiting_on(pending_results, task_id):\n+    \"\"\"Return every result object waiting on ``task_id``.\n+\n+    Several :class:`~celery.result.AsyncResult` objects can wait on the\n+    same id, e.g. a group member and a poll of that member's id, so each\n+    mapping holds the waiters for an id keyed by object identity.\n+    \"\"\"\n+    return [result for mapping in pending_results\n+            for result in list(mapping.get(task_id, {}).values())]\n+\n+\n class EventletAdaptedEvent:\n     \"\"\"\n     An adapted eventlet event, designed to match the API of `threading.Event` and\n@@ -272,10 +283,15 @@ def _maybe_resolve_from_buffer(self, result):\n         result._maybe_set_cache(self._pending_messages.take(result.id))\n \n     def _add_pending_result(self, task_id, result, weak=False):\n+        # Every waiter is kept until its task is ready or forgotten (Issue\n+        # #4925), so a get() that timed out keeps its result object alive\n+        # until then: one object per call for a task that never finishes.\n+        if any(r is result for r in _waiting_on(self._pending_results, task_id)):\n+            return\n         concrete, weak_ = self._pending_results\n-        if task_id not in weak_ and result.id not in concrete:\n-            (weak_ if weak else concrete)[task_id] = result\n-            self.result_consumer.consume_from(task_id)\n+        mapping, waiters = (weak_, WeakValueDictionary) if weak else (concrete, dict)\n+        mapping.setdefault(task_id, waiters())[id(result)] = result\n+        self.result_consumer.consume_from(task_id)\n \n     def add_pending_results(self, results, weak=False):\n         self.result_consumer.drainer.start()\n@@ -283,13 +299,24 @@ def add_pending_results(self, results, weak=False):\n                 for result in results]\n \n     def remove_pending_result(self, result):\n-        self._remove_pending_result(result.id)\n-        self.on_result_fulfilled(result)\n+        # Another result object may still be waiting on this id (Issue #4925),\n+        # only stop consuming once the last one is gone.\n+        if not self._remove_pending_result(result):\n+            self.on_result_fulfilled(result)\n         return result\n \n-    def _remove_pending_result(self, task_id):\n+    def _remove_pending_result(self, result):\n+        \"\"\"Stop tracking ``result``, return the results still waiting on its id.\"\"\"\n         for mapping in self._pending_results:\n-            mapping.pop(task_id, None)\n+            waiters = mapping.get(result.id)\n+            if waiters is None:\n+                continue\n+            if waiters.get(id(result)) is result:\n+                waiters.pop(id(result), None)\n+            # also when the cycle collector already cleared a weak waiter\n+            if not waiters:\n+                mapping.pop(result.id, None)\n+        return _waiting_on(self._pending_results, result.id)\n \n     def on_result_fulfilled(self, result):\n         self.result_consumer.cancel_for(result.id)\n@@ -418,26 +445,17 @@ def on_wait_for_pending(self, result, timeout=None, **kwargs):\n     def on_out_of_band_result(self, message):\n         self.on_state_change(message.payload, message)\n \n-    def _get_pending_result(self, task_id):\n-        for mapping in self._pending_results:\n-            try:\n-                return mapping[task_id]\n-            except KeyError:\n-                pass\n-        raise KeyError(task_id)\n-\n     def on_state_change(self, meta, message):\n         if self.on_message:\n             self.on_message(meta)\n         if meta['status'] in states.READY_STATES:\n             task_id = meta['task_id']\n-            try:\n-                result = self._get_pending_result(task_id)\n-            except KeyError:\n+            results = _waiting_on(self._pending_results, task_id)\n+            if not results:\n                 # send to buffer in case we received this result\n                 # before it was added to _pending_results.\n                 self._pending_messages.put(task_id, meta)\n-            else:\n+            for result in results:\n                 result._maybe_set_cache(meta)\n                 buckets = self.buckets\n                 try:\ndiff --git a/celery/backends/base.py b/celery/backends/base.py\nindex f506c20af6d..15aafe865b1 100644\n--- a/celery/backends/base.py\n+++ b/celery/backends/base.py\n@@ -12,7 +12,6 @@\n from datetime import timedelta\n from functools import partial\n from uuid import UUID\n-from weakref import WeakValueDictionary\n \n from billiard.einfo import ExceptionInfo\n from kombu.compression import compress, decompress\n@@ -271,7 +270,7 @@ def __init__(self, app,\n         self.max_retries = conf.get('result_backend_max_retries', float(\"inf\"))\n         self.thread_safe = conf.get('result_backend_thread_safe', False)\n \n-        self._pending_results = pending_results_t({}, WeakValueDictionary())\n+        self._pending_results = pending_results_t({}, {})\n         self._pending_messages = BufferMap(MESSAGE_BUFFER_MAX)\n         self.url = url\n \n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/t/integration/test_canvas.py b/t/integration/test_canvas.py\nindex 62358fcc8cf..cd5fc3069fe 100644\n--- a/t/integration/test_canvas.py\n+++ b/t/integration/test_canvas.py\n@@ -211,7 +211,6 @@ def test_complex_chain(self, manager):\n         res = c()\n         assert res.get(timeout=TIMEOUT) == [64, 65, 66, 67]\n \n-    @pytest.mark.xfail(raises=TimeoutError, reason=\"Task is timeout\")\n     def test_group_results_in_chain(self, manager):\n         # This adds in an explicit test for the special case added in commit\n         # 1e3fcaa969de6ad32b52a3ed8e74281e5e5360e6\n@@ -648,8 +647,7 @@ def test_chain_of_a_chord_and_three_tasks_and_a_group(self, manager):\n         res = c()\n         assert res.get(timeout=TIMEOUT) == [8, 8]\n \n-    @pytest.mark.xfail(raises=TimeoutError, reason=\"Task is timeout\")\n-    def test_nested_chain_group_lone(self, manager):  # Fails with Redis 5.x\n+    def test_nested_chain_group_lone(self, manager):\n         \"\"\"\n         Test that a lone group in a chain completes.\n         \"\"\"\ndiff --git a/t/unit/backends/test_asynchronous.py b/t/unit/backends/test_asynchronous.py\nindex 3f2b9033f67..4e9b369fe41 100644\n--- a/t/unit/backends/test_asynchronous.py\n+++ b/t/unit/backends/test_asynchronous.py\n@@ -1,17 +1,20 @@\n+import gc\n import logging\n import os\n import socket\n import sys\n import threading\n import time\n+from collections import deque\n from unittest.mock import Mock, patch\n \n import pytest\n from vine import promise\n \n-from celery import chain, group\n+from celery import chain, group, states\n from celery.backends.asynchronous import E_CELERY_RESTART_REQUIRED, BaseResultConsumer, greenletDrainer\n from celery.backends.base import Backend\n+from celery.backends.redis import RedisBackend\n from celery.backends.rpc import RPCBackend\n from celery.utils import cached_property\n \n@@ -746,3 +749,113 @@ def test_join_native_with_ready_nested_group(self, app, timeout):\n \n         result.on_ready()\n         assert result.join_native(timeout=timeout) == [101, [1001, 2001]]\n+\n+    @staticmethod\n+    def _backend(app, backend_cls):\n+        backend = backend_cls(app=app)\n+        backend.result_consumer.consume_from = Mock(name='consume_from')\n+        backend.result_consumer.cancel_for = Mock(name='cancel_for')\n+        app._backend = backend\n+        return backend\n+\n+    @staticmethod\n+    def _success(task_id):\n+        return {'task_id': task_id, 'status': states.SUCCESS, 'result': 42,\n+                'traceback': None, 'children': None}\n+\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize('backend_cls', [RedisBackend, RPCBackend])\n+    def test_dropping_a_duplicate_keeps_the_pending_result(\n+            self, app, backend_cls):\n+        # Issue #4925: a throwaway AsyncResult for an id that is already\n+        # pending, e.g. a progress poll of a group member, must not take the\n+        # pending one with it when it is garbage collected.\n+        backend = self._backend(app, backend_cls)\n+        pending = app.AsyncResult('tid')\n+        backend.add_pending_result(pending, weak=True)\n+\n+        duplicate = app.AsyncResult('tid')\n+        del duplicate\n+\n+        backend.result_consumer.cancel_for.assert_not_called()\n+        backend.result_consumer.on_state_change(self._success('tid'), None)\n+        assert pending._cache['result'] == 42\n+\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize('backend_cls', [RedisBackend, RPCBackend])\n+    def test_every_waiter_for_an_id_is_resolved(self, app, backend_cls):\n+        # Issue #4925: AsyncResult(id).get() while another AsyncResult for\n+        # the same id is pending hung forever, the second one was never\n+        # registered so nothing ever filled its cache.\n+        backend = self._backend(app, backend_cls)\n+        first, second = app.AsyncResult('tid'), app.AsyncResult('tid')\n+        backend.add_pending_result(first)\n+        backend.add_pending_result(second)\n+\n+        backend.result_consumer.on_state_change(self._success('tid'), None)\n+\n+        assert first._cache['result'] == 42\n+        assert second._cache['result'] == 42\n+\n+    def test_weak_result_freed_by_the_cycle_collector_is_untracked(self, app):\n+        # The cycle collector clears weak references before it calls\n+        # __del__, so the result is already gone from its waiters when\n+        # __del__ asks the backend to remove it.\n+        backend = self._backend(app, RedisBackend)\n+        result = app.AsyncResult('tid')\n+        backend.add_pending_result(result, weak=True)\n+        result.cycle = result\n+        del result\n+        gc.collect()\n+\n+        assert 'tid' not in backend._pending_results.weak\n+        backend.result_consumer.cancel_for.assert_called_once_with('tid')\n+\n+    def test_subscription_cancelled_when_last_waiter_is_removed(self, app):\n+        backend = self._backend(app, RedisBackend)\n+        cancel_for = backend.result_consumer.cancel_for\n+        first, second = app.AsyncResult('tid'), app.AsyncResult('tid')\n+        backend.add_pending_result(first)\n+        backend.add_pending_result(second)\n+\n+        backend.remove_pending_result(first)\n+        cancel_for.assert_not_called()\n+        backend.remove_pending_result(second)\n+        cancel_for.assert_called_once_with('tid')\n+\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize('first_weak,second_weak', [\n+        (False, False), (True, True), (True, False), (False, True),\n+    ])\n+    def test_same_result_added_twice_is_tracked_once(\n+            self, app, first_weak, second_weak):\n+        # e.g. result.then() and then result.get() on the same object\n+        backend = self._backend(app, RedisBackend)\n+        result = app.AsyncResult('tid')\n+\n+        backend.add_pending_result(result, weak=first_weak)\n+        backend.add_pending_result(result, weak=second_weak)\n+\n+        backend.result_consumer.consume_from.assert_called_once_with('tid')\n+        assert sum(len(m.get('tid', {})) for m in backend._pending_results) == 1\n+        backend.remove_pending_result(result)\n+        assert not any(backend._pending_results)\n+        backend.result_consumer.cancel_for.assert_called_once_with('tid')\n+\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize('backend_cls', [RedisBackend, RPCBackend])\n+    def test_native_join_gets_each_member_once_with_a_duplicate_waiter(\n+            self, app, backend_cls):\n+        # Issue #4925 at the iter_native level: a member is collected into\n+        # the group's bucket exactly once, resolved, while another\n+        # AsyncResult for its id is also waiting.\n+        backend = self._backend(app, backend_cls)\n+        member = app.AsyncResult('tid')\n+        backend.add_pending_result(member, weak=True)\n+        bucket = deque()\n+        backend._collect_into(member, bucket)\n+        duplicate = app.AsyncResult('tid')\n+        backend.add_pending_result(duplicate)\n+\n+        backend.result_consumer.on_state_change(self._success('tid'), None)\n+\n+        assert [node.id for node in bucket] == ['tid']\n+        assert bucket[0]._cache['result'] == 42\n+        assert member._cache['result'] == 42\n+        assert duplicate._cache['result'] == 42\n", "problem_statement": "Unstable group workflow feature. \n## Checklist\nsoftware -> celery:4.2.0 (windowlicker) kombu:4.2.1 py:3.6.3\n            billiard:3.5.0.4 py-amqp:2.3.2\nplatform -> system:Linux arch:64bit imp:CPython\nloader   -> celery.loaders.app.AppLoader\nsettings -> transport:amqp results:redis://127.0.0.1:6379/\n\nBROKER_URL: 'amqp://guest:********@127.0.0.1:5672//'\nCELERY_ACCEPT_CONTENT: ['json']\nCELERY_RESULT_BACKEND: 'redis://127.0.0.1:6379/'\nCELERY_RESULT_SERIALIZER: 'json'\nCELERY_TASK_PROTOCOL: 2\nCELERY_TASK_SERIALIZER: 'json'\nos: <module 'os' from '/home/ehengao/miniconda3/envs/nia-dcs/lib/python3.6/os.py'>\nCELERY_INCLUDE: ['nia_dcs.ccwp.tasks']\n\n* [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `master` branch of Celery.\nyes\n\n## Steps to reproduce\nRun following test\n\n\n```\ndef test_add_with_group():\n    \"\"\"test add task from the tutorial as a group\n    \"\"\"\n    for i in range(100):\n        print(f'Runnint test {i}')\n        add1 = add.s(1, 2)\n        add2 = add.s(3, 4)\n        g = group(add1, add2)()\n        print(f'GroupTasks:{g} sent!')\n        r = g.get()\n        print(r)\n```\n\n## Expected behavior\nTask would pass every time. (randomly not always happen)\n\n## Actual behavior\nTask some time blocked and causing pytest in continues integration never returns. After doing some digging into the celery code. noticed that things randomly happen when the AsyncResult.**del** triggered somehow to delete RedisBacken._pending_result before the result is adding to the ResultConsumer.buckets causing the GroupResult still waiting for one of the task to finish.\n\n\n", "hints_text": "Hello,\r\nwe witnessed the same issue on our side. We had to re-implement polling in order to have a consistent behavior.\r\n\r\n```\r\nwhile not async_res.ready():\r\n            sleep(interval)\r\n            if timeout and time() - start > timeout:\r\n                raise TimeoutError\r\nreturn async_res.get(propagate=propagate)\r\n```\r\n\r\nAny news on this issue ? On version 3.X we never had any issue of this kind.\nI'm experiencing the same issue. https://github.com/celery/celery/issues/5040\r\n\nSaw this as well, although it's certainly more like a race condition where we couldn't reliably reproduce it locally. We did have see it sometimes using a docker-compose file (slightly modified since we have separate worker and requestor nodes) that looks something like:\r\n\r\n```yaml\r\nversion: '3'\r\nservices:\r\n    rabbitmq:\r\n        image: rabbitmq:3.6-alpine\r\n        ports:\r\n            - \"5672\"\r\n        networks:\r\n            - messaging\r\n\r\n    redis:\r\n        image: redis:3-alpine\r\n        ports:\r\n            - \"6379\"\r\n        networks:\r\n            - messaging\r\n\r\n    uut:\r\n        image: <your testing image>\r\n        depends_on:\r\n            - rabbitmq\r\n            - redis\r\n        networks:\r\n            - messaging\r\n        environment:\r\n            - CELERY_BROKER=amqp://guest:guest@rabbitmq:5672\r\n            - CELERY_RESULT_BACKEND=redis://redis:6379/0\r\n\r\nnetworks:\r\n    messaging:\r\n```\r\n\r\n(Fill in the unit under test (uut) image above, or change it to build a Docker image). Larger executions, e.g. with 10 tasks taking ~5-10s each, was pretty reliably reproducing the problem, so hopefully that can be helpful for reproducing the bug. The comment above (https://github.com/celery/celery/issues/4925#issuecomment-410233096) did help us work around the issue for now.\nMaybe #5040 is a duplicate, but I commented there and found this issue there. Maybe the Redis backend is the culprit.\nanyone with a proper test case?\nThe problem is with Redis as a results backend, and the use of pub-sub to track successful completion of tasks. The first task in the `group` often doesn't get fully subscribed to. So when the task complete's it's not counted.\r\n\r\nThe `barrier` datastructure counts promises to know when everything is completed, at least for async based backends, and with the first task being missed. \r\n\r\nEach tasks goes two rounds of subscribing then unsubscribing. A regular task does sub-unsub-sub-WAIT-unsub-unsub. However, when the issue occurs, the first task goes through sub-unsub\n@auvipy will try to create one. It's a troublesome bug -- I suspect that there is a timing/synchronisation aspect to it as well.\nSorry, if anyone can step up and create a proper test case, that would be appreciated.\nI guess the test case is already attached?\r\ndef test_add_with_group():\r\n    \"\"\"test add task from the tutorial as a group\r\n    \"\"\"\r\n    for i in range(100):\r\n        print(f'Runnint test {i}')\r\n        add1 = add.s(1, 2)\r\n        add2 = add.s(3, 4)\r\n        g = group(add1, add2)()\r\n        print(f'GroupTasks:{g} sent!')\r\n        r = g.get()\r\n        print(r)\n@auvipy you asked for a test case, no?\nyes but later check there is already one :dagger: \nNeither `asynresult.ready()` nor `asynresult.successful()` ever returns True for me. I just get stuck in the loop until timeout. I have verified that the tasks do indeed succeed.\r\nUsing `apply` instead of `apply_async` obviously works fine.\r\n\r\nAny ideas? Using Celery 4.3.0\n@fdemmer  the only current work around is to wait on each task iteratively till they are all done -- ie. pretend the 'group' construct is just a collection of tasks.\nHi guys, it's been a month now... any updates? This really breaks the workflows as of now.\nHi guys has this been resolved? Can we confirm that this issue only presents when using redis for the backend?\n@kirkjules I chased this issue for several hours.. I'm pretty confident it's a Redis only problem... and it's only because the code switched to using PUB/SUB on Redis. As far as I can tell, this is still an issue.\r\n\r\nAnyone looking to take a bounty on this bug?\nWe have a more pressing issue with Redis at the moment.\r\nPlease stand by or attempt to resolve this issue and provide us with a PR.\n+1 on this issue, causing gridlocks on our backend\nPlease avoid +1s.\r\nThe issue reactions like :+1: are there for a reason.\nAny updates on this? We are using a Redis backend and I am seeing this issue. Please suggest workarounds/fixes\nUnfortunately, none of the maintainers had the time or need to fix this issue.\r\nIf you can, please help us debug the problem.", "created_at": "2026-10-25T14:04:14Z"}
{"repo": "celery/celery", "pull_number": 10939, "instance_id": "celery__celery-10939", "issue_numbers": ["9236"], "base_commit": "1e90daa777abfe1cb1becf01f90b60ce61f3b629", "patch": "diff --git a/celery/worker/consumer/consumer.py b/celery/worker/consumer/consumer.py\nindex 35952a0e1a3..4ce7ea86db5 100644\n--- a/celery/worker/consumer/consumer.py\n+++ b/celery/worker/consumer/consumer.py\n@@ -464,7 +464,11 @@ def on_connection_error_before_connected(self, exc):\n               'Trying to reconnect...')\n \n     def on_connection_error_after_connected(self, exc):\n-        warn(CONNECTION_RETRY, exc_info=True)\n+        # Keep the exception in the warning so default-level logs still say\n+        # what went wrong, but leave the traceback for debug logging where\n+        # application log handlers will not capture it.\n+        warn('%s (%r)', CONNECTION_RETRY, exc)\n+        debug('Connection error: %r', exc, exc_info=exc)\n         # Bound the sockets that are already open before cleanup reads from\n         # them; collect()'s socket_timeout only applies to new sockets.\n         bound_open_broker_sockets(self.connection, COLLECT_SOCKET_TIMEOUT)\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/t/unit/worker/test_consumer.py b/t/unit/worker/test_consumer.py\nindex 826803b0009..d09d871ac74 100644\n--- a/t/unit/worker/test_consumer.py\n+++ b/t/unit/worker/test_consumer.py\n@@ -22,8 +22,8 @@\n from celery.utils.time import utcoffset\n from celery.worker import state as worker_state\n from celery.worker.consumer.agent import Agent\n-from celery.worker.consumer.consumer import (CANCEL_TASKS_BY_DEFAULT, CLOSE, COLLECT_SOCKET_TIMEOUT, TERMINATE,\n-                                             Consumer)\n+from celery.worker.consumer.consumer import (CANCEL_TASKS_BY_DEFAULT, CLOSE, COLLECT_SOCKET_TIMEOUT,\n+                                             CONNECTION_RETRY, TERMINATE, Consumer)\n from celery.worker.consumer.events import Events\n from celery.worker.consumer.gossip import Gossip\n from celery.worker.consumer.heart import Heart\n@@ -686,6 +686,24 @@ def test_collects_with_socket_timeout_on_connection_error(self):\n         old_conn.close.assert_called_once()\n         assert c.connection is None\n \n+    def test_connection_error_traceback_is_logged_at_debug_level(self, caplog):\n+        c = self.get_consumer()\n+        exc = ConnectionError('broker went away')\n+\n+        with caplog.at_level(logging.DEBUG, logger='celery.worker.consumer.consumer'):\n+            c.on_connection_error_after_connected(exc)\n+\n+        warning = next(record for record in caplog.records\n+                       if record.levelno == logging.WARNING\n+                       and record.getMessage().startswith(CONNECTION_RETRY))\n+\n+        assert warning.exc_info is None\n+        assert warning.getMessage() == f'{CONNECTION_RETRY} ({exc!r})'\n+        debug_record = next(record for record in caplog.records\n+                            if record.levelno == logging.DEBUG\n+                            and record.getMessage() == f'Connection error: {exc!r}')\n+        assert debug_record.exc_info[1] is exc\n+\n     def test_bounds_already_open_sockets_before_collect(self):\n         # ``collect(socket_timeout=...)`` only sets the *default* timeout,\n         # which applies to sockets created afterwards.  It cannot bound a\n", "problem_statement": "Option to disable stack trace in case of - on_connection_error_after_connected\n# Checklist\r\n- [x ] I have checked the [issues list](https://github.com/celery/celery/issues?q=is%3Aissue+label%3A%22Issue+Type%3A+Enhancement%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)\r\n  for similar or identical enhancement to an existing feature.\r\n- [ x] I have checked the [pull requests list](https://github.com/celery/celery/pulls?q=is%3Apr+label%3A%22Issue+Type%3A+Enhancement%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)\r\n  for existing proposed enhancements.\r\n- [ x] I have checked the [commit log](https://github.com/celery/celery/commits/main)\r\n  to find out if the same enhancement was already implemented in the\r\n  main branch.\r\n- [ x] I have included all related issues and possible duplicate issues in this issue\r\n      (If there are none, check this box anyway).\r\n\r\n# Brief Summary\r\nIn case broker connection is lost after worker has started, it raises CONNECTION_RETRY warning. This warning by default carries the stack trace with it. When celery log is integrated with application log, this shows the strack trace in the log files. Doesn't look great for applications where careful logging has been implemented. Allow an option to disable sending this strack trace for this event.\r\n\r\n# Design\r\n\r\n## Architectural Considerations\r\nNone\r\n\r\n## Proposed Behavior\r\nAllow an option to disable sending this strack trace for this event.\r\n\n", "hints_text": "Contributions are welcome.\nhmm, in this case, should we split the logging so the RETRY message is still a warning, but the stack trace is in a `.debug()` or `.info()` log message? \r\n\r\nExample:\r\n\r\n```python\r\nwarn(CONNECTION_RETRY)\r\ndebug(\"Stack trace:\", exc_info=True)\r\n```\r\n\r\nWe can also hint to the user that they can access the stack trace via the debug logs\r\n", "created_at": "2026-10-23T06:08:58Z"}
{"repo": "celery/celery", "pull_number": 10938, "instance_id": "celery__celery-10938", "issue_numbers": ["4697"], "base_commit": "bae6b445979b387476b18f878d1ef0f5d17dadde", "patch": "diff --git a/celery/apps/multi.py b/celery/apps/multi.py\nindex b9c552f3050..f6219d8dfdc 100644\n--- a/celery/apps/multi.py\n+++ b/celery/apps/multi.py\n@@ -356,8 +356,8 @@ def _node_from_options(self, p, name, prefix,\n                            suffix, cmd, append, options):\n         namespace, nodename, _ = build_nodename(name, prefix, suffix)\n         namespace = nodename if nodename in p.namespaces else namespace\n-        return Node(nodename, cmd, append,\n-                    p.optmerge(namespace, options), p.passthrough)\n+        return self.Node(nodename, cmd, append,\n+                         p.optmerge(namespace, options), p.passthrough)\n \n     def _get_ranges(self, names):\n         noderange = int(names[0])\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/t/unit/apps/test_multi.py b/t/unit/apps/test_multi.py\nindex eb2b4a6556c..793df26b584 100644\n--- a/t/unit/apps/test_multi.py\n+++ b/t/unit/apps/test_multi.py\n@@ -57,6 +57,24 @@ def multi_args(p, *args, **kwargs):\n \n class test_multi_args:\n \n+    def test_parse_uses_custom_node_class(self, tmp_path):\n+        class CustomNode(Node):\n+            pass\n+\n+        class CustomMultiParser(MultiParser):\n+            Node = CustomNode\n+\n+        p = NamespacedOptionParser([\n+            'foo', 'bar',\n+            f'--pidfile={tmp_path}/%n.pid',\n+            f'--logfile={tmp_path}/%n.log',\n+        ])\n+        p.parse()\n+        nodes = list(CustomMultiParser(suffix='example.com').parse(p))\n+\n+        assert [node.name for node in nodes] == ['foo@example.com', 'bar@example.com']\n+        assert all(isinstance(node, CustomNode) for node in nodes)\n+\n     @pytest.mark.parametrize('option', ['--pidfile', '-p'])\n     @pytest.mark.parametrize('template,expected', [\n         ('%n.pid', 'worker.pid'),\n", "problem_statement": "`MultiParser` does not instantiate the `MultiParser.Node` class.\nOn the `tip`:\n\nhttps://github.com/celery/celery/blob/8c753d7febd3be4e56cb0fda78b0704130a85299/celery/apps/multi.py#L307\n\n`MultiParser` defines a class variable `Node` (by default assigned to the default `Node` implementation), presumably for subclasses to easily customize which Node is instantiated by the parser.\n\nHowever, it seems the `MultiParser._node_from_options()` method always instantiates a default `celery.apps.multi.Node`, disregarding `MultiParser.Node`.\n\nCould we change this to use the `MultiParser.Node` class instead?\n\n```python\nreturn self.Node(nodename, cmd, append,\n                 p.optmerge(namespace, options), p.passthrough)\n```\n\nI am happy to submit a patch if this seems reasonable.\n\n\n", "hints_text": "Really!! sorry for not noticing this in 1 year!! you can submit a patch with a test case.", "created_at": "2026-10-23T05:42:43Z"}
{"repo": "celery/celery", "pull_number": 10926, "instance_id": "celery__celery-10926", "issue_numbers": ["10914"], "base_commit": "bff6c2f5390c3abda5bb02bdd1eabb99891f5970", "patch": "diff --git a/docs/getting-started/backends-and-brokers/rabbitmq.rst b/docs/getting-started/backends-and-brokers/rabbitmq.rst\nindex b3f3d722a74..4096d10b8f9 100644\n--- a/docs/getting-started/backends-and-brokers/rabbitmq.rst\n+++ b/docs/getting-started/backends-and-brokers/rabbitmq.rst\n@@ -238,6 +238,55 @@ we can no longer increase the prefetch count and fetch another task from the que\n In order to properly schedule ETA/Countdown tasks we automatically detect if quorum queues are used\n and in case they are, Celery automatically enables :ref:`Native Delayed Delivery <native-delayed-delivery>`.\n \n+.. _quorum-queues-delivery-limit:\n+\n+Delivery Limit\n+--------------\n+\n+Starting with RabbitMQ 4.0, quorum queues have a default delivery limit of 20.\n+When a message is redelivered more times than that, RabbitMQ drops it, or\n+dead-letters it if the queue has a `dead letter exchange`_.\n+\n+This affects :setting:`task_acks_late`. If a task keeps crashing its worker,\n+the message goes back to the queue after each crash, and each return counts\n+toward the limit. That happens when the pool process is killed and\n+:setting:`task_reject_on_worker_lost` is enabled, or when the whole worker is\n+killed, such as when its container runs out of memory. Once the limit is\n+reached, the task is dropped. Its state stays ``PENDING`` (``STARTED`` with\n+:setting:`task_track_started`). The worker logs each crash as\n+``WorkerLostError``, but it doesn't log the drop.\n+\n+To keep control over this:\n+\n+- Change the limit with the ``x-delivery-limit`` queue argument when the queue\n+  is declared:\n+\n+  .. code-block:: python\n+\n+      from kombu import Queue\n+\n+      app.conf.task_queues = [\n+          Queue('celery', queue_arguments={\n+              'x-queue-type': 'quorum',\n+              'x-delivery-limit': 50,\n+          }),\n+      ]\n+\n+  RabbitMQ refuses to redeclare an existing queue with different arguments\n+  (``PRECONDITION_FAILED - inequivalent arg``), so for a queue that already\n+  exists, use a ``delivery-limit`` policy instead.\n+- Configure a `dead letter exchange`_ so dropped tasks are kept instead of lost.\n+- Use :setting:`task_retry_on_worker_lost` to cap how many times the task runs\n+  again after a pool process is lost. The cap is the task's ``max_retries``\n+  (3 by default). Each retry is published as a new message, so these retries\n+  don't count toward the delivery limit. It doesn't cover the whole worker\n+  being killed.\n+\n+See RabbitMQ's `poison message handling`_ docs for details.\n+\n+.. _`dead letter exchange`: https://www.rabbitmq.com/docs/dlx\n+.. _`poison message handling`: https://www.rabbitmq.com/docs/quorum-queues#poison-message-handling\n+\n .. _native-delayed-delivery:\n \n Native Delayed Delivery\ndiff --git a/docs/userguide/configuration.rst b/docs/userguide/configuration.rst\nindex 167a49a928d..e97f6b27066 100644\n--- a/docs/userguide/configuration.rst\n+++ b/docs/userguide/configuration.rst\n@@ -662,6 +662,13 @@ has been executed, not *right before* (the default behavior).\n \n     FAQ: :ref:`faq-acks_late-vs-retry`.\n \n+.. note::\n+\n+    On RabbitMQ 4.0 and later, quorum queues drop a message (or dead-letter\n+    it) after 20 redeliveries by default. A task that keeps crashing its\n+    worker can disappear this way, and the worker won't log the drop.\n+    See :ref:`quorum-queues-delivery-limit` for details.\n+\n .. setting:: task_acks_on_failure_or_timeout\n \n ``task_acks_on_failure_or_timeout``\n@@ -732,6 +739,13 @@ worker.\n     what you're doing. See :setting:`task_retry_on_worker_lost` for a way\n     to limit how many times a task is sent again.\n \n+.. note::\n+\n+    On RabbitMQ 4.0 and later, quorum queues drop a message (or dead-letter\n+    it) after 20 redeliveries by default. A task that keeps crashing its\n+    worker can disappear this way, and the worker won't log the drop.\n+    See :ref:`quorum-queues-delivery-limit` for details.\n+\n .. setting:: task_retry_on_worker_lost\n \n ``task_retry_on_worker_lost``\n", "test_patch": "", "problem_statement": "Docs: quorum queues drop a task after 20 redeliveries\n# Checklist\n\n- [x] I have checked the [issues list](https://github.com/celery/celery/issues?utf8=%E2%9C%93&q=is%3Aissue+label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22+)\n  for similar or identical bug reports.\n- [x] I have checked the [pull requests list](https://github.com/celery/celery/pulls?q=is%3Apr+label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)\n  for existing proposed fixes.\n- [x] I have checked the [commit log](https://github.com/celery/celery/commits/main)\n  to find out if the bug was already fixed in the main branch.\n- [x] I have included all related issues and possible duplicate issues in this issue\n      (If there are none, check this box anyway).\n\n## Related Issues and Possible Duplicates\n\n#### Related Issues\n\n- None\n\n#### Possible Duplicates\n\n- None\n\n# Description\n\nSince RabbitMQ 4.0, quorum queues give each message a delivery limit of 20 by default. When a message passes that limit, RabbitMQ drops it, or sends it to a dead letter exchange if the queue has one. Celery's docs on quorum queues and on `task_reject_on_worker_lost` don't mention this.\n\nIt matters most with `task_acks_late` and `task_reject_on_worker_lost`. If a task keeps killing its worker process, Celery puts it back on the queue each time. On a quorum queue the task runs 21 times and then disappears. Afterward its state is still PENDING, and the worker log has no message about it.\n\n<details>\n<summary><code>tasks.py</code> and the commands to run it</summary>\n\n```python\n# tasks.py\nimport os\nimport signal\n\nfrom celery import Celery\n\napp = Celery(\"tasks\", broker=\"amqp://guest:guest@localhost:5672//\")\napp.conf.update(\n    task_default_queue_type=\"quorum\",\n    worker_detect_quorum_queues=True,\n    broker_transport_options={\"confirm_publish\": True},\n    task_acks_late=True,\n    task_reject_on_worker_lost=True,\n)\n\n\n@app.task\ndef crash():\n    with open(\"runs.txt\", \"a\") as f:\n        f.write(\"run\\n\")\n    os.kill(os.getpid(), signal.SIGKILL)  # dies every time, like an OOM kill\n```\n\n```\npython -c \"from tasks import crash; crash.delay()\"\ncelery -A tasks worker -c 1\n# after a minute, stop the worker\nwc -l runs.txt     # 21\n```\n\n</details>\n\nI got 21 runs on RabbitMQ 4.1.8 with Celery 5.5.3, 5.7.0rc1 and main (`d6d754e`).\n\n# Suggestions\n\nAdd a note to the quorum queue section of the RabbitMQ broker page, and to `task_reject_on_worker_lost`. The note could say:\n\n- On RabbitMQ 4.0 and later, quorum queues drop a message after 20 redeliveries by default.\n- The `x-delivery-limit` queue argument or a policy changes the limit, and a dead letter exchange keeps the dropped tasks.\n- `task_retry_on_worker_lost`, new in 5.7, is another way to cap how many times the task runs.\n\n", "hints_text": "", "created_at": "2026-10-22T06:34:13Z"}
{"repo": "celery/celery", "pull_number": 10925, "instance_id": "celery__celery-10925", "issue_numbers": ["6968"], "base_commit": "55e23e8b52af9cf75334668b07a304c92dc1bdeb", "patch": "diff --git a/t/integration/tasks.py b/t/integration/tasks.py\nindex 98cdcd86116..ff86ce19887 100644\n--- a/t/integration/tasks.py\n+++ b/t/integration/tasks.py\n@@ -155,6 +155,19 @@ def add_replaced(self, x, y):\n     raise self.replace(add.s(x, y))\n \n \n+@shared_task(bind=True)\n+def record_replaced_task_nesting(self, replacements, nesting=None):\n+    \"\"\"Record the nesting level across successive task replacements.\"\"\"\n+    if nesting is None:\n+        nesting = []\n+    nesting.append(self.request.replaced_task_nesting)\n+    if replacements:\n+        replacement = record_replaced_task_nesting.s(replacements - 1, nesting)\n+        # Keep the caller's result queue when using the RPC backend.\n+        return self.replace(replacement.set(reply_to=self.request.reply_to))\n+    return nesting\n+\n+\n @shared_task(bind=True)\n def replace_with_chain(self, *args, link_msg=None):\n     c = chain(identity.s(*args), identity.s())\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/t/integration/test_tasks.py b/t/integration/test_tasks.py\nindex e807135bb27..560b09d9e63 100644\n--- a/t/integration/test_tasks.py\n+++ b/t/integration/test_tasks.py\n@@ -18,8 +18,8 @@\n from .conftest import TEST_BACKEND, get_active_redis_channels, get_redis_connection\n from .tasks import (AddParameterModel, ClassBasedAutoRetryTask, ExpectedException, add, add_ignore_result,\n                     add_not_typed, add_pydantic, add_pydantic_string_annotations, fail, fail_unpickleable,\n-                    print_unicode, reject_then_succeed, reject_without_requeue, retry, retry_once,\n-                    retry_once_headers, retry_once_priority, retry_unpickleable, return_properties,\n+                    print_unicode, record_replaced_task_nesting, reject_then_succeed, reject_without_requeue, retry,\n+                    retry_once, retry_once_headers, retry_once_priority, retry_unpickleable, return_properties,\n                     return_request_time_limits, second_order_replace1, sleeping,\n                     soft_time_limit_must_exceed_time_limit, task_with_declared_time_limits)\n \n@@ -803,6 +803,11 @@ def test_asyncresult_get_cancels_subscription(self, manager):\n \n \n class test_task_replacement:\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize('replacements', (0, 1, 3))\n+    def test_replaced_task_nesting_increments(self, manager, replacements):\n+        result = record_replaced_task_nesting.delay(replacements)\n+        assert result.get(timeout=TIMEOUT) == list(range(replacements + 1))\n+\n     def test_replaced_task_nesting_level_0(self, manager):\n         @task_received.connect\n         def task_received_handler(request, **kwargs):\n", "problem_statement": "replaced_task_nesting property should have an integration test\nIn #6916 we didn't include an integration test.\nWe should write one once #6649 is finally merged.\n\n\n", "hints_text": "I checked this against current `main` (`f0b1320073ba27b6b2d29377138be7e749589ac5`).\n\nIt looks like the original request was partially addressed by #8500 and #8501, which added integration coverage for `replaced_task_nesting`.\n\nHowever, the current integration tests only assert upper bounds such as:\n\n```python\nrequest.replaced_task_nesting < 1\nrequest.replaced_task_nesting <= 2\nrequest.replaced_task_nesting <= 3\n```\n\nThose assertions would still pass if every replacement incorrectly reported `0`, so they don't verify the intended increment semantics.\n\nI verified the current behavior with a real worker and nested replacements:\n\n```text\n[0, 1, 2, 3]\n```\n\nA small test-only improvement could add a bound integration task that records `self.request.replaced_task_nesting` through recursive replacements and asserts the exact sequence `[0, 1, 2, 3]`.\n\nThat would exercise the full publish \u2192 protocol header \u2192 worker request \u2192 task context path and would have caught the regression fixed by #8500.\n\nWould a focused test-only PR for that stronger assertion be useful here?\n@Dattuog yes sure thing", "created_at": "2026-10-22T03:39:39Z"}
{"repo": "celery/celery", "pull_number": 10922, "instance_id": "celery__celery-10922", "issue_numbers": ["10913"], "base_commit": "06291f66536467124907c4d8e5a2cfc60913fa4b", "patch": "diff --git a/celery/worker/control.py b/celery/worker/control.py\nindex 5e82fa02bb6..265e159cc07 100644\n--- a/celery/worker/control.py\n+++ b/celery/worker/control.py\n@@ -229,10 +229,15 @@ def _revoke(state, task_ids, terminate=False, signal=None, **kwargs):\n         # Tasks may override their backend.\n         backend = request.task.backend if request else state.app.backend\n         try:\n-            if backend.get_state(task_id) in states.READY_STATES:\n+            task_state = backend.get_state(task_id)\n+            if task_state in states.READY_STATES:\n                 # The task already has a result (or the chord error handler\n                 # failed it on its behalf); a revoke must not overwrite it.\n                 continue\n+            if task_state not in (states.PENDING, states.RECEIVED, states.RETRY, states.REJECTED):\n+                # Another worker started the task (STARTED, or a custom state\n+                # it set), so leave its state to that worker.\n+                continue\n             backend.mark_as_revoked(task_id, reason='revoked', store_result=True)\n         except Exception as exc:\n             logger.warning('Failed to mark task %s as revoked in backend: %s', task_id, exc)\ndiff --git a/docs/userguide/workers.rst b/docs/userguide/workers.rst\nindex 058261b5433..8fba3098b23 100644\n--- a/docs/userguide/workers.rst\n+++ b/docs/userguide/workers.rst\n@@ -590,6 +590,12 @@ When a worker receives a revoke request it will skip executing\n the task, but it won't terminate an already executing task unless\n the `terminate` option is set.\n \n+A worker that isn't running the task marks it ``REVOKED``, unless it has\n+finished or another worker has started it. A started task shows\n+``STARTED`` (with :setting:`task_track_started`) or a custom state it set.\n+Without :setting:`task_track_started`, a running task shows ``PENDING``, so\n+it's marked ``REVOKED`` and then stores its result when it's done.\n+\n .. note::\n \n     The terminate option is a last resort for administrators when\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/t/integration/test_tasks.py b/t/integration/test_tasks.py\nindex 560b09d9e63..3196de6182b 100644\n--- a/t/integration/test_tasks.py\n+++ b/t/integration/test_tasks.py\n@@ -313,6 +313,22 @@ def test_revoked(self, manager):\n         assert result.failed() is False\n         assert result.successful() is False\n \n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize('task_state', [celery.states.STARTED, 'PROGRESS'])\n+    def test_revoke_keeps_state_of_task_started_elsewhere(self, manager, task_state):\n+        if TEST_BACKEND.startswith('rpc'):\n+            raise pytest.skip('Requires a backend that stores task states.')\n+        backend = manager.app.backend\n+        started, not_started = str(uuid4()), str(uuid4())\n+        # Play another worker that has started a task. The session worker\n+        # gets the revoke but doesn't run the task, so without terminate it\n+        # must leave the state to the other worker.\n+        backend.store_result(started, {'hostname': 'other@host'}, task_state)\n+\n+        # The worker replies once it has handled the revoke\n+        assert manager.app.control.revoke([started, not_started], reply=True, timeout=10)\n+        assert backend.get_state(started) == task_state\n+        assert backend.get_state(not_started) == celery.states.REVOKED\n+\n     def test_revoked_by_headers_simple_canvas(self, manager):\n         \"\"\"Testing revoking of task using a stamped header\"\"\"\n         target_monitoring_id = uuid4().hex\ndiff --git a/t/smoke/tests/test_revoke.py b/t/smoke/tests/test_revoke.py\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 00000000000..9af1e704d26\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/t/smoke/tests/test_revoke.py\n@@ -0,0 +1,88 @@\n+\"\"\"Revoking a task while one of several workers runs it (#10913).\"\"\"\n+from __future__ import annotations\n+\n+from time import monotonic, sleep\n+\n+import pytest\n+from pytest_celery import RESULT_TIMEOUT, CeleryTestSetup, CeleryTestWorker, CeleryWorkerCluster\n+\n+from celery import Celery, states\n+from celery.exceptions import TaskRevokedError\n+from celery.result import AsyncResult\n+from t.smoke.tasks import long_running_task\n+\n+\n+@pytest.fixture\n+def celery_worker_cluster(\n+    celery_worker: CeleryTestWorker,\n+    celery_alt_dev_worker: CeleryTestWorker,\n+) -> CeleryWorkerCluster:\n+    cluster = CeleryWorkerCluster(celery_worker, celery_alt_dev_worker)\n+    yield cluster\n+    cluster.teardown()\n+\n+\n+def wait_until_started(result: AsyncResult) -> None:\n+    deadline = monotonic() + RESULT_TIMEOUT\n+    while result.app.AsyncResult(result.id).state != states.STARTED:\n+        assert monotonic() < deadline, f\"{result.id} never started\"\n+        sleep(0.1)\n+\n+\n+def poll_until_ready(result: AsyncResult) -> list[str]:\n+    \"\"\"Read the state the way a client does, and return each state seen.\"\"\"\n+    seen = []\n+    deadline = monotonic() + RESULT_TIMEOUT\n+    while True:\n+        state = result.state\n+        if not seen or seen[-1] != state:\n+            seen.append(state)\n+        if state in states.READY_STATES:\n+            return seen\n+        assert monotonic() < deadline, f\"{result.id} never finished, saw {seen}\"\n+        sleep(0.1)\n+\n+\n+class test_revoke:\n+    @pytest.fixture\n+    def default_worker_app(self, default_worker_app: Celery) -> Celery:\n+        app = default_worker_app\n+        app.conf.task_track_started = True\n+        return app\n+\n+    def test_running_task_is_not_marked_revoked(self, celery_setup: CeleryTestSetup):\n+        assert len(celery_setup.worker_cluster) > 1\n+        result = long_running_task.s(5).apply_async(queue=celery_setup.worker.worker_queue)\n+        wait_until_started(result)\n+\n+        # Every worker gets the revoke, but only one runs the task. Without\n+        # terminate the task keeps running, so it must not be reported as\n+        # REVOKED, and the client that revoked it must get its result.\n+        result.revoke()\n+        assert poll_until_ready(result) == [states.STARTED, states.SUCCESS]\n+        assert result.get(timeout=RESULT_TIMEOUT) is True\n+\n+    def test_terminated_task_is_marked_revoked(self, celery_setup: CeleryTestSetup):\n+        assert len(celery_setup.worker_cluster) > 1\n+        result = long_running_task.s(30).apply_async(queue=celery_setup.worker.worker_queue)\n+        wait_until_started(result)\n+\n+        result.revoke(terminate=True)\n+        seen = poll_until_ready(result)\n+        assert seen[-1] == states.REVOKED\n+        assert states.SUCCESS not in seen\n+        with pytest.raises(TaskRevokedError):\n+            result.get(timeout=RESULT_TIMEOUT)\n+\n+    def test_task_not_started_is_marked_revoked(self, celery_setup: CeleryTestSetup):\n+        assert len(celery_setup.worker_cluster) > 1\n+        result = long_running_task.s(1).apply_async(\n+            queue=celery_setup.worker.worker_queue, countdown=30,\n+        )\n+\n+        result.revoke()\n+        seen = poll_until_ready(result)\n+        assert seen[-1] == states.REVOKED\n+        assert set(seen) <= {states.PENDING, states.REVOKED}\n+        with pytest.raises(TaskRevokedError):\n+            result.get(timeout=RESULT_TIMEOUT)\ndiff --git a/t/unit/worker/test_control.py b/t/unit/worker/test_control.py\nindex 59f07e162c6..7a7bff5d819 100644\n--- a/t/unit/worker/test_control.py\n+++ b/t/unit/worker/test_control.py\n@@ -702,6 +702,7 @@ def test_repeated_revoke_uses_task_backend_for_known_request(self):\n         request = Mock()\n         request.id = tid = uuid()\n         task_backend = Mock()\n+        task_backend.get_state.return_value = states.PENDING\n         request.task.backend = task_backend\n         state = self.create_state()\n         state.consumer = Mock()\n@@ -1087,6 +1088,7 @@ def test_query_task_scheduled(self):\n \n     @patch('celery.Celery.backend', new=PropertyMock(name='backend'))\n     def test_revoke_backend_status_update(self):\n+        self.app.backend.get_state.return_value = states.PENDING\n         state = self.create_state()\n         task_ids = ['task-1', 'task-2']\n \n@@ -1113,7 +1115,7 @@ def test_revoke_keeps_finished_result(self, ready_state):\n             self.app.backend.mark_as_revoked.assert_called_once_with(\n                 'task-2', reason='revoked', store_result=True)\n \n-    @pytest.mark.parametrize('unready_state', sorted(states.UNREADY_STATES))\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize('unready_state', sorted(states.UNREADY_STATES - {states.STARTED}))\n     def test_revoke_marks_unfinished_task(self, unready_state):\n         with patch('celery.Celery.backend', new=PropertyMock(name='backend')):\n             self.app.backend.get_state.return_value = unready_state\n@@ -1124,6 +1126,21 @@ def test_revoke_marks_unfinished_task(self, unready_state):\n             self.app.backend.mark_as_revoked.assert_called_once_with(\n                 'task-1', reason='revoked', store_result=True)\n \n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize('terminate', [False, True])\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize('task_state', [states.STARTED, 'PROGRESS'])\n+    def test_revoke_keeps_state_of_task_started_elsewhere(self, task_state, terminate):\n+        # Another worker runs the task: it keeps running without terminate,\n+        # and with terminate, that worker stores REVOKED when it stops it.\n+        with patch('celery.Celery.backend', new=PropertyMock(name='backend')):\n+            self.app.backend.get_state.return_value = task_state\n+            state = self.create_state()\n+\n+            with patch('celery.worker.control._find_requests_by_id', return_value=[]):\n+                control._revoke(state, ['task-1'], terminate=terminate)\n+\n+            assert 'task-1' in worker_state.revoked\n+            self.app.backend.mark_as_revoked.assert_not_called()\n+\n     @patch('celery.Celery.backend', new=PropertyMock(name='backend'))\n     def test_revoke_state_lookup_failure_defensive(self):\n         self.app.backend.get_state.side_effect = Exception('Backend error')\n@@ -1144,6 +1161,7 @@ def test_revoke_backend_failure_defensive(self):\n \n     @patch('celery.Celery.backend', new=PropertyMock(name='backend'))\n     def test_revoke_terminate_backend_update(self):\n+        self.app.backend.get_state.return_value = states.PENDING\n         state = self.create_state()\n \n         with patch('celery.worker.control._find_requests_by_id', return_value=[]):\n", "problem_statement": "`revoke()` without `terminate` stores REVOKED for a running task that then succeeds\n# Checklist\n- [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `main` branch of Celery.\n- [ ] This has already been asked to the [discussions forum](https://github.com/celery/celery/discussions) first.\n- [x] I have read the relevant section in the\n  [contribution guide](https://docs.celeryq.dev/en/main/contributing.html#other-bugs)\n  on reporting bugs.\n- [x] I have checked the [issues list](https://github.com/celery/celery/issues?q=is%3Aissue+label%3A%22Issue+Type%3A+Bug+Report%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)\n  for similar or identical bug reports.\n- [x] I have checked the [pull requests list](https://github.com/celery/celery/pulls?q=is%3Apr+label%3A%22PR+Type%3A+Bugfix%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)\n  for existing proposed fixes.\n- [x] I have checked the [commit log](https://github.com/celery/celery/commits/main)\n  to find out if the bug was already fixed in the main branch.\n- [x] I have included all related issues and possible duplicate issues\n  in this issue (If there are none, check this box anyway).\n- [ ] I have tried to reproduce the issue with [pytest-celery](https://docs.celeryq.dev/projects/pytest-celery/en/latest/userguide/celery-bug-report.html) and added the reproduction script below.\n\n## Mandatory Debugging Information\n\n- [x] I have included the output of ``celery -A proj report`` in the issue.\n    (if you are not able to do this, then at least specify the Celery\n     version affected).\n- [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `main` branch of Celery.\n- [x] I have included the contents of ``pip freeze`` in the issue.\n- [x] I have included all the versions of all the external dependencies required\n  to reproduce this bug.\n\n## Optional Debugging Information\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one Python version\n  and/or implementation.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one message broker and/or\n  result backend.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one version of the message\n  broker and/or result backend.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one operating system.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one workers pool.\n- [x] I have tried reproducing the issue with autoscaling, retries,\n  ETA/Countdown & rate limits disabled.\n- [x] I have tried reproducing the issue after downgrading\n  and/or upgrading Celery and its dependencies.\n\n## Related Issues and Possible Duplicates\n\n#### Related Issues\n\n- #9869 started writing REVOKED right away.\n- #10679 keeps a revoke from replacing a finished result.\n\n#### Possible Duplicates\n\n- None\n\n## Environment & Settings\n**Celery version**: 5.7.0rc1 (collider). It also happens on 5.6.2, 5.6.3, 5.7.0a1, 5.7.0b1 and main (`d6d754e`). 5.6.1 and older work as expected.\n\n<details>\n<summary><b><code>celery report</code> Output:</b></summary>\n<p>\n\n```\nsoftware -> celery:5.7.0rc1 (collider) kombu:5.7.0rc1 py:3.13.16\n            billiard:4.3.1 redis:8.1.0\nplatform -> system:Linux arch:64bit, ELF\n            kernel version:6.18.44-fc-v139 imp:CPython\nloader   -> celery.loaders.app.AppLoader\nsettings -> transport:redis results:redis://localhost:6379/1\n\nbroker_url: 'redis://:********@localhost:6379/0'\nresult_backend: 'redis://localhost:6379/1'\ndeprecated_settings: None\ntask_track_started: True\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n# Steps to Reproduce\n\n## Required Dependencies\n- **Minimal Python Version**: N/A or Unknown (seen on 3.13.16)\n- **Minimal Celery Version**: 5.6.2 (first release with #9869)\n- **Minimal Kombu Version**: N/A or Unknown\n- **Minimal Broker Version**: N/A or Unknown (tested on Redis 7.0.15)\n- **Minimal Result Backend Version**: N/A or Unknown (tested on Redis 7.0.15)\n- **Minimal OS and/or Kernel Version**: N/A or Unknown (Linux)\n- **Minimal Broker Client Version**: N/A or Unknown\n- **Minimal Result Backend Client Version**: N/A or Unknown\n\n### Python Packages\n<details>\n<summary><b><code>pip freeze</code> Output:</b></summary>\n<p>\n\n```\namqp==5.4.1\nbilliard==4.3.1\ncelery==5.7.0rc1\nclick==8.5.0\nclick-didyoumean==0.3.1\nclick-plugins==1.1.1.2\nclick-repl==0.4.1\nkombu==5.7.0rc1\npackaging==26.3\nprompt-toolkit==3.0.53\npython-dateutil==2.9.0.post0\nredis==8.1.0\nsix==1.17.0\ntyping-extensions==4.16.0\ntzdata==2026.4\ntzlocal==5.4.4\nvine==5.1.0\nwcwidth==0.9.3\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n### Other Dependencies\n<details>\n<p>\nRedis 7.0.15 on localhost:6379, as both broker and result backend. Two workers.\n</p>\n</details>\n\n## Minimally Reproducible Test Case\n\n<details>\n<summary><code>tasks.py</code>, <code>check.py</code>, and the commands to run them</summary>\n<p>\n\n```python\n# tasks.py\nimport time\n\nfrom celery import Celery\n\napp = Celery(\"tasks\", broker=\"redis://localhost:6379/0\", backend=\"redis://localhost:6379/1\")\napp.conf.task_track_started = True\n\n\n@app.task\ndef slow():\n    time.sleep(10)\n    return \"done\"\n```\n\n```python\n# check.py\nimport time\n\nfrom tasks import app, slow\n\nresult = slow.delay()\ntime.sleep(2)                  # the task is now running on one of the workers\napp.control.revoke(result.id)  # without terminate=True, so the task keeps running\ntime.sleep(1)\nprint(\"state after revoke:\", result.state)\n\ntime.sleep(10)                 # the task has finished by now\nprint(\"new AsyncResult:   \", app.AsyncResult(result.id).state)\ntry:\n    print(\"result.get():      \", result.get(timeout=5))\nexcept Exception as exc:\n    print(\"result.get():      \", type(exc).__name__)\n```\n\n```\ncelery -A tasks worker -c 1 -n one@%h    # terminal 1\ncelery -A tasks worker -c 1 -n two@%h    # terminal 2\npython check.py                          # terminal 3\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n# Expected Behavior\n\nThe docs say a revoke without `terminate` doesn't stop a task that is already running. The task does keep running and finishes, so this is what 5.6.1 prints:\n\n```\nstate after revoke: STARTED\nnew AsyncResult:    SUCCESS\nresult.get():       done\n```\n\n# Actual Behavior\n\n```\nstate after revoke: REVOKED\nnew AsyncResult:    SUCCESS\nresult.get():       TaskRevokedError\n```\n\nThe task finished and stored SUCCESS, but the client that called `revoke()` gets `TaskRevokedError` from `get()`. It read REVOKED first, and `AsyncResult` caches the first final state it sees. Other clients see SUCCESS.\n\nEvery worker that isn't running the task writes REVOKED. The check from #10679 only skips tasks that already have a final state, and STARTED isn't one of them. When the task ends, it stores SUCCESS on top.\n\nOne fix would be to skip the REVOKED write when the stored state is STARTED, and leave the state to the worker that runs the task.\n\n", "hints_text": "", "created_at": "2026-10-21T23:09:10Z"}
{"repo": "celery/celery", "pull_number": 10921, "instance_id": "celery__celery-10921", "issue_numbers": ["10911"], "base_commit": "db0da7c5a9343f0b49c72cb90d2cf87e92f07b71", "patch": "diff --git a/celery/app/utils.py b/celery/app/utils.py\nindex 6039873ff74..6c7107581bf 100644\n--- a/celery/app/utils.py\n+++ b/celery/app/utils.py\n@@ -146,7 +146,9 @@ def without_defaults(self):\n         return Settings({}, self.maps[:-1])\n \n     def value_set_for(self, key):\n-        return key in self.without_defaults()\n+        self.finalize()\n+        # Also find settings given with a namespace or an old setting name.\n+        return key in Settings({}, self.maps[:-1], self._keys, self.prefix)\n \n     def find_option(self, name, namespace=''):\n         \"\"\"Search for option by name.\ndiff --git a/celery/backends/database/__init__.py b/celery/backends/database/__init__.py\nindex e0d7523b23f..a740574c3fe 100644\n--- a/celery/backends/database/__init__.py\n+++ b/celery/backends/database/__init__.py\n@@ -68,8 +68,12 @@ def __init__(self, dburi=None, engine_options=None, url=None, **kwargs):\n         # Override retry defaults to preserve backward compatibility.\n         # Previously, DatabaseBackend used a custom @retry decorator that always\n         # retried with max_retries=3. We maintain this behavior by default.\n-        self.always_retry = conf.get('result_backend_always_retry', True)\n-        self.max_retries = conf.get('result_backend_max_retries', 3)\n+        # Both settings always have a value, so check if the user set them.\n+        # None also means the default.\n+        if not conf.value_set_for('result_backend_always_retry') or self.always_retry is None:\n+            self.always_retry = True\n+        if not conf.value_set_for('result_backend_max_retries') or self.max_retries is None:\n+            self.max_retries = 3\n \n         if self.extended_result:\n             self.task_cls = TaskExtended\ndiff --git a/docs/userguide/configuration.rst b/docs/userguide/configuration.rst\nindex 3a75eac28ee..167a49a928d 100644\n--- a/docs/userguide/configuration.rst\n+++ b/docs/userguide/configuration.rst\n@@ -902,7 +902,7 @@ Default: :const:`False`\n     setting to :const:`True` by default to preserve backward compatibility\n     with the automatic retry behavior that was previously provided by an\n     internal ``@retry`` decorator. Other backends continue to default to\n-    :const:`False`.\n+    :const:`False`. Setting it to :const:`None` also gives :const:`True`.\n \n If enabled, the backend will try to retry on the event of recoverable\n exceptions instead of propagating the exception.\n@@ -942,6 +942,7 @@ Default: Inf\n     setting to ``3`` by default to preserve backward compatibility with the\n     behavior previously provided by an internal ``@retry`` decorator.\n     Other backends continue to default to :const:`Inf` (unlimited retries).\n+    Setting it to :const:`None` also gives ``3``.\n \n This is the maximum number of retries in case of recoverable exceptions.\n \n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/t/unit/app/test_utils.py b/t/unit/app/test_utils.py\nindex d5507b440f2..933804a74b6 100644\n--- a/t/unit/app/test_utils.py\n+++ b/t/unit/app/test_utils.py\n@@ -34,6 +34,17 @@ def test_copy_app_settings(self, copy_fun, configured, namespace, config):\n             assert copied.task_always_eager is False\n             assert app.conf.task_always_eager is True\n \n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize('namespace, config', [\n+        (None, {'task_always_eager': True}),\n+        ('CELERY', {'CELERY_TASK_ALWAYS_EAGER': True}),\n+        (None, {'CELERY_ALWAYS_EAGER': True}),\n+    ])\n+    def test_value_set_for(self, namespace, config):\n+        with self.Celery(set_as_current=False) as app:\n+            app.config_from_object(config, namespace=namespace)\n+            assert app.conf.value_set_for('task_always_eager')\n+            assert not app.conf.value_set_for('task_eager_propagates')\n+\n     @pytest.mark.parametrize('copy_fun', [lambda settings: settings.copy(), copy])\n     @pytest.mark.parametrize('cleared', [False, True])\n     def test_copy_preserves_deprecated_settings(self, copy_fun, cleared):\ndiff --git a/t/unit/backends/test_database.py b/t/unit/backends/test_database.py\nindex 33c52ed707c..639ce575f3a 100644\n--- a/t/unit/backends/test_database.py\n+++ b/t/unit/backends/test_database.py\n@@ -398,6 +398,30 @@ def test_without_fallback_exc_reaching_max_retries(self):\n             self.app.conf.result_backend_always_retry = prev\n             self.app.conf.result_backend_max_retries = prev_max_retries\n \n+    def test_retry_settings_defaults(self):\n+        tb = DatabaseBackend(self.uri, app=self.app)\n+        assert tb.always_retry is True\n+        assert tb.max_retries == 3\n+\n+    def test_retry_settings_set_to_none_use_the_defaults(self):\n+        self.app.conf.result_backend_always_retry = None\n+        self.app.conf.result_backend_max_retries = None\n+        tb = DatabaseBackend(self.uri, app=self.app)\n+        assert tb.always_retry is True\n+        assert tb.max_retries == 3\n+\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize('config', [\n+        {'result_backend_always_retry': False, 'result_backend_max_retries': 0},\n+        # The old setting names\n+        {'CELERY_RESULT_BACKEND_ALWAYS_RETRY': False, 'CELERY_RESULT_BACKEND_MAX_RETRIES': 0},\n+    ])\n+    def test_retry_settings_from_config_object(self, config):\n+        with self.Celery(set_as_current=False) as app:\n+            app.config_from_object(config)\n+            tb = DatabaseBackend(self.uri, app=app)\n+            assert tb.always_retry is False\n+            assert tb.max_retries == 0\n+\n     def test_missing_dburi_raises_ImproperlyConfigured(self):\n         self.app.conf.database_url = None\n         with pytest.raises(ImproperlyConfigured):\n", "problem_statement": "DatabaseBackend ignores its documented retry defaults\n# Checklist\n- [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `main` branch of Celery.\n- [ ] This has already been asked to the [discussions forum](https://github.com/celery/celery/discussions) first.\n- [x] I have read the relevant section in the\n  [contribution guide](https://docs.celeryq.dev/en/main/contributing.html#other-bugs)\n  on reporting bugs.\n- [x] I have checked the [issues list](https://github.com/celery/celery/issues?q=is%3Aissue+label%3A%22Issue+Type%3A+Bug+Report%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)\n  for similar or identical bug reports.\n- [x] I have checked the [pull requests list](https://github.com/celery/celery/pulls?q=is%3Apr+label%3A%22PR+Type%3A+Bugfix%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)\n  for existing proposed fixes.\n- [x] I have checked the [commit log](https://github.com/celery/celery/commits/main)\n  to find out if the bug was already fixed in the main branch.\n- [x] I have included all related issues and possible duplicate issues\n  in this issue (If there are none, check this box anyway).\n- [ ] I have tried to reproduce the issue with [pytest-celery](https://docs.celeryq.dev/projects/pytest-celery/en/latest/userguide/celery-bug-report.html) and added the reproduction script below.\n\n## Mandatory Debugging Information\n\n- [x] I have included the output of ``celery -A proj report`` in the issue.\n    (if you are not able to do this, then at least specify the Celery\n     version affected).\n- [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `main` branch of Celery.\n- [x] I have included the contents of ``pip freeze`` in the issue.\n- [x] I have included all the versions of all the external dependencies required\n  to reproduce this bug.\n\n## Optional Debugging Information\n- [x] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one Python version\n  and/or implementation.\n- [x] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one message broker and/or\n  result backend.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one version of the message\n  broker and/or result backend.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one operating system.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one workers pool.\n- [x] I have tried reproducing the issue with autoscaling, retries,\n  ETA/Countdown & rate limits disabled.\n- [x] I have tried reproducing the issue after downgrading\n  and/or upgrading Celery and its dependencies.\n\n## Related Issues and Possible Duplicates\n\n#### Related Issues\n\n- #10130 added these defaults, to fix #6540.\n- #10334 also assumes these defaults are on.\n\n#### Possible Duplicates\n\n- None\n\n## Environment & Settings\n**Celery version**: 5.7.0rc1 (collider). It also happens on 5.7.0a1, 5.7.0b1 and main (`d6d754e`). 5.6.3 and older retry these errors with a `@retry` decorator.\n\n<details>\n<summary><b><code>celery report</code> Output:</b></summary>\n<p>\n\n```\nsoftware -> celery:5.7.0rc1 (collider) kombu:5.7.0rc1 py:3.13.16\n            billiard:4.3.1 py-amqp:5.4.1\nplatform -> system:Linux arch:64bit, ELF\n            kernel version:6.18.44-fc-v139 imp:CPython\nloader   -> celery.loaders.app.AppLoader\nsettings -> transport:amqp results:db+sqlite:///results.db\n\nresult_backend: 'db+sqlite:///results.db'\ndeprecated_settings: None\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n# Steps to Reproduce\n\n## Required Dependencies\n- **Minimal Python Version**: N/A or Unknown\n- **Minimal Celery Version**: 5.7.0a1\n- **Minimal Kombu Version**: N/A or Unknown\n- **Minimal Broker Version**: N/A or Unknown\n- **Minimal Result Backend Version**: N/A or Unknown\n- **Minimal OS and/or Kernel Version**: N/A or Unknown\n- **Minimal Broker Client Version**: N/A or Unknown\n- **Minimal Result Backend Client Version**: N/A or Unknown\n\n### Python Packages\n<details>\n<summary><b><code>pip freeze</code> Output:</b></summary>\n<p>\n\n```\namqp==5.4.1\nbilliard==4.3.1\ncelery==5.7.0rc1\nclick==8.5.0\nclick-didyoumean==0.3.1\nclick-plugins==1.1.1.2\nclick-repl==0.4.1\nkombu==5.7.0rc1\npackaging==26.3\nprompt-toolkit==3.0.53\npython-dateutil==2.9.0.post0\nredis==8.1.0\nsix==1.17.0\nsqlalchemy==2.1.5\ntyping-extensions==4.16.0\ntzdata==2026.4\ntzlocal==5.4.4\nvine==5.1.0\nwcwidth==0.9.3\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n### Other Dependencies\n<details>\n<p>\nN/A. The scripts use SQLite, and you don't need a broker.\n</p>\n</details>\n\n## Minimally Reproducible Test Case\n\n<details>\n<summary><code>check.py</code> and <code>dropped.py</code></summary>\n<p>\n\n```python\n# check.py\nfrom celery import Celery\n\napp = Celery(backend=\"db+sqlite:///results.db\")\nprint(\"always_retry:\", app.backend.always_retry, \"max_retries:\", app.backend.max_retries)\n```\n\n```python\n# dropped.py\nfrom unittest.mock import patch\n\nfrom sqlalchemy.exc import OperationalError\n\nfrom celery import Celery, states, uuid\n\napp = Celery(backend=\"db+sqlite:///results.db\")\nbackend = app.backend\nreal_session = backend.ResultSession\ncalls = []\n\n\ndef session_that_drops_once(*args, **kwargs):\n    calls.append(1)\n    if len(calls) == 1:  # the database drops the first connection\n        raise OperationalError(\"SELECT 1\", {}, Exception(\"server closed the connection\"))\n    return real_session(*args, **kwargs)\n\n\ntask_id = uuid()\nwith patch.object(backend, \"ResultSession\", session_that_drops_once):\n    backend.store_result(task_id, 42, states.SUCCESS)\nprint(\"stored:\", backend.get_task_meta(task_id, cache=False)[\"status\"])\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n# Expected Behavior\n\nThe docs for `result_backend_always_retry` say that in 5.7 the database backend turns it on by default, to keep the retries it had before. So `check.py` should print:\n\n```\nalways_retry: True max_retries: 3\n```\n\nAnd `dropped.py` should print `stored: SUCCESS`, as every release from 4.4.7 to 5.6.3 does.\n\n# Actual Behavior\n\n```\nalways_retry: False max_retries: inf\n```\n\n`dropped.py` doesn't retry. It fails on the first dropped connection:\n\n```\nsqlalchemy.exc.OperationalError: (builtins.Exception) server closed the connection\n```\n\nPR #10130 removed the `@retry` decorator and set the new defaults with `conf.get('result_backend_always_retry', True)` and `conf.get('result_backend_max_retries', 3)`. Both settings always have a value in `celery/app/defaults.py` (`False` and `inf`), so `conf.get` never falls back to `True` and `3`. The tests in `t/unit/backends/test_database.py` all set the option, so they never see the defaults.\n\nSo 5.7 gives up on errors that 5.6 retried, and loses those results. In a fault test, Postgres went down every few seconds while 200 tasks ran. Main lost 87 results and 5.6.3 lost 58. With `result_backend_always_retry=True` and unlimited `max_retries`, main lost none.\n\nI have a patch that applies the 5.7 defaults only when the user hasn't set the option, with `Settings.value_set_for()`. I can open a PR.\n\n", "hints_text": "", "created_at": "2026-10-21T23:08:37Z"}
{"repo": "celery/celery", "pull_number": 10920, "instance_id": "celery__celery-10920", "issue_numbers": ["10912"], "base_commit": "fc8019a81fc6b62945aecabe6107444a46bb0c6b", "patch": "diff --git a/celery/backends/database/__init__.py b/celery/backends/database/__init__.py\nindex a740574c3fe..f4046a4fb9b 100644\n--- a/celery/backends/database/__init__.py\n+++ b/celery/backends/database/__init__.py\n@@ -19,6 +19,7 @@\n try:\n     from sqlalchemy.exc import DatabaseError, InterfaceError, InvalidRequestError\n     from sqlalchemy.orm import defer\n+    from sqlalchemy.orm.attributes import flag_modified\n     from sqlalchemy.orm.exc import StaleDataError\n except ImportError:\n     raise ImproperlyConfigured(\n@@ -142,10 +143,14 @@ def ResultSession(self, session_manager=None):\n             short_lived_sessions=self.short_lived_sessions,\n             **self.engine_options)\n \n-    def _query_task(self, session, task_id):\n+    def _query_task(self, session, task_id, for_update=False):\n         \"\"\"Query task by id, falling back to deferring missing columns from database.\"\"\"\n+        query = session.query(self.task_cls)\n+        if for_update:\n+            # Lock the row, and read it again even if the session has it.\n+            query = query.with_for_update().populate_existing()\n         try:\n-            tasks = list(session.query(self.task_cls).filter(self.task_cls.task_id == task_id))\n+            tasks = list(query.filter(self.task_cls.task_id == task_id))\n             return tasks and tasks[0]\n         except DatabaseError as exc:\n             exc_str = str(exc).lower()\n@@ -155,7 +160,7 @@ def _query_task(self, session, task_id):\n             if 'stamps' in exc_str and hasattr(self.task_cls, 'stamps'):\n                 defers.append(defer(self.task_cls.stamps))\n             if defers:\n-                tasks = list(session.query(self.task_cls).options(*defers).filter(\n+                tasks = list(query.options(*defers).filter(\n                     self.task_cls.task_id == task_id\n                 ))\n                 return tasks and tasks[0]\n@@ -167,13 +172,28 @@ def _store_result(self, task_id, result, state, traceback=None,\n         session = self.ResultSession()\n         with session_cleanup(session):\n             task = self._query_task(session, task_id)\n+            if task:\n+                # Lock the row and read it again, so a run that stores SUCCESS\n+                # at the same time can't be written over. Only an existing row\n+                # is locked: on MySQL, locking a missing row takes a gap lock,\n+                # which deadlocks concurrent inserts.\n+                task = self._query_task(session, task_id, for_update=True)\n             if not task:\n                 task = self.task_cls(task_id)\n                 task.task_id = task_id\n                 session.add(task)\n                 session.flush()\n+            elif task.status == states.SUCCESS:\n+                # Like BaseKeyValueStoreBackend, ignore any write after a\n+                # SUCCESS is saved, so a repeated run of the task doesn't\n+                # replace its result.\n+                return\n \n             self._update_result(task, result, state, traceback=traceback, request=request)\n+            # Write the state even if it looks unchanged. Without row locks, as\n+            # on SQLite, another run may have saved a SUCCESS after we read the\n+            # row, and that SUCCESS must not stay next to the result written here.\n+            flag_modified(task, 'status')\n             session.commit()\n \n     def _update_result(self, task, result, state, traceback=None,\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/t/unit/backends/test_database.py b/t/unit/backends/test_database.py\nindex 639ce575f3a..38ed8fe3390 100644\n--- a/t/unit/backends/test_database.py\n+++ b/t/unit/backends/test_database.py\n@@ -817,6 +817,68 @@ def test_mark_as_failure(self):\n             assert isinstance(tb.get_result(tid3), KeyError)\n             assert tb.get_traceback(tid3) == trace\n \n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize('state, result', [\n+        (states.STARTED, {'pid': 1}),\n+        (states.FAILURE, KeyError('foo')),\n+        (states.SUCCESS, 43),\n+    ])\n+    def test_store_result_after_success_is_ignored(self, state, result):\n+        # e.g. the task runs again after its worker was lost before the ack\n+        tb = DatabaseBackend(self.uri, app=self.app)\n+        tid = uuid()\n+        tb.mark_as_done(tid, 42)\n+        tb.store_result(tid, result, state)\n+        meta = tb.get_task_meta(tid, cache=False)\n+        assert meta['status'] == states.SUCCESS\n+        assert meta['result'] == 42\n+\n+    def test_store_result_before_success_is_saved(self):\n+        tb = DatabaseBackend(self.uri, app=self.app)\n+        tid = uuid()\n+        tb.mark_as_started(tid)\n+        tb.mark_as_retry(tid, KeyError('foo'))\n+        tb.mark_as_failure(tid, KeyError('foo'))\n+        assert tb.get_task_meta(tid, cache=False)['status'] == states.FAILURE\n+        tb.mark_as_done(tid, 42)\n+        meta = tb.get_task_meta(tid, cache=False)\n+        assert meta['status'] == states.SUCCESS\n+        assert meta['result'] == 42\n+\n+    def start_while_other_run_succeeds(self, tb, tid, on_locked_read):\n+        # A second run starts just as the first one saves its SUCCESS\n+        query_task = tb._query_task\n+\n+        def query_task_then_other_run_succeeds(session, task_id, for_update=False):\n+            task = query_task(session, task_id, for_update=for_update)\n+            if for_update == on_locked_read:\n+                tb._query_task = query_task\n+                tb.mark_as_done(task_id, 42)\n+            return task\n+\n+        tb._query_task = query_task_then_other_run_succeeds\n+        tb.mark_as_started(tid, pid=2)\n+\n+    def test_store_result_racing_a_success_keeps_it(self):\n+        tb = DatabaseBackend(self.uri, app=self.app)\n+        tid = uuid()\n+        tb.mark_as_started(tid, pid=1)\n+        # The SUCCESS is saved before the second run locks the row\n+        self.start_while_other_run_succeeds(tb, tid, on_locked_read=False)\n+        meta = tb.get_task_meta(tid, cache=False)\n+        assert (meta['status'], meta['result']) == (states.SUCCESS, 42)\n+\n+    def test_store_result_racing_a_success_without_row_locks(self):\n+        tb = DatabaseBackend(self.uri, app=self.app)\n+        tid = uuid()\n+        tb.mark_as_started(tid, pid=1)\n+        # SQLite has no row locks, so the SUCCESS can be saved even after the\n+        # second run read the row. The row then has that run's state.\n+        self.start_while_other_run_succeeds(tb, tid, on_locked_read=True)\n+        meta = tb.get_task_meta(tid, cache=False)\n+        assert (meta['status'], meta['result']) == (states.STARTED, {'pid': 2})\n+        tb.mark_as_done(tid, 43)\n+        assert tb.get_task_meta(tid, cache=False)['result'] == 43\n+\n     def test_forget(self):\n         tb = DatabaseBackend(self.uri, backend='memory://', app=self.app)\n         tid = uuid()\n@@ -1006,6 +1068,19 @@ def test_store_none_result(self, result_serializer, args, kwargs):\n         assert meta['retries'] == 2\n         assert meta['worker'] == \"celery@worker_1\"\n \n+    def test_store_result_after_success_keeps_extended_fields(self):\n+        tb = DatabaseBackend(self.uri, app=self.app)\n+        tid = uuid()\n+        request = Context(args=(1, 2), kwargs={'foo': 'bar'}, task='mytask',\n+                          retries=0, hostname='celery@worker_1',\n+                          delivery_info={'routing_key': 'celery'})\n+        tb.store_result(tid, {'fizz': 'buzz'}, states.SUCCESS, request=request)\n+        meta = tb.get_task_meta(tid, cache=False)\n+        # The task runs again on another worker\n+        request.update(hostname='celery@worker_2', retries=1)\n+        tb.store_result(tid, {'pid': 2}, states.STARTED, request=request)\n+        assert tb.get_task_meta(tid, cache=False) == meta\n+\n     @pytest.mark.parametrize(\n         'result_serializer',\n         ['pickle', 'json'],\n", "problem_statement": "Database backend lets a second run of a task overwrite its stored SUCCESS\n# Checklist\n- [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `main` branch of Celery.\n- [ ] This has already been asked to the [discussions forum](https://github.com/celery/celery/discussions) first.\n- [x] I have read the relevant section in the\n  [contribution guide](https://docs.celeryq.dev/en/main/contributing.html#other-bugs)\n  on reporting bugs.\n- [x] I have checked the [issues list](https://github.com/celery/celery/issues?q=is%3Aissue+label%3A%22Issue+Type%3A+Bug+Report%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)\n  for similar or identical bug reports.\n- [x] I have checked the [pull requests list](https://github.com/celery/celery/pulls?q=is%3Apr+label%3A%22PR+Type%3A+Bugfix%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)\n  for existing proposed fixes.\n- [x] I have checked the [commit log](https://github.com/celery/celery/commits/main)\n  to find out if the bug was already fixed in the main branch.\n- [x] I have included all related issues and possible duplicate issues\n  in this issue (If there are none, check this box anyway).\n- [ ] I have tried to reproduce the issue with [pytest-celery](https://docs.celeryq.dev/projects/pytest-celery/en/latest/userguide/celery-bug-report.html) and added the reproduction script below.\n\n## Mandatory Debugging Information\n\n- [x] I have included the output of ``celery -A proj report`` in the issue.\n    (if you are not able to do this, then at least specify the Celery\n     version affected).\n- [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `main` branch of Celery.\n- [x] I have included the contents of ``pip freeze`` in the issue.\n- [x] I have included all the versions of all the external dependencies required\n  to reproduce this bug.\n\n## Optional Debugging Information\n- [x] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one Python version\n  and/or implementation.\n- [x] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one message broker and/or\n  result backend.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one version of the message\n  broker and/or result backend.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one operating system.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one workers pool.\n- [x] I have tried reproducing the issue with autoscaling, retries,\n  ETA/Countdown & rate limits disabled.\n- [x] I have tried reproducing the issue after downgrading\n  and/or upgrading Celery and its dependencies.\n\n## Related Issues and Possible Duplicates\n\n#### Related Issues\n\n- #10724 shows FAILURE replacing SUCCESS on this backend by another path. #10752 (open) fixes that path, but not a second run.\n\n#### Possible Duplicates\n\n- None\n\n## Environment & Settings\n**Celery version**: 5.7.0rc1 (collider). It also happens on main (`d6d754e`), 5.6.3, and every older release I tried, back to 4.4.7.\n\n<details>\n<summary><b><code>celery report</code> Output:</b></summary>\n<p>\n\n```\nsoftware -> celery:5.7.0rc1 (collider) kombu:5.7.0rc1 py:3.13.16\n            billiard:4.3.1 redis:8.1.0\nplatform -> system:Linux arch:64bit, ELF\n            kernel version:6.18.44-fc-v139 imp:CPython\nloader   -> celery.loaders.app.AppLoader\nsettings -> transport:redis results:db+sqlite:///results.db\n\nbroker_url: 'redis://:********@localhost:6379/0'\nresult_backend: 'db+sqlite:///results.db'\ndeprecated_settings: None\ntask_acks_late: True\ntask_track_started: True\nbroker_transport_options: \n 'visibility_timeout': 10}\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n# Steps to Reproduce\n\n## Required Dependencies\n- **Minimal Python Version**: N/A or Unknown\n- **Minimal Celery Version**: 4.4.7 or older (the oldest I tried)\n- **Minimal Kombu Version**: N/A or Unknown\n- **Minimal Broker Version**: N/A or Unknown\n- **Minimal Result Backend Version**: N/A or Unknown (SQLite here, Postgres 17 and MySQL 8.4 in fault tests)\n- **Minimal OS and/or Kernel Version**: N/A or Unknown\n- **Minimal Broker Client Version**: N/A or Unknown\n- **Minimal Result Backend Client Version**: N/A or Unknown\n\n### Python Packages\n<details>\n<summary><b><code>pip freeze</code> Output:</b></summary>\n<p>\n\n```\namqp==5.4.1\nbilliard==4.3.1\ncelery==5.7.0rc1\nclick==8.5.0\nclick-didyoumean==0.3.1\nclick-plugins==1.1.1.2\nclick-repl==0.4.1\nkombu==5.7.0rc1\npackaging==26.3\nprompt-toolkit==3.0.53\npython-dateutil==2.9.0.post0\nredis==8.1.0\nsix==1.17.0\nsqlalchemy==2.1.5\ntyping-extensions==4.16.0\ntzdata==2026.4\ntzlocal==5.4.4\nvine==5.1.0\nwcwidth==0.9.3\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n### Other Dependencies\n<details>\n<p>\nRedis 7.0.15 on localhost:6379 as the broker. Results go to SQLite.\n</p>\n</details>\n\n## Minimally Reproducible Test Case\n\n<details>\n<summary><code>tasks.py</code>, <code>run.py</code>, and the commands to run them</summary>\n<p>\n\n```python\n# tasks.py\nimport os\nimport random\nimport signal\nimport time\n\nfrom celery import Celery\nfrom celery.signals import task_success\n\napp = Celery(\"tasks\", broker=\"redis://localhost:6379/0\", backend=\"db+sqlite:///results.db\")\napp.conf.update(\n    task_acks_late=True,\n    task_track_started=True,\n    broker_transport_options={\"visibility_timeout\": 10},  # redeliver after 10 s, not 1 hour\n)\n\n\n@app.task\ndef report():\n    time.sleep(1)\n    return f\"report-{random.randint(1000, 9999)}\"\n\n\n@task_success.connect\ndef kill_worker_once(sender=None, **kwargs):\n    # The task saved SUCCESS but hasn't acked yet. Kill the worker here, once.\n    if not os.path.exists(\"killed\"):\n        open(\"killed\", \"w\").close()\n        os.killpg(os.getpgid(os.getppid()), signal.SIGKILL)\n```\n\n```python\n# run.py\nimport time\n\nfrom tasks import app, report\n\ntask_id = report.delay().id\nseen = []\nfor _ in range(60):\n    result = app.AsyncResult(task_id)  # a new object each time, so nothing is cached\n    state = result.state\n    if state == \"SUCCESS\":\n        state += f\" {result.result}\"\n    if not seen or seen[-1] != state:\n        seen.append(state)\n    time.sleep(0.5)\nprint(\" -> \".join(seen))\n```\n\n```\ncelery -A tasks worker -c 3    # terminal 1\npython run.py                  # terminal 2\n# The worker dies about a second later. Wait 15 seconds, then start it again:\ncelery -A tasks worker -c 3    # terminal 1\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n# Expected Behavior\n\nOnce the task has a SUCCESS, readers keep seeing that result:\n\n```\nPENDING -> STARTED -> SUCCESS report-9160\n```\n\nThe same script prints this with `backend=\"redis://localhost:6379/1\"`. The key-value backends ignore any update once a task has a SUCCESS.\n\n# Actual Behavior\n\n```\nPENDING -> STARTED -> SUCCESS report-8671 -> STARTED -> SUCCESS report-5803\n```\n\nAfter the restart, the task runs again. The database backend has no SUCCESS check. The second run writes STARTED over the stored SUCCESS, and then a new result. Any crash between saving a result and the ack can do this under `acks_late`. In a fault test on Postgres with crashes at that point, 7 of 150 results changed. `ready()` also goes from `True` back to `False`, and `GroupResult` and `chord_unlock` rely on it.\n\nI have a patch that keeps the first SUCCESS and locks the row with `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE`. I can open a PR.\n", "hints_text": "", "created_at": "2026-10-21T23:08:04Z"}
{"repo": "celery/celery", "pull_number": 10919, "instance_id": "celery__celery-10919", "issue_numbers": ["10910"], "base_commit": "37624e97dbca9ab6e1c483edd5c902e9403ccc6a", "patch": "diff --git a/Changelog.rst b/Changelog.rst\nindex 32310b60ca0..5750391ff88 100644\n--- a/Changelog.rst\n+++ b/Changelog.rst\n@@ -9,6 +9,19 @@ in the main branch & 5.6.x series, please see :ref:`whatsnew-5.6` for\n an overview of what's new in Celery 5.6.\n \n \n+Unreleased\n+==========\n+\n+- The Redis result backend now counts each chord header task only once,\n+  even when the task runs again, as it can after a lost worker with\n+  ``acks_late``. Header results are now saved with a Lua script. If your\n+  Redis user has an ACL that lists the commands it can run, add\n+  ``EVALSHA``, ``SCRIPT LOAD``, ``SISMEMBER`` and ``SADD``. Without them,\n+  header tasks fail when they store their results, and neither the chord\n+  body nor its error callbacks run. A Redis-compatible service or proxy has\n+  to support ``EVALSHA`` and ``SCRIPT LOAD`` too (#10919).\n+\n+\n .. _version-5.7.0rc1:\n \n 5.7.0rc1\ndiff --git a/celery/backends/redis.py b/celery/backends/redis.py\nindex db529b60b96..d9904ba3648 100644\n--- a/celery/backends/redis.py\n+++ b/celery/backends/redis.py\n@@ -81,6 +81,43 @@\n \n E_LOST = 'Connection to Redis lost: Retry (%s/%s) %s.'\n \n+# Records the result of a chord header task, unless a result for the same\n+# task was already recorded: a header task can run more than once (e.g.\n+# redelivered after a lost worker when using acks_late), and must only count\n+# once, even if the runs returned different results. Returns the number of\n+# results recorded so far, and the chord's total diff and size.\n+CHORD_PART_RETURN_LUA = \"\"\"\n+local jkey, tkey, skey, dkey, gkey = KEYS[1], KEYS[2], KEYS[3], KEYS[4], KEYS[5]\n+local tid, value, ordered, score = ARGV[1], ARGV[2], ARGV[3], ARGV[4]\n+local expires, group_expires = tonumber(ARGV[5]), tonumber(ARGV[6])\n+-- Key the check on the task id and header index, so it doesn't merge two\n+-- header entries that share the task id of a reused frozen signature.\n+local member = tid .. ':' .. score\n+if redis.call('SISMEMBER', dkey, member) == 0 then\n+    if ordered == '1' then\n+        redis.call('ZADD', jkey, score, value)\n+    else\n+        redis.call('RPUSH', jkey, value)\n+    end\n+    redis.call('SADD', dkey, member)\n+end\n+local readycount\n+if ordered == '1' then\n+    readycount = redis.call('ZCOUNT', jkey, '-inf', '+inf')\n+else\n+    readycount = redis.call('LLEN', jkey)\n+end\n+if expires > 0 then\n+    for _, key in ipairs({jkey, tkey, skey, dkey}) do\n+        redis.call('EXPIRE', key, expires)\n+    end\n+    if group_expires > 0 then\n+        redis.call('EXPIRE', gkey, group_expires)\n+    end\n+end\n+return {readycount, redis.call('GET', tkey), redis.call('GET', skey)}\n+\"\"\"\n+\n logger = get_logger(__name__)\n \n _NO_SOCKET = object()\n@@ -784,6 +821,10 @@ def _chord_zset(self):\n     def _transport_options(self):\n         return self.app.conf.get('result_backend_transport_options', {})\n \n+    @cached_property\n+    def _chord_part_return_script(self):\n+        return self.client.register_script(CHORD_PART_RETURN_LUA)\n+\n     def on_chord_part_return(self, request, state, result,\n                              propagate=None, **kwargs):\n         app = self.app\n@@ -797,27 +838,15 @@ def on_chord_part_return(self, request, state, result,\n         jkey = self.get_key_for_group(gid, '.j')\n         tkey = self.get_key_for_group(gid, '.t')\n         skey = self.get_key_for_group(gid, '.s')\n+        dkey = self.get_key_for_group(gid, '.d')\n         result = self.encode_result(result, state)\n         encoded = self.encode([1, tid, state, result])\n-        with client.pipeline() as pipe:\n-            pipeline = (\n-                pipe.zadd(jkey, {encoded: group_index}).zcount(jkey, \"-inf\", \"+inf\")\n-                if self._chord_zset\n-                else pipe.rpush(jkey, encoded).llen(jkey)\n-            ).get(tkey).get(skey)\n-            expires = self.chord_expires\n-            if expires:\n-                pipeline = pipeline \\\n-                    .expire(jkey, expires) \\\n-                    .expire(tkey, expires) \\\n-                    .expire(skey, expires)\n-                if self.expires:\n-                    # The group key can also hold a GroupResult the user\n-                    # saved, so never shorten it below result_expires.\n-                    pipeline = pipeline.expire(\n-                        self.get_key_for_group(gid), max(expires, self.expires))\n-\n-            _, readycount, totaldiff, chord_size_bytes = pipeline.execute()[:4]\n+        readycount, totaldiff, chord_size_bytes = self._chord_part_return_script(\n+            keys=[jkey, tkey, skey, dkey, self.get_key_for_group(gid)],\n+            args=[tid, encoded, 1 if self._chord_zset else 0, group_index,\n+                  self.chord_expires or 0, self._chord_result_expires],\n+            client=client,\n+        )\n \n         totaldiff = int(totaldiff or 0)\n \n@@ -874,16 +903,26 @@ def on_chord_part_return(self, request, state, result,\n                                 .delete(jkey) \\\n                                 .delete(tkey) \\\n                                 .delete(skey) \\\n+                                .delete(dkey) \\\n                                 .execute()\n             except ChordError as exc:\n                 logger.exception('Chord %r raised: %r', request.group, exc)\n-                return self.chord_error_from_stack(callback, exc)\n+                try:\n+                    return self.chord_error_from_stack(callback, exc)\n+                finally:\n+                    # Drop the chord size, so a copy of a header task that\n+                    # arrives later doesn't fail the chord again. The saved\n+                    # results stay, as before.\n+                    client.delete(skey)\n             except Exception as exc:  # pylint: disable=broad-except\n                 logger.exception('Chord %r raised: %r', request.group, exc)\n-                return self.chord_error_from_stack(\n-                    callback,\n-                    ChordError(f'Join error: {exc!r}'),\n-                )\n+                try:\n+                    return self.chord_error_from_stack(\n+                        callback,\n+                        ChordError(f'Join error: {exc!r}'),\n+                    )\n+                finally:\n+                    client.delete(skey)\n \n     def _create_client(self, **params):\n         return self._get_client()(\ndiff --git a/docs/getting-started/backends-and-brokers/redis.rst b/docs/getting-started/backends-and-brokers/redis.rst\nindex faf549159ba..51b125e1833 100644\n--- a/docs/getting-started/backends-and-brokers/redis.rst\n+++ b/docs/getting-started/backends-and-brokers/redis.rst\n@@ -101,6 +101,14 @@ you should configure these settings::\n For a complete list of options supported by the Redis result backend, see\n :ref:`conf-redis-result-backend`.\n \n+.. versionchanged:: 5.7\n+\n+    A chord now counts each header task only once, even when the task runs\n+    again. It saves header results with a Lua script. If you limit the\n+    commands the Redis user can run, also allow ``EVALSHA``,\n+    ``SCRIPT LOAD``, ``SISMEMBER`` and ``SADD``. A Redis-compatible service\n+    or proxy has to support ``EVALSHA`` and ``SCRIPT LOAD`` too.\n+\n If you are using Sentinel, you should specify the master_name using the :setting:`result_backend_transport_options` setting:\n \n .. code-block:: python\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/requirements/test-ci-default.txt b/requirements/test-ci-default.txt\nindex 3ce07587253..e93a5ca535c 100644\n--- a/requirements/test-ci-default.txt\n+++ b/requirements/test-ci-default.txt\n@@ -17,6 +17,9 @@\n # platforms without wheels: no PyPy builds, no CPython 3.15 wheel, and the\n # Windows job only runs 3.14 which has no wheel either.\n couchbase>=3.0.0; platform_python_implementation != 'PyPy' and platform_system != 'Windows' and python_version < '3.15'\n+# The Redis chord tests run the backend's Lua script with fakeredis, which\n+# needs lupa to run Lua. lupa has no wheel for CPython 3.15 yet.\n+fakeredis[lua]>=2.38.0; python_version < '3.15'\n -r extras/arangodb.txt\n -r extras/consul.txt\n -r extras/cosmosdbsql.txt\ndiff --git a/t/integration/test_redis_backend.py b/t/integration/test_redis_backend.py\nindex 813c9de1ece..92f9395a367 100644\n--- a/t/integration/test_redis_backend.py\n+++ b/t/integration/test_redis_backend.py\n@@ -4,6 +4,7 @@\n import pytest\n \n from celery import states, uuid\n+from celery.app.task import Context\n from celery.result import AsyncResult, GroupResult\n from t.integration.conftest import get_active_redis_channels\n from t.integration.tasks import identity\n@@ -237,7 +238,7 @@ def test_chord_expires(app):\n     header = GroupResult(group_id, [GroupResult(uuid(), [AsyncResult(uuid())])])\n     request = SimpleNamespace(id=uuid(), group=group_id, group_index=0, chord=None)\n     keys = [backend.get_key_for_group(group_id, suffix)\n-            for suffix in ('', '.j', '.s')]\n+            for suffix in ('', '.j', '.s', '.d')]\n     try:\n         backend.apply_chord((group_id, header.results), None)\n         backend.set_chord_size(group_id, 2)\n@@ -249,3 +250,57 @@ def test_chord_expires(app):\n         assert GroupResult.restore(group_id, backend=backend) == header\n     finally:\n         backend.client.delete(*keys)\n+\n+\n+@pytest.mark.parametrize('ordered', (True, False))\n+def test_chord_part_return_records_each_header_task_once(\n+    app, celery_session_worker, ordered,\n+):\n+    # A chord header task that is executed twice (e.g. redelivered after its\n+    # worker was lost with acks_late) must only be recorded once, even when\n+    # it returned a different value: the chord body must neither run before\n+    # the other header tasks finished nor receive a result twice.\n+    url = app.conf.result_backend\n+    if not url.startswith('redis'):\n+        pytest.skip('Requires redis result backend.')\n+\n+    app.conf.result_backend_transport_options = dict(\n+        result_chord_ordered=ordered,\n+    )\n+    backend = app.backend\n+    client = backend.client\n+    gid = uuid()\n+    body = identity.s()\n+    body_id = body.freeze().id\n+    keys = [backend.get_key_for_group(gid, suffix)\n+            for suffix in ('.j', '.t', '.s', '.d')]\n+    jkey = keys[0]\n+    count = client.zcard if ordered else client.llen\n+    header_ids = [uuid() for _ in range(3)]\n+\n+    def part_return(index, result):\n+        request = Context(id=header_ids[index], group=gid,\n+                          group_index=index, chord=body)\n+        backend.on_chord_part_return(request, states.SUCCESS, result)\n+\n+    backend.set_chord_size(gid, 3)\n+    try:\n+        part_return(0, 1)\n+        part_return(0, 42)\n+        assert count(jkey) == 1\n+\n+        # A worker running an older version records its part without the\n+        # dedupe set, which must not stall the chord.\n+        encoded = backend.encode([1, header_ids[1], states.SUCCESS, 2])\n+        if ordered:\n+            client.zadd(jkey, {encoded: 1})\n+        else:\n+            client.rpush(jkey, encoded)\n+        assert count(jkey) == 2\n+\n+        part_return(2, 3)\n+        assert app.AsyncResult(body_id).get(timeout=10) == [1, 2, 3]\n+        assert client.exists(*keys) == 0\n+    finally:\n+        client.delete(*keys)\n+        backend.forget(body_id)\ndiff --git a/t/unit/backends/test_redis.py b/t/unit/backends/test_redis.py\nindex 83bdef83d28..c7648b458e6 100644\n--- a/t/unit/backends/test_redis.py\n+++ b/t/unit/backends/test_redis.py\n@@ -24,22 +24,12 @@\n from celery.backends.base import COMPRESSED_PAYLOAD_MAGIC\n from celery.canvas import Signature\n from celery.contrib.testing.mocks import ContextMock\n-from celery.exceptions import BackendStoreError, ChordError, ImproperlyConfigured\n+from celery.exceptions import BackendStoreError, ChordError, ImproperlyConfigured, WorkerLostError\n from celery.result import AsyncResult, GroupResult\n from celery.utils.collections import AttributeDict\n from t.unit import conftest\n \n \n-def raise_on_second_call(mock, exc, *retval):\n-    def on_first_call(*args, **kwargs):\n-        mock.side_effect = exc\n-        return mock.return_value\n-\n-    mock.side_effect = on_first_call\n-    if retval:\n-        mock.return_value, = retval\n-\n-\n class ConnectionError(Exception):\n     pass\n \n@@ -171,6 +161,38 @@ def zrangebyscore(self, key, min_, max_):\n     def zcount(self, key, min_, max_):\n         return len(self.zrangebyscore(key, min_, max_))\n \n+    def sadd(self, key, value):\n+        members = self.keyspace.setdefault(key, set())\n+        if value in members:\n+            return 0\n+        members.add(value)\n+        return 1\n+\n+    def register_script(self, script):\n+        # Only the chord part script of the backend is used; emulate it\n+        # with the fake commands above.\n+        def chord_part_return(keys, args, client=None):\n+            jkey, tkey, skey, dkey, gkey = keys\n+            tid, value, ordered, score, expires, group_expires = args\n+            member = f'{tid}:{score}'\n+            if member not in self.keyspace.get(dkey, set()):\n+                if ordered:\n+                    self.zadd(jkey, {value: score})\n+                else:\n+                    self.rpush(jkey, value)\n+                self.sadd(dkey, member)\n+            if ordered:\n+                readycount = self.zcount(jkey, '-inf', '+inf')\n+            else:\n+                readycount = self.llen(jkey)\n+            if expires:\n+                for key in (jkey, tkey, skey, dkey):\n+                    self.expire(key, expires)\n+                if group_expires:\n+                    self.expire(gkey, group_expires)\n+            return [readycount, self.get(tkey), self.get(skey)]\n+        return chord_part_return\n+\n \n class Sentinel(conftest.MockCallbacks):\n     def __init__(self, sentinels, min_other_sentinels=0, sentinel_kwargs=None,\n@@ -2181,9 +2203,10 @@ def test_on_chord_part_return(self):\n         jkey = self.b.get_key_for_group('group_id', '.j')\n         tkey = self.b.get_key_for_group('group_id', '.t')\n         skey = self.b.get_key_for_group('group_id', '.s')\n-        self.b.client.delete.assert_has_calls([call(jkey), call(tkey), call(skey)])\n+        dkey = self.b.get_key_for_group('group_id', '.d')\n+        self.b.client.delete.assert_has_calls([call(jkey), call(tkey), call(skey), call(dkey)])\n         self.b.client.expire.assert_has_calls([\n-            call(jkey, 86400), call(tkey, 86400), call(skey, 86400),\n+            call(jkey, 86400), call(tkey, 86400), call(skey, 86400), call(dkey, 86400),\n         ])\n \n     @pytest.mark.parametrize('result_expires,group_expires', [\n@@ -2202,8 +2225,9 @@ def test_on_chord_part_return_uses_chord_expires(\n         jkey = self.b.get_key_for_group('group_id', '.j')\n         tkey = self.b.get_key_for_group('group_id', '.t')\n         skey = self.b.get_key_for_group('group_id', '.s')\n+        dkey = self.b.get_key_for_group('group_id', '.d')\n         gkey = self.b.get_key_for_group('group_id')\n-        expected = [call(jkey, 600), call(tkey, 600), call(skey, 600)]\n+        expected = [call(jkey, 600), call(tkey, 600), call(skey, 600), call(dkey, 600)]\n         if group_expires:\n             expected.append(call(gkey, group_expires))\n         assert self.b.client.expire.call_args_list == expected\n@@ -2331,10 +2355,10 @@ def test_on_chord_part_return_no_expiry__ordered(self):\n         self.b.expires = old_expires\n \n     def test_on_chord_part_return__success(self):\n-        with self.chord_context(2) as (_, request, callback):\n-            self.b.on_chord_part_return(request, states.SUCCESS, 10)\n+        with self.chord_context(2) as (tasks, _, callback):\n+            self.b.on_chord_part_return(tasks[0].request, states.SUCCESS, 10)\n             callback.delay.assert_not_called()\n-            self.b.on_chord_part_return(request, states.SUCCESS, 20)\n+            self.b.on_chord_part_return(tasks[1].request, states.SUCCESS, 20)\n             callback.delay.assert_called_with([10, 20])\n \n     def test_on_chord_part_return__success__unordered(self):\n@@ -2342,10 +2366,10 @@ def test_on_chord_part_return__success__unordered(self):\n             result_chord_ordered=False,\n         )\n \n-        with self.chord_context(2) as (_, request, callback):\n-            self.b.on_chord_part_return(request, states.SUCCESS, 10)\n+        with self.chord_context(2) as (tasks, _, callback):\n+            self.b.on_chord_part_return(tasks[0].request, states.SUCCESS, 10)\n             callback.delay.assert_not_called()\n-            self.b.on_chord_part_return(request, states.SUCCESS, 20)\n+            self.b.on_chord_part_return(tasks[1].request, states.SUCCESS, 20)\n             callback.delay.assert_called_with([10, 20])\n \n     def test_on_chord_part_return__success__ordered(self):\n@@ -2353,10 +2377,10 @@ def test_on_chord_part_return__success__ordered(self):\n             result_chord_ordered=True,\n         )\n \n-        with self.chord_context(2) as (_, request, callback):\n-            self.b.on_chord_part_return(request, states.SUCCESS, 10)\n+        with self.chord_context(2) as (tasks, _, callback):\n+            self.b.on_chord_part_return(tasks[0].request, states.SUCCESS, 10)\n             callback.delay.assert_not_called()\n-            self.b.on_chord_part_return(request, states.SUCCESS, 20)\n+            self.b.on_chord_part_return(tasks[1].request, states.SUCCESS, 20)\n             callback.delay.assert_called_with([10, 20])\n \n     def test_on_chord_part_return__callback_raises(self):\n@@ -2397,9 +2421,7 @@ def test_on_chord_part_return__callback_raises__ordered(self):\n     def test_on_chord_part_return__ChordError(self):\n         with self.chord_context(1) as (_, request, callback):\n             self.b.client.pipeline = ContextMock()\n-            raise_on_second_call(self.b.client.pipeline, ChordError())\n-            self.b.client.pipeline.return_value.zadd().zcount().get().get().expire(\n-            ).expire().expire().expire().execute.return_value = (1, 1, 0, b'1', 4, 5, 6)\n+            self.b.client.pipeline.side_effect = ChordError()\n             task = self.app._tasks['add'] = Mock(name='add_task')\n             self.b.on_chord_part_return(request, states.SUCCESS, 10)\n             task.backend.fail_from_current_stack.assert_called_with(\n@@ -2413,9 +2435,7 @@ def test_on_chord_part_return__ChordError__unordered(self):\n \n         with self.chord_context(1) as (_, request, callback):\n             self.b.client.pipeline = ContextMock()\n-            raise_on_second_call(self.b.client.pipeline, ChordError())\n-            self.b.client.pipeline.return_value.rpush().llen().get().get().expire(\n-            ).expire().expire().expire().execute.return_value = (1, 1, 0, b'1', 4, 5, 6)\n+            self.b.client.pipeline.side_effect = ChordError()\n             task = self.app._tasks['add'] = Mock(name='add_task')\n             self.b.on_chord_part_return(request, states.SUCCESS, 10)\n             task.backend.fail_from_current_stack.assert_called_with(\n@@ -2429,9 +2449,7 @@ def test_on_chord_part_return__ChordError__ordered(self):\n \n         with self.chord_context(1) as (_, request, callback):\n             self.b.client.pipeline = ContextMock()\n-            raise_on_second_call(self.b.client.pipeline, ChordError())\n-            self.b.client.pipeline.return_value.zadd().zcount().get().get().expire(\n-            ).expire().expire().expire().execute.return_value = (1, 1, 0, b'1', 4, 5, 6)\n+            self.b.client.pipeline.side_effect = ChordError()\n             task = self.app._tasks['add'] = Mock(name='add_task')\n             self.b.on_chord_part_return(request, states.SUCCESS, 10)\n             task.backend.fail_from_current_stack.assert_called_with(\n@@ -2441,9 +2459,7 @@ def test_on_chord_part_return__ChordError__ordered(self):\n     def test_on_chord_part_return__other_error(self):\n         with self.chord_context(1) as (_, request, callback):\n             self.b.client.pipeline = ContextMock()\n-            raise_on_second_call(self.b.client.pipeline, RuntimeError())\n-            self.b.client.pipeline.return_value.zadd().zcount().get().get().expire(\n-            ).expire().expire().expire().execute.return_value = (1, 1, 0, b'1', 4, 5, 6)\n+            self.b.client.pipeline.side_effect = RuntimeError()\n             task = self.app._tasks['add'] = Mock(name='add_task')\n             self.b.on_chord_part_return(request, states.SUCCESS, 10)\n             task.backend.fail_from_current_stack.assert_called_with(\n@@ -2457,9 +2473,7 @@ def test_on_chord_part_return__other_error__unordered(self):\n \n         with self.chord_context(1) as (_, request, callback):\n             self.b.client.pipeline = ContextMock()\n-            raise_on_second_call(self.b.client.pipeline, RuntimeError())\n-            self.b.client.pipeline.return_value.rpush().llen().get().get().expire(\n-            ).expire().expire().expire().execute.return_value = (1, 1, 0, b'1', 4, 5, 6)\n+            self.b.client.pipeline.side_effect = RuntimeError()\n             task = self.app._tasks['add'] = Mock(name='add_task')\n             self.b.on_chord_part_return(request, states.SUCCESS, 10)\n             task.backend.fail_from_current_stack.assert_called_with(\n@@ -2473,15 +2487,179 @@ def test_on_chord_part_return__other_error__ordered(self):\n \n         with self.chord_context(1) as (_, request, callback):\n             self.b.client.pipeline = ContextMock()\n-            raise_on_second_call(self.b.client.pipeline, RuntimeError())\n-            self.b.client.pipeline.return_value.zadd().zcount().get().get().expire(\n-            ).expire().expire().expire().execute.return_value = (1, 1, 0, b'1', 4, 5, 6)\n+            self.b.client.pipeline.side_effect = RuntimeError()\n             task = self.app._tasks['add'] = Mock(name='add_task')\n             self.b.on_chord_part_return(request, states.SUCCESS, 10)\n             task.backend.fail_from_current_stack.assert_called_with(\n                 callback.id, exc=ANY,\n             )\n \n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize('ordered', (True, False))\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize(['dup_state', 'dup_result'], [\n+        # The header task was executed again and returned another value\n+        (states.SUCCESS, 99),\n+        # An \"already done\" idempotency guard returned early\n+        (states.SUCCESS, None),\n+        # The worker was lost after the part was recorded (#10724)\n+        (states.FAILURE, WorkerLostError()),\n+    ])\n+    def test_on_chord_part_return__duplicate_part(\n+        self, ordered, dup_state, dup_result,\n+    ):\n+        self.app.conf.result_backend_transport_options = dict(\n+            result_chord_ordered=ordered,\n+        )\n+\n+        with self.chord_context(3) as (tasks, _, callback):\n+            jkey = self.b.get_key_for_group(tasks[0].request.group, '.j')\n+            self.b.on_chord_part_return(tasks[0].request, states.SUCCESS, 10)\n+            self.b.on_chord_part_return(tasks[0].request, dup_state, dup_result)\n+            assert len(self.b.client.keyspace[jkey]) == 1\n+            self.b.on_chord_part_return(tasks[1].request, states.SUCCESS, 20)\n+            callback.delay.assert_not_called()\n+            self.b.on_chord_part_return(tasks[2].request, states.SUCCESS, 30)\n+            callback.delay.assert_called_once_with([10, 20, 30])\n+            # A duplicate arriving after the chord completed is ignored\n+            self.b.on_chord_part_return(tasks[2].request, states.SUCCESS, 99)\n+            callback.delay.assert_called_once_with([10, 20, 30])\n+\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize('join_error', (False, True))\n+    def test_on_chord_part_return__duplicate_after_chord_failed(self, join_error):\n+        if join_error:\n+            # Reading the results fails with an error other than ChordError\n+            self.b._unpack_chord_result = Mock(side_effect=RuntimeError())\n+        with self.chord_context(2) as (tasks, _, callback):\n+            jkey = self.b.get_key_for_group(tasks[0].request.group, '.j')\n+            with patch.object(self.b, 'chord_error_from_stack') as chord_error:\n+                self.b.on_chord_part_return(tasks[0].request, states.FAILURE, KeyError('x'))\n+                self.b.on_chord_part_return(tasks[1].request, states.SUCCESS, 20)\n+                assert chord_error.call_count == 1\n+                # The results are kept, so the user can look at them\n+                assert len(self.b.client.keyspace[jkey]) == 2\n+                # A copy of a header part arrives after the chord failed\n+                self.b.on_chord_part_return(tasks[0].request, states.SUCCESS, 10)\n+                assert chord_error.call_count == 1\n+                callback.delay.assert_not_called()\n+\n+    def test_on_chord_part_return__last_part_runs_again(self):\n+        with self.chord_context(2) as (tasks, _, callback):\n+            self.b.on_chord_part_return(tasks[0].request, states.SUCCESS, 10)\n+            # The last part is saved, but its worker is lost before the body\n+            # is sent, so the redelivered part must still send it.\n+            request = tasks[1].request\n+            keys = [self.b.get_key_for_group(request.group, suffix)\n+                    for suffix in ('.j', '.t', '.s', '.d', '')]\n+            encoded = self.b.encode([1, request.id, states.SUCCESS,\n+                                     self.b.encode_result(20, states.SUCCESS)])\n+            self.b._chord_part_return_script(\n+                keys=keys, args=[request.id, encoded, 1, request.group_index, 0, 0])\n+            callback.delay.assert_not_called()\n+            self.b.on_chord_part_return(request, states.SUCCESS, 20)\n+            callback.delay.assert_called_once_with([10, 20])\n+\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize('ordered', (True, False))\n+    def test_on_chord_part_return__same_task_id_at_two_indexes(self, ordered):\n+        # A header that reuses one frozen signature has its task id twice\n+        # (with different results; equal ones merge in the ordered zset)\n+        self.app.conf.result_backend_transport_options = dict(\n+            result_chord_ordered=ordered,\n+        )\n+\n+        with self.chord_context(2) as (tasks, _, callback):\n+            tasks[1].request.id = tasks[0].request.id\n+            self.b.on_chord_part_return(tasks[0].request, states.SUCCESS, 10)\n+            self.b.on_chord_part_return(tasks[1].request, states.SUCCESS, 20)\n+            callback.delay.assert_called_once_with([10, 20])\n+\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize(['ordered', 'expected'], [\n+        (True, [10, 20, 30]),\n+        (False, [20, 10, 30]),\n+    ])\n+    def test_on_chord_part_return__part_recorded_by_older_worker(\n+        self, ordered, expected,\n+    ):\n+        self.app.conf.result_backend_transport_options = dict(\n+            result_chord_ordered=ordered,\n+        )\n+\n+        with self.chord_context(3) as (tasks, _, callback):\n+            # Workers running an older version record their part without\n+            # marking the task id as done, which must not stall the chord.\n+            request = tasks[1].request\n+            jkey = self.b.get_key_for_group(request.group, '.j')\n+            encoded = self.b.encode([1, request.id, states.SUCCESS, 20])\n+            if ordered:\n+                self.b.client.zadd(jkey, {encoded: request.group_index})\n+            else:\n+                self.b.client.rpush(jkey, encoded)\n+            self.b.on_chord_part_return(tasks[0].request, states.SUCCESS, 10)\n+            callback.delay.assert_not_called()\n+            self.b.on_chord_part_return(tasks[2].request, states.SUCCESS, 30)\n+            callback.delay.assert_called_once_with(expected)\n+\n+\n+class test_RedisBackend_chord_part_script(basetest_RedisBackend):\n+    # The other chord tests run a Python copy of the chord part script.\n+    # These run the Lua script itself, with fakeredis.\n+\n+    @pytest.fixture(autouse=True)\n+    def fake_redis(self):\n+        self.FakeRedis = pytest.importorskip('fakeredis').FakeRedis\n+        pytest.importorskip('lupa')\n+        self.b.client = self.FakeRedis()\n+        with patch('celery.result.GroupResult.restore', return_value=None):\n+            yield\n+\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize(['ordered', 'indexed', 'expected'], [\n+        (True, True, [10, 20, 30]),\n+        (False, True, [10, 20, 30]),\n+        # Without an index, the parts have the same score and sort by task id\n+        (True, False, [30, 20, 10]),\n+        (False, False, [10, 20, 30]),\n+    ])\n+    def test_on_chord_part_return__duplicate_part(self, ordered, indexed, expected):\n+        self.app.conf.result_backend_transport_options = dict(\n+            result_chord_ordered=ordered,\n+        )\n+\n+        with self.chord_context(3) as (tasks, _, callback):\n+            for i, task in enumerate(tasks):\n+                # Task ids that sort in the reverse order of the header\n+                task.request.id = f'task-{2 - i}'\n+                if not indexed:\n+                    task.request.group_index = None\n+            self.b.on_chord_part_return(tasks[0].request, states.SUCCESS, 10)\n+            self.b.on_chord_part_return(tasks[0].request, states.SUCCESS, 99)\n+            self.b.on_chord_part_return(tasks[1].request, states.SUCCESS, 20)\n+            callback.delay.assert_not_called()\n+            self.b.on_chord_part_return(tasks[2].request, states.SUCCESS, 30)\n+            callback.delay.assert_called_once_with(expected)\n+            # A copy of the last part, after the chord completed\n+            self.b.on_chord_part_return(tasks[2].request, states.SUCCESS, 30)\n+            callback.delay.assert_called_once()\n+\n+    def test_on_chord_part_return__same_task_id_at_two_indexes(self):\n+        with self.chord_context(2) as (tasks, _, callback):\n+            tasks[1].request.id = tasks[0].request.id\n+            self.b.on_chord_part_return(tasks[0].request, states.SUCCESS, 10)\n+            self.b.on_chord_part_return(tasks[1].request, states.SUCCESS, 20)\n+            callback.delay.assert_called_once_with([10, 20])\n+\n+    def test_on_chord_part_return__expires(self):\n+        self.app.conf.result_chord_expires = 600\n+        self.b = self.Backend(app=self.app)\n+        self.b.client = client = self.FakeRedis()\n+\n+        with self.chord_context(2) as (tasks, request, _):\n+            gkey = self.b.get_key_for_group(request.group)\n+            client.set(gkey, 'a saved GroupResult')\n+            self.b.on_chord_part_return(tasks[0].request, states.SUCCESS, 10)\n+            for suffix in ('.j', '.s', '.d'):\n+                key = self.b.get_key_for_group(request.group, suffix)\n+                assert client.ttl(key) == 600\n+            # The group key is kept for at least result_expires (one day)\n+            assert client.ttl(gkey) == 86400\n+\n \n class test_RedisBackend_chords_complex(basetest_RedisBackend):\n     @pytest.fixture(scope=\"function\", autouse=True)\n", "problem_statement": "Redis backend: a chord header that runs twice makes the body run early with the wrong input\n# Checklist\n- [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `main` branch of Celery.\n- [ ] This has already been asked to the [discussions forum](https://github.com/celery/celery/discussions) first.\n- [x] I have read the relevant section in the\n  [contribution guide](https://docs.celeryq.dev/en/main/contributing.html#other-bugs)\n  on reporting bugs.\n- [x] I have checked the [issues list](https://github.com/celery/celery/issues?q=is%3Aissue+label%3A%22Issue+Type%3A+Bug+Report%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)\n  for similar or identical bug reports.\n- [x] I have checked the [pull requests list](https://github.com/celery/celery/pulls?q=is%3Apr+label%3A%22PR+Type%3A+Bugfix%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)\n  for existing proposed fixes.\n- [x] I have checked the [commit log](https://github.com/celery/celery/commits/main)\n  to find out if the bug was already fixed in the main branch.\n- [x] I have included all related issues and possible duplicate issues\n  in this issue (If there are none, check this box anyway).\n- [ ] I have tried to reproduce the issue with [pytest-celery](https://docs.celeryq.dev/projects/pytest-celery/en/latest/userguide/celery-bug-report.html) and added the reproduction script below.\n\n## Mandatory Debugging Information\n\n- [x] I have included the output of ``celery -A proj report`` in the issue.\n    (if you are not able to do this, then at least specify the Celery\n     version affected).\n- [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `main` branch of Celery.\n- [x] I have included the contents of ``pip freeze`` in the issue.\n- [x] I have included all the versions of all the external dependencies required\n  to reproduce this bug.\n\n## Optional Debugging Information\n- [x] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one Python version\n  and/or implementation.\n- [x] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one message broker and/or\n  result backend.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one version of the message\n  broker and/or result backend.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one operating system.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one workers pool.\n- [x] I have tried reproducing the issue with autoscaling, retries,\n  ETA/Countdown & rate limits disabled.\n- [x] I have tried reproducing the issue after downgrading\n  and/or upgrading Celery and its dependencies.\n\n## Related Issues and Possible Duplicates\n\n#### Related Issues\n\n- #10724 counts a header twice by another path.\n\n#### Possible Duplicates\n\n- None\n\n## Environment & Settings\n**Celery version**: 5.7.0rc1 (collider). It also happens on main (`d6d754e`), 5.6.3, and every older release I tried, back to 4.4.7.\n\n<details>\n<summary><b><code>celery report</code> Output:</b></summary>\n<p>\n\n```\nsoftware -> celery:5.7.0rc1 (collider) kombu:5.7.0rc1 py:3.13.16\n            billiard:4.3.1 redis:8.1.0\nplatform -> system:Linux arch:64bit, ELF\n            kernel version:6.18.44-fc-v139 imp:CPython\nloader   -> celery.loaders.app.AppLoader\nsettings -> transport:redis results:redis://localhost:6379/1\n\nbroker_url: 'redis://:********@localhost:6379/0'\nresult_backend: 'redis://localhost:6379/1'\ndeprecated_settings: None\ntask_acks_late: True\nbroker_transport_options: \n 'visibility_timeout': 10}\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n# Steps to Reproduce\n\n## Required Dependencies\n- **Minimal Python Version**: N/A or Unknown\n- **Minimal Celery Version**: 4.4.7 or older (the oldest I tried)\n- **Minimal Kombu Version**: N/A or Unknown\n- **Minimal Broker Version**: N/A or Unknown\n- **Minimal Result Backend Version**: N/A or Unknown\n- **Minimal OS and/or Kernel Version**: N/A or Unknown\n- **Minimal Broker Client Version**: N/A or Unknown\n- **Minimal Result Backend Client Version**: N/A or Unknown\n\n### Python Packages\n<details>\n<summary><b><code>pip freeze</code> Output:</b></summary>\n<p>\n\n```\namqp==5.4.1\nbilliard==4.3.1\ncelery==5.7.0rc1\nclick==8.5.0\nclick-didyoumean==0.3.1\nclick-plugins==1.1.1.2\nclick-repl==0.4.1\nkombu==5.7.0rc1\npackaging==26.3\nprompt-toolkit==3.0.53\npython-dateutil==2.9.0.post0\nredis==8.1.0\nsix==1.17.0\ntyping-extensions==4.16.0\ntzdata==2026.4\ntzlocal==5.4.4\nvine==5.1.0\nwcwidth==0.9.3\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n### Other Dependencies\n<details>\n<p>\nRedis 7.0.15 on localhost:6379, as both broker and result backend.\n</p>\n</details>\n\n## Minimally Reproducible Test Case\n\n<details>\n<summary><code>tasks.py</code>, <code>run.py</code>, and the commands to run them</summary>\n<p>\n\n```python\n# tasks.py\nimport os\nimport random\nimport signal\nimport time\n\nfrom celery import Celery\nfrom celery.signals import task_success\n\napp = Celery(\"tasks\", broker=\"redis://localhost:6379/0\", backend=\"redis://localhost:6379/1\")\napp.conf.update(\n    task_acks_late=True,\n    broker_transport_options={\"visibility_timeout\": 10},  # redeliver after 10 s, not 1 hour\n)\n\n\n@app.task\ndef header(name, seconds):\n    time.sleep(seconds)\n    return f\"{name}-{random.randint(1000, 9999)}\"\n\n\n@app.task\ndef body(results):\n    return results\n\n\n@task_success.connect\ndef kill_worker_once(sender=None, result=None, **kwargs):\n    # Header \"a\" saved its result but hasn't acked yet. Kill the worker here, once.\n    if sender.name == \"tasks.header\" and result.startswith(\"a-\") and not os.path.exists(\"killed\"):\n        open(\"killed\", \"w\").close()\n        os.killpg(os.getpgid(os.getppid()), signal.SIGKILL)\n```\n\n```python\n# run.py\nfrom celery import chord\n\nfrom tasks import body, header\n\nresult = chord([header.s(\"a\", 1), header.s(\"b\", 0), header.s(\"c\", 20)])(body.s())\nprint(\"body got:\", result.get(timeout=120))\n```\n\n```\ncelery -A tasks worker -c 3    # terminal 1\npython run.py                  # terminal 2\n# The worker dies about a second later. Wait 15 seconds, then start it again:\ncelery -A tasks worker -c 3    # terminal 1\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n# Expected Behavior\n\nThe body runs once all three headers are done, and gets one result from each:\n\n```\nbody got: ['a-4930', 'b-3339', 'c-5537']\n```\n\n# Actual Behavior\n\nThe body runs shortly after the restart, while header c is still running:\n\n```\nbody got: ['a-5654', 'a-6300', 'b-3016']\n```\n\nIt has two results for header a and none for header c. When header c finishes, Celery drops its result and doesn't log an error.\n\nAfter the restart, Redis hands back the message for header a, and header a runs again with a new value. The Redis backend counts finished headers in a sorted set. Each one adds `[1, tid, state, result]`, and the body runs when the count equals the number of headers. The second run of header a adds a second member, so it counts twice.\n\nAny crash between saving a result and the ack can do this under `acks_late`. So can a broker that delivers a message twice. In a fault test with RabbitMQ and 3 second network drops, 2 of 60 chords got the wrong input on main, and 4 of 60 on 5.6.3. `worker_deduplicate_successful_tasks` helps after a crash, but not when two copies run in parallel.\n\nI have a patch that counts each task id once, with a Lua script. I can open a PR.\n", "hints_text": "", "created_at": "2026-10-21T23:06:49Z"}
{"repo": "celery/celery", "pull_number": 10918, "instance_id": "celery__celery-10918", "issue_numbers": ["10909"], "base_commit": "451381d5229627058c9a647fe54c34d7292e3dce", "patch": "diff --git a/celery/backends/base.py b/celery/backends/base.py\nindex bbaa40fdd3e..07f3929c5a5 100644\n--- a/celery/backends/base.py\n+++ b/celery/backends/base.py\n@@ -822,11 +822,7 @@ def _ensure_retryable(self, func, *args, fallback_exc=None, fallback_msg=None, *\n                         self._sleep(sleep_amount)\n                     else:\n                         if fallback_exc:\n-                            exc_kwargs = {}\n-                            for key in (\"task_id\", \"state\"):\n-                                if key in kwargs:\n-                                    exc_kwargs[key] = kwargs[key]\n-                            raise_with_context(fallback_exc(fallback_msg, **exc_kwargs))\n+                            raise_with_context(fallback_exc(fallback_msg))\n                         raise\n                 else:\n                     raise\n@@ -840,14 +836,13 @@ def store_result(self, task_id, result, state,\n         \"\"\"\n         result = self.encode_result(result, state)\n \n-        kwargs.update({'task_id': task_id, 'state': state})\n-\n+        # Pass the values by position, as 5.6 did: backends outside Celery,\n+        # such as django-celery-results, name these parameters differently.\n         self._ensure_retryable(\n             self._store_result,\n-            fallback_exc=BackendStoreError,\n+            task_id, result, state, traceback,\n+            fallback_exc=partial(BackendStoreError, task_id=task_id, state=state),\n             fallback_msg=\"failed to store result on the backend\",\n-            result=result,\n-            traceback=traceback,\n             request=request,\n             **kwargs\n         )\n@@ -855,7 +850,7 @@ def store_result(self, task_id, result, state,\n \n     def forget(self, task_id):\n         self._cache.pop(task_id, None)\n-        self._ensure_retryable(self._forget, task_id=task_id)\n+        self._ensure_retryable(self._forget, task_id)\n \n     def _forget(self, task_id):\n         raise NotImplementedError('backend does not implement forget.')\n@@ -919,9 +914,9 @@ def get_task_meta(self, task_id, cache=True):\n \n         meta = self._ensure_retryable(\n             self._get_task_meta_for,\n-            fallback_exc=BackendGetMetaError,\n+            task_id,\n+            fallback_exc=partial(BackendGetMetaError, task_id=task_id),\n             fallback_msg=\"failed to get meta\",\n-            task_id=task_id\n         )\n \n         if cache and meta.get('status') == states.SUCCESS:\n@@ -955,7 +950,7 @@ def get_group_meta(self, group_id, cache=True):\n             except KeyError:\n                 pass\n \n-        meta = self._ensure_retryable(self._restore_group, group_id=group_id)\n+        meta = self._ensure_retryable(self._restore_group, group_id)\n         if cache and meta is not None:\n             self._cache[group_id] = meta\n         return meta\n@@ -968,15 +963,11 @@ def restore_group(self, group_id, cache=True):\n \n     def save_group(self, group_id, result):\n         \"\"\"Store the result of an executed group.\"\"\"\n-        return self._ensure_retryable(\n-            self._save_group,\n-            group_id=group_id,\n-            result=result\n-        )\n+        return self._ensure_retryable(self._save_group, group_id, result)\n \n     def delete_group(self, group_id):\n         self._cache.pop(group_id, None)\n-        return self._ensure_retryable(self._delete_group, group_id=group_id)\n+        return self._ensure_retryable(self._delete_group, group_id)\n \n     def cleanup(self):\n         \"\"\"Backend cleanup.\"\"\"\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/t/unit/backends/test_base.py b/t/unit/backends/test_base.py\nindex e5b2033e18a..b1e900f7d9e 100644\n--- a/t/unit/backends/test_base.py\n+++ b/t/unit/backends/test_base.py\n@@ -552,7 +552,44 @@ def test_save_group(self):\n         b = BaseBackend(self.app)\n         b._save_group = Mock()\n         b.save_group('foofoo', 'xxx')\n-        b._save_group.assert_called_with(group_id='foofoo', result='xxx')\n+        b._save_group.assert_called_with('foofoo', 'xxx')\n+\n+    def test_backend_hooks_get_positional_arguments(self):\n+        # Backends outside Celery (e.g. django-celery-results) give these\n+        # hooks other parameter names, so the values must go by position.\n+        stored, groups = {}, {}\n+\n+        class RenamedBackend(BaseBackend):\n+            def _store_result(self, tid, value, status, tb=None, request=None, using=None):\n+                stored[tid] = {'status': status, 'result': value, 'traceback': tb, 'using': using}\n+\n+            def _get_task_meta_for(self, tid):\n+                return stored.get(tid, {'status': states.PENDING, 'result': None})\n+\n+            def _forget(self, tid):\n+                stored.pop(tid)\n+\n+            def _save_group(self, gid, group_result):\n+                groups[gid] = group_result\n+\n+            def _restore_group(self, gid):\n+                return {'result': groups[gid]} if gid in groups else None\n+\n+            def _delete_group(self, gid):\n+                groups.pop(gid)\n+\n+        b = RenamedBackend(self.app)\n+        b.store_result('t1', 42, states.SUCCESS, traceback='tb', using='replica')\n+        assert stored['t1']['status'] == states.SUCCESS\n+        assert stored['t1']['traceback'] == 'tb'\n+        assert stored['t1']['using'] == 'replica'\n+        assert b.get_task_meta('t1', cache=False)['result'] == 42\n+        b.forget('t1')\n+        assert 't1' not in stored\n+        b.save_group('g1', 'xxx')\n+        assert b.restore_group('g1', cache=False) == 'xxx'\n+        b.delete_group('g1')\n+        assert 'g1' not in groups\n \n     def test_add_to_chord_interface(self):\n         b = BaseBackend(self.app)\n@@ -2234,8 +2271,9 @@ def test_get_reaching_max_retries(self):\n             try:\n                 b.get_task_meta(sentinel.task_id)\n                 assert False\n-            except BackendGetMetaError:\n+            except BackendGetMetaError as exc:\n                 assert b._sleep.call_count == 0\n+                assert exc.task_id is sentinel.task_id\n         finally:\n             self.app.conf.result_backend_always_retry = prev\n             self.app.conf.result_backend_max_retries = prev_max_retries\n@@ -2395,7 +2433,9 @@ def test_store_result_reaching_max_retries(self):\n             try:\n                 b.store_result(sentinel.task_id, 42, states.SUCCESS)\n                 assert False\n-            except BackendStoreError:\n+            except BackendStoreError as exc:\n+                assert exc.task_id is sentinel.task_id\n+                assert exc.state == states.SUCCESS\n                 assert b._sleep.call_count == 0\n         finally:\n             self.app.conf.result_backend_always_retry = prev\n", "problem_statement": "5.7.0rc1 breaks `django-celery-results`: every result write raises `TypeError`\n# Checklist\n- [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `main` branch of Celery.\n- [ ] This has already been asked to the [discussions forum](https://github.com/celery/celery/discussions) first.\n- [x] I have read the relevant section in the\n  [contribution guide](https://docs.celeryq.dev/en/main/contributing.html#other-bugs)\n  on reporting bugs.\n- [x] I have checked the [issues list](https://github.com/celery/celery/issues?q=is%3Aissue+label%3A%22Issue+Type%3A+Bug+Report%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)\n  for similar or identical bug reports.\n- [x] I have checked the [pull requests list](https://github.com/celery/celery/pulls?q=is%3Apr+label%3A%22PR+Type%3A+Bugfix%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)\n  for existing proposed fixes.\n- [x] I have checked the [commit log](https://github.com/celery/celery/commits/main)\n  to find out if the bug was already fixed in the main branch.\n- [x] I have included all related issues and possible duplicate issues\n  in this issue (If there are none, check this box anyway).\n- [ ] I have tried to reproduce the issue with [pytest-celery](https://docs.celeryq.dev/projects/pytest-celery/en/latest/userguide/celery-bug-report.html) and added the reproduction script below.\n\n## Mandatory Debugging Information\n\n- [x] I have included the output of ``celery -A proj report`` in the issue.\n    (if you are not able to do this, then at least specify the Celery\n     version affected).\n- [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `main` branch of Celery.\n- [x] I have included the contents of ``pip freeze`` in the issue.\n- [x] I have included all the versions of all the external dependencies required\n  to reproduce this bug.\n\n## Optional Debugging Information\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one Python version\n  and/or implementation.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one message broker and/or\n  result backend.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one version of the message\n  broker and/or result backend.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one operating system.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one workers pool.\n- [x] I have tried reproducing the issue with autoscaling, retries,\n  ETA/Countdown & rate limits disabled.\n- [x] I have tried reproducing the issue after downgrading\n  and/or upgrading Celery and its dependencies.\n\n## Related Issues and Possible Duplicates\n\n#### Related Issues\n\n- #10130 made this change.\n\n#### Possible Duplicates\n\n- None\n\n## Environment & Settings\n**Celery version**: 5.7.0rc1 (collider). It also fails on 5.7.0a1, 5.7.0b1 and main (`d6d754e`). It works on 5.6.3.\n\n<details>\n<summary><b><code>celery report</code> Output:</b></summary>\n<p>\n\n```\nsoftware -> celery:5.7.0rc1 (collider) kombu:5.7.0rc1 py:3.13.16\n            billiard:4.3.1 redis:8.1.0\nplatform -> system:Linux arch:64bit, ELF\n            kernel version:6.18.44-fc-v139 imp:CPython\nloader   -> celery.loaders.app.AppLoader\nsettings -> transport:redis results:django-db\n\nbroker_url: 'redis://:********@localhost:6379/0'\nresult_backend: 'django-db'\ndeprecated_settings: None\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n# Steps to Reproduce\n\n## Required Dependencies\n- **Minimal Python Version**: N/A or Unknown (seen on 3.13.16)\n- **Minimal Celery Version**: 5.7.0a1 (first release with #10130)\n- **Minimal Kombu Version**: N/A or Unknown\n- **Minimal Broker Version**: N/A or Unknown\n- **Minimal Result Backend Version**: django-celery-results 2.6.0 (latest)\n- **Minimal OS and/or Kernel Version**: N/A or Unknown\n- **Minimal Broker Client Version**: N/A or Unknown\n- **Minimal Result Backend Client Version**: N/A or Unknown\n\n### Python Packages\n<details>\n<summary><b><code>pip freeze</code> Output:</b></summary>\n<p>\n\n```\namqp==5.4.1\nasgiref==3.12.1\nbilliard==4.3.1\ncelery==5.7.0rc1\nclick==8.5.0\nclick-didyoumean==0.3.1\nclick-plugins==1.1.1.2\nclick-repl==0.4.1\ndjango==5.2.18\ndjango-celery-results==2.6.0\nkombu==5.7.0rc1\npackaging==26.3\nprompt-toolkit==3.0.53\npython-dateutil==2.9.0.post0\nredis==8.1.0\nsix==1.17.0\nsqlparse==0.6.0\ntyping-extensions==4.16.0\ntzdata==2026.4\ntzlocal==5.4.4\nvine==5.1.0\nwcwidth==0.9.3\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n### Other Dependencies\n<details>\n<p>\nRedis 7.0.15 on localhost:6379 as the broker. Django uses SQLite.\n</p>\n</details>\n\n## Minimally Reproducible Test Case\n\n<details>\n<summary><code>settings.py</code>, <code>tasks.py</code>, and the commands to run them</summary>\n<p>\n\n```python\n# settings.py\nSECRET_KEY = \"x\"\nINSTALLED_APPS = [\"django_celery_results\"]\nDATABASES = {\"default\": {\"ENGINE\": \"django.db.backends.sqlite3\", \"NAME\": \"db.sqlite3\"}}\nUSE_TZ = True\n```\n\n```python\n# tasks.py\nimport os\n\nimport django\n\nos.environ.setdefault(\"DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE\", \"settings\")\ndjango.setup()\n\nfrom celery import Celery\n\napp = Celery(\"tasks\", broker=\"redis://localhost:6379/0\", backend=\"django-db\")\n\n\n@app.task\ndef add(x, y):\n    return x + y\n```\n\n```\npython -m django migrate django_celery_results --settings=settings\ncelery -A tasks worker --loglevel=INFO                                    # terminal 1\npython -c \"from tasks import add; print(add.delay(2, 2).get(timeout=10))\"  # terminal 2\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n# Expected Behavior\n\nThe client prints `4`. That is what happens on 5.6.3 with the same `django-celery-results` 2.6.0.\n\n# Actual Behavior\n\nThe task runs, but Celery never saves its result. The client waits and then times out:\n\n```\ncelery.exceptions.TimeoutError: The operation timed out.\n```\n\nThe worker log shows why:\n\n```\nERROR/MainProcess] Pool callback raised exception: TypeError(\"DatabaseBackend._store_result() got an unexpected keyword argument 'state'. Did you mean 'status'?\")\n```\n\nSince #10130, `BaseBackend` calls the backend hooks with keyword arguments, as in `_store_result(task_id=..., result=..., state=...)`. Celery 5.6 passed the same values by position. `django-celery-results` gives two of these parameters other names. It calls the state `status` in `_store_result`, and the group `group_result` in `_save_group`. So every result write fails, and `save_group()` fails with a similar `TypeError`.\n\nWith `task_track_started=True`, the task doesn't run at all. The worker saves STARTED before it runs the task body, and that write fails. The worker still acks the message, and the task is lost.\n\nGoing back to positional arguments, as in 5.6, fixes it. I have a patch with a regression test and can open a PR. I hope this can land before 5.7.0, since any project that stores results with `django-celery-results` will hit it on upgrade.\n\n", "hints_text": "", "created_at": "2026-10-21T19:35:57Z"}
{"repo": "celery/celery", "pull_number": 10901, "instance_id": "celery__celery-10901", "issue_numbers": ["10888"], "base_commit": "75ca8ccf5aa6976e437ae8e0ecfbb51d516385b1", "patch": "diff --git a/celery/concurrency/asynpool.py b/celery/concurrency/asynpool.py\nindex 0bf701b177a..cb77e2ceb2e 100644\n--- a/celery/concurrency/asynpool.py\n+++ b/celery/concurrency/asynpool.py\n@@ -624,7 +624,12 @@ def register_with_event_loop(self, hub):\n     def _create_timelimit_handlers(self, hub):\n         \"\"\"Create handlers used to implement time limits.\"\"\"\n         call_later = hub.call_later\n-        trefs = self._tref_for_id = WeakValueDictionary()\n+        # Keep the existing mapping when registering again after a broker\n+        # reconnect, so the time limits of tasks that are still running\n+        # can still be cancelled when they finish.\n+        trefs = getattr(self, '_tref_for_id', None)\n+        if trefs is None:\n+            trefs = self._tref_for_id = WeakValueDictionary()\n \n         def on_timeout_set(R, soft, hard):\n             if soft:\ndiff --git a/celery/worker/consumer/consumer.py b/celery/worker/consumer/consumer.py\nindex 0725e47e295..35952a0e1a3 100644\n--- a/celery/worker/consumer/consumer.py\n+++ b/celery/worker/consumer/consumer.py\n@@ -159,6 +159,14 @@ class Consumer:\n     #: as sending heartbeats.\n     timer = None\n \n+    #: Entries on the event loop timer before the consumer last started\n+    #: connecting to the broker.\n+    timer_entries_before_start = None\n+\n+    #: Entries the event loop registered on its timer for the current\n+    #: connection, or :const:`None` if it has not registered them yet.\n+    timer_entries_registered = None\n+\n     restart_count = -1  # first start is the same as a restart\n \n     #: This flag will be turned off after the first failed\n@@ -387,6 +395,13 @@ def start(self):\n                 recoverable_errors = (self.connection_errors + self.channel_errors)\n             else:\n                 recoverable_errors = self.connection_errors\n+            if self.hub is not None:\n+                # Entries added to the timer from here on, by connecting\n+                # and starting the event loop, belong to this connection\n+                # (see :func:`celery.worker.loops.asynloop`).\n+                self.timer_entries_before_start = loops._timer_entries(\n+                    self.hub.timer)\n+                self.timer_entries_registered = None\n             try:\n                 blueprint.start(self)\n             except recoverable_errors as exc:\n@@ -413,8 +428,31 @@ def start(self):\n                     else:\n                         self.on_connection_error_before_connected(exc)\n                     self.on_close()\n+                    self._cancel_connection_timer_entries()\n                     blueprint.restart(self)\n \n+    def _cancel_connection_timer_entries(self):\n+        \"\"\"Cancel the timer entries registered for the failed connection.\n+\n+        :func:`~celery.worker.loops.asynloop` records the entries it\n+        registered for the connection.  If the connection failed before\n+        that, e.g. in a bootstep that runs after the transport connected,\n+        every entry added since the consumer started connecting belongs to\n+        it: the event loop was not running, so nothing else added entries\n+        in the meantime.  Without this, those entries would be part of the\n+        next attempt's snapshot and never be cancelled.\n+\n+        Cancelling an entry twice is harmless, so this also runs after\n+        :func:`~celery.worker.loops.asynloop` cancelled its own entries.\n+        \"\"\"\n+        if self.hub is None or self.timer_entries_before_start is None:\n+            return\n+        entries = self.timer_entries_registered\n+        if entries is None:\n+            entries = (loops._timer_entries(self.hub.timer) -\n+                       self.timer_entries_before_start)\n+        loops._cancel_timer_entries(self.hub.timer, entries)\n+\n     def _get_connection_retry_type(self, is_connection_loss_on_startup):\n         return ('broker_connection_retry_on_startup'\n                 if (is_connection_loss_on_startup\n@@ -555,10 +593,11 @@ def on_close(self):\n         # entries (through self.timer.cancel(), not entry.cancel()\n         # directly, since e.g. the Eventlet timer relies on that to catch\n         # GreenletExit) so the callback can't fire after we've torn down\n-        # this connection (the synloop error path doesn't clear the timer\n-        # the way asynloop's hub.reset()/hub.timer.clear() does), then drop\n-        # our copies since the broker may redeliver these tasks to another\n-        # worker on reconnect.\n+        # this connection (neither event loop cancels them: synloop's error\n+        # path cancels nothing, and asynloop's only cancels the entries it\n+        # registered for the connection, which doesn't include these), then\n+        # drop our copies since the broker may redeliver these tasks to\n+        # another worker on reconnect.\n         for r in tuple(scheduled_requests):\n             entry = r._eta_timer_entry\n             if entry is not None and self.timer is not None:\ndiff --git a/celery/worker/loops.py b/celery/worker/loops.py\nindex c0c71dd8a81..ea32b1bbd1d 100644\n--- a/celery/worker/loops.py\n+++ b/celery/worker/loops.py\n@@ -47,6 +47,21 @@ def tick(rate):\n     return heartbeat_error\n \n \n+def _timer_entries(timer):\n+    return {scheduled.entry for scheduled in timer.queue}\n+\n+\n+def _cancel_timer_entries(timer, entries):\n+    # Cancel each entry on its own, so one that fails to cancel does not\n+    # leave the remaining entries of the connection live.\n+    for entry in entries:\n+        try:\n+            timer.cancel(entry)\n+        except Exception as exc:  # pylint: disable=broad-except\n+            logger.exception(\n+                'Error cancelling hub timer entry %r: %r', entry, exc)\n+\n+\n def asynloop(obj, connection, consumer, blueprint, hub, qos,\n              heartbeat, clock, hbrate=2.0):\n     \"\"\"Non-blocking event loop.\"\"\"\n@@ -56,6 +71,13 @@ def asynloop(obj, connection, consumer, blueprint, hub, qos,\n \n     on_task_received = obj.create_task_handler()\n \n+    # The consumer records the timer entries that existed before it started\n+    # connecting, so that the entries registered by the transport when\n+    # connecting are cancelled along with the ones registered below.\n+    timer_entries_before = obj.timer_entries_before_start\n+    if timer_entries_before is None:\n+        timer_entries_before = _timer_entries(hub.timer)\n+\n     heartbeat_error = _enable_amqheartbeats(hub.timer, connection, rate=hbrate)\n     dispatcher_heartbeat_error = [None]\n     if obj.event_dispatcher:\n@@ -65,6 +87,11 @@ def asynloop(obj, connection, consumer, blueprint, hub, qos,\n     consumer.on_message = on_task_received\n     obj.controller.register_with_event_loop(hub)\n     obj.register_with_event_loop(hub)\n+    registered_timer_entries = (\n+        _timer_entries(hub.timer) - timer_entries_before)\n+    # The consumer cancels these if the connection fails before the loop\n+    # below is running (see :meth:`Consumer.start`).\n+    obj.timer_entries_registered = registered_timer_entries\n     consumer.consume()\n     obj.on_ready()\n \n@@ -106,12 +133,11 @@ def asynloop(obj, connection, consumer, blueprint, hub, qos,\n     except Exception:\n         # Reset the hub on error (e.g. connection loss) to clean up\n         # stale file descriptors and callbacks from the old connection.\n-        # Also clear the timer queue so that stale periodic entries added by\n-        # register_with_event_loop (e.g. maybe_restore_messages) do not fire\n-        # against the broken connection after reconnect and trigger another\n-        # crash before the new connection is fully established.\n-        # All hub timers are re-registered during blueprint.start() once this\n-        # exception propagates and the consumer reconnects.\n+        # Also cancel the timer entries registered while connecting and\n+        # starting this loop, so that stale periodic entries (e.g.\n+        # maybe_restore_messages) do not fire against the broken connection\n+        # after reconnect and trigger another crash before the new\n+        # connection is fully established.\n         # We intentionally do NOT reset on normal exit (graceful shutdown)\n         # so that timers (e.g. heartbeat) keep firing while the pool drains.\n         # WorkerShutdown/WorkerTerminate extend SystemExit (not Exception)\n@@ -121,18 +147,14 @@ def asynloop(obj, connection, consumer, blueprint, hub, qos,\n         except Exception as exc:  # pylint: disable=broad-except\n             logger.exception(\n                 'Error cleaning up after event loop: %r', exc)\n-        # Clear stale timer entries accumulated across reconnects (e.g.\n-        # maybe_restore_messages registered via call_repeatedly). Without\n-        # this, each reconnect appends a new entry; all of them fire during\n-        # the reconnect window, raise again, and trigger another restart.\n-        # Use a separate try/except so this always runs even if hub.reset()\n-        # raised above. Timers are re-registered by register_with_event_loop\n-        # when blueprint.start() is called after reconnect.\n-        try:\n-            hub.timer.clear()\n-        except Exception as exc:  # pylint: disable=broad-except\n-            logger.exception(\n-                'Error clearing hub timer after event loop: %r', exc)\n+        # These entries are registered again when the consumer reconnects\n+        # and calls asynloop again; without cancelling them, each reconnect\n+        # adds another copy.  The timer must not be cleared: it is shared\n+        # with the rest of the worker, and other entries on it, such as the\n+        # soft/hard time limits of tasks that keep running across the\n+        # reconnect, are not registered again (#10888).\n+        # This runs even if hub.reset() raised above.\n+        _cancel_timer_entries(hub.timer, registered_timer_entries)\n         raise\n \n \n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/t/unit/concurrency/test_prefork.py b/t/unit/concurrency/test_prefork.py\nindex b3fc97229ea..bb06acd6791 100644\n--- a/t/unit/concurrency/test_prefork.py\n+++ b/t/unit/concurrency/test_prefork.py\n@@ -716,6 +716,26 @@ def test_register_with_event_loop__no_on_tick_dupes(self):\n         pool.register_with_event_loop(hub)\n         hub.on_tick.add.assert_called_once()\n \n+    def test_create_timelimit_handlers_keeps_trefs_on_reregister(self):\n+        \"\"\"Time limits armed before the pool registers with the event loop\n+        again (e.g. after a broker reconnect) can still be cancelled when\n+        the task finishes (#10888).\n+        \"\"\"\n+        pool = Mock(spec=asynpool.AsynPool)\n+        hub = Hub()\n+        try:\n+            asynpool.AsynPool._create_timelimit_handlers(pool, hub)\n+            pool.on_timeout_set(Mock(_job='job-1'), None, 60)\n+            tref = pool._tref_for_id['job-1']\n+\n+            asynpool.AsynPool._create_timelimit_handlers(pool, hub)\n+            assert pool._tref_for_id['job-1'] is tref\n+            pool.on_timeout_cancel(Mock(_job='job-1'))\n+            assert tref.canceled\n+            assert 'job-1' not in pool._tref_for_id\n+        finally:\n+            hub.close()\n+\n     @t.skip.if_pypy\n     @patch('billiard.pool.Pool._create_worker_process')\n     def test_before_create_process_signal(self, create_process):\ndiff --git a/t/unit/worker/test_consumer.py b/t/unit/worker/test_consumer.py\nindex 2da24c880d3..826803b0009 100644\n--- a/t/unit/worker/test_consumer.py\n+++ b/t/unit/worker/test_consumer.py\n@@ -11,6 +11,7 @@\n from billiard.exceptions import RestartFreqExceeded\n from kombu import Queue\n from kombu.asynchronous import Hub\n+from kombu.asynchronous.timer import Timer\n \n from celery import bootsteps\n from celery.contrib.testing.mocks import ContextMock\n@@ -77,6 +78,8 @@ def get_consumer(self, no_hub=False, **kwargs):\n             **kwargs\n         )\n         consumer.blueprint = Mock(name='blueprint')\n+        if consumer.hub is not None:\n+            consumer.hub.timer.queue = []\n         consumer.pool.num_processes = 2\n         consumer._restart_state = Mock(name='_restart_state')\n         consumer.connection = _amqp_connection()\n@@ -484,6 +487,94 @@ def se(*args, **kwargs):\n             c.start()\n             sleep.assert_called_with(1)\n \n+    def test_records_timer_entries_before_start(self):\n+        # asynloop cancels the timer entries added after this point when the\n+        # connection is lost, e.g. the periodic entries the transport\n+        # registers when connecting (#10888).\n+        c = self.get_consumer()\n+        worker_entry = Mock(name='worker_entry')\n+        c.hub.timer.queue = [Mock(entry=worker_entry)]\n+\n+        def bp_start(*args, **kwargs):\n+            assert c.timer_entries_before_start == {worker_entry}\n+            c.hub.timer.queue.append(Mock(entry=Mock(name='transport')))\n+            c.blueprint.state = CLOSE\n+\n+        c.blueprint.start.side_effect = bp_start\n+        with patch('celery.worker.consumer.consumer.sleep'):\n+            c.start()\n+        c.blueprint.start.assert_called_once_with(c)\n+\n+    def test_no_timer_entries_recorded_without_hub(self):\n+        c = self.get_consumer(no_hub=True)\n+\n+        def bp_start(*args, **kwargs):\n+            c.blueprint.state = CLOSE\n+\n+        c.blueprint.start.side_effect = bp_start\n+        with patch('celery.worker.consumer.consumer.sleep'):\n+            c.start()\n+        assert c.timer_entries_before_start is None\n+\n+    def _restart_once_with_real_timer(self, c):\n+        c.app.conf['broker_connection_retry'] = True\n+        c.app.conf['broker_connection_retry_on_startup'] = True\n+        c.hub.timer = Timer()\n+        # stops test from running indefinitely in the while loop\n+        c.blueprint.restart.side_effect = self._closer(c)\n+\n+    def test_cancels_timer_entries_when_connection_fails_before_loop(self):\n+        # The connection can fail after the transport registered its timer\n+        # entries but before asynloop records them, e.g. in a later\n+        # bootstep.  These entries must still be cancelled: otherwise they\n+        # are part of the next attempt's snapshot and are never cancelled.\n+        c = self.get_consumer()\n+        self._restart_once_with_real_timer(c)\n+        worker_entry = c.hub.timer.call_repeatedly(5, Mock())\n+        transport_entries = []\n+\n+        def bp_start(*args, **kwargs):\n+            transport_entries.append(c.hub.timer.call_repeatedly(10, Mock()))\n+            raise OSError('connection lost')\n+\n+        c.blueprint.start.side_effect = bp_start\n+        c.start()\n+        c.blueprint.restart.assert_called_once_with(c)\n+        assert transport_entries[0].canceled\n+        assert not worker_entry.canceled\n+\n+    def test_cancels_only_loop_entries_once_the_loop_started(self):\n+        # Once asynloop has recorded the entries it registered for the\n+        # connection, entries added while it ran (e.g. the time limits of\n+        # tasks that keep running across the reconnect) must survive.\n+        c = self.get_consumer()\n+        self._restart_once_with_real_timer(c)\n+        loop_entries, time_limits = [], []\n+\n+        def bp_start(*args, **kwargs):\n+            loop_entry = c.hub.timer.call_repeatedly(10, Mock())\n+            c.timer_entries_registered = {loop_entry}\n+            loop_entries.append(loop_entry)\n+            time_limits.append(c.hub.timer.call_after(60, Mock()))\n+            raise OSError('connection lost')\n+\n+        c.blueprint.start.side_effect = bp_start\n+        c.start()\n+        assert loop_entries[0].canceled\n+        assert not time_limits[0].canceled\n+\n+    def test_timer_entries_registered_reset_before_each_attempt(self):\n+        c = self.get_consumer()\n+        c.timer_entries_registered = {Mock(name='previous_connection')}\n+\n+        def bp_start(*args, **kwargs):\n+            assert c.timer_entries_registered is None\n+            c.blueprint.state = CLOSE\n+\n+        c.blueprint.start.side_effect = bp_start\n+        with patch('celery.worker.consumer.consumer.sleep'):\n+            c.start()\n+\n     def test_do_not_restart_when_closed(self):\n         c = self.get_consumer()\n \ndiff --git a/t/unit/worker/test_loops.py b/t/unit/worker/test_loops.py\nindex ac9b80f1ca7..3c4a755c0a9 100644\n--- a/t/unit/worker/test_loops.py\n+++ b/t/unit/worker/test_loops.py\n@@ -5,6 +5,7 @@\n \n import pytest\n from kombu.asynchronous import ERR, READ, WRITE, Hub\n+from kombu.asynchronous.timer import Timer\n from kombu.exceptions import DecodeError\n \n from celery.bootsteps import CLOSE, RUN\n@@ -66,6 +67,8 @@ def __init__(self, app, heartbeat=None, on_task_message=None,\n         self.hub.readers = {}\n         self.hub.timer = Mock(name='hub.timer')\n         self.hub.timer._queue = [Mock()]\n+        self.hub.timer.queue = []\n+        self.obj.timer_entries_before_start = None\n         self.hub.fire_timers = Mock(name='hub.fire_timers')\n         self.hub.fire_timers.return_value = 1.7\n         self.hub.poller = Mock(name='hub.poller')\n@@ -504,34 +507,96 @@ def test_hub_reset_on_connection_error(self):\n             asynloop(*x.args)\n         x.hub.reset.assert_called_once()\n \n-    def test_hub_timer_cleared_on_connection_error(self):\n-        # Stale timer entries (e.g. maybe_restore_messages) must be cleared\n-        # when the event loop exits due to a connection error.  Without this,\n-        # entries accumulated across reconnects can fire against the broken\n-        # connection and crash the loop again before the new connection is\n-        # fully established, causing a rapid restart loop.\n-        x = X(self.app)\n+    def _with_real_timer(self, x):\n+        # Use a real timer, and register a periodic entry with the event loop\n+        # the way the transport does (e.g. maybe_restore_messages).\n+        x.hub.timer = Timer()\n+        x.loop_entries = []\n+\n+        def register_with_event_loop(hub):\n+            x.loop_entries.append(hub.timer.call_repeatedly(10, Mock()))\n+        x.obj.register_with_event_loop.side_effect = register_with_event_loop\n+        return x\n+\n+    def _error_after_first_poll(self, x):\n         x.hub.readers = {6: Mock()}\n-        x.hub.timer._queue = [1]\n         x.hub.reset = Mock(name='hub.reset()')\n         x.close_then_error(x.hub.poller.poll)\n         x.hub.fire_timers.return_value = 33.37\n         x.hub.poller.poll.return_value = []\n+        return x\n+\n+    def _queued(self, timer):\n+        return [scheduled.entry for scheduled in timer.queue]\n+\n+    def test_hub_timer_entries_cancelled_on_connection_error(self):\n+        # Stale timer entries (e.g. maybe_restore_messages) must be cancelled\n+        # when the event loop exits due to a connection error.  Without this,\n+        # entries accumulated across reconnects can fire against the broken\n+        # connection and crash the loop again before the new connection is\n+        # fully established, causing a rapid restart loop.\n+        x = self._error_after_first_poll(self._with_real_timer(X(self.app)))\n+        with pytest.raises(socket.error):\n+            asynloop(*x.args)\n+        assert x.loop_entries\n+        assert all(entry.canceled for entry in x.loop_entries)\n+\n+    def test_hub_timer_entries_cancelled_across_reconnects(self):\n+        x = self._error_after_first_poll(self._with_real_timer(X(self.app)))\n+        for _ in range(3):\n+            x.blueprint.state = RUN\n+            with pytest.raises(socket.error):\n+                asynloop(*x.args)\n+        assert len(x.loop_entries) == 3\n+        assert all(entry.canceled for entry in x.loop_entries)\n+\n+    def test_hub_timer_entries_registered_when_connecting_cancelled(self):\n+        # The transport registers its periodic entries when the consumer\n+        # connects, before asynloop is called.  The consumer records the\n+        # timer entries before it starts connecting, so these are cancelled\n+        # as well.\n+        x = self._error_after_first_poll(self._with_real_timer(X(self.app)))\n+        worker_entry = x.hub.timer.call_repeatedly(5, Mock())\n+        x.obj.timer_entries_before_start = {worker_entry}\n+        transport_entry = x.hub.timer.call_repeatedly(10, Mock())\n+        with pytest.raises(socket.error):\n+            asynloop(*x.args)\n+        assert transport_entry.canceled\n+        assert not worker_entry.canceled\n+\n+    def test_hub_timer_keeps_other_entries_on_connection_error(self):\n+        # The timer is shared with the rest of the worker: e.g. the\n+        # soft/hard time limits of tasks that keep running across the\n+        # reconnect are not registered again, so they must survive (#10888).\n+        x = self._error_after_first_poll(self._with_real_timer(X(self.app)))\n+        existing = x.hub.timer.call_after(60, Mock())\n+        added_while_running = []\n+\n+        def fire_timers(propagate=()):\n+            if not added_while_running:\n+                added_while_running.append(\n+                    x.hub.timer.call_after(60, Mock()))\n+            return 33.37\n+        x.hub.fire_timers.side_effect = fire_timers\n         with pytest.raises(socket.error):\n             asynloop(*x.args)\n-        x.hub.timer.clear.assert_called_once()\n+        assert added_while_running\n+        for entry in (existing, *added_while_running):\n+            assert not entry.canceled\n+            assert entry in self._queued(x.hub.timer)\n \n-    def test_hub_timer_not_cleared_on_graceful_shutdown(self):\n-        # On graceful shutdown the timer queue must be left intact so that\n+    def test_hub_timer_entries_kept_on_graceful_shutdown(self):\n+        # On graceful shutdown the timer entries must be left intact so that\n         # periodic timers (e.g. heartbeat) keep firing while the pool drains.\n-        x = X(self.app)\n+        x = self._with_real_timer(X(self.app))\n         x.hub.reset = Mock(name='hub.reset()')\n         x.hub.on_tick.add(x.closer(mod=2))\n         asynloop(*x.args)\n-        x.hub.timer.clear.assert_not_called()\n+        assert x.loop_entries\n+        assert not any(entry.canceled for entry in x.loop_entries)\n \n-    def test_hub_timer_not_cleared_on_worker_shutdown(self):\n-        x = X(self.app)\n+    def test_hub_timer_entries_kept_on_worker_shutdown(self):\n+        x = self._with_real_timer(X(self.app))\n         x.hub.reset = Mock(name='hub.reset()')\n         state.should_stop = 303\n         try:\n@@ -539,10 +604,10 @@ def test_hub_timer_not_cleared_on_worker_shutdown(self):\n                 asynloop(*x.args)\n         finally:\n             state.should_stop = None\n-        x.hub.timer.clear.assert_not_called()\n+        assert not any(entry.canceled for entry in x.loop_entries)\n \n-    def test_hub_timer_not_cleared_on_worker_terminate(self):\n-        x = X(self.app)\n+    def test_hub_timer_entries_kept_on_worker_terminate(self):\n+        x = self._with_real_timer(X(self.app))\n         x.hub.reset = Mock(name='hub.reset()')\n         state.should_terminate = True\n         try:\n@@ -550,40 +615,59 @@ def test_hub_timer_not_cleared_on_worker_terminate(self):\n                 asynloop(*x.args)\n         finally:\n             state.should_terminate = None\n-        x.hub.timer.clear.assert_not_called()\n-\n-    def test_hub_timer_clear_error_still_reraises_original(self):\n-        # If hub.timer.clear() itself raises, the original connection error\n-        # must still be propagated, not the cleanup error.\n-        x = X(self.app)\n-        x.hub.readers = {6: Mock()}\n-        x.hub.timer._queue = [1]\n-        x.hub.reset = Mock(name='hub.reset()')\n-        x.hub.timer.clear = Mock(\n-            name='hub.timer.clear()', side_effect=RuntimeError('clear failed')\n+        assert not any(entry.canceled for entry in x.loop_entries)\n+\n+    def test_hub_timer_cancel_error_still_reraises_original(self):\n+        # If cancelling the timer entries raises, the original connection\n+        # error must still be propagated, not the cleanup error.\n+        x = self._error_after_first_poll(self._with_real_timer(X(self.app)))\n+        x.hub.timer.cancel = Mock(\n+            name='hub.timer.cancel()', side_effect=RuntimeError('cancel failed')\n         )\n-        x.close_then_error(x.hub.poller.poll)\n-        x.hub.fire_timers.return_value = 33.37\n-        x.hub.poller.poll.return_value = []\n         with pytest.raises(socket.error):\n             asynloop(*x.args)\n-        x.hub.timer.clear.assert_called_once()\n+        x.hub.timer.cancel.assert_called_once()\n+\n+    def test_hub_timer_cancel_error_does_not_skip_other_entries(self):\n+        # A failure to cancel one entry must not leave the connection's\n+        # other entries live.\n+        x = self._error_after_first_poll(self._with_real_timer(X(self.app)))\n+\n+        def register_with_event_loop(hub):\n+            x.loop_entries.append(hub.timer.call_repeatedly(10, Mock()))\n+            x.loop_entries.append(hub.timer.call_repeatedly(20, Mock()))\n+        x.obj.register_with_event_loop.side_effect = register_with_event_loop\n+        cancel, attempted = x.hub.timer.cancel, []\n+\n+        def cancel_failing_once(entry):\n+            attempted.append(entry)\n+            if len(attempted) == 1:\n+                raise RuntimeError('cancel failed')\n+            return cancel(entry)\n+        x.hub.timer.cancel = cancel_failing_once\n+        with pytest.raises(socket.error):\n+            asynloop(*x.args)\n+        assert sorted(attempted, key=id) == sorted(x.loop_entries, key=id)\n+        assert sum(entry.canceled for entry in x.loop_entries) == 1\n \n-    def test_hub_timer_cleared_even_when_reset_raises(self):\n-        # hub.timer.clear() must still be called even if hub.reset() raises.\n-        # The two cleanup calls are in separate try/except blocks so that a\n-        # failure in hub.reset() does not prevent stale timer entries from\n-        # being discarded, avoiding stale timers persisting after a reset error.\n-        x = X(self.app)\n-        x.hub.readers = {6: Mock()}\n-        x.hub.timer._queue = [1]\n-        x.hub.reset = Mock(name='hub.reset()', side_effect=RuntimeError('reset failed'))\n-        x.close_then_error(x.hub.poller.poll)\n-        x.hub.fire_timers.return_value = 33.37\n-        x.hub.poller.poll.return_value = []\n+    def test_registered_timer_entries_recorded_on_consumer(self):\n+        # The consumer cancels these if the connection fails before the\n+        # loop is running (see Consumer._cancel_connection_timer_entries).\n+        x = self._with_real_timer(X(self.app))\n+        x.hub.on_tick.add(x.closer(mod=2))\n+        asynloop(*x.args)\n+        assert x.obj.timer_entries_registered == set(x.loop_entries)\n+\n+    def test_hub_timer_entries_cancelled_even_when_reset_raises(self):\n+        # The timer entries must still be cancelled even if hub.reset()\n+        # raises.  The two cleanup steps are in separate try/except blocks so\n+        # that a failure in hub.reset() does not prevent stale timer entries\n+        # from being discarded.\n+        x = self._error_after_first_poll(self._with_real_timer(X(self.app)))\n+        x.hub.reset.side_effect = RuntimeError('reset failed')\n         with pytest.raises(socket.error):\n             asynloop(*x.args)\n-        x.hub.timer.clear.assert_called_once()\n+        assert all(entry.canceled for entry in x.loop_entries)\n \n     def test_hub_not_reset_on_graceful_shutdown(self):\n         x = X(self.app)\n", "problem_statement": "5.7 regression: broker reconnect clears the shared worker timer, silently dropping soft/hard time limits of running tasks\n<!--\nPlease fill this template entirely and do not erase parts of it.\nWe reserve the right to close without a response\nbug reports which are incomplete.\n-->\n# Checklist\n- [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `main` branch of Celery.\n- [ ] This has already been asked to the [discussions forum](https://github.com/celery/celery/discussions) first.\n- [x] I have read the relevant section in the\n  [contribution guide](https://docs.celeryq.dev/en/main/contributing.html#other-bugs)\n  on reporting bugs.\n- [x] I have checked the [issues list](https://github.com/celery/celery/issues?q=is%3Aissue+label%3A%22Issue+Type%3A+Bug+Report%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)\n  for similar or identical bug reports.\n- [x] I have checked the [pull requests list](https://github.com/celery/celery/pulls?q=is%3Apr+label%3A%22PR+Type%3A+Bugfix%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)\n  for existing proposed fixes.\n- [x] I have checked the [commit log](https://github.com/celery/celery/commits/main)\n  to find out if the bug was already fixed in the main branch.\n- [x] I have included all related issues and possible duplicate issues\n  in this issue (If there are none, check this box anyway).\n- [ ] I have tried to reproduce the issue with [pytest-celery](https://docs.celeryq.dev/projects/pytest-celery/en/latest/userguide/celery-bug-report.html) and added the reproduction script below.\n\n## Mandatory Debugging Information\n\n- [x] I have included the output of ``celery -A proj report`` in the issue.\n- [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `main` branch of Celery.\n- [x] I have included the contents of ``pip freeze`` in the issue.\n- [x] I have included all the versions of all the external dependencies required\n  to reproduce this bug.\n\n## Optional Debugging Information\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one Python version\n  and/or implementation.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one message broker and/or\n  result backend.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one version of the message\n  broker and/or result backend.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one operating system.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one workers pool.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue with autoscaling, retries,\n  ETA/Countdown & rate limits disabled.\n- [x] I have tried reproducing the issue after downgrading\n  and/or upgrading Celery and its dependencies.\n\n## Related Issues and Possible Duplicates\n\n#### Related Issues\n\n- #10205 \u2014 worker stops consuming after broker connection reset (the problem #10218 fixed)\n- #10218 \u2014 PR that added `hub.timer.clear()` to `asynloop`'s exception path (first released in v5.7.0a1)\n- #7844 \u2014 user timers cancelled when the consumer closes (older code path, same class of problem)\n\n#### Possible Duplicates\n\n- None\n\n## Environment & Settings\n\n**Celery version**: 5.7.0rc1 (collider). The affected code is also in v5.7.0a1 and v5.7.0b1; 5.6.x is not affected.\nEdit: I said 5.6.x wasn't affected, but #10218 was backported, so **5.6.3 is affected as well**. 5.6.2 is the last good release. \n\n<details>\n<summary><b><code>celery report</code> Output:</b></summary>\n<p>\n\n```\n\nsoftware -> celery:5.7.0rc1 (collider) kombu:5.7.0rc1 py:3.12.13\n            billiard:4.3.1 py-amqp:5.4.1\nplatform -> system:Linux arch:64bit, ELF\n            kernel version:6.8.0-138-generic imp:CPython\nloader   -> celery.loaders.default.Loader\nsettings -> transport:amqp results:disabled\n\ndeprecated_settings: None\n\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n# Steps to Reproduce\n\n## Required Dependencies\n\n- **Minimal Python Version**: 3.12.13 (tested)\n- **Minimal Celery Version**: 5.7.0a1\n- **Minimal Kombu Version**: 5.7.0rc1 (tested)\n- **Minimal Broker Version**: any (Redis / RabbitMQ). Triggered by any connection error caught by `asynloop`\n- **Minimal Result Backend Version**: N/A\n- **Minimal OS and/or Kernel Version**: Linux 6.8 (tested)\n- **Minimal Broker Client Version**: N/A\n- **Minimal Result Backend Client Version**: N/A\n\n### Python Packages\n\n<details>\n<summary><b><code>pip freeze</code> Output:</b></summary>\n<p>\n\n```\n-e git+https://github.com/celery/celery@0731c053b#egg=celery  # 5.7.0rc1\namqp==5.4.1\nbilliard==4.3.1\nclick==8.5.0\nclick-didyoumean==0.3.1\nclick-plugins==1.1.1.2\nclick-repl==0.4.1\nkombu==5.7.0rc1\npackaging==26.3\nprompt-toolkit==3.0.53\npython-dateutil==2.9.0.post0\nsix==1.17.0\ntzdata==2026.4\ntzlocal==5.4.4\nvine==5.1.0\nwcwidth==0.9.2\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n### Other Dependencies\n\n<details>\n<p>\nN/A\n</p>\n</details>\n\n## Minimally Reproducible Test Case\n\nThis uses the real kombu `Hub`/`Timer`, the real `AsynPool` time-limit handlers and the real `asynloop` hitting a broker connection error. Only the pool object and the connection are mocked.\n\n<details>\n<p>\n\n```python\nimport socket\nimport time\nfrom unittest.mock import Mock\n\nfrom kombu.asynchronous import Hub\nfrom kombu.asynchronous.timer import Timer\n\nfrom celery import bootsteps\nfrom celery.concurrency.asynpool import AsynPool\nfrom celery.worker.loops import asynloop\n\nhub = Hub(Timer())\n\n# AsynPool's own time-limit machinery, bound to a stub pool object\nfired = []\npool = Mock(spec=AsynPool)\npool._cache = {'job-1': Mock(name='ApplyResult')}\npool.on_hard_timeout = lambda r: fired.append('HARD TIMEOUT -> kill child')\npool._on_hard_timeout = lambda job: AsynPool._on_hard_timeout(pool, job)\npool._on_soft_timeout = lambda *a: AsynPool._on_soft_timeout(pool, *a)\nAsynPool._create_timelimit_handlers(pool, hub)\npool.on_timeout_set(Mock(_job='job-1'), None, 0.05)   # hard time limit = 50ms\nprint('timer entries before reconnect:', len(hub.timer.queue))\n\n# Consumer event loop hits a broker connection error\nobj = Mock(restart_count=1, event_dispatcher=None, connection=Mock())\nconn = Mock(connection_errors=(socket.error,))\nconn.get_heartbeat_interval.return_value = 0\nconn.transport.driver_type = 'redis'\n\n\ndef broken_loop():\n    raise socket.error('connection reset by broker')\n    yield\n\n\nhub.create_loop = broken_loop\ntry:\n    asynloop(obj, conn, Mock(), Mock(state=bootsteps.RUN), hub,\n             Mock(prev=1, value=1), None, None)\nexcept socket.error as exc:\n    print('asynloop raised:', exc, '-> consumer will reconnect')\nprint('timer entries after reconnect:', len(hub.timer.queue))\n\ntime.sleep(0.1)\nfor _ in range(3):        # drive the timer as the restarted event loop would\n    hub.fire_timers(propagate=(Exception,))\nprint('hard time limit fired?', fired or 'NO - task can run forever')\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n# Expected Behavior\n\nA task that is still executing in a pool child when the broker connection drops keeps its `soft_time_limit` / `time_limit`. By default `worker_cancel_long_running_tasks_on_connection_loss=False`, so such tasks deliberately keep running across the reconnect.\n\nIf you remove the `asynloop(...)` call from the script above, the hard limit fires as expected:\n\n```\ntimer entries before reconnect: 1\nhard time limit fired? ['HARD TIMEOUT -> kill child']\n```\n\n# Actual Behavior\n\n```\ntimer entries before reconnect: 1\nasynloop raised: connection reset by broker -> consumer will reconnect\ntimer entries after reconnect: 0\nhard time limit fired? NO - task can run forever\n```\n\n### Root cause\n\n`celery/worker/loops.py` (`asynloop`, exception path, added by #10218):\n\n```python\n        try:\n            hub.timer.clear()\n        except Exception as exc:  # pylint: disable=broad-except\n            logger.exception(\n                'Error clearing hub timer after event loop: %r', exc)\n        raise\n```\n\n`hub.timer` is the **worker-wide** timer (`WorkController.timer`). It is shared with:\n\n- **`AsynPool`'s per-job time limits** (`celery/concurrency/asynpool.py`, `_create_timelimit_handlers` / `on_timeout_set`). These timers are armed with `hub.call_later` only once, when the job is sent to a child process. Nothing re-arms them after the consumer restarts.\n- **The prefork `verify_process_alive` timers**, plus any timers that custom bootsteps register on `worker.timer`.\n\nThe comment above the call says \"Timers are re-registered by `register_with_event_loop`\". That is true for the consumer/transport timers that #10218 targeted (e.g. `maybe_restore_messages`), but not for the pool's per-job timers.\n\n### Impact\n\n- Every task that is running when a broker connection error occurs (Redis restart, RabbitMQ failover, network blip, heartbeat miss) silently loses both its soft and hard time limit.\n- Hung tasks are never killed. They hold pool slots indefinitely, and a few reconnects can starve the worker.\n- It is a silent regression in the 5.7 pre-releases. Nothing is logged, and users rely on `time_limit` as a safety net.\n\n### Suggested fix\n\nEither of these would work. I'm happy to send a PR.\n\n1. **Cancel only the consumer's own timers instead of clearing the shared timer.** Record the timer entries created during `consumer.register_with_event_loop` / `connection.transport.register_with_event_loop` (the entries #10218 was concerned with) and cancel just those on the error path.\n2. **Re-arm pool time limits on re-registration.** In `AsynPool.register_with_event_loop`, for every job in `self._cache` that has been accepted, re-arm the soft and hard timers with the remaining time (`limit - (now - job._time_accepted)`).\n\nAdd a regression test asserting that pool time-limit entries survive `asynloop` raising a connection error.\n\n", "hints_text": "so it is also reproduces on celery v5.7.0rc1 too?\nYes, it reproduces on v5.7.0rc1. I re-ran the repro from the issue against the released packages from PyPI, each in a clean virtualenv (Python 3.12.13):\n\n| Celery                    | `hub.timer.clear()` in `asynloop`  | Hard time limit fires after reconnect |\n|---------------------------|------------------------------------|---------------------------------------|\n| 5.6.2 (kombu 5.6.2)       | no                                 |yes                                |\n| 5.6.3                     | yes (backport a989e8cf8 of #10218) |  no                                 |\n| 5.7.0rc1 (kombu 5.7.0rc1) | yes (c38600d42)                    |  no                                 |\n| `main` @ 0731c053b        | yes                                |  no                                 |\n\nOn 5.7.0rc1:\n\n```\ntimer entries before reconnect: 1\nasynloop raised: connection reset by broker -> consumer will reconnect\ntimer entries after reconnect: 0\nhard time limit fired? NO - task can run forever\n```\n\nOn 5.6.2, before the change:\n\n```\ntimer entries before reconnect: 1\nasynloop raised: connection reset by broker -> consumer will reconnect\ntimer entries after reconnect: 1\nhard time limit fired? ['HARD TIMEOUT -> kill child']\n```\n\nOne correction to my original report: I said 5.6.x wasn't affected, but #10218 was backported, so **5.6.3 is affected as well**. 5.6.2 is the last good release.\n\nI'd be happy to open a PR before 5.7.0 final. My plan is to cancel only the timer entries the consumer/transport registers (the `maybe_restore_messages` entries #10218 was fixing) instead of clearing the shared worker timer. I'd add a regression test that pool time-limit entries survive an `asynloop` connection error, so the #10205 fix keeps working. Does that approach sound good to you?\nPR welcome", "created_at": "2026-10-20T08:46:12Z"}
{"repo": "celery/celery", "pull_number": 10896, "instance_id": "celery__celery-10896", "issue_numbers": ["10893"], "base_commit": "3611b8f0523175d809a7d2e6c132d8c6c7e1c0c4", "patch": "diff --git a/celery/beat.py b/celery/beat.py\nindex b3514b7003c..4f7c8aaebf1 100644\n--- a/celery/beat.py\n+++ b/celery/beat.py\n@@ -303,6 +303,14 @@ def adjust(self, n, drift=-0.010):\n     def is_due(self, entry):\n         return entry.is_due()\n \n+    def _is_due_failed(self, entry):\n+        # One entry whose next run can't be calculated must not stop beat\n+        # (and every other entry with it), so log it and try again later.\n+        error('beat: Cannot calculate the next run of %r, '\n+              'trying again in %s.', entry.name,\n+              humanize_seconds(self.max_interval), exc_info=True)\n+        return False, self.max_interval\n+\n     def _when(self, entry, next_time_to_run, mktime=timegm):\n         \"\"\"Return a utc timestamp, make sure heapq in correct order.\"\"\"\n         adjust = self.adjust\n@@ -318,7 +326,10 @@ def populate_heap(self, event_t=event_t, heapify=heapq.heapify):\n         priority = 5\n         self._heap = []\n         for entry in self.schedule.values():\n-            is_due, next_call_delay = entry.is_due()\n+            try:\n+                is_due, next_call_delay = entry.is_due()\n+            except Exception:  # pylint: disable=broad-except\n+                is_due, next_call_delay = self._is_due_failed(entry)\n             self._heap.append(event_t(\n                 self._when(\n                     entry,\n@@ -357,7 +368,10 @@ def tick(self, event_t=event_t, min=min, heappop=heapq.heappop,\n         if event[0] > now:\n             return min(event[0] - now, max_interval)\n \n-        is_due, next_time_to_run = self.is_due(entry)\n+        try:\n+            is_due, next_time_to_run = self.is_due(entry)\n+        except Exception:  # pylint: disable=broad-except\n+            is_due, next_time_to_run = self._is_due_failed(entry)\n         if is_due:\n             verify = heappop(H)\n             if verify is event:\n@@ -617,8 +631,13 @@ def _create_schedule(self):\n         for _ in (1, 2):\n             try:\n                 self._store['entries']\n-            except (KeyError, UnicodeDecodeError, TypeError, UnpicklingError):\n-                # new schedule db\n+            except (KeyError, UnicodeDecodeError, TypeError, UnpicklingError,\n+                    ValueError) as exc:\n+                # new schedule db, or the stored entries can't be loaded\n+                # anymore (e.g. a crontab that is now invalid).\n+                if isinstance(exc, ValueError):\n+                    warning('DB Reset: Cannot load the stored entries, '\n+                            'recreating them from beat_schedule: %r', exc)\n                 try:\n                     self._store['entries'] = {}\n                 except (KeyError, UnicodeDecodeError, TypeError, UnpicklingError) + dbm.error as exc:\ndiff --git a/celery/schedules.py b/celery/schedules.py\nindex 3f8d59b63db..a5fd3492231 100644\n--- a/celery/schedules.py\n+++ b/celery/schedules.py\n@@ -34,6 +34,17 @@\n int, str, or an iterable type. {type!r} was given.\\\n \"\"\"\n \n+CRON_INVALID_DATE = \"\"\"\\\n+Invalid crontab: day_of_month {day_of_month!r} never occurs in \\\n+month_of_year {month_of_year!r}.\\\n+\"\"\"\n+\n+# Number of days in each month, using the longest February (leap years).\n+CRON_DAYS_IN_MONTH = {\n+    1: 31, 2: 29, 3: 31, 4: 30, 5: 31, 6: 30,\n+    7: 31, 8: 31, 9: 30, 10: 31, 11: 30, 12: 31,\n+}\n+\n CRON_REPR = \"\"\"\\\n <crontab: {0._orig_minute} {0._orig_hour} {0._orig_day_of_month} {0._orig_month_of_year} \\\n {0._orig_day_of_week} (m/h/dM/MY/d)>\\\n@@ -416,6 +427,9 @@ class crontab(BaseSchedule):\n     execution events.  Or, ``day_of_week`` is 1 and ``day_of_month``\n     is '1-7,15-21' means every first and third Monday of every month\n     present in ``month_of_year``.\n+\n+    :exc:`ValueError` is raised if none of the days in ``day_of_month``\n+    occur in any month in ``month_of_year`` (e.g., the 30th of February).\n     \"\"\"\n \n     def __init__(self, minute: Cronspec = '*', hour: Cronspec = '*', day_of_week: Cronspec = '*',\n@@ -431,6 +445,13 @@ def __init__(self, minute: Cronspec = '*', hour: Cronspec = '*', day_of_week: Cr\n         self.day_of_week = self._expand_cronspec(day_of_week, 7)\n         self.day_of_month = self._expand_cronspec(day_of_month, 31, 1)\n         self.month_of_year = self._expand_cronspec(month_of_year, 12, 1)\n+        if not any(day <= CRON_DAYS_IN_MONTH[month]\n+                   for month in self.month_of_year\n+                   for day in self.day_of_month):\n+            raise ValueError(CRON_INVALID_DATE.format(\n+                day_of_month=self._orig_day_of_month,\n+                month_of_year=self._orig_month_of_year,\n+            ))\n         super().__init__(**kwargs)\n \n     @classmethod\n@@ -803,6 +824,10 @@ class solar(BaseSchedule):\n         'dusk_astronomical': True,\n     }\n \n+    #: How many days ahead to search for an event that doesn't happen\n+    #: every day (see :meth:`_next_event`).\n+    max_search_days = 366\n+\n     def __init__(self, event: str, lat: int | float, lon: int | float, **\n                  kwargs: Any) -> None:\n         try:\n@@ -842,6 +867,31 @@ def __repr__(self) -> str:\n             self.event, self.lat, self.lon,\n         )\n \n+    def _next_event(self, start: Any) -> Any:\n+        \"\"\"Return the first occurrence of the event after ``start``.\n+\n+        At high latitudes some events don't happen every day, e.g.\n+        astronomical dawn in summer or sunrise during the polar night,\n+        and :mod:`ephem` raises :exc:`~ephem.CircumpolarError` for them.\n+        In that case the search continues from the next anti-transit\n+        (one day later), for at most :attr:`max_search_days` days.\n+\n+        Returns:\n+            ephem.Date: the time of the event, or, if it doesn't happen\n+                within :attr:`max_search_days` days, the time at which\n+                the search stopped, so the schedule is checked again then.\n+        \"\"\"\n+        sun = self.ephem.Sun()\n+        kwargs = {'use_center': True} if self.use_center else {}\n+        for _ in range(self.max_search_days):\n+            try:\n+                return getattr(self.cal, self.method)(\n+                    sun, start=start, **kwargs\n+                )\n+            except self.ephem.CircumpolarError:\n+                start = self.cal.next_antitransit(sun, start=start)\n+        return start\n+\n     def remaining_estimate(self, last_run_at: datetime) -> timedelta:\n         \"\"\"Return estimate of next time to run.\n \n@@ -854,24 +904,7 @@ def remaining_estimate(self, last_run_at: datetime) -> timedelta:\n         last_run_at = self.maybe_make_aware(last_run_at)\n         last_run_at_utc = localize(last_run_at, timezone.utc)\n         self.cal.date = last_run_at_utc\n-        try:\n-            if self.use_center:\n-                next_utc = getattr(self.cal, self.method)(\n-                    self.ephem.Sun(),\n-                    start=last_run_at_utc, use_center=self.use_center\n-                )\n-            else:\n-                next_utc = getattr(self.cal, self.method)(\n-                    self.ephem.Sun(), start=last_run_at_utc\n-                )\n-\n-        except self.ephem.CircumpolarError:  # pragma: no cover\n-            # Sun won't rise/set today.  Check again tomorrow\n-            # (specifically, after the next anti-transit).\n-            next_utc = (\n-                self.cal.next_antitransit(self.ephem.Sun()) +\n-                timedelta(minutes=1)\n-            )\n+        next_utc = self._next_event(self.ephem.Date(last_run_at_utc))\n         next = self.maybe_make_aware(next_utc.datetime())\n         now = self.maybe_make_aware(self.now())\n         delta = next - now\ndiff --git a/docs/userguide/periodic-tasks.rst b/docs/userguide/periodic-tasks.rst\nindex 0fa389d1b51..83d6ada5e7a 100644\n--- a/docs/userguide/periodic-tasks.rst\n+++ b/docs/userguide/periodic-tasks.rst\n@@ -356,6 +356,13 @@ Some examples:\n \n See :class:`celery.schedules.crontab` for more documentation.\n \n+.. versionchanged:: 5.7\n+\n+    A crontab whose ``day_of_month`` never occurs in its ``month_of_year``\n+    (e.g., ``crontab(0, 0, day_of_month='30', month_of_year='2')``) now raises\n+    :exc:`ValueError` when created. Previously it was accepted, and made\n+    :program:`celery beat` exit when it tried to calculate the next run.\n+\n .. _beat-solar:\n \n Solar schedules\n@@ -453,12 +460,20 @@ Possible event types are:\n All solar events are calculated using UTC, and are therefore\n unaffected by your timezone setting.\n \n-In polar regions, the sun may not rise or set every day. The scheduler\n+In polar regions, the sun may not rise or set every day, and at high\n+latitudes some twilight events don't happen for weeks around the summer\n+solstice (e.g., astronomical dawn in London or Berlin). The scheduler\n is able to handle these cases (i.e., a ``sunrise`` event won't run on a day\n-when the sun doesn't rise). The one exception is ``solar_noon``, which is\n+when the sun doesn't rise, and will next run on the first day it does).\n+The one exception is ``solar_noon``, which is\n formally defined as the moment the sun transits the celestial meridian,\n and will occur every day even if the sun is below the horizon.\n \n+.. versionchanged:: 5.7\n+\n+    Previously, a solar event that didn't happen on a given day raised\n+    :exc:`TypeError` and made :program:`celery beat` exit.\n+\n Twilight is defined as the period between dawn and sunrise; and between\n sunset and dusk. You can schedule an event according to \"twilight\"\n depending on your definition of twilight (civil, nautical, or astronomical),\n@@ -660,6 +675,20 @@ The default scheduler is the :class:`celery.beat.PersistentScheduler`,\n that simply keeps track of the last run times in a local :mod:`shelve`\n database file.\n \n+.. versionchanged:: 5.7\n+\n+    If an entry's ``is_due()`` raises an exception, :program:`celery beat`\n+    now logs the error with its traceback and tries that entry again after\n+    ``max_interval``, while the other entries keep running. Previously the\n+    exception made :program:`celery beat` exit. This also applies to\n+    custom schedulers and schedule classes, e.g. one whose ``is_due()``\n+    reads from a database that is temporarily unavailable.\n+\n+    If the schedule database contains entries that can no longer be loaded\n+    (e.g. a crontab for the 30th of February, which is now rejected), a\n+    warning is logged and the stored entries are recreated from\n+    :setting:`beat_schedule`. The last run times of all entries are lost.\n+\n There's also the :pypi:`django-celery-beat` extension that stores the schedule\n in the Django database, and presents a convenient admin interface to manage\n periodic tasks at runtime.\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/t/integration/test_beat.py b/t/integration/test_beat.py\nindex ead4692365f..09c50735e27 100644\n--- a/t/integration/test_beat.py\n+++ b/t/integration/test_beat.py\n@@ -284,6 +284,57 @@ def mutating_first_entry_is_due(_last_run_at):\n         assert scheduler.schedule['first'].total_run_count == 0\n \n \n+class test_beat_failing_entry:\n+    @flaky\n+    @pytest.mark.usefixtures('celery_session_worker')\n+    def test_dispatches_other_entries_when_is_due_raises(self, app):\n+        healthy_task_id = uuid4().hex\n+        last_run = datetime(2022, 12, 5, 10, 20)\n+        scheduler = beat.Scheduler(app=app, lazy=True)\n+        failing = scheduler.add(\n+            name='failing',\n+            task=add.name,\n+            args=(1, 2),\n+            schedule=timedelta(seconds=1),\n+            last_run_at=last_run,\n+        )\n+        healthy = scheduler.add(\n+            name='healthy',\n+            task=add.name,\n+            args=(3, 4),\n+            schedule=timedelta(seconds=1),\n+            last_run_at=last_run,\n+            options={'task_id': healthy_task_id},\n+        )\n+\n+        def failing_is_due(_last_run_at):\n+            # e.g. a schedule whose next run can't be calculated\n+            raise TypeError('cannot calculate the next run')\n+\n+        failing.schedule.is_due = failing_is_due\n+\n+        # populate_heap() doesn't raise, and moves failing max_interval ahead\n+        scheduler.populate_heap()\n+        heap = {event[2].name: event[0] for event in scheduler._heap}\n+        now = scheduler._when(failing, 0)\n+        assert heap['failing'] > now + scheduler.max_interval - 1\n+        assert heap['healthy'] <= now\n+\n+        # so populate_heap() doesn't run again and override our setup\n+        scheduler.old_schedulers = scheduler.schedule\n+        scheduler._heap = [\n+            beat.event_t(now - 2, 5, failing),\n+            beat.event_t(now - 1, 5, healthy),\n+        ]\n+        # tick() doesn't raise either: failing is moved back, and healthy is\n+        # now the top, so tick() asks to be called again right away\n+        assert scheduler.tick() == 0\n+        # tick() returns 0 only when it dispatches a due task\n+        assert scheduler.tick() == 0\n+        assert app.AsyncResult(healthy_task_id).get(timeout=30) == 7\n+        assert scheduler.schedule['failing'].total_run_count == 0\n+\n+\n class test_beat_remote_control:\n     \"\"\"A running beat must answer while it ticks, and stop when it wedges.\n \ndiff --git a/t/unit/app/test_beat.py b/t/unit/app/test_beat.py\nindex 339426db3ba..08b68e21e88 100644\n--- a/t/unit/app/test_beat.py\n+++ b/t/unit/app/test_beat.py\n@@ -177,6 +177,15 @@ def is_due(self, last_run_at):\n         return self._is_due, self._next_run_at\n \n \n+class raising_schedule(mocked_schedule):\n+\n+    def __init__(self):\n+        super().__init__(False, 1)\n+\n+    def is_due(self, last_run_at):\n+        raise RuntimeError('cannot calculate next run')\n+\n+\n always_due = mocked_schedule(True, 1)\n always_pending = mocked_schedule(False, 1)\n always_pending_left_10_milliseconds = mocked_schedule(False, 0.01)\n@@ -406,6 +415,35 @@ def test_due_tick_SchedulingError(self, error):\n         assert scheduler.tick() == 0\n         error.assert_called()\n \n+    @patch('celery.beat.error')\n+    def test_tick_survives_entry_failing_in_populate_heap(self, error):\n+        scheduler = mScheduler(app=self.app)\n+        scheduler.add(name='broken', task='c.broken',\n+                      schedule=raising_schedule())\n+        scheduler.add(name='healthy', task='c.healthy', schedule=always_due)\n+        assert scheduler.tick() == 0\n+        assert [s['name'] for s in scheduler.sent] == ['c.healthy']\n+        error.assert_called()\n+        assert 'broken' in error.call_args[0]\n+        broken_event = next(\n+            e for e in scheduler._heap if e[2].name == 'broken')\n+        assert broken_event[0] == pytest.approx(scheduler._when(\n+            broken_event[2], scheduler.max_interval), abs=1)\n+\n+    @patch('celery.beat.error')\n+    def test_tick_survives_entry_failing_when_due(self, error):\n+        scheduler = mScheduler(app=self.app)\n+        broken = scheduler.add(name='broken', task='c.broken',\n+                               schedule=raising_schedule())\n+        # so populate_heap() doesn't run and override our setup\n+        scheduler.old_schedulers = scheduler.schedule\n+        scheduler._heap = [event_t(scheduler._when(broken, 0) - 1, 5, broken)]\n+        assert scheduler.tick() == scheduler.adjust(scheduler.max_interval)\n+        assert not scheduler.sent\n+        error.assert_called()\n+        assert scheduler._heap[0][0] == pytest.approx(\n+            scheduler._when(broken, scheduler.max_interval), abs=1)\n+\n     def test_pending_tick(self):\n         scheduler = mScheduler(app=self.app)\n         scheduler.add(name='test_pending_tick',\n@@ -1146,6 +1184,39 @@ def test_create_schedule_missing_entries(self):\n         s._create_schedule()\n         s._store.__setitem__.assert_called_with(\"entries\", {})\n \n+    def test_create_schedule_invalid_stored_crontab(self):\n+        \"\"\"\n+        Test that entries stored by an older version, containing a crontab\n+        that is now rejected (e.g. the 30th of February), are recreated\n+        \"\"\"\n+        stored = crontab(day_of_month=28, month_of_year=2)\n+        stored._orig_day_of_month = '30'\n+        pickled_entries = pickle.dumps({'invalid': stored})\n+        s = create_persistent_scheduler()[0](app=self.app, schedule_filename=\"schedule\")\n+        s._store = MagicMock()\n+\n+        # self._store['entries'] will throw a ValueError when unpickled\n+        s._store.__getitem__.side_effect = lambda key: pickle.loads(pickled_entries)\n+\n+        with patch('celery.beat.warning') as warning:\n+            s._create_schedule()\n+        s._store.__setitem__.assert_called_with(\"entries\", {})\n+        warning.assert_called_once()\n+        assert isinstance(warning.call_args[0][1], ValueError)\n+\n+    def test_create_schedule_missing_entries_not_logged(self):\n+        \"\"\"\n+        Test that creating the entries of a new schedule db doesn't log a warning\n+        \"\"\"\n+        s = create_persistent_scheduler()[0](app=self.app, schedule_filename=\"schedule\")\n+        s._store = MagicMock()\n+        s._store.__getitem__.side_effect = KeyError('entries')\n+\n+        with patch('celery.beat.warning') as warning:\n+            s._create_schedule()\n+        s._store.__setitem__.assert_called_with(\"entries\", {})\n+        warning.assert_not_called()\n+\n     def test_setup_schedule(self):\n         s = create_persistent_scheduler()[0](app=self.app,\n                                              schedule_filename='schedule')\ndiff --git a/t/unit/app/test_schedules.py b/t/unit/app/test_schedules.py\nindex 0bd952fea67..faa0ab427b5 100644\n--- a/t/unit/app/test_schedules.py\n+++ b/t/unit/app/test_schedules.py\n@@ -100,6 +100,50 @@ def test_event_uses_center(self):\n                     f\"{s.method} was called with 'use_center' which is not a \"\n                     \"valid keyword for the function.\")\n \n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize('event,lat,lon,last_run_at,expected', [\n+        # Astronomical twilight lasts all night in London around the\n+        # summer solstice, so there is no astronomical dawn.\n+        ('dawn_astronomical', 51.5074, -0.1278,\n+         datetime(2026, 6, 15, tzinfo=timezone.utc),\n+         datetime(2026, 7, 22, 0, 30, tzinfo=timezone.utc)),\n+        # Same for nautical dawn in Stockholm.\n+        ('dawn_nautical', 59.3293, 18.0686,\n+         datetime(2026, 6, 15, tzinfo=timezone.utc),\n+         datetime(2026, 7, 29, 23, 14, tzinfo=timezone.utc)),\n+        # Polar night in Troms\u00f8: the sun doesn't rise until mid-January.\n+        ('sunrise', 69.6492, 18.9553,\n+         datetime(2026, 12, 15, tzinfo=timezone.utc),\n+         datetime(2027, 1, 15, 10, 34, tzinfo=timezone.utc)),\n+    ])\n+    def test_remaining_estimate_skips_days_without_event(\n+            self, event, lat, lon, last_run_at, expected):\n+        s = solar(event, lat, lon, app=self.app, nowfun=lambda: last_run_at)\n+        next_run = last_run_at + s.remaining_estimate(last_run_at)\n+        assert expected <= next_run < expected + timedelta(minutes=1)\n+\n+    def test_is_due_not_due_on_days_without_event(self):\n+        # Before the fix, the entry was due every night in the summer.\n+        s = solar('dawn_astronomical', 51.5074, -0.1278, app=self.app,\n+                  nowfun=lambda: datetime(2026, 6, 16, 3,\n+                                          tzinfo=timezone.utc))\n+        state = s.is_due(datetime(2026, 6, 15, 2, tzinfo=timezone.utc))\n+        assert not state.is_due\n+        assert state.next > timedelta(days=30).total_seconds()\n+\n+    def test_remaining_estimate_event_never_happens(self):\n+        last_run_at = datetime(2026, 6, 15, tzinfo=timezone.utc)\n+        s = solar('dawn_astronomical', 51.5074, -0.1278, app=self.app,\n+                  nowfun=lambda: last_run_at)\n+        s.max_search_days = 3\n+        s.cal = Mock(name='cal')\n+        s.cal.next_rising.side_effect = s.ephem.CircumpolarError()\n+        s.cal.next_antitransit.side_effect = (\n+            lambda body, start: s.ephem.Date(start + 1)\n+        )\n+        rem = s.remaining_estimate(last_run_at)\n+        assert s.cal.next_rising.call_count == 3\n+        assert rem == timedelta(days=3)\n+\n \n class test_solar_without_ephem:\n \n@@ -847,13 +891,17 @@ def test_not_weekmonthdayyear(self):\n         assert next == datetime(2010, 5, 29, 0, 5)\n \n     def test_invalid_specification(self):\n+        with pytest.raises(ValueError):\n+            self.crontab(day_of_month=31, month_of_year=4)\n+\n+    def test_rollover_gives_up_on_invalid_date(self):\n         # *** WARNING ***\n         # This test triggers an infinite loop in case of a regression\n+        c = self.crontab(day_of_month=30, month_of_year=4)\n+        # bypass the validation done by the constructor\n+        c.day_of_month = {31}\n         with pytest.raises(RuntimeError):\n-            self.next_occurrence(\n-                self.crontab(day_of_month=31, month_of_year=4),\n-                datetime(2010, 1, 28, 14, 30, 15),\n-            )\n+            self.next_occurrence(c, datetime(2010, 1, 28, 14, 30, 15))\n \n     def test_leapyear(self):\n         next = self.next_occurrence(\n@@ -1098,6 +1146,28 @@ def test_crontab_spec_invalid_moy(self, month_of_year):\n         with pytest.raises(ValueError):\n             self.crontab(month_of_year=month_of_year)\n \n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize('day_of_month,month_of_year', [\n+        (30, 2),\n+        ('30,31', 'feb'),\n+        (31, 4),\n+        (31, '4,6,9,11'),\n+        ([], '*'),\n+        ('*', []),\n+    ])\n+    def test_crontab_spec_date_never_occurs(self, day_of_month, month_of_year):\n+        with pytest.raises(ValueError, match='never occurs'):\n+            self.crontab(day_of_month=day_of_month,\n+                         month_of_year=month_of_year)\n+\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize('day_of_month,month_of_year', [\n+        (29, 2),\n+        ('30,31', '2,3'),\n+        (31, '4-5'),\n+        (31, '*'),\n+    ])\n+    def test_crontab_spec_date_occurs(self, day_of_month, month_of_year):\n+        self.crontab(day_of_month=day_of_month, month_of_year=month_of_year)\n+\n     def seconds_almost_equal(self, a, b, precision):\n         for index, skew in enumerate((+1, -1, 0)):\n             try:\n", "problem_statement": "celery beat exits when a single schedule entry fails: solar CircumpolarError raises TypeError, impossible crontab raises RuntimeError\n<!--\nPlease fill this template entirely and do not erase parts of it.\nWe reserve the right to close without a response\nbug reports which are incomplete.\n-->\n# Checklist\n- [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `main` branch of Celery.\n- [ ] This has already been asked to the [discussions forum](https://github.com/celery/celery/discussions) first.\n- [x] I have read the relevant section in the\n  [contribution guide](https://docs.celeryq.dev/en/main/contributing.html#other-bugs)\n  on reporting bugs.\n- [x] I have checked the [issues list](https://github.com/celery/celery/issues?q=is%3Aissue+label%3A%22Issue+Type%3A+Bug+Report%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)\n  for similar or identical bug reports.\n- [x] I have checked the [pull requests list](https://github.com/celery/celery/pulls?q=is%3Apr+label%3A%22PR+Type%3A+Bugfix%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)\n  for existing proposed fixes.\n- [x] I have checked the [commit log](https://github.com/celery/celery/commits/main)\n  to find out if the bug was already fixed in the main branch.\n- [x] I have included all related issues and possible duplicate issues\n  in this issue (If there are none, check this box anyway).\n- [ ] I have tried to reproduce the issue with [pytest-celery](https://docs.celeryq.dev/projects/pytest-celery/en/latest/userguide/celery-bug-report.html) and added the reproduction script below.\n\n## Mandatory Debugging Information\n\n- [x] I have included the output of ``celery -A proj report`` in the issue.\n- [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `main` branch of Celery.\n- [x] I have included the contents of ``pip freeze`` in the issue.\n- [x] I have included all the versions of all the external dependencies required\n  to reproduce this bug.\n\n## Optional Debugging Information\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one Python version\n  and/or implementation.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one message broker and/or\n  result backend.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one version of the message\n  broker and/or result backend.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one operating system.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one workers pool.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue with autoscaling, retries,\n  ETA/Countdown & rate limits disabled.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue after downgrading\n  and/or upgrading Celery and its dependencies.\n\n## Related Issues and Possible Duplicates\n\n#### Related Issues\n\n- #9514: reported the same `TypeError` in the `CircumpolarError` branch of `solar`. It was closed as not planned because the template wasn't followed and the use case wasn't explained. This report fills both gaps (full template, real locations and dates, and the effect on a running `celery beat`). The code is unchanged on `main`.\n- #10101: a different `solar` bug (wrong dusk elevation)\n\n#### Possible Duplicates\n\n- None. I searched for \"CircumpolarError\", \"AlwaysUpError\", \"NeverUpError\", \"unable to rollover\", \"solar ephem.Date\" and \"beat crash invalid schedule\".\n\n## Environment & Settings\n\n**Celery version**: 5.7.0rc1 (collider), `main` @ 0731c053b\n\n<details>\n<summary><b><code>celery report</code> Output:</b></summary>\n<p>\n\n```\n\nsoftware -> celery:5.7.0rc1 (collider) kombu:5.7.0rc1 py:3.12.13\n            billiard:4.3.1 memory:N/A\nplatform -> system:Linux arch:64bit, ELF\n            kernel version:6.8.0-138-generic imp:CPython\nloader   -> celery.loaders.app.AppLoader\nsettings -> transport:memory results:disabled\n\nbroker_url: 'memory://:********@localhost//'\ndeprecated_settings: None\nbeat_schedule:\n    'bad-entry': {   'schedule': <crontab: 0 0 30 2 * (m/h/dM/MY/d)>,\n                     'task': 'beatapp.ping'},\n    'healthy-every-10s': {'schedule': 10.0, 'task': 'beatapp.ping'}}\n\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n# Steps to Reproduce\n\n## Required Dependencies\n\n- **Minimal Python Version**: 3.12.13 (tested)\n- **Minimal Celery Version**: 5.7.0rc1 (tested). The affected code has not changed in a long time; see #9514 (2025).\n- **Minimal Kombu Version**: N/A (reproduces with the `memory://` transport)\n- **Minimal Broker Version**: N/A\n- **Minimal Result Backend Version**: N/A\n- **Minimal OS and/or Kernel Version**: Linux 6.8 (tested)\n- **Minimal Broker Client Version**: N/A\n- **Minimal Result Backend Client Version**: N/A\n\n### Python Packages\n\n<details>\n<summary><b><code>pip freeze</code> Output:</b></summary>\n<p>\n\n```\n-e git+https://github.com/celery/celery@0731c053b#egg=celery  # 5.7.0rc1\namqp==5.4.1\nbilliard==4.3.1\nclick==8.5.0\nclick-didyoumean==0.3.1\nclick-plugins==1.1.1.2\nclick-repl==0.4.1\nephem==4.2.1\nkombu==5.7.0rc1\npackaging==26.3\nprompt-toolkit==3.0.53\npython-dateutil==2.9.0.post0\nsix==1.17.0\ntzdata==2026.4\ntzlocal==5.4.4\nvine==5.1.0\nwcwidth==0.9.2\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n### Other Dependencies\n\n<details>\n<p>\n\n`ephem==4.2.1` is required for `solar` schedules.\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n## Minimally Reproducible Test Case\n\nTwo triggers, one root problem: if **any single** schedule entry raises in `is_due()`, the whole `celery beat` process exits, and **every** periodic task stops.\n\n<details>\n<p>\n\n**1. Each trigger raises inside `Scheduler.tick()`**, even with a healthy entry next to it:\n\n```python\nfrom datetime import datetime, timezone\nfrom unittest.mock import patch\n\nfrom celery import Celery\nfrom celery.beat import Scheduler\nfrom celery.schedules import crontab, solar\n\ncases = {\n    'solar: astronomical dawn, London, mid-June': (\n        solar('dawn_astronomical', 51.5074, -0.1278),\n        datetime(2026, 6, 15, 12, tzinfo=timezone.utc)),\n    'solar: nautical dusk, Stockholm, mid-June': (\n        solar('dusk_nautical', 59.3293, 18.0686),\n        datetime(2026, 6, 15, 12, tzinfo=timezone.utc)),\n    'solar: sunrise, Tromso, mid-December': (\n        solar('sunrise', 69.6492, 18.9553),\n        datetime(2026, 12, 15, 12, tzinfo=timezone.utc)),\n    'crontab: February 30': (\n        crontab(minute=0, hour=0, day_of_month=30, month_of_year=2),\n        datetime(2026, 1, 15, 12, tzinfo=timezone.utc)),\n    'crontab: 31st of Apr/Jun/Sep/Nov': (\n        crontab(minute=0, hour=0, day_of_month=31, month_of_year='4,6,9,11'),\n        datetime(2026, 1, 15, 12, tzinfo=timezone.utc)),\n}\n\nfor name, (schedule, now) in cases.items():\n    app = Celery(set_as_current=False, broker='memory://')\n    app.conf.beat_schedule = {\n        'healthy-every-minute': {'task': 'x', 'schedule': crontab()},\n        'bad-entry': {'task': 'y', 'schedule': schedule},\n    }\n    with patch.object(Celery, 'now', lambda self, now=now: now):\n        try:\n            Scheduler(app).tick()\n            result = 'ok'\n        except Exception as exc:\n            result = f'{type(exc).__name__}: {exc}'\n    print(f'{name:45} -> {result}')\n```\n\n**2. A real `celery beat` process exits** (`beatapp.py`). The solar entry fails *today* (October), with no clock patching:\n\n```python\nimport os\n\nfrom celery import Celery\nfrom celery.schedules import crontab, solar\n\napp = Celery('beatapp', broker='memory://')\n\nbad = {\n    'solar': solar('dawn_astronomical', -62.2, -58.9),  # King George Island, Antarctica\n    'crontab': crontab(minute=0, hour=0, day_of_month=30, month_of_year=2),\n}[os.environ['BAD']]\n\napp.conf.beat_schedule = {\n    'healthy-every-10s': {'task': 'beatapp.ping', 'schedule': 10.0},\n    'bad-entry': {'task': 'beatapp.ping', 'schedule': bad},\n}\n\n\n@app.task\ndef ping():\n    return 'pong'\n```\n\n```console\n$ BAD=solar   celery -A beatapp beat -S celery.beat:Scheduler -l info; echo \"exit code: $?\"\n$ BAD=crontab celery -A beatapp beat -S celery.beat:Scheduler -l info; echo \"exit code: $?\"\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n# Expected Behavior\n\n- One entry whose next run can't be computed should be logged and skipped (or retried later). The other periodic tasks should keep running.\n- `solar`: when the event doesn't happen on a given day (polar day/night, or astronomical/nautical twilight that never ends around the summer solstice), the entry should be **not due**. Beat should look for the next day on which the event actually happens.\n- `crontab`: a spec that can never match (Feb 30, Apr 31, \u2026) should be rejected with a `ValueError` when the `crontab` is created, not crash beat at runtime.\n\n# Actual Behavior\n\nOutput of script 1:\n\n```\nsolar: astronomical dawn, London, mid-June    -> TypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'ephem.Date' and 'datetime.timedelta'\nsolar: nautical dusk, Stockholm, mid-June     -> TypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'ephem.Date' and 'datetime.timedelta'\nsolar: sunrise, Tromso, mid-December          -> TypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'ephem.Date' and 'datetime.timedelta'\ncrontab: February 30                          -> RuntimeError: unable to rollover, time specification is probably invalid\ncrontab: 31st of Apr/Jun/Sep/Nov              -> RuntimeError: unable to rollover, time specification is probably invalid\n```\n\nWith script 2, `celery beat` exits with **exit code 1** at startup in both cases, and **no task is ever sent, including the healthy 10s entry**:\n\n```\nbeat: Starting...\nbeat raised exception <class 'TypeError'>: TypeError(\"unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'ephem.Date' and 'datetime.timedelta'\")\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"celery/schedules.py\", line 859, in remaining_estimate\n    next_utc = getattr(self.cal, self.method)(\n  File \"ephem/__init__.py\", line 518, in _find_rise_or_set\n    raise AlwaysUpError('%r is above the horizon at %s'\nephem.AlwaysUpError: 'Sun' is above the horizon at 2026/10/19 03:40:38\n\nDuring handling of the above exception, another exception occurred:\n\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"celery/apps/beat.py\", line 119, in start_scheduler\n    service.start()\n  File \"celery/beat.py\", line 1045, in start\n    interval = self.scheduler.tick()\n  File \"celery/beat.py\", line 347, in tick\n    self.populate_heap()\n  File \"celery/beat.py\", line 321, in populate_heap\n    is_due, next_call_delay = entry.is_due()\n  File \"celery/beat.py\", line 169, in is_due\n    return self.schedule.is_due(self.last_run_at)\n  File \"celery/schedules.py\", line 889, in is_due\n    rem_delta = self.remaining_estimate(last_run_at)\n  File \"celery/schedules.py\", line 872, in remaining_estimate\n    self.cal.next_antitransit(self.ephem.Sun()) +\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'ephem.Date' and 'datetime.timedelta'\n```\n\nThe crontab case is the same, ending in `RuntimeError: unable to rollover, time specification is probably invalid`.\n\n### Root cause\n\n1. **`solar`**: the `CircumpolarError` branch can never succeed ([`celery/schedules.py` L868-L874](https://github.com/celery/celery/blob/0731c053b0e09f231363ec62988fded6c9846878/celery/schedules.py#L868-L874)):\n   ```python\n   except self.ephem.CircumpolarError:  # pragma: no cover\n       next_utc = (\n           self.cal.next_antitransit(self.ephem.Sun()) +\n           timedelta(minutes=1)\n       )\n   ```\n   `ephem.Date` is a float (days), so adding a `timedelta` raises `TypeError`. The branch is excluded from coverage, so no test exercises it.\n\n   This is not just a polar edge case. Astronomical/nautical twilight doesn't end for weeks around the summer solstice at roughly 49\u201360\u00b0 latitude (London, Paris, Berlin, Amsterdam, Stockholm \u2026), so a `dawn_astronomical` entry takes beat down every summer in much of Europe.\n\n   **The obvious one-line fix is not enough.** If you only change it to `ephem.Date(... + ephem.minute)`, the crash goes away, but the returned time is \"1 minute after the next antitransit\", which is *not* an occurrence of the event. Since the schedule always searches from `last_run_at`, the entry becomes due **every night**. With that one-line fix, a `solar('dawn_astronomical', 51.5074, -0.1278)` entry is due at 2026-06-15 02:00 UTC and again at 2026-06-16 02:00 UTC, although astronomical dawn never happens in London on those days.\n\n2. **`crontab`**: `_expand_cronspec` validates each field on its own ([`celery/schedules.py` L482-L498](https://github.com/celery/celery/blob/0731c053b0e09f231363ec62988fded6c9846878/celery/schedules.py#L482-L498)). `crontab(day_of_month=30, month_of_year=2)` is therefore accepted, and only fails later in `roll_over()` with `RuntimeError('unable to rollover, ...')`.\n\n3. **No fault isolation in beat**: `Scheduler.populate_heap()` ([L321](https://github.com/celery/celery/blob/0731c053b0e09f231363ec62988fded6c9846878/celery/beat.py#L321)) and `tick()` call `entry.is_due()` for every entry with no error handling. `Service.start()` ([L1045-L1053](https://github.com/celery/celery/blob/0731c053b0e09f231363ec62988fded6c9846878/celery/beat.py#L1045-L1053)) only catches `KeyboardInterrupt`/`SystemExit`. One bad entry therefore stops all periodic tasks. Users deploying a schedule change typically see beat crash-loop under their process supervisor.\n\n### Suggested fix\n\nI'm happy to send a PR, in three focused parts (they could be separate PRs if preferred):\n\n1. **`solar`**: on `CircumpolarError`, move `start` to the next antitransit and retry `next_rising`/`next_setting`/`next_transit`, up to about one year of days. Return the first **real** occurrence of the event. If none is found within that window, return a long \"check again\" delay instead of a fake event time. Remove the `# pragma: no cover` and add tests for London/June, Stockholm/June and Troms\u00f8/December.\n2. **`crontab`**: in `crontab.__init__`, raise `ValueError` if no (month, day_of_month) pair in the spec is a valid date (allowing Feb 29), so the error shows up when the config is loaded.\n3. **beat**: in `populate_heap()` and `tick()`, catch exceptions from a single entry's `is_due()`, log them with `logger.exception` (naming the entry), and reschedule that entry after `max_interval`, so one bad entry can't stop the others.\n\n", "hints_text": "Thanks for the detailed report. I can reproduce the `solar` part on `main` @ 0731c053b with ephem 4.2.1: all three of your solar cases raise the `TypeError` from the `CircumpolarError` branch.\n\n@Kavin-Nithil, you offered to send PRs. If you haven't started on the `solar` part, I have a fix ready that follows your suggestion 1. On `CircumpolarError` it resumes the search after the next anti-transit, one day at a time for up to 366 days, and returns the first real occurrence of the event. Results for your examples:\n\n| Entry | From | Next run |\n|---|---|---|\n| `dawn_astronomical`, London | 2026-06-15 | 2026-07-22 00:30 UTC |\n| `dusk_nautical`, Stockholm | 2026-06-15 | 2026-07-29 22:34 UTC |\n| `sunrise`, Troms\u00f8 | 2026-12-15 | 2027-01-15 10:34 UTC |\n| `dawn_astronomical`, King George Island | 2026-10-18 | 2027-02-24 04:39 UTC |\n\nThe entry is no longer due on the days in between, so it doesn't fire every night as it would with the one-line fix.\n\nI'd keep that PR to `solar` only and leave the crontab validation and beat fault isolation (your parts 2 and 3) to separate PRs, which I'm happy to leave with you. If you've already started on `solar`, tell me and I'll review yours instead.\n\n@HassanRady can you check and fix this?\n@ag3ntsc4rn thanks for confirming it, and for the fix! I haven't started on the solar part, so please go ahead with your PR. Your results match what I get for London, Stockholm and Troms\u00f8.\n\nI'll take parts 2 and 3 in a separate PR that doesn't touch solar, so the two shouldn't conflict:\n- crontab: a spec whose day_of_month never occurs in its month_of_year (Feb 30, Apr 31, \u2026) raises ValueError when it's created, the same way crontab(minute=61) already does. It no longer crashes beat at runtime.\n- beat: if one entry's is_due() raises, beat logs the error, names the entry, and tries that entry again after max_interval. The other periodic tasks keep running.\n\nPart 3 also keeps beat alive until the solar fix lands. With the King George Island entry from the report, beat on main exits immediately, and with the change it logs the TypeError and keeps sending the healthy entry.", "created_at": "2026-10-19T07:52:54Z"}
{"repo": "celery/celery", "pull_number": 10895, "instance_id": "celery__celery-10895", "issue_numbers": ["6494"], "base_commit": "bff6c2f5390c3abda5bb02bdd1eabb99891f5970", "patch": "diff --git a/CONTRIBUTORS.txt b/CONTRIBUTORS.txt\nindex ceedb08a75f..fe48a3cd91d 100644\n--- a/CONTRIBUTORS.txt\n+++ b/CONTRIBUTORS.txt\n@@ -315,3 +315,4 @@ Phan K\u1ef3, 2026/08/28\n Arockia Rajamanickam, 2026/08/29\n Micha\u0142 Furga\u0142a, 2026/09/29\n Hassan Rady, 2026/09/30\n+Stanislav Shkolnyi, 2026/10/18\ndiff --git a/celery/app/task.py b/celery/app/task.py\nindex ba6dc29569e..94f02f849fc 100644\n--- a/celery/app/task.py\n+++ b/celery/app/task.py\n@@ -1,4 +1,5 @@\n \"\"\"Task implementation: request context and the task base class.\"\"\"\n+import inspect\n import sys\n import types\n \n@@ -861,6 +862,43 @@ def retry(self, args=None, kwargs=None, exc=None, throw=True,\n         if not eta and countdown is None:\n             countdown = self.default_retry_delay\n \n+        # Explicit args/kwargs can change how a task's parameters are passed\n+        # on retry. Keep inherited values that do not conflict with the new\n+        # calling convention (see #6494).\n+        if ((args is not None and kwargs is None and request.kwargs) or\n+                (kwargs is not None and args is None and request.args)):\n+            try:\n+                signature = inspect.signature(self.run)\n+            except (TypeError, ValueError):\n+                pass  # Let normal task argument validation handle this case.\n+            else:\n+                if args is not None:\n+                    positional = [\n+                        p for p in signature.parameters.values()\n+                        if p.kind in (inspect.Parameter.POSITIONAL_ONLY,\n+                                      inspect.Parameter.POSITIONAL_OR_KEYWORD)\n+                    ]\n+                    kwargs = dict(request.kwargs)\n+                    for p in positional[:len(args)]:\n+                        if p.kind == inspect.Parameter.POSITIONAL_OR_KEYWORD:\n+                            kwargs.pop(p.name, None)\n+                else:\n+                    # An explicit keyword may replace an inherited positional\n+                    # argument. Bind inherited values to names so *args and\n+                    # positional-only parameters keep their original positions.\n+                    try:\n+                        bound = signature.bind_partial(*request.args)\n+                    except TypeError:\n+                        pass  # Let normal task argument validation handle this case.\n+                    else:\n+                        kwargs = dict(kwargs)\n+                        for name in list(kwargs):\n+                            if (name in bound.arguments and\n+                                    signature.parameters[name].kind ==\n+                                    inspect.Parameter.POSITIONAL_OR_KEYWORD):\n+                                bound.arguments[name] = kwargs.pop(name)\n+                        args, kwargs = bound.args, {**bound.kwargs, **kwargs}\n+\n         is_eager = request.is_eager\n         S = self.signature_from_request(\n             request, args, kwargs,\ndiff --git a/t/integration/tasks.py b/t/integration/tasks.py\nindex 484186cf4ef..29dccbd81ee 100644\n--- a/t/integration/tasks.py\n+++ b/t/integration/tasks.py\n@@ -289,6 +289,26 @@ def retry_unpickleable(self, foo, bar, *, retry_kwargs):\n     raise self.retry(exc=UnpickleableException(foo, bar), **retry_kwargs)\n \n \n+@shared_task(bind=True, max_retries=1)\n+def retry_with_args_after_kwargs(self, foo, bar, label=None):\n+    if not self.request.retries:\n+        raise self.retry(args=(foo + 1, bar + 1), countdown=0)\n+    return {\n+        'foo': foo, 'bar': bar, 'label': label,\n+        'retries': self.request.retries, 'is_eager': self.request.is_eager,\n+    }\n+\n+\n+@shared_task(bind=True, max_retries=1)\n+def retry_with_kwargs_after_args(self, foo, bar):\n+    if not self.request.retries:\n+        raise self.retry(kwargs={'foo': foo + 1}, countdown=0)\n+    return {\n+        'foo': foo, 'bar': bar,\n+        'retries': self.request.retries, 'is_eager': self.request.is_eager,\n+    }\n+\n+\n @shared_task(bind=True, expires=120.0, max_retries=1)\n def retry_once(self, *args, expires=None, max_retries=1, countdown=0.1):\n     \"\"\"Task that fails and is retried. Returns the number of retries.\"\"\"\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/t/integration/test_tasks.py b/t/integration/test_tasks.py\nindex 3196de6182b..9443ae81fed 100644\n--- a/t/integration/test_tasks.py\n+++ b/t/integration/test_tasks.py\n@@ -19,7 +19,8 @@\n from .tasks import (AddParameterModel, ClassBasedAutoRetryTask, ExpectedException, add, add_ignore_result,\n                     add_not_typed, add_pydantic, add_pydantic_string_annotations, fail, fail_unpickleable,\n                     print_unicode, record_replaced_task_nesting, reject_then_succeed, reject_without_requeue, retry,\n-                    retry_once, retry_once_headers, retry_once_priority, retry_unpickleable, return_properties,\n+                    retry_once, retry_once_headers, retry_once_priority, retry_unpickleable,\n+                    retry_with_args_after_kwargs, retry_with_kwargs_after_args, return_properties,\n                     return_request_time_limits, second_order_replace1, sleeping,\n                     soft_time_limit_must_exceed_time_limit, task_with_declared_time_limits)\n \n@@ -491,6 +492,21 @@ def test_retry(self, manager):\n         assert result.get() == 'bar'\n         assert result.status == 'SUCCESS'\n \n+    def test_retry_switches_from_kwargs_to_args(self, manager):\n+        result = retry_with_args_after_kwargs.apply_async(\n+            kwargs={'foo': 1, 'bar': 2, 'label': 'kept'},\n+        )\n+        assert result.get(timeout=TIMEOUT) == {\n+            'foo': 2, 'bar': 3, 'label': 'kept',\n+            'retries': 1, 'is_eager': False,\n+        }\n+\n+    def test_retry_switches_from_args_to_kwargs(self, manager):\n+        result = retry_with_kwargs_after_args.apply_async(args=(1, 2))\n+        assert result.get(timeout=TIMEOUT) == {\n+            'foo': 2, 'bar': 2, 'retries': 1, 'is_eager': False,\n+        }\n+\n     def test_retry_with_unpickleable_exception(self, manager):\n         \"\"\"Test a task that retries with an unpickleable exception.\n \ndiff --git a/t/unit/tasks/test_tasks.py b/t/unit/tasks/test_tasks.py\nindex 923ee5dbbc7..07896b5596e 100644\n--- a/t/unit/tasks/test_tasks.py\n+++ b/t/unit/tasks/test_tasks.py\n@@ -418,6 +418,186 @@ def test_retry_no_args(self):\n         self.retry_task_noargs.apply(propagate=True).get()\n         assert self.retry_task_noargs.iterations == 4\n \n+    def test_retry_with_args_after_initial_kwargs(self):\n+        @self.app.task(bind=True, shared=False)\n+        def task(self_, foo, bar, label=None):\n+            if not self_.request.retries:\n+                raise self_.retry(args=(foo + 1, bar + 1), countdown=0)\n+            return foo, bar, label\n+\n+        result = task.apply(kwargs={'foo': 1, 'bar': 2, 'label': 'kept'},\n+                            propagate=True)\n+        assert result.get() == (2, 3, 'kept')\n+\n+    def test_retry_with_kwargs_after_initial_args(self):\n+        @self.app.task(bind=True, shared=False)\n+        def task(self_, foo, bar):\n+            if not self_.request.retries:\n+                raise self_.retry(kwargs={'foo': foo + 1}, countdown=0)\n+            return foo, bar\n+\n+        result = task.apply(args=(1, 2), propagate=True)\n+        assert result.get() == (2, 2)\n+\n+    def test_retry_with_kwargs_overriding_last_positional(self):\n+        @self.app.task(bind=True, shared=False)\n+        def task(self_, foo, bar):\n+            if not self_.request.retries:\n+                raise self_.retry(kwargs={'bar': bar + 1}, countdown=0)\n+            return foo, bar\n+\n+        result = task.apply(args=(1, 2), propagate=True)\n+        assert result.get() == (1, 3)\n+\n+    def test_retry_preserves_non_conflicting_request_kwargs(self):\n+        @self.app.task(bind=True, shared=False)\n+        def task(self_, foo, bar=None):\n+            if not self_.request.retries:\n+                raise self_.retry(args=(foo + 1,), countdown=0)\n+            return foo, bar\n+\n+        result = task.apply(args=(1,), kwargs={'bar': 2}, propagate=True)\n+        assert result.get() == (2, 2)\n+\n+    def test_retry_keeps_explicit_args_and_kwargs(self):\n+        @self.app.task(bind=True, shared=False)\n+        def task(self_, foo, bar=None):\n+            if not self_.request.retries:\n+                raise self_.retry(args=(foo + 1,), kwargs={'bar': 3}, countdown=0)\n+            return foo, bar\n+\n+        result = task.apply(kwargs={'foo': 1, 'bar': 2}, propagate=True)\n+        assert result.get() == (2, 3)\n+\n+    def test_retry_with_kwargs_overriding_varargs(self):\n+        @self.app.task(bind=True, shared=False)\n+        def task(self_, foo, *rest):\n+            if not self_.request.retries:\n+                raise self_.retry(kwargs={'foo': foo + 1}, countdown=0)\n+            return foo, rest\n+\n+        result = task.apply(args=(1, 2, 3), propagate=True)\n+        assert result.get() == (2, (2, 3))\n+\n+    def test_retry_with_kwargs_overriding_multiple_varargs_parameters(self):\n+        @self.app.task(bind=True, shared=False)\n+        def task(self_, foo, bar, *rest):\n+            if not self_.request.retries:\n+                raise self_.retry(kwargs={'foo': foo + 1, 'bar': bar + 1},\n+                                  countdown=0)\n+            return foo, bar, rest\n+\n+        result = task.apply(args=(1, 2, 4, 5), propagate=True)\n+        assert result.get() == (2, 3, (4, 5))\n+\n+    def test_retry_does_not_mutate_explicit_kwargs_with_varargs(self):\n+        retry_kwargs = {'foo': 2}\n+\n+        @self.app.task(bind=True, shared=False)\n+        def task(self_, foo, *rest):\n+            if not self_.request.retries:\n+                raise self_.retry(kwargs=retry_kwargs, countdown=0)\n+            return foo, rest\n+\n+        result = task.apply(args=(1, 2, 3), propagate=True)\n+        assert result.get() == (2, (2, 3))\n+        assert retry_kwargs == {'foo': 2}\n+\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize('retry_kwargs, expected', (\n+        ({'foo': 10}, (10, 2, (3, 4))),\n+        ({'bar': 20}, (1, 20, (3, 4))),\n+    ))\n+    def test_retry_with_kwargs_overriding_one_parameter_before_varargs(\n+            self, retry_kwargs, expected):\n+        @self.app.task(bind=True, shared=False)\n+        def task(self_, foo, bar, *rest):\n+            if not self_.request.retries:\n+                raise self_.retry(kwargs=retry_kwargs, countdown=0)\n+            return foo, bar, rest\n+\n+        original_kwargs = retry_kwargs.copy()\n+        result = task.apply(args=(1, 2, 3, 4), propagate=True)\n+        assert result.get() == expected\n+        assert retry_kwargs == original_kwargs\n+\n+    def test_retry_keeps_positional_only_name_in_extra_kwargs(self):\n+        @self.app.task(bind=True, shared=False)\n+        def task(self_, foo, /, bar, **extra):\n+            if not self_.request.retries:\n+                raise self_.retry(kwargs={'foo': 10, 'bar': 20}, countdown=0)\n+            return foo, bar, extra\n+\n+        result = task.apply(args=(1, 2), propagate=True)\n+        assert result.get() == (1, 20, {'foo': 10})\n+\n+    def test_retry_with_unbindable_request_args(self):\n+        task = self.retry_task_mockapply\n+        task.push_request(args=(1, 2, 3, 4), kwargs={},\n+                          called_directly=False, is_eager=True)\n+        try:\n+            with pytest.raises(Retry) as exc_info:\n+                task.retry(kwargs={'unknown': 5}, countdown=0)\n+            assert exc_info.value.sig.args == (1, 2, 3, 4)\n+            assert exc_info.value.sig.kwargs == {'unknown': 5}\n+        finally:\n+            task.pop_request()\n+\n+    def test_retry_with_kwargs_after_positional_only_arg(self):\n+        @self.app.task(bind=True, shared=False)\n+        def task(self_, prefix, /, foo, bar):\n+            if not self_.request.retries:\n+                raise self_.retry(kwargs={'foo': foo + 1}, countdown=0)\n+            return prefix, foo, bar\n+\n+        result = task.apply(args=(10, 1, 2), propagate=True)\n+        assert result.get() == (10, 2, 2)\n+\n+    def test_retry_with_args_preserves_positional_only_arg(self):\n+        @self.app.task(bind=True, shared=False)\n+        def task(self_, foo, /, bar=None):\n+            if not self_.request.retries:\n+                raise self_.retry(args=(foo + 1,), countdown=0)\n+            return foo, bar\n+\n+        result = task.apply(args=(1,), kwargs={'bar': 2}, propagate=True)\n+        assert result.get() == (2, 2)\n+\n+    def test_retry_with_keyword_only_argument(self):\n+        @self.app.task(bind=True, shared=False)\n+        def task(self_, foo, *, label=None):\n+            if not self_.request.retries:\n+                raise self_.retry(kwargs={'label': 'new'}, countdown=0)\n+            return foo, label\n+\n+        result = task.apply(args=(1,), kwargs={'label': 'old'},\n+                            propagate=True)\n+        assert result.get() == (1, 'new')\n+\n+    def test_retry_preserves_arguments_when_not_overridden(self):\n+        @self.app.task(bind=True, shared=False)\n+        def task(self_, foo, bar=None):\n+            if not self_.request.retries:\n+                raise self_.retry(countdown=0)\n+            return foo, bar\n+\n+        result = task.apply(args=(1,), kwargs={'bar': 2}, propagate=True)\n+        assert result.get() == (1, 2)\n+\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize('error_type', (TypeError, ValueError))\n+    def test_retry_with_uninspectable_signature(self, error_type):\n+        task = self.retry_task_mockapply\n+        task.push_request(args=(1, 2), kwargs={},\n+                          called_directly=False, is_eager=True)\n+        try:\n+            with patch('celery.app.task.inspect.signature',\n+                       side_effect=error_type):\n+                with pytest.raises(Retry) as exc_info:\n+                    task.retry(kwargs={'arg1': 3}, countdown=0)\n+            assert exc_info.value.sig.args == (1, 2)\n+            assert exc_info.value.sig.kwargs == {'arg1': 3}\n+        finally:\n+            task.pop_request()\n+\n     def test_signature_from_request__passes_headers(self):\n         self.retry_task.push_request()\n         self.retry_task.request.headers = {'custom': 10.1}\n", "problem_statement": "Arguments specification when retrying task has to match the form used for calling the task\n# Checklist\n* [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `master` branch of Celery.\n* [ ] This has already been asked to the [discussion group](https://groups.google.com/forum/#!forum/celery-users) first.\n* [x] I have read the relevant section in the\n  [contribution guide](http://docs.celeryproject.org/en/latest/contributing.html#other-bugs)\n  on reporting bugs.\n* [x] I have checked the [issues list](https://github.com/celery/celery/issues?q=is%3Aissue+label%3A%22Issue+Type%3A+Bug+Report%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)\n  for similar or identical bug reports.\n* [x] I have checked the [pull requests list](https://github.com/celery/celery/pulls?q=is%3Apr+label%3A%22PR+Type%3A+Bugfix%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)\n  for existing proposed fixes.\n* [x] I have checked the [commit log](https://github.com/celery/celery/commits/master)\n  to find out if the bug was already fixed in the master branch.\n* [x] I have included all related issues and possible duplicate issues\n  in this issue (If there are none, check this box anyway).\n\n## Mandatory Debugging Information\n* [x] I have included the output of ``celery -A proj report`` in the issue.\n    (if you are not able to do this, then at least specify the Celery\n     version affected).\n* [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `master` branch of Celery.\n* [x] I have included the contents of ``pip freeze`` in the issue.\n* [x] I have included all the versions of all the external dependencies required\n  to reproduce this bug.\n\n## Environment & Settings\n**Celery version**: `5.0.2 (singularity)`\n\n<details>\n<summary><b><code>celery report</code> Output: </b></summary>\n<p>\n\n```\nsoftware -> celery:5.0.2 (singularity) kombu:5.0.2 py:3.6.9\n            billiard:3.6.3.0 py-amqp:5.0.2\nplatform -> system:Linux arch:64bit, ELF\n            kernel version:5.3.0-59-generic imp:CPython\nloader   -> celery.loaders.app.AppLoader\nsettings -> transport:amqp results:disabled\n\ndeprecated_settings: None\nbroker_url: 'amqp://test:********@192.168.84.224:5672/test_vhost'\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n# Steps to Reproduce\n### Python Packages\n<details>\n<summary><b><code>pip freeze</code> Output:</b></summary>\n<p>\n\n```\namqp==5.0.2\nbilliard==3.6.3.0\ncelery==5.0.2\nclick==7.1.2\nclick-didyoumean==0.0.3\nclick-repl==0.1.6\nimportlib-metadata==3.0.0\nkombu==5.0.2\npkg-resources==0.0.0\nprompt-toolkit==3.0.8\npytz==2020.4\nsix==1.15.0\nvine==5.0.0\nwcwidth==0.2.5\nzipp==3.4.0\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n## Minimally Reproducible Test Case\n<details>\n<p>\n\nTask definition:\n\n\n```python\nfrom celery import Celery\n\napp = Celery('tasks')\napp.conf.update({\n    'broker_url': 'amqp://test:secret@192.168.84.224:5672/test_vhost'\n})\n\n@app.task(bind=True)\ndef retry(self, foo, bar=None):\n    self.retry(args=(foo, bar), countdown=5)\n```\n\nRun worker:\n\n\n```\ncelery -A tasks worker --loglevel=INFO\n```\n\nSubmit task:\n\n\n```python\nfrom tasks import retry\n\nretry.apply_async(kwargs={'foo': 1, 'bar': 'baz'})\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n# Expected Behavior\nWhen retrying the task using `self.retry`, I can provide the task arguments either using `args` or `kwargs` (or mix both).\n\n# Actual Behavior\nWhen retrying the task using `self.retry`, I have to supply the task arguments in the same form as was used to call the task originally, otherwise an exception is raised:\n\n```\n -------------- celery@jinx v5.0.2 (singularity)\n--- ***** ----- \n-- ******* ---- Linux-5.3.0-59-generic-x86_64-with-Ubuntu-18.04-bionic 2020-11-23 12:32:02\n- *** --- * --- \n- ** ---------- [config]\n- ** ---------- .> app:         tasks:0x7fed5c609ba8\n- ** ---------- .> transport:   amqp://test:**@192.168.84.224:5672/test_vhost\n- ** ---------- .> results:     disabled://\n- *** --- * --- .> concurrency: 4 (prefork)\n-- ******* ---- .> task events: OFF (enable -E to monitor tasks in this worker)\n--- ***** ----- \n -------------- [queues]\n                .> celery           exchange=celery(direct) key=celery\n                \n\n[tasks]\n  . tasks.retry\n\n[2020-11-23 12:32:02,653: INFO/MainProcess] Connected to amqp://test:**@192.168.84.224:5672/test_vhost\n[2020-11-23 12:32:02,664: INFO/MainProcess] mingle: searching for neighbors\n[2020-11-23 12:32:03,731: INFO/MainProcess] mingle: all alone\n[2020-11-23 12:32:03,765: INFO/MainProcess] celery@jinx ready.\n[2020-11-23 12:32:06,515: INFO/MainProcess] Received task: tasks.retry[1346d9aa-22da-4de2-a0f4-e42bd8cab57d]  \n[2020-11-23 12:32:06,520: WARNING/ForkPoolWorker-2] Task tasks.retry[1346d9aa-22da-4de2-a0f4-e42bd8cab57d] reject requeue=False: retry() got multiple values for argument 'foo'\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"/home/pasmen/tmp/x-celery-retry-bug/venv/lib/python3.6/site-packages/celery/app/task.py\", line 720, in retry\n    S.apply_async()\n  File \"/home/pasmen/tmp/x-celery-retry-bug/venv/lib/python3.6/site-packages/celery/canvas.py\", line 225, in apply_async\n    return _apply(args, kwargs, **options)\n  File \"/home/pasmen/tmp/x-celery-retry-bug/venv/lib/python3.6/site-packages/celery/app/task.py\", line 526, in apply_async\n    check_arguments(*(args or ()), **(kwargs or {}))\nTypeError: retry() got multiple values for argument 'foo'\n\nDuring handling of the above exception, another exception occurred:\n\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"/home/pasmen/tmp/x-celery-retry-bug/venv/lib/python3.6/site-packages/celery/app/trace.py\", line 409, in trace_task\n    R = retval = fun(*args, **kwargs)\n  File \"/home/pasmen/tmp/x-celery-retry-bug/venv/lib/python3.6/site-packages/celery/app/trace.py\", line 701, in __protected_call__\n    return self.run(*args, **kwargs)\n  File \"/home/pasmen/tmp/x-celery-retry-bug/tasks.py\", line 10, in retry\n    self.retry(args=(foo, bar), countdown=5)\n  File \"/home/pasmen/tmp/x-celery-retry-bug/venv/lib/python3.6/site-packages/celery/app/task.py\", line 722, in retry\n    raise Reject(exc, requeue=False)\ncelery.exceptions.Reject: (TypeError(\"retry() got multiple values for argument 'foo'\",), False)\n```\n\nThe exception is raised in both cases, i.e. when calling the task providing arguments using `args` and then using `kwargs` in `self.retry`, and the other way around, when calling the task with `kwargs` and using `args` in `self.retry`. When I use the same form in `self.retry` as was used when calling the task, everything works without an error.\n\nThis seems to come from [`signature_from_request`](https://github.com/celery/celery/blob/e2031688284484d5b5a57ba29cd9cae2d9a81e39/celery/app/task.py#L592) method or `Task` class. Both `args` and `kwargs` are take from the request if they are `None` in `self.retry` call which leads to the described behavior. There is nothing about it in the documentation and I believe the task itself shouldn't assume anything about how it was called originally in orded to successfully call `self.retry`.\n\n\n", "hints_text": "Thanks for the bug report.\r\nI agree you should be allowed to call retry with other arguments.\r\nIt seems like you have a basic analysis of the problem. Can you come up with a fix?", "created_at": "2026-10-19T05:47:20Z"}
{"repo": "celery/celery", "pull_number": 10889, "instance_id": "celery__celery-10889", "issue_numbers": ["6679"], "base_commit": "933183fc38e62d07b58873fdc38cb5d0018bfef3", "patch": "diff --git a/celery/app/__init__.py b/celery/app/__init__.py\nindex 4a946d93053..6e320c8aeb4 100644\n--- a/celery/app/__init__.py\n+++ b/celery/app/__init__.py\n@@ -48,6 +48,11 @@ def shared_task(*args, **kwargs):\n     def create_shared_task(**options):\n \n         def __inner(fun):\n+            if type(fun) is Proxy:\n+                raise TypeError(\n+                    '@shared_task was applied more than once to the same '\n+                    'function. Remove the extra @shared_task decorator.'\n+                )\n             name = options.get('name')\n             # Set as shared task so that unfinalized apps,\n             # and future apps will register a copy of this task.\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/t/unit/app/test_app.py b/t/unit/app/test_app.py\nindex b9e46848c25..516000764d0 100644\n--- a/t/unit/app/test_app.py\n+++ b/t/unit/app/test_app.py\n@@ -3120,3 +3120,27 @@ def baz():\n                     return 168\n \n                 assert baz.app is yproj\n+\n+    def test_applied_twice_raises(self):\n+        with pytest.raises(TypeError, match='more than once'):\n+            @shared_task\n+            @shared_task\n+            def foo():\n+                return 42\n+\n+        with pytest.raises(TypeError, match='more than once'):\n+            @shared_task(name='xproj.foo')\n+            @shared_task()\n+            def bar():\n+                return 42\n+\n+    def test_can_wrap_app_task(self):\n+        with self.Celery('xproj', set_as_current=True) as xproj:\n+            xproj.conf.task_always_eager = True\n+\n+            @shared_task\n+            @xproj.task\n+            def mul(x, y):\n+                return x * y\n+\n+            assert mul.delay(2, 3).get() == 6\n", "problem_statement": "Multiple shared_task decorators on the same function causes celery to hang with no output\n<!--\nPlease fill this template entirely and do not erase parts of it.\nWe reserve the right to close without a response\nenhancement requests which are incomplete.\n-->\n\n\n# Checklist\n<!--\nTo check an item on the list replace [ ] with [x].\n-->\n\n* [x] I have checked the [issues list](https://github.com/celery/celery/issues?q=is%3Aissue+label%3A%22Issue+Type%3A+Enhancement%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)\n  for similar or identical enhancement to an existing feature.\n* [ ] I have checked the [pull requests list](https://github.com/celery/celery/pulls?q=is%3Apr+label%3A%22Issue+Type%3A+Enhancement%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)\n  for existing proposed enhancements.\n* [ ] I have checked the [commit log](https://github.com/celery/celery/commits/master)\n  to find out if the if the same enhancement was already implemented in the\n  master branch.\n* [x] I have included all related issues and possible duplicate issues in this issue\n      (If there are none, check this box anyway).\n\n## Related Issues and Possible Duplicates\n<!--\nPlease make sure to search and mention any related issues\nor possible duplicates to this issue as requested by the checklist above.\n\nThis may or may not include issues in other repositories that the Celery project\nmaintains or other repositories that are dependencies of Celery.\n\nIf you don't know how to mention issues, please refer to Github's documentation\non the subject: https://help.github.com/en/articles/autolinked-references-and-urls#issues-and-pull-requests\n-->\n\n#### Related Issues\n* None\n\n#### Possible Duplicates\n* None\n\n# Brief Summary\nHaving multiple `shared_task` decorators on a function causes celery to hang with no output given, even with `loglevel=DEBUG`. This bug was very hard to track down without any output from celery.\n\n# Design\n## Architectural Considerations\n<!--\nIf more components other than Celery are involved,\ndescribe them here and the effect it would have on Celery.\n-->\nNone\n\n## Proposed Behavior\nAdditional logging should be added to make scenarios like this identifiable.\n\n## Proposed UI/UX\n<!--\nPlease provide your ideas for the API, CLI options,\nconfiguration key names etc. that will be adjusted for this enhancement.\n-->\n\n## Diagrams\n<!--\nPlease include any diagrams that might be relevant\nto the implementation of this enhancement such as:\n\n\n* Class Diagrams\n* Sequence Diagrams\n* Activity Diagrams\nYou can drag and drop images into the text box to attach them to this issue.\n-->\nN/A\n\n## Alternatives\n<!--\nIf you have considered any alternative implementations\ndescribe them in detail below.\n-->\nNone\n\n\n", "hints_text": "Hey @l3s2d :wave:,\nThank you for opening an issue. We will get back to you as soon as we can.\nAlso, check out our [Open Collective](https://opencollective.com/celery) and consider backing us - every little helps!\n\nWe also offer priority support for our sponsors.\nIf you require immediate assistance please consider sponsoring us.\n\nI've run into this before too!\nI'd like to work on this. I'll open a PR that raises a clear error when @shared_task is used twice on the same function.\nok", "created_at": "2026-10-18T17:14:55Z"}
{"repo": "celery/celery", "pull_number": 10884, "instance_id": "celery__celery-10884", "issue_numbers": ["10883"], "base_commit": "2e22ce074ceced174b7f62c7b85e7e714ef919a5", "patch": "diff --git a/celery/worker/control.py b/celery/worker/control.py\nindex 265e159cc07..f29e956440a 100644\n--- a/celery/worker/control.py\n+++ b/celery/worker/control.py\n@@ -202,8 +202,11 @@ def revoke_by_stamped_headers(state, headers, terminate=False, signal=None, **kw\n             # if so, check if any stamps match a revoked stamp\n             for expected_header_key, expected_header_value in headers.items():\n                 if expected_header_key in req.stamps:\n+                    actual_header = req.stamps.get(expected_header_key)\n+                    if actual_header is None:\n+                        continue\n                     expected_header_value = maybe_list(expected_header_value)\n-                    actual_header = maybe_list(req.stamps[expected_header_key])\n+                    actual_header = maybe_list(actual_header)\n                     matching_stamps_for_request = set(actual_header) & set(expected_header_value)\n                     # Check any possible match regardless if the stamps are a sequence or not\n                     if matching_stamps_for_request:\ndiff --git a/celery/worker/request.py b/celery/worker/request.py\nindex 66581c09e45..7e8d2a8fe32 100644\n--- a/celery/worker/request.py\n+++ b/celery/worker/request.py\n@@ -488,7 +488,9 @@ def revoked(self):\n             for stamp in self.stamped_headers:\n                 if stamp in revoked_stamps:\n                     revoked_header = revoked_stamps[stamp]\n-                    stamped_header = self._message.headers['stamps'][stamp]\n+                    stamped_header = self.stamps.get(stamp)\n+                    if stamped_header is None:\n+                        continue\n \n                     if isinstance(stamped_header, (list, tuple)):\n                         for stamped_value in stamped_header:\n@@ -502,7 +504,8 @@ def revoked(self):\n                             stamped_header == revoked_header,  # When the header is a single set value\n                         ])\n                         revoking_header = {stamp: stamped_header}\n-                    break\n+                    if revoked_by_header:\n+                        break\n \n         if any((expired, revoked_by_id, revoked_by_header)):\n             log_msg = 'Discarding revoked task: %s[%s]'\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/t/unit/worker/test_control.py b/t/unit/worker/test_control.py\nindex 7a7bff5d819..435da65f4e0 100644\n--- a/t/unit/worker/test_control.py\n+++ b/t/unit/worker/test_control.py\n@@ -1191,3 +1191,63 @@ def test_revoke_by_stamped_headers_terminates_matching_request(self):\n         assert 'test-123' in worker_state.revoked_stamps.get('monitoring_id', [])\n \n         worker_state.active_requests.clear()\n+\n+    def test_revoke_by_stamped_headers_handles_missing_stamp_value_with_terminate(self):\n+        \"\"\"Test that terminate=True handles missing stamped header values gracefully.\n+\n+        Regression test for case where a header is listed in stamped_headers\n+        and flagged in revoked_stamps, but has no corresponding value in stamps.\n+        This should not raise TypeError and should not terminate the task.\n+        \"\"\"\n+        state = self.create_state()\n+        state.consumer = Mock()\n+\n+        request = Mock()\n+        request.id = 'task-with-missing-stamp'\n+        # 'monitoring_id' is in stamps but its value is None\n+        request.stamps = {'monitoring_id': None}\n+        request.terminate = Mock()\n+\n+        worker_state.active_requests.add(request)\n+\n+        headers = {'monitoring_id': 'test-123'}\n+\n+        # Should not raise TypeError\n+        with patch('celery.worker.control._signals.signum', return_value=15):\n+            result = control.revoke_by_stamped_headers(state, headers, terminate=True)\n+\n+        # Task should not be terminated due to missing stamp value\n+        request.terminate.assert_not_called()\n+        # Should report that headers were not terminated\n+        assert 'were not terminated' in result['ok']\n+\n+        worker_state.active_requests.clear()\n+\n+    def test_revoke_by_stamped_headers_terminate_checks_later_headers(self):\n+        state = self.create_state()\n+        state.consumer = Mock()\n+\n+        request = Mock()\n+        request.id = 'task-with-one-missing-stamp'\n+        request.stamps = {\n+            'a': None,\n+            'b': 'y',\n+        }\n+\n+        worker_state.active_requests.add(request)\n+\n+        try:\n+            with patch(\n+                'celery.worker.control._signals.signum',\n+                return_value=15,\n+            ):\n+                control.revoke_by_stamped_headers(\n+                    state,\n+                    {'a': 'x', 'b': 'y'},\n+                    terminate=True,\n+                )\n+\n+            request.terminate.assert_called_once()\n+        finally:\n+            worker_state.active_requests.clear()\n+            worker_state.revoked_stamps.clear()\ndiff --git a/t/unit/worker/test_request.py b/t/unit/worker/test_request.py\nindex 2f39f1f3be9..092fdf1780c 100644\n--- a/t/unit/worker/test_request.py\n+++ b/t/unit/worker/test_request.py\n@@ -987,6 +987,109 @@ def test_revoked_by_stamped_headers(self, header_to_revoke):\n             assert job._already_revoked\n             assert job.acknowledged\n \n+    def _stamp(self, job, stamps):\n+        job._message.headers['stamps'] = stamps\n+        job._message.headers['stamped_headers'] = list(stamps)\n+        job._request_dict['stamps'] = stamps\n+        job._request_dict['stamped_headers'] = list(stamps)\n+\n+    def test_revoked_by_second_stamped_header(self):\n+        \"\"\"Test that revocation works when a later stamped header matches.\n+\n+        Regression test for issue where the loop broke unconditionally after\n+        checking the first header in revoked_stamps, even if it didn't match.\n+        \"\"\"\n+        revoked_stamps.clear()\n+        try:\n+            job = self.xRequest()\n+            self._stamp(job, {'a': 'x', 'b': 'y'})\n+            revoked_stamps.update({'a': ['other'], 'b': ['y']})\n+            with self.assert_signal_called(\n+                    task_revoked, sender=job.task, request=job._context,\n+                    terminated=False, expired=False, signum=None):\n+                assert job.revoked()\n+                assert job._already_revoked\n+                assert job.acknowledged\n+        finally:\n+            revoked_stamps.clear()\n+\n+    def test_not_revoked_when_no_stamped_header_matches(self):\n+        \"\"\"Test that task is not revoked when no stamped header matches.\"\"\"\n+        revoked_stamps.clear()\n+        try:\n+            job = self.xRequest()\n+            self._stamp(job, {'a': 'x', 'b': 'y'})\n+            revoked_stamps.update({'a': ['other'], 'b': ['nope']})\n+            assert not job.revoked()\n+            assert not job._already_revoked\n+        finally:\n+            revoked_stamps.clear()\n+\n+    def test_revoked_by_later_list_valued_stamped_header(self):\n+        \"\"\"Test that revocation works when a later list-valued stamped header matches.\"\"\"\n+        revoked_stamps.clear()\n+        try:\n+            job = self.xRequest()\n+            self._stamp(job, {'a': 'x', 'b': ['y1', 'y2']})\n+            revoked_stamps.update({'a': ['other'], 'b': ['y2']})\n+            assert job.revoked()\n+            assert job._already_revoked\n+        finally:\n+            revoked_stamps.clear()\n+\n+    def test_revoked_by_header_skips_unflagged_headers(self):\n+        \"\"\"Test that revocation works when only one header is flagged and it matches.\"\"\"\n+        revoked_stamps.clear()\n+        try:\n+            job = self.xRequest()\n+            self._stamp(job, {'a': 'x', 'b': 'y'})\n+            revoked_stamps.update({'b': ['y']})\n+            assert job.revoked()\n+        finally:\n+            revoked_stamps.clear()\n+\n+    def test_revoked_by_header_handles_missing_stamp_value(self):\n+        \"\"\"Test that revocation handles missing stamped header values gracefully.\n+\n+        Regression test for case where a header is listed in stamped_headers\n+        and flagged in revoked_stamps, but has no corresponding value in stamps.\n+        This should not raise KeyError and should not revoke the task.\n+        \"\"\"\n+        revoked_stamps.clear()\n+        try:\n+            job = self.xRequest()\n+            # 'b' is in stamped_headers but not in stamps\n+            job._message.headers['stamps'] = {'a': 'x'}\n+            job._message.headers['stamped_headers'] = ['a', 'b']\n+            job._request_dict['stamps'] = {'a': 'x'}\n+            job._request_dict['stamped_headers'] = ['a', 'b']\n+            revoked_stamps.update({'a': ['other'], 'b': ['y']})\n+            # Should not raise KeyError\n+            assert not job.revoked()\n+            assert not job._already_revoked\n+        finally:\n+            revoked_stamps.clear()\n+\n+    def test_revoked_by_header_after_missing_stamp_value(self):\n+        revoked_stamps.clear()\n+        try:\n+            job = self.xRequest()\n+\n+            job._message.headers['stamps'] = {'b': 'y'}\n+            job._message.headers['stamped_headers'] = ['a', 'b']\n+\n+            job._request_dict['stamps'] = {'b': 'y'}\n+            job._request_dict['stamped_headers'] = ['a', 'b']\n+\n+            revoked_stamps.update({\n+                'a': ['x'],\n+                'b': ['y'],\n+            })\n+\n+            assert job.revoked()\n+        finally:\n+            revoked_stamps.clear()\n+\n     def test_execute_does_not_execute_revoked(self):\n         job = self.xRequest()\n         revoked.add(job.id)\n", "problem_statement": "Request.revoked() ignores later stamped headers: revoke_by_stamped_headers fails to revoke a task when an earlier flagged header doesn't match\n# Checklist\n- [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `main` branch of Celery.\n- [ ] This has already been asked to the [discussions forum](https://github.com/celery/celery/discussions) first.\n- [x] I have read the relevant section in the\n  [contribution guide](https://docs.celeryq.dev/en/main/contributing.html#other-bugs)\n  on reporting bugs.\n- [x] I have checked the [issues list](https://github.com/celery/celery/issues?q=is%3Aissue+label%3A%22Issue+Type%3A+Bug+Report%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22) for similar or identical bug reports.\n- [x] I have checked the [pull requests list](https://github.com/celery/celery/pulls?q=is%3Apr+label%3A%22PR+Type%3A+Bugfix%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22) for existing proposed fixes.\n- [x] I have checked the [commit log](https://github.com/celery/celery/commits/main) to find out if the bug was already fixed in the main branch.\n- [x] I have included all related issues and possible duplicate issues\n  in this issue (If there are none, check this box anyway).\n- [x] I have tried to reproduce the issue with a minimal standalone script (below).\n\n## Mandatory Debugging Information\n\n- [x] I have included the output of ``celery -A proj report`` in the issue.\n    (if you are not able to do this, then at least specify the Celery\n     version affected).\n- [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `main` branch of Celery.\n- [x] I have included the contents of ``pip freeze`` in the issue.\n- [x] I have included all the versions of all the external dependencies required\n  to reproduce this bug.\n\n## Related Issues and Possible Duplicates\n\n#### Related Issues\n\n- None\n\n#### Possible Duplicates\n\n- None\n\n## Environment & Settings\n**Celery version**: 5.7.0b1 (main)\n\n# Steps to Reproduce\n\n## Required Dependencies\n- **Minimal Python Version**: N/A or Unknown (tested on 3.12.3)\n- **Minimal Celery Version**: N/A or Unknown (reproduced on 5.7.0b1 / main)\n- **Minimal Kombu Version**: N/A or Unknown (tested with 5.7.0rc1)\n- **Minimal Broker Version**: N/A (not broker dependent)\n- **Minimal Result Backend Version**: N/A (not backend dependent)\n- **Minimal OS and/or Kernel Version**: N/A or Unknown\n- **Minimal Broker Client Version**: N/A\n- **Minimal Result Backend Client Version**: N/A\n\n### Python Packages\n<details>\n<summary><b><code>pip freeze</code> Output (relevant packages):</b></summary>\n<p>\n\n```\namqp==5.4.1\nbilliard==4.3.1\nclick==8.3.1\nkombu==5.7.0rc1\npython-dateutil==2.9.0.post0\ntzlocal==5.4.4\nvine==5.1.0\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n## Minimally Reproducible Test Case\n\n<details>\n<p>\n\n```python\nfrom unittest.mock import Mock\n\nfrom celery import Celery\nfrom celery.worker import state\nfrom celery.worker.control import revoke_by_stamped_headers\nfrom celery.worker.request import Request\nfrom kombu.utils.uuid import uuid\n\napp = Celery('repro', set_as_current=False, broker='memory://',\n             backend='cache+memory://')\n\n\n@app.task(shared=False)\ndef add(x, y):\n    return x + y\n\n\ndef make_request(stamps):\n    tid = uuid()\n    msg = Mock()\n    msg.headers = {\n        'id': tid, 'task': add.name, 'stamped_headers': list(stamps),\n        'stamps': stamps, 'argsrepr': '', 'kwargsrepr': '', 'origin': 'x',\n        'retries': 0, 'timelimit': [None, None],\n    }\n    msg.payload = ([1, 2], {}, {})\n    msg.delivery_info = {'exchange': '', 'routing_key': 'celery'}\n    msg.properties = {'correlation_id': tid, 'reply_to': 'x'}\n    msg.content_type, msg.content_encoding, msg.body = 'application/json', 'utf-8', b''\n    return Request(msg, app=app, task=add, on_ack=Mock(), body=([1, 2], {}, {}))\n\n\n# Flag two headers; only \"b\" matches the task below.\nrevoke_by_stamped_headers(Mock(), {'a': ['other'], 'b': ['y']})\nprint('revoked_stamps:', state.revoked_stamps)\n\nprint('task stamped {a: x, b: y} revoked? ->',\n      make_request({'a': 'x', 'b': 'y'}).revoked())\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n# Expected Behavior\nThe task is stamped with `{'a': 'x', 'b': 'y'}`, and header `b` with value `y` has been flagged via\n`revoke_by_stamped_headers`. The task should be revoked, as a match on any one of its\nstamped headers should revoke it. This is also how the `terminate=True` path of\n`revoke_by_stamped_headers` behaves: it checks every header.\n\n`revoked()` should return `True`.\n\n# Actual Behavior\n`revoked()` returns `False`:\n\n```\nrevoked_stamps: {'a': ['other'], 'b': ['y']}\ntask stamped {a: x, b: y} revoked? -> False\n```\n\nThe task is not revoked and is executed by the worker.\n\n## Root cause\nIn `Request.revoked()` (`celery/worker/request.py`), the loop over the task's stamped\nheaders ends with an unconditional `break` after the first header that is present in\n`revoked_stamps`, whether or not that header matched:\n\n```python\nfor stamp in self.stamped_headers:\n    if stamp in revoked_stamps:\n        ...\n        if isinstance(stamped_header, (list, tuple)):\n            ...\n        else:\n            revoked_by_header = any([...])\n            revoking_header = {stamp: stamped_header}\n        break          # <-- stops even when revoked_by_header is False\n```\n\nSo only the first flagged header is ever compared, and later headers that would match are\nnever checked. The result then depends on the order of the headers in the message.\n\n## Proposed fix\nOnly stop once a stamp has actually matched:\n\n```python\n        if revoked_by_header:\n            break\n```\n\nI have a patch plus a regression test (`test_revoked_by_second_stamped_header` in\n`t/unit/worker/test_request.py`) that fails before the change and passes after it, and I'm\nhappy to open a PR.\n", "hints_text": "", "created_at": "2026-10-18T07:35:14Z"}
{"repo": "celery/celery", "pull_number": 10878, "instance_id": "celery__celery-10878", "issue_numbers": ["10877"], "base_commit": "be75e2cf136ea024fb6b8d89ce0254f8417b759c", "patch": "diff --git a/celery/backends/arangodb.py b/celery/backends/arangodb.py\nindex 22c7eec6fe1..a01b0c16a57 100644\n--- a/celery/backends/arangodb.py\n+++ b/celery/backends/arangodb.py\n@@ -13,9 +13,8 @@\n \n try:\n     from pyArango import connection as py_arango_connection\n-    from pyArango.theExceptions import AQLQueryError\n except ImportError:\n-    py_arango_connection = AQLQueryError = None\n+    py_arango_connection = None\n \n __all__ = ('ArangoDbBackend',)\n \n", "test_patch": "", "problem_statement": "Remove unused AQLQueryError import from the ArangoDB backend\n`celery/backends/arangodb.py` imports `AQLQueryError` from `pyArango.theExceptions` (with a `= None` fallback when pyArango is missing), but the name is never referenced anywhere in the repo, including tests. A grep across `celery` and `t` only finds the import and the fallback assignment.\n\nIt looks like a leftover from before the backend's delete handling changed. Removing it has no behaviour change. I'll send a PR.\n", "hints_text": "", "created_at": "2026-10-17T07:11:33Z"}
{"repo": "celery/celery", "pull_number": 10876, "instance_id": "celery__celery-10876", "issue_numbers": ["10875"], "base_commit": "8c12eb50fd50c4d4402b25e6f58d2ff06cbf048e", "patch": "diff --git a/celery/worker/strategy.py b/celery/worker/strategy.py\nindex 7f56e0f5c2c..a5f1b1685b7 100644\n--- a/celery/worker/strategy.py\n+++ b/celery/worker/strategy.py\n@@ -186,7 +186,12 @@ def task_message_handler(message, body, ack, reject, callbacks,\n             except (OverflowError, ValueError) as exc:\n                 error(\"Couldn't convert ETA %r to timestamp: %r. Task: %r\",\n                       req.eta, exc, req.info(safe=True), exc_info=True)\n+                req.task.backend.mark_as_failure(\n+                    req.id, exc, request=req._context,\n+                    store_result=req.store_errors,\n+                )\n                 req.reject(requeue=False)\n+                return\n         if rate_limits_enabled:\n             bucket = get_bucket(task.name)\n \n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/t/unit/worker/test_strategy.py b/t/unit/worker/test_strategy.py\nindex 8bf8d07ad0c..103235939c9 100644\n--- a/t/unit/worker/test_strategy.py\n+++ b/t/unit/worker/test_strategy.py\n@@ -324,6 +324,81 @@ def test_when_revoked(self):\n         finally:\n             state.revoked.discard(task.id)\n \n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize(\"exception_type\", [OverflowError, ValueError])\n+    def test_eta_conversion_error_rejects_without_executing(self, exception_type):\n+        # Regression test: when ETA timestamp conversion raises OverflowError\n+        # or ValueError, the worker must reject the message and NOT execute\n+        # the task.\n+        with patch('celery.worker.strategy.to_timestamp') as mock_to_timestamp:\n+            mock_to_timestamp.side_effect = exception_type(\n+                \"timestamp conversion failed\"\n+            )\n+\n+            with self._context(self.add.s(2, 2).set(countdown=10)) as C:\n+                C()\n+\n+            mock_to_timestamp.assert_called_once()\n+\n+            C.message.reject.assert_called_once()\n+            assert C.message.reject.call_args[0][2] is False\n+\n+            assert not C.was_reserved()\n+            C.consumer.on_task_request.assert_not_called()\n+\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize(\"exception_type\", [OverflowError, ValueError])\n+    def test_eta_conversion_error_records_failure_in_backend(self, exception_type):\n+        # Regression test: when ETA timestamp conversion raises OverflowError\n+        # or ValueError, the worker must record a terminal FAILURE result in\n+        # the backend so the task doesn't remain PENDING forever.\n+        with patch('celery.worker.strategy.to_timestamp') as mock_to_timestamp:\n+            mock_to_timestamp.side_effect = exception_type(\n+                \"timestamp conversion failed\"\n+            )\n+\n+            # Get task_id before context manager (signature is frozen in _context)\n+            task = self.add.s(2, 2).set(countdown=10)\n+            task.freeze()\n+            task_id = task.id\n+\n+            with self._context(task) as C:\n+                C()\n+\n+            # Verify the observable result state is FAILURE\n+            from celery import states\n+            assert self.add.backend.get_state(task_id) == states.FAILURE\n+            # Verify the exception is stored as the result\n+            result = self.add.backend.get_result(task_id)\n+            assert isinstance(result, exception_type)\n+\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize(\"exception_type\", [OverflowError, ValueError])\n+    def test_eta_conversion_error_with_disabled_backend(self, exception_type):\n+        # Regression test: when ETA timestamp conversion fails with a\n+        # DisabledBackend, the worker must still reject and return without\n+        # crashing (DisabledBackend.store_result is a no-op).\n+        from celery.backends.base import DisabledBackend\n+\n+        # Temporarily replace the task's backend with DisabledBackend\n+        original_backend = self.add.backend\n+        self.add.backend = DisabledBackend(app=self.app)\n+\n+        try:\n+            with patch('celery.worker.strategy.to_timestamp') as mock_to_timestamp:\n+                mock_to_timestamp.side_effect = exception_type(\n+                    \"timestamp conversion failed\"\n+                )\n+\n+                with self._context(self.add.s(2, 2).set(countdown=10)) as C:\n+                    # Should not raise even with DisabledBackend\n+                    C()\n+\n+                # Verify the message was still rejected\n+                C.message.reject.assert_called_once()\n+                assert C.message.reject.call_args[0][2] is False\n+                assert not C.was_reserved()\n+        finally:\n+            # Restore original backend\n+            self.add.backend = original_backend\n+\n \n class test_default_strategy_proto1(test_default_strategy_proto2):\n \n", "problem_statement": "Worker executes task after rejecting it when ETA timestamp conversion fails\n\n# Checklist\n\n<!--\nTo check an item on the list replace [ ] with [x].\n-->\n\n* [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `main` branch of Celery.\n* [ ] This has already been asked to the [[discussions forum](https://github.com/celery/celery/discussions)](https://github.com/celery/celery/discussions) first.\n* [x] I have read the relevant section in the\n  [[contribution guide](https://docs.celeryq.dev/en/main/contributing.html#other-bugs)](https://docs.celeryq.dev/en/main/contributing.html#other-bugs)\n  on reporting bugs.\n* [x] I have checked the [[issues list](https://github.com/celery/celery/issues?q=is%3Aissue+label%3A%22Issue+Type%3A+Bug+Report%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)](https://github.com/celery/celery/issues?q=is%3Aissue+label%3A%22Issue+Type%3A+Bug+Report%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)\n  for similar or identical bug reports.\n* [x] I have checked the [[pull requests list](https://github.com/celery/celery/pulls?q=is%3Apr+label%3A%22PR+Type%3A+Bugfix%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)](https://github.com/celery/celery/pulls?q=is%3Apr+label%3A%22PR+Type%3A+Bugfix%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)\n  for existing proposed fixes.\n* [x] I have checked the [[commit log](https://github.com/celery/celery/commits/main)](https://github.com/celery/celery/commits/main)\n  to find out if the bug was already fixed in the main branch.\n* [x] I have included all related issues and possible duplicate issues\n  in this issue (If there are none, check this box anyway).\n* [ ] I have tried to reproduce the issue with [[pytest-celery](https://docs.celeryq.dev/projects/pytest-celery/en/latest/userguide/celery-bug-report.html)](https://docs.celeryq.dev/projects/pytest-celery/en/latest/userguide/celery-bug-report.html) and added the reproduction script below.\n\n## Mandatory Debugging Information\n\n* [ ] I have included the output of `celery -A proj report` in the issue.\n  (if you are not able to do this, then at least specify the Celery\n  version affected).\n* [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `main` branch of Celery.\n* [ ] I have included the contents of `pip freeze` in the issue.\n* [x] I have included all the versions of all the external dependencies required\n  to reproduce this bug.\n\n## Optional Debugging Information\n\n* [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one Python version\n  and/or implementation.\n* [x] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one message broker and/or\n  result backend.\n* [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one version of the message\n  broker and/or result backend.\n* [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one operating system.\n* [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one workers pool.\n* [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue with autoscaling, retries,\n  ETA/Countdown & rate limits disabled.\n* [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue after downgrading\n  and/or upgrading Celery and its dependencies.\n\n## Related Issues and Possible Duplicates\n\n#### Related Issues\n\n* None found.\n\n#### Possible Duplicates\n\n* None found.\n\n## Environment & Settings\n\n**Celery version**: `5.7.0b1`\n\n<details>\n<summary><b><code>celery report</code> Output:</b></summary>\n<p>\n\n```text\nsoftware -> celery:5.7.0b1 (collider) kombu:5.7.0b1 py:3.13.15\n            billiard:4.3.1 py-amqp:5.4.1\nplatform -> system:Windows arch:64bit, WindowsPE\n            kernel version:11 imp:CPython\nloader   -> celery.loaders.default.Loader\nsettings -> transport:amqp results:disabled\n\ndeprecated_settings: None\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\nThe issue was reproduced against the current `main` branch corresponding to\nCelery `5.7.0b1`.\n\n# Steps to Reproduce\n\n## Required Dependencies\n\n* **Python**: 3.13.15\n* **Celery**: 5.7.0b1 / current `main`\n* **Kombu**: 5.7.0b1\n* **Billiard**: 4.3.1\n* **py-amqp**: 5.4.1\n* **OS**: Windows 11, 64-bit\n* **Broker**: The behavior can be reproduced with an in-process `memory://` broker; the `celery report` above shows the development environment's default AMQP transport.\n* **Result backend**: Not required\n\n### Python Packages\n\nThe relevant dependency versions from `celery report` are:\n\n```text\ncelery==5.7.0b1\nkombu==5.7.0b1\nbilliard==4.3.1\npy-amqp==5.4.1\nPython 3.13.15\n```\n\nA complete `pip freeze` can be added separately if required.\n\n### Other Dependencies\n\nNo external result backend is required.\n\nThe issue is in the worker's ETA handling in\n`celery/worker/strategy.py`.\n\nA `memory://` broker is sufficient to reproduce the control-flow issue.\n\n## Minimally Reproducible Test Case\n\nThe problematic code path is:\n\n```python\ntry:\n    eta = to_timestamp(to_system_tz(req.eta))\nexcept (OverflowError, ValueError) as exc:\n    error(...)\n    req.reject(requeue=False)\n```\n\nThere is no `return` after `req.reject(requeue=False)`.\n\nWhen ETA conversion raises `OverflowError` or `ValueError`, execution therefore\ncontinues into the normal task-processing path:\n\n```python\ntask_reserved(req)\nhandle(req)\n```\n\nFor example, with the worker timezone set to `Asia/Kolkata`, an ETA such as:\n\n```text\n9999-12-31T23:59:59+00:00\n```\n\ncan raise `OverflowError` during timezone conversion/timestamp conversion.\n\nThe observed behavior is:\n\n```text\nCouldn't convert ETA ...\nRequest.reject(requeue=False)\ntask_reserved(req)\nhandle(req)\n```\n\nThe task is consequently executed immediately even though its message has\nalready been rejected.\n\n## Expected Behavior\n\nWhen ETA conversion fails with `OverflowError` or `ValueError`, the worker\nshould reject the message and stop processing that request.\n\nAfter:\n\n```python\nreq.reject(requeue=False)\n```\n\nthe worker should not reserve or execute the task.\n\n## Actual Behavior\n\nThe worker rejects the message but then continues processing the same request.\n\nAs a result, the task can execute immediately despite having just been rejected\nwith `requeue=False`.\n\nThe problematic control flow is:\n\n```python\nexcept (OverflowError, ValueError) as exc:\n    error(...)\n    req.reject(requeue=False)\n\n# execution incorrectly continues\ntask_reserved(req)\nhandle(req)\n```\n\nThe minimal fix is to return immediately after rejecting the request:\n\n```diff\n except (OverflowError, ValueError) as exc:\n     error(...)\n     req.reject(requeue=False)\n+    return\n```\n\nI reproduced the issue against the current `main` branch. The regression tests\nfail on the original implementation and pass with this control-flow fix.\n\n", "hints_text": "come with a new pr if anything related is not open already in the PR Q", "created_at": "2026-10-17T06:12:24Z"}
{"repo": "celery/celery", "pull_number": 10866, "instance_id": "celery__celery-10866", "issue_numbers": ["10865"], "base_commit": "c45016323d44867d291e3e7e94d4036ea0a37ac1", "patch": "diff --git a/celery/backends/database/__init__.py b/celery/backends/database/__init__.py\nindex 2cd19905223..e0d7523b23f 100644\n--- a/celery/backends/database/__init__.py\n+++ b/celery/backends/database/__init__.py\n@@ -2,6 +2,7 @@\n import logging\n import pickle\n from contextlib import contextmanager\n+from datetime import datetime, timezone\n \n from kombu.utils.encoding import ensure_bytes\n \n@@ -334,7 +335,7 @@ def cleanup(self):\n     def _cleanup(self):\n         session = self.ResultSession()\n         expires = self.expires\n-        now = self.app.now()\n+        now = datetime.now(timezone.utc)\n         with session_cleanup(session):\n             session.query(self.task_cls).filter(\n                 self.task_cls.date_done < (now - expires)).delete()\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/t/unit/backends/test_database.py b/t/unit/backends/test_database.py\nindex e404d01c3fc..33c52ed707c 100644\n--- a/t/unit/backends/test_database.py\n+++ b/t/unit/backends/test_database.py\n@@ -1,6 +1,6 @@\n import json\n import os\n-from datetime import datetime\n+from datetime import datetime, timedelta, timezone\n from pickle import dumps, loads\n from unittest.mock import Mock, patch\n \n@@ -866,6 +866,50 @@ def test_cleanup(self):\n \n         tb.cleanup()\n \n+    def test_cleanup_respects_timezone_for_expiration(self):\n+        \"\"\"Regression test for timezone bug where cleanup uses app.now() while date_done is UTC.\"\"\"\n+        original_timezone = self.app.conf.timezone\n+        original_expires = self.app.conf.result_expires\n+\n+        try:\n+            self.app.conf.timezone = 'Asia/Tokyo'\n+            self.app.conf.result_expires = 86400\n+\n+            tb = DatabaseBackend(self.uri, app=self.app)\n+\n+            unexpired_task_id = uuid()\n+            expired_task_id = uuid()\n+            tb.mark_as_done(unexpired_task_id, 42)\n+            tb.mark_as_done(expired_task_id, 100)\n+\n+            session = tb.ResultSession()\n+            now = datetime.now(timezone.utc)\n+\n+            session.query(Task).filter(\n+                Task.task_id == unexpired_task_id\n+            ).first().date_done = now - timedelta(hours=20)\n+\n+            session.query(Task).filter(\n+                Task.task_id == expired_task_id\n+            ).first().date_done = now - timedelta(hours=30)\n+\n+            session.commit()\n+            session.close()\n+\n+            tb.cleanup()\n+\n+            session = tb.ResultSession()\n+            assert session.query(Task).filter(\n+                Task.task_id == unexpired_task_id\n+            ).first() is not None\n+            assert session.query(Task).filter(\n+                Task.task_id == expired_task_id\n+            ).first() is None\n+            session.close()\n+        finally:\n+            self.app.conf.timezone = original_timezone\n+            self.app.conf.result_expires = original_expires\n+\n     def test_Task__repr__(self):\n         assert 'foo' in repr(Task('foo'))\n \n", "problem_statement": "Database backend cleanup can delete task results before result_expires with non-UTC timezones\n# Checklist\n\n* [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `main` branch of Celery.\n* [ ] This has already been asked to the [[discussions forum](https://github.com/celery/celery/discussions)](https://github.com/celery/celery/discussions) first.\n* [x] I have read the relevant section in the [[contribution guide](https://docs.celeryq.dev/en/main/contributing.html#other-bugs)](https://docs.celeryq.dev/en/main/contributing.html#other-bugs) on reporting bugs.\n* [x] I have checked the [[issues list](https://github.com/celery/celery/issues?q=is%3Aissue+label%3A%22Issue+Type%3A+Bug+Report%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)](https://github.com/celery/celery/issues?q=is%3Aissue+label%3A%22Issue+Type%3A+Bug+Report%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22) for similar or identical bug reports.\n* [x] I have checked the [[pull requests list](https://github.com/celery/celery/pulls?q=is%3Apr+label%3A%22PR+Type%3A+Bugfix%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)](https://github.com/celery/celery/pulls?q=is%3Apr+label%3A%22PR+Type%3A+Bugfix%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22) for existing proposed fixes.\n* [x] I have checked the [[commit log](https://github.com/celery/celery/commits/main)](https://github.com/celery/celery/commits/main) to find out if the bug was already fixed in the main branch.\n* [x] I have included all related issues and possible duplicate issues in this issue (If there are none, check this box anyway).\n* [ ] I have tried to reproduce the issue with [[pytest-celery](https://docs.celeryq.dev/projects/pytest-celery/en/latest/userguide/celery-bug-report.html)](https://docs.celeryq.dev/projects/pytest-celery/en/latest/userguide/celery-bug-report.html) and added the reproduction script below.\n\n## Mandatory Debugging Information\n\n* [x] I have included the output of `celery -A proj report` in the issue.\n* [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `main` branch of Celery.\n* [x] I have included the contents of `pip freeze` in the issue.\n* [x] I have included all the versions of all the external dependencies required to reproduce this bug.\n\n## Optional Debugging Information\n\n* [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one Python version and/or implementation.\n* [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one message broker and/or result backend.\n* [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one version of the message broker and/or result backend.\n* [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one operating system.\n* [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one workers pool.\n* [x] I have tried reproducing the issue with autoscaling, retries, ETA/Countdown & rate limits disabled.\n* [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue after downgrading and/or upgrading Celery and its dependencies.\n\n## Related Issues and Possible Duplicates\n\n#### Related Issues\n\n* None found.\n\n#### Possible Duplicates\n\n* None found.\n\n## Environment & Settings\n\n**Celery version:** `5.7.0b1`\n\n<details>\n<summary><b><code>celery report</code> Output</b></summary>\n\n```text\nsoftware -> celery:5.7.0b1 (collider) kombu:5.7.0b1 py:3.13.15\n            billiard:4.3.1 py-amqp:5.4.1\nplatform -> system:Windows arch:64bit, WindowsPE\n            kernel version:11 imp:CPython\nloader   -> celery.loaders.app.AppLoader\nsettings -> transport:amqp results:rpc://\n\nbroker_url: 'amqp://guest:********@localhost:5672//'\nresult_backend: 'rpc://'\ninclude: ['proj.tasks']\ndeprecated_settings: None\nresult_expires: 3600\n```\n\n</details>\n\n> The issue itself is reproduced with the SQLAlchemy database backend using SQLite. The `rpc://` backend above is from the example application used to generate the report.\n\n# Steps to Reproduce\n\n## Required Dependencies\n\n* **Python:** 3.13.15\n* **Celery:** 5.7.0b1\n* **Kombu:** 5.7.0b1\n* **OS:** Windows 11, 64-bit\n* **Result backend:** SQLAlchemy + SQLite\n* **SQLAlchemy:** 2.0.46\n\n### Python Packages\n\n<details>\n<summary><b><code>pip freeze</code> Output</b></summary>\n\n```text\namqp==5.4.1\nbilliard==4.3.1\ncelery==5.7.0b1\nclick==8.4.2\nkombu==5.7.0b1\nmsgpack==1.1.2\nmsgspec==0.21.0\npytest==9.1.1\npytest-celery==1.3.0\nredis==6.4.0\nSQLAlchemy==2.0.46\nvine==5.1.0\n```\n\n</details>\n\n### Configuration\n\n```python\napp.conf.update(\n    timezone=\"Asia/Tokyo\",\n    result_expires=86400,\n)\n```\n\nThe issue does not require retries, ETA/countdown, rate limits, or autoscaling.\n\n## Minimally Reproducible Test Case\n\n```python\nfrom celery import Celery\n\napp = Celery(\n    \"reproduce\",\n    broker=\"memory://\",\n    backend=\"db+sqlite:///results.sqlite\",\n)\n\napp.conf.update(\n    timezone=\"Asia/Tokyo\",\n    result_expires=86400,\n)\n```\n\nCreate a task result that is less than 24 hours old, run the database backend cleanup, and inspect whether the result remains.\n\nWith the affected implementation, a result younger than `result_expires` can be deleted when the application timezone is east of UTC.\n\n# Expected Behavior\n\n`result_expires` should represent an elapsed retention period independent of the configured timezone.\n\nFor example, with:\n\n```python\ntimezone = \"Asia/Tokyo\"\nresult_expires = 86400\n```\n\na result that is 20 hours old should remain after `celery.backend_cleanup`.\n\n# Actual Behavior\n\nWith timezones east of UTC, the database backend can delete results before `result_expires` has elapsed.\n\n| Timezone         | Result age | Expected | Actual      |\n| ---------------- | ---------: | -------- | ----------- |\n| UTC              |        20h | Keep     | Keep        |\n| Asia/Kolkata     |        20h | Keep     | **Deleted** |\n| Asia/Tokyo       |        17h | Keep     | **Deleted** |\n| Europe/Berlin    |       ~23h | Keep     | **Deleted** |\n| America/New_York |        20h | Keep     | Keep        |\n\nThe deletion is silent. After cleanup, `AsyncResult` can return `PENDING` because the stored result has already been removed.\n\n### Suspected Cause\n\n`DatabaseBackend._cleanup()` calculates its cutoff using:\n\n```python\nnow = self.app.now()\n```\n\nwhile `date_done` is stored using UTC:\n\n```python\ndatetime.now(timezone.utc)\n```\n\nThe cleanup cutoff should use UTC as well, so both timestamps use the same time basis:\n\n```python\nnow = datetime.now(timezone.utc)\n```\n\nThis would make database result expiration independent of the application's configured timezone.\n", "hints_text": "", "created_at": "2026-10-15T05:20:16Z"}
{"repo": "celery/celery", "pull_number": 10861, "instance_id": "celery__celery-10861", "issue_numbers": ["10858"], "base_commit": "f5dbef350688eb7250cdf1e389db93ea9cc605fd", "patch": "diff --git a/celery/concurrency/asynpool.py b/celery/concurrency/asynpool.py\nindex 8c1454d859c..0bf701b177a 100644\n--- a/celery/concurrency/asynpool.py\n+++ b/celery/concurrency/asynpool.py\n@@ -1468,6 +1468,8 @@ def _help_stuff_finish(cls, pool):\n         fileno_to_proc = {}\n         inqR = set()\n         for w in pool:\n+            if w.exitcode is not None:\n+                continue  # nothing will ever read this inqueue again\n             try:\n                 fd = w.inq._reader.fileno()\n                 inqR.add(fd)\n@@ -1481,7 +1483,11 @@ def _help_stuff_finish(cls, pool):\n             if not readable:\n                 break\n             for fd in readable:\n-                fileno_to_proc[fd].inq._reader.recv()\n+                try:\n+                    fileno_to_proc[fd].inq._reader.recv()\n+                except (EOFError, OSError):\n+                    # the child died mid-message; nothing will read this pipe again\n+                    inqR.discard(fd)\n             sleep(0)\n \n     @property\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/t/unit/concurrency/test_prefork.py b/t/unit/concurrency/test_prefork.py\nindex dc758f094c9..b3fc97229ea 100644\n--- a/t/unit/concurrency/test_prefork.py\n+++ b/t/unit/concurrency/test_prefork.py\n@@ -1353,6 +1353,42 @@ def test_finish_partial_read_not_registered_with_event_loop(self):\n         handler = make_result_handler(3, proc)\n         assert handler._finish_partial_read(3, proc) is False\n \n+    @t.skip.if_win32\n+    def test_help_stuff_finish_skips_exited_processes(self):\n+        # A child killed between reading the 4-byte length and the body of\n+        # its None sentinel leaves only the pickle body in its inqueue.\n+        def proc(exitcode, data):\n+            inq = asynpool._SimpleQueue(wnonblock=True)\n+            os.write(inq._writer.fileno(), data)\n+            return Bunch(exitcode=exitcode, inq=inq)\n+\n+        exited = proc(15, pickle.dumps(None))\n+        alive = proc(None, pack('>I', len(pickle.dumps(None))) + pickle.dumps(None))\n+        try:\n+            asynpool.AsynPool._help_stuff_finish([exited, alive])\n+            assert not select.select([alive.inq._reader], [], [], 0)[0]\n+        finally:\n+            for p in (exited, alive):\n+                p.inq.close()\n+\n+    @t.skip.if_win32\n+    def test_help_stuff_finish_survives_truncated_message_from_live_process(self):\n+        # A child can still look alive when its exitcode is checked and then\n+        # die mid-way through reading its sentinel, leaving only the body.\n+        def proc(exitcode, data):\n+            inq = asynpool._SimpleQueue(wnonblock=True)\n+            os.write(inq._writer.fileno(), data)\n+            return Bunch(exitcode=exitcode, inq=inq)\n+\n+        dying = proc(None, pickle.dumps(None))\n+        alive = proc(None, pack('>I', len(pickle.dumps(None))) + pickle.dumps(None))\n+        try:\n+            asynpool.AsynPool._help_stuff_finish([dying, alive])\n+            assert not select.select([alive.inq._reader], [], [], 0)[0]\n+        finally:\n+            for p in (dying, alive):\n+                p.inq.close()\n+\n \n class test_TaskPool:\n \n", "problem_statement": "Prefork: EOFError: Ran out of input from _help_stuff_finish at exit when pool children get SIGTERM during warm shutdown\n<!--\nPlease fill this template entirely and do not erase parts of it.\nWe reserve the right to close without a response\nbug reports which are incomplete.\n-->\n# Checklist\n<!--\nTo check an item on the list replace [ ] with [x].\n-->\n- [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `main` branch of Celery.\n- [ ] This has already been asked to the [discussions forum](https://github.com/celery/celery/discussions) first.\n- [x] I have read the relevant section in the\n  [contribution guide](https://docs.celeryq.dev/en/main/contributing.html#other-bugs)\n  on reporting bugs.\n- [x] I have checked the [issues list](https://github.com/celery/celery/issues?q=is%3Aissue+label%3A%22Issue+Type%3A+Bug+Report%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)\n  for similar or identical bug reports.\n- [x] I have checked the [pull requests list](https://github.com/celery/celery/pulls?q=is%3Apr+label%3A%22PR+Type%3A+Bugfix%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)\n  for existing proposed fixes.\n- [x] I have checked the [commit log](https://github.com/celery/celery/commits/main)\n  to find out if the bug was already fixed in the main branch.\n- [x] I have included all related issues and possible duplicate issues\n  in this issue (If there are none, check this box anyway).\n- [ ] I have tried to reproduce the issue with [pytest-celery](https://docs.celeryq.dev/projects/pytest-celery/en/latest/userguide/celery-bug-report.html) and added the reproduction script below.\n\n## Mandatory Debugging Information\n\n- [x] I have included the output of ``celery -A proj report`` in the issue.\n    (if you are not able to do this, then at least specify the Celery\n     version affected).\n- [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `main` branch of Celery.\n- [x] I have included the contents of ``pip freeze`` in the issue.\n- [x] I have included all the versions of all the external dependencies required\n  to reproduce this bug.\n\n## Optional Debugging Information\n<!--\nTry some of the below if you think they are relevant.\nIt will help us figure out the scope of the bug and how many users it affects.\n-->\n- [x] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one Python version\n  and/or implementation.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one message broker and/or\n  result backend.\n- [x] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one version of the message\n  broker and/or result backend.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one operating system.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one workers pool.\n- [x] I have tried reproducing the issue with autoscaling, retries,\n  ETA/Countdown & rate limits disabled.\n- [x] I have tried reproducing the issue after downgrading\n  and/or upgrading Celery and its dependencies.\n\nNotes on the boxes above:\n- Python 3.14.8 and 3.13.16, CPython only.\n- Redis 7.0.15 locally and 7.4 (Docker `redis:7.4-alpine`, Azure Managed Redis). Only Redis was tried, but the broker isn't involved: the failing read is on the prefork pool's own pipes.\n- Linux only. The bug is specific to the prefork pool (`AsynPool`), so other pools don't apply.\n- The reproduction app has no tasks, autoscaling, retries, ETA or rate limits.\n- Reproduced on celery 5.6.3 and `main` (155a8f7, 5.7.0b1), and on billiard 4.3.0 and 4.3.1.\n\n## Related Issues and Possible Duplicates\n<!--\nPlease make sure to search and mention any related issues\nor possible duplicates to this issue as requested by the checklist above.\n\nThis may or may not include issues in other repositories that the Celery project\nmaintains or other repositories that are dependencies of Celery.\n\nIf you don't know how to mention issues, please refer to Github's documentation\non the subject: https://help.github.com/en/articles/autolinked-references-and-urls#issues-and-pull-requests\n-->\n\n#### Related Issues\n\n- celery/billiard#434 and celery/billiard#435 changed `Pool._terminate_pool`'s shutdown sequence, which is where this traceback starts. They don't touch `_help_stuff_finish`.\n- billiard 4.3.1, released 2026-10-05, also fixes a related problem on the same platforms. A child that receives SIGTERM while running a task no longer reports it as failed, so with `task_acks_late` the task is requeued (`WorkerLostError`) instead of acknowledged and lost. That release doesn't change the EOFError below.\n\n#### Possible Duplicates\n\n- None. #5013 mentions `_help_stuff_finish` only as a debug log line in an unrelated, unexplained worker exit. #3780 and #3559 (\"Ran out of input\") are Windows spawn pickling errors.\n\n## Environment & Settings\n<!-- Include the contents of celery --version below -->\n**Celery version**: 5.7.0b1 (collider), installed from `main` at 155a8f720b01ebd7999b34e525c121b8f824ac73; also 5.6.3 (recovery)\n<!-- Include the output of celery -A proj report below -->\n<details>\n<summary><b><code>celery report</code> Output:</b></summary>\n<p>\n\n```\nsoftware -> celery:5.7.0b1 (collider) kombu:5.7.0b1 py:3.14.8\n            billiard:4.3.1 redis:8.1.0\nplatform -> system:Linux arch:64bit, ELF\n            kernel version:6.18.44-fc-v114 imp:CPython\nloader   -> celery.loaders.app.AppLoader\nsettings -> transport:redis results:disabled\n\nbroker_url: 'redis://:********@localhost:6390/0'\ndeprecated_settings: None\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n# Steps to Reproduce\n\n## Required Dependencies\n<!-- Please fill the required dependencies to reproduce this issue -->\n- **Minimal Python Version**: Unknown (reproduced on 3.13.16 and 3.14.8)\n- **Minimal Celery Version**: Unknown (reproduced on 5.6.3 and `main`)\n- **Minimal Kombu Version**: Unknown (reproduced on 5.6.2 and 5.7.0b1)\n- **Minimal Broker Version**: N/A (reproduced on Redis 7.0.15 and 7.4; the broker isn't involved)\n- **Minimal Result Backend Version**: N/A (results disabled)\n- **Minimal OS and/or Kernel Version**: Unknown (Linux 6.18; also Debian trixie containers, `python:3.14.8-slim`)\n- **Minimal Broker Client Version**: N/A (redis-py 6.4.0 and 8.1.0)\n- **Minimal Result Backend Client Version**: N/A\n\n### Python Packages\n<!-- Please fill the contents of pip freeze below -->\n<details>\n<summary><b><code>pip freeze</code> Output:</b></summary>\n<p>\n\n```\namqp==5.4.1\nbilliard==4.3.1\ncelery @ git+https://github.com/celery/celery@155a8f720b01ebd7999b34e525c121b8f824ac73\nclick==8.5.0\nclick-didyoumean==0.3.1\nclick-plugins==1.1.1.2\nclick-repl==0.4.1\nkombu==5.7.0b1\npackaging==26.3\nprompt-toolkit==3.0.53\npython-dateutil==2.9.0.post0\nredis==8.1.0\nsix==1.17.0\ntyping-extensions==4.16.0\ntzdata==2026.4\ntzlocal==5.4.4\nvine==5.1.0\nwcwidth==0.9.2\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n### Other Dependencies\n<!--\nPlease provide system dependencies, configuration files\nand other dependency information if applicable\n-->\n<details>\n<p>\nA Redis server, used only as the broker: `redis-server --port 6390 --save ''`. No configuration files.\n</p>\n</details>\n\n## Minimally Reproducible Test Case\n<!--\nPlease provide a reproducible test case.\nRefer to the Reporting Bugs section in our contribution guide.\n\nWe prefer submitting test cases in the form of a PR to our integration test suite.\nIf you can provide one, please mention the PR number below.\nIf not, please attach the most minimal code example required to reproduce the issue below.\nIf the test case is too large, please include a link to a gist or a repository below.\n\nAlternatively, the pytest-celery plugin can be used to create standalone reproduction scripts\nthat can be added to this report. See the pytest-celery documentation for more information at\npytest-celery.readthedocs.io\n-->\n\n<details>\n<p>\n\nSome container platforms deliver SIGTERM to every process in the container; we hit this on Azure Container Apps. The script stands in for that by sending SIGTERM to the pool children from `AsynPool._stop_task_handler`, just after warm shutdown's `Pool.join()` has written each child's `None` sentinel.\n\n```python\n# repro_app.py\nimport os\nimport signal\n\nfrom celery import Celery\nfrom celery.concurrency import asynpool\n\napp = Celery(\"repro\", broker=os.environ.get(\"BROKER\", \"redis://localhost:6379/0\"))\n\n_original = asynpool.AsynPool._stop_task_handler\n_joined = []\n\n\ndef _stop_task_handler(task_handler):\n    _original(task_handler)  # writes the 8-byte None sentinel to each child's inqueue\n\n    if not _joined:  # only the warm-shutdown join, not the atexit finalizer's second call\n        _joined.append(True)\n\n        for proc in task_handler.pool:\n            os.kill(proc.pid, signal.SIGTERM)\n\n\nasynpool.AsynPool._stop_task_handler = staticmethod(_stop_task_handler)\n```\n\n```bash\nredis-server --port 6390 --save '' --daemonize yes\nBROKER=redis://localhost:6390/0 celery -A repro_app worker --concurrency=2 --loglevel=warning & pid=$!\nsleep 4; kill -TERM $pid; wait $pid; echo \"exit $?\"\n```\n\nResult: the traceback below in 5 of 5 runs on `main` with Python 3.14.8, and in 5 of 5 runs on celery 5.6.3 with Python 3.13.16.\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n# Expected Behavior\n<!-- Describe in detail what you expect to happen -->\n\nThe warm shutdown completes and the worker exits without a traceback. The pool has already been closed and joined, and every child has exited, so nothing in a child's inqueue needs draining.\n\n# Actual Behavior\n<!--\nDescribe in detail what actually happened.\nPlease include a backtrace and surround it with triple backticks (```).\nIn addition, include the Celery daemon logs, the broker logs,\nthe result backend logs and system logs below if they will help us debug\nthe issue.\n-->\n\nAbout 2 seconds after `worker: Warm shutdown (MainProcess)`, the worker prints the following (from `main`). The process still exits 0, because `multiprocessing.util._run_finalizers` catches and prints the exception. But on stderr it reads as an error, and log pipelines report it on every deploy.\n\n```\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \".../lib/python3.14/multiprocessing/util.py\", line 371, in _run_finalizers\n    finalizer()\n    ~~~~~~~~~^^\n  File \".../lib/python3.14/multiprocessing/util.py\", line 295, in __call__\n    res = self._callback(*self._args, **self._kwargs)\n  File \".../site-packages/billiard/pool.py\", line 1738, in _terminate_pool\n    cls._help_stuff_finish(*help_stuff_finish_args)\n    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n  File \".../site-packages/celery/concurrency/asynpool.py\", line 1484, in _help_stuff_finish\n    fileno_to_proc[fd].inq._reader.recv()\n    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~^^\n  File \".../site-packages/billiard/connection.py\", line 276, in recv\n    return ForkingPickler.loadbuf(buf)\n           ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~^^^^^\n  File \".../site-packages/billiard/reduction.py\", line 60, in loadbuf\n    return cls.loads(buf.getbuffer())\n           ~~~~~~~~~^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\nEOFError: Ran out of input\n```\n\n### Cause\n\n1. Warm shutdown runs `TaskPool.on_stop` \u2192 `Pool.close()` + `Pool.join()`. `join()` calls `AsynPool._stop_task_handler`, which writes a `None` sentinel to each child's inqueue: a 4-byte `!i` length followed by the 4-byte pickle `b'\\x80\\x05N.'`.\n2. A child that receives SIGTERM as it wakes to read the sentinel can read the 4-byte header before its terminate handler (`billiard.common._shutdown_cleanup`) runs. Linux's `pipe_read` returns available data before checking for pending signals, and Python runs the handler at the next bytecode boundary, between `_recv(4)` and `_recv(size)`. The child exits and the 4-byte body stays in the pipe.\n3. `on_stop` never calls `Pool.terminate()`, so the pool's `Finalize(_terminate_pool, exitpriority=15)` runs at interpreter exit, on the already-joined pool. `_help_stuff_finish` selects on every child's inqueue, including those of children that have exited, and calls `recv()` on the readable one.\n4. `recv()` reads `b'\\x80\\x05N.'` as the length header (-2147135954), so `_recv(size)` returns an empty buffer and `pickle.loads(b'')` raises `EOFError: Ran out of input`.\n\n### Suggested fix\n\nSkip children that have already exited, since nothing will ever read their inqueues again:\n\n```python\n    @classmethod\n    def _help_stuff_finish(cls, pool):\n        ...\n        for w in pool:\n            if w.exitcode is not None:\n                continue  # nothing will ever read this inqueue again\n            try:\n                fd = w.inq._reader.fileno()\n```\n\nWith this applied as a monkeypatch to the reproduction above, the traceback went away: 0 of 5 runs, exit code 0.\n\nTwo alternatives, both untested: `TaskPool.on_stop` could cancel the pool's finalizer (`self._pool._terminate.cancel()`) once `join()` returns, or billiard's `Pool.join()` could do the same. I'm happy to open a PR for whichever approach you prefer.\n\n", "hints_text": "@GangEunzzang can you help with this?\nReproduced it on Linux (Docker, Python 3.13, Redis 7.4) with the script from the report: 20 of 20 runs on `main` (8e1621c, billiard 4.3.1), and 10 of 10 on celery 5.3.6 with billiard 4.2.0, so it isn't a recent regression. Dumping each child's inqueue at finalize time shows exactly the leftover described: 4 bytes, `b'\\x80\\x04N.'` (`-2147201490` as a header), with the child's `exitcode` already 15.\n\nThe window is narrow, though. Without the injected kill, SIGTERM to the whole process group, or to the children 1 ms to 1 s after the main process, gave 0 in 170 runs, also with the container limited to 0.2 CPU. Delaying the injected kill by 50 \u00b5s after the sentinel write also gave 0 of 20. The child has to get the signal while it's waking up to read the sentinel.\n\nSkipping exited children in `AsynPool._help_stuff_finish` fixes it:\n\n| | `main` | with the `exitcode` check |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| report's repro | 20/20 tracebacks | 0/20 |\n| plain warm shutdown, idle worker | 2.6 s | 2.0 s |\n| warm shutdown, 4 tasks sent (`acks_late`, concurrency 2) | 2 done, 2 left in the queue, exit 0 | same |\n| cold shutdown (SIGQUIT), same tasks | 0 done, 4 left in the queue, exit 0 | same |\n\nThe shutdown gets faster because on a joined pool the drain loop otherwise waits out its 0.5 s `select` timeout on empty pipes. Cancelling the pool finalizer in `TaskPool.on_stop` after `join()` also gave 0/20 and the same timing, but it skips the rest of `_terminate_pool` too (closing the queues, stopping the result handler), so the `exitcode` check is the smaller change.\n\nA unit test that fails on `main` with this `EOFError` and passes with the check, in `test_AsynPool`:\n\n```python\n@t.skip.if_win32\ndef test_help_stuff_finish_skips_exited_processes(self):\n    # A child killed between reading the 4-byte length and the body of\n    # its None sentinel leaves only the pickle body in its inqueue.\n    def proc(exitcode, data):\n        inq = asynpool._SimpleQueue(wnonblock=True)\n        os.write(inq._writer.fileno(), data)\n        return Bunch(exitcode=exitcode, inq=inq)\n\n    exited = proc(15, pickle.dumps(None))\n    alive = proc(None, pack('>I', len(pickle.dumps(None))) + pickle.dumps(None))\n    try:\n        asynpool.AsynPool._help_stuff_finish([exited, alive])\n        assert not select.select([alive.inq._reader], [], [], 0)[0]\n    finally:\n        for p in (exited, alive):\n            p.inq.close()\n```\n\n@esauser, the `exitcode` check you suggested looks like the way to go if you want to open the PR.\n", "created_at": "2026-10-14T14:08:16Z"}
{"repo": "celery/celery", "pull_number": 10857, "instance_id": "celery__celery-10857", "issue_numbers": ["10856"], "base_commit": "0120a9ec17493a689c2f1eb5d1ef25f42656c918", "patch": "diff --git a/celery/canvas.py b/celery/canvas.py\nindex 420b8ee8765..2998e7bad62 100644\n--- a/celery/canvas.py\n+++ b/celery/canvas.py\n@@ -2089,7 +2089,7 @@ def _freeze_group_tasks(self, _id=None, group_id=None, chord=None,\n         try:\n             gid = opts['task_id']\n         except KeyError:\n-            gid = opts['task_id'] = group_id or uuid()\n+            gid = opts['task_id'] = uuid()\n         if group_id:\n             opts['group_id'] = group_id\n         if chord:\n@@ -2539,6 +2539,7 @@ def run(self, header, body, partial_args, app=None, interval=None,\n         options.pop('chord', None)\n         options.pop('task_id', None)\n \n+        header.options['task_id'] = group_id\n         header_result_args = header._freeze_group_tasks(group_id=group_id, chord=body, root_id=root_id)\n \n         if header.tasks:\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/t/integration/test_canvas.py b/t/integration/test_canvas.py\nindex 62cae8364a0..098c3d3d7a8 100644\n--- a/t/integration/test_canvas.py\n+++ b/t/integration/test_canvas.py\n@@ -1670,6 +1670,25 @@ def test_nested_group_chord_body_chain(self, manager):\n         assert res.children[0].get(timeout=TIMEOUT) == expected_result[0]\n         assert res.get(timeout=TIMEOUT) == expected_result\n \n+    def test_group_ending_a_chain_in_a_group_gets_its_own_id(self, manager):\n+        \"\"\"Regression test for https://github.com/celery/celery/issues/10856.\n+\n+        The inner group must not take the outer group's id as its own.\n+        \"\"\"\n+        try:\n+            manager.app.backend.ensure_chords_allowed()\n+        except NotImplementedError as e:\n+            raise pytest.skip(e.args[0])\n+\n+        res = group(\n+            identity.si(1),\n+            chain(identity.si(2), group(identity.si(3), identity.si(4))),\n+        ).apply_async()\n+        assert res.get(timeout=TIMEOUT) == [1, [3, 4]]\n+        ids = ids_in_as_tuple(res.as_tuple())\n+        # Two GroupResults and four tasks.\n+        assert len(ids) == len(set(ids)) == 6, ids\n+\n     def test_callback_called_by_group(self, manager, subtests):\n         if not manager.app.conf.result_backend.startswith(\"redis\"):\n             raise pytest.skip(\"Requires redis result backend.\")\n@@ -2600,6 +2619,22 @@ def test_nested_chord_group_chain_group_tail(self, manager):\n         res = sig.delay()\n         assert res.get(timeout=TIMEOUT) == [[42, 42]]\n \n+    def test_group_body_does_not_take_the_header_id(self, manager):\n+        \"\"\"Regression test for https://github.com/celery/celery/issues/10856.\"\"\"\n+        try:\n+            manager.app.backend.ensure_chords_allowed()\n+        except NotImplementedError as e:\n+            raise pytest.skip(e.args[0])\n+\n+        res = chord(\n+            group(identity.si(1), identity.si(2)),\n+            group(identity.si(3), identity.si(4)),\n+        ).apply_async()\n+        assert res.get(timeout=TIMEOUT) == [3, 4]\n+        ids = ids_in_as_tuple(res.as_tuple())\n+        # The header and body GroupResults and four tasks.\n+        assert len(ids) == len(set(ids)) == 6, ids\n+\n     @pytest.mark.xfail(TEST_BACKEND.startswith('redis://'), reason=\"Issue #6437\")\n     def test_error_propagates_from_chord(self, manager):\n         try:\ndiff --git a/t/unit/tasks/test_canvas.py b/t/unit/tasks/test_canvas.py\nindex a48557d17e3..d44e04dc2e4 100644\n--- a/t/unit/tasks/test_canvas.py\n+++ b/t/unit/tasks/test_canvas.py\n@@ -2041,6 +2041,19 @@ def test_apply_async_with_parent(self):\n         finally:\n             _task_stack.pop()\n \n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize('task_protocol', [2, 1])\n+    def test_group_ending_a_chain_in_a_group_gets_its_own_id(self, task_protocol):\n+        # group_id names the group a signature belongs to, so the inner\n+        # group must not adopt the outer group's id as its own.\n+        self.app.conf.task_protocol = task_protocol\n+        res = group(\n+            self.add.si(1, 1),\n+            chain(self.add.si(2, 2), group(self.add.si(3, 3), self.add.si(4, 4))),\n+        ).apply_async()\n+        inner = res.results[1]\n+        assert isinstance(inner, GroupResult)\n+        assert inner.id != res.id\n+\n     @pytest.mark.parametrize(\"group_type\", (group, group_subclass))\n     def test_from_dict(self, group_type):\n         x = group_type([self.add.s(2, 2), self.add.s(4, 4)])\n@@ -2499,6 +2512,14 @@ def _assert_header_received_delay(self, canvas, header_size, **delay):\n         # chord.unlock retries keep their own default countdown\n         assert mock_apply_chord.call_args.kwargs.get(\"countdown\") == 1\n \n+    def test_group_body_does_not_take_the_header_id(self):\n+        res = chord(\n+            group(self.add.si(1, 1), self.add.si(2, 2)),\n+            group(self.add.si(3, 3), self.add.si(4, 4)),\n+        ).apply_async()\n+        assert isinstance(res, GroupResult)\n+        assert res.id != res.parent.id\n+\n     def test_group_or_task_apply_async_countdown_delays_header(self):\n         \"\"\"countdown on group|task must delay the header, not only the body.\n \n", "problem_statement": "A group nested in a chain inside another group takes the outer group's id\n<!--\nPlease fill this template entirely and do not erase parts of it.\nWe reserve the right to close without a response\nbug reports which are incomplete.\n-->\n# Checklist\n- [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `main` branch of Celery.\n- [ ] This has already been asked to the [discussions forum](https://github.com/celery/celery/discussions) first.\n- [x] I have read the relevant section in the\n  [contribution guide](https://docs.celeryq.dev/en/main/contributing.html#other-bugs)\n  on reporting bugs.\n- [x] I have checked the [issues list](https://github.com/celery/celery/issues?q=is%3Aissue+label%3A%22Issue+Type%3A+Bug+Report%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)\n  for similar or identical bug reports.\n- [x] I have checked the [pull requests list](https://github.com/celery/celery/pulls?q=is%3Apr+label%3A%22PR+Type%3A+Bugfix%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)\n  for existing proposed fixes.\n- [x] I have checked the [commit log](https://github.com/celery/celery/commits/main)\n  to find out if the bug was already fixed in the main branch.\n- [x] I have included all related issues and possible duplicate issues\n  in this issue (If there are none, check this box anyway).\n- [ ] I have tried to reproduce the issue with [pytest-celery](https://docs.celeryq.dev/projects/pytest-celery/en/latest/userguide/celery-bug-report.html) and added the reproduction script below.\n\n## Mandatory Debugging Information\n\n- [x] I have included the output of ``celery -A proj report`` in the issue.\n    (if you are not able to do this, then at least specify the Celery\n     version affected).\n- [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `main` branch of Celery.\n- [x] I have included the contents of ``pip freeze`` in the issue.\n- [x] I have included all the versions of all the external dependencies required\n  to reproduce this bug.\n\n## Optional Debugging Information\n- [x] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one Python version\n  and/or implementation.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one message broker and/or\n  result backend.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one version of the message\n  broker and/or result backend.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one operating system.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one workers pool.\n- [x] I have tried reproducing the issue with autoscaling, retries,\n  ETA/Countdown & rate limits disabled.\n- [x] I have tried reproducing the issue after downgrading\n  and/or upgrading Celery and its dependencies.\n\n## Related Issues and Possible Duplicates\n\n#### Related Issues\n\n- #10829 (reported there in review; that PR fixes a different as_tuple() duplicate and does not change this one)\n\n#### Possible Duplicates\n\n- None\n\n## Environment & Settings\n**Celery version**: main (b69c634), also 5.6.3 and 5.3.6\n\n<details>\n<summary><b><code>celery report</code> Output:</b></summary>\n<p>\n\n```\nsoftware -> celery:5.7.0b1 (collider) kombu:5.7.0b1 py:3.12.3\n            billiard:4.3.1 py-amqp:5.4.1\nplatform -> system:Linux arch:64bit, ELF\n            kernel version:6.18.44-fc-v114 imp:CPython\nloader   -> celery.loaders.default.Loader\nsettings -> transport:amqp results:disabled\n\ndeprecated_settings: None\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n# Steps to Reproduce\n\n## Required Dependencies\n- **Minimal Python Version**: N/A or Unknown\n- **Minimal Celery Version**: 4.4.0rc5 (the chord-body case below: 5.6.0)\n- **Minimal Kombu Version**: N/A or Unknown\n- **Minimal Broker Version**: N/A (reproduces with `memory://`, no worker needed)\n- **Minimal Result Backend Version**: N/A (reproduces with `cache+memory://`)\n- **Minimal OS and/or Kernel Version**: N/A or Unknown\n- **Minimal Broker Client Version**: N/A or Unknown\n- **Minimal Result Backend Client Version**: N/A or Unknown\n\n### Python Packages\n<details>\n<summary><b><code>pip freeze</code> Output:</b></summary>\n<p>\n\n```\namqp==5.4.1\nbilliard==4.3.1\ncelery @ main (b69c634)\nclick==8.5.0\nkombu==5.7.0b1\npython-dateutil==2.9.0.post0\ntzdata==2026.4\nvine==5.1.0\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n### Other Dependencies\n<details>\n<p>\nN/A\n</p>\n</details>\n\n## Minimally Reproducible Test Case\n\n<details>\n<p>\n\n```python\nfrom celery import Celery, chain, chord, group\n\napp = Celery('repro', broker='memory://', backend='cache+memory://')\n\n\n@app.task\ndef t(x):\n    return x\n\n\ndef ids(node):\n    (task_id, parent), children = node\n    yield task_id\n    if parent:\n        yield from ids(parent)\n    for child in children or ():\n        yield from ids(child)\n\n\nres = group(t.si(1), chain(t.si(2), group(t.si(3), t.si(4)))).apply_async()\nall_ids = list(ids(res.as_tuple()))\nprint('group  :', len(all_ids), 'nodes,', len(set(all_ids)), 'unique ids')\nprint('         inner group id == outer group id:', res.results[1].id == res.id)\n\nres = chord(group(t.si(1), t.si(2)), group(t.si(3), t.si(4))).apply_async()\nprint('chord  : body group id == header group id:', res.id == res.parent.id)\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n# Expected Behavior\n\nEvery result in the tree has its own id: 6 nodes, 6 unique ids, and the inner\nGroupResult's id differs from the outer one. Likewise, a chord's body group has\nan id of its own, not the header's.\n\n# Actual Behavior\n\nOn main and 5.6.3, with task protocol 1 and 2:\n\n```\ngroup  : 6 nodes, 5 unique ids\n         inner group id == outer group id: True\nchord  : body group id == header group id: True\n```\n\nThe group case is the same on 5.3.6, and with or without a `task_id` passed to\n`apply_async()`. `freeze()` of the same group is fine (6 nodes, 6 unique ids);\nonly `apply_async()` is affected. 4.4.0rc4 gives 6/6, 4.4.0rc5 gives 6/5. The\nchord case prints `False` up to 5.5.3 (5.3.6 included) and `True` from 5.6.0\non.\n\nRoot cause: `group._freeze_group_tasks()` uses the `group_id` it is given as the\ngroup's own id (`gid = opts['task_id'] = group_id or uuid()`, from a537c2d in\n4.4.0rc5). Callers pass `group_id` to mean \"the group this signature belongs\nto\": the outer group passes its id to the chain, and the chain passes it on to\nits last task, so the inner group adopts the outer id. Since 2a03096 (5.6.0),\n`chord.run()` also passes the header's `group_id` when freezing the body, which\ngives a group body the header's id. The tasks inside the inner group carrying\nthe outer `group_id` is intended (that is how a chord over the outer group\ncounts them); only the inner group's own id is wrong.\n\nReported by @GangEunzzang in #10829, thank you.\n\nWritten with help from Claude (AI).\n\n", "hints_text": "", "created_at": "2026-10-13T18:22:32Z"}
{"repo": "celery/celery", "pull_number": 10849, "instance_id": "celery__celery-10849", "issue_numbers": ["4163"], "base_commit": "30535856f08bf7d3a0b67404bf97c6fbfbb052da", "patch": "diff --git a/celery/concurrency/prefork.py b/celery/concurrency/prefork.py\nindex d7050b86321..0a38823b35e 100644\n--- a/celery/concurrency/prefork.py\n+++ b/celery/concurrency/prefork.py\n@@ -39,6 +39,10 @@\n logger = get_logger(__name__)\n warning, debug = logger.warning, logger.debug\n \n+#: Host name of the worker this pool process belongs to, set by\n+#: :func:`process_initializer` for :func:`process_destructor`.\n+_process_hostname = None\n+\n \n def process_initializer(app, hostname):\n     \"\"\"Pool child process initializer.\n@@ -91,7 +95,9 @@ def process_initializer(app, hostname):\n                                       app=app)\n     from celery.worker import state as worker_state\n     worker_state.reset_state()\n-    signals.worker_process_init.send(sender=None)\n+    global _process_hostname\n+    _process_hostname = hostname\n+    signals.worker_process_init.send(sender=hostname)\n \n \n def process_destructor(pid, exitcode):\n@@ -100,7 +106,7 @@ def process_destructor(pid, exitcode):\n     Dispatch the :signal:`worker_process_shutdown` signal.\n     \"\"\"\n     signals.worker_process_shutdown.send(\n-        sender=None, pid=pid, exitcode=exitcode,\n+        sender=_process_hostname, pid=pid, exitcode=exitcode,\n     )\n \n \ndiff --git a/celery/concurrency/solo.py b/celery/concurrency/solo.py\nindex e7e9c7f3ba4..f3a979a2e1d 100644\n--- a/celery/concurrency/solo.py\n+++ b/celery/concurrency/solo.py\n@@ -17,7 +17,9 @@ def __init__(self, *args, **kwargs):\n         super().__init__(*args, **kwargs)\n         self.on_apply = apply_target\n         self.limit = 1\n-        signals.worker_process_init.send(sender=None)\n+        # The worker passes (app, hostname) as initargs to every pool.\n+        initargs = self.options.get('initargs') or (None, None)\n+        signals.worker_process_init.send(sender=initargs[1])\n \n     def _get_info(self):\n         info = super()._get_info()\ndiff --git a/docs/userguide/signals.rst b/docs/userguide/signals.rst\nindex 4fd938fa343..6021b225ae4 100644\n--- a/docs/userguide/signals.rst\n+++ b/docs/userguide/signals.rst\n@@ -613,7 +613,42 @@ Provides arguments:\n ``worker_process_init``\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n \n-Dispatched in all pool child processes when they start.\n+Dispatched in each pool process when it starts: in every child process of\n+the prefork pool, and once in the main process when a solo pool is created.\n+The threads, gevent and eventlet pools don't send it.\n+\n+The sender is the host name (node name) of the worker the process belongs\n+to, the value given to :option:`celery worker -n`, such as\n+``worker1@example.com``, as for :signal:`celeryd_init`. Connecting with a\n+sender sets up only what that worker needs:\n+\n+.. code-block:: python\n+\n+    from celery.signals import worker_process_init\n+\n+    @worker_process_init.connect(sender='db@example.com')\n+    def setup_db_connection(**kwargs):\n+        ...\n+\n+A handler connected without a sender is called for every worker, and can\n+check the sender itself:\n+\n+.. code-block:: python\n+\n+    from celery.signals import worker_process_init\n+\n+    @worker_process_init.connect\n+    def setup_connections(sender=None, **kwargs):\n+        if sender in ('db1@example.com', 'db2@example.com'):\n+            ...\n+\n+.. versionchanged:: 5.7\n+\n+    The sender used to be :const:`None` in every case; it is now the host\n+    name of the worker. A solo pool created directly, outside a worker,\n+    still sends :const:`None`. Handlers connected without a sender are\n+    unaffected, so only a handler that checks for ``sender is None`` itself\n+    needs updating.\n \n Note that handlers attached to this signal mustn't be blocking\n for more than 4 seconds, or the process will be killed assuming\n@@ -624,7 +659,15 @@ it failed to start.\n ``worker_process_shutdown``\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n \n-Dispatched in all pool child processes just before they exit.\n+Dispatched in all prefork pool child processes just before they exit.\n+\n+The sender is the host name of the worker the process belongs to, as for\n+:signal:`worker_process_init`, so a handler connected with a sender can\n+tear down what the matching :signal:`worker_process_init` handler set up.\n+\n+.. versionchanged:: 5.7\n+\n+    The sender used to be :const:`None`.\n \n Note: There's no guarantee that this signal will be dispatched,\n similarly to :keyword:`finally` blocks it's impossible to guarantee that\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/t/unit/concurrency/test_prefork.py b/t/unit/concurrency/test_prefork.py\nindex d8924ec8396..dc758f094c9 100644\n--- a/t/unit/concurrency/test_prefork.py\n+++ b/t/unit/concurrency/test_prefork.py\n@@ -162,6 +162,7 @@ def test_process_initializer(self, _get_start_method, _signals, set_mp_process_t\n             _signals.reset.assert_any_call(*WORKER_SIGRESET)\n             assert app.loader.init_worker.call_count\n             on_worker_process_init.assert_called()\n+            assert on_worker_process_init.call_args.kwargs['sender'] == 'awesome.worker.com'\n             assert _tls.current_app is app\n             set_mp_process_title.assert_called_with(\n                 'celeryd', hostname='awesome.worker.com',\n@@ -230,9 +231,29 @@ def test_pdeath_sig(self, _get_start_method, _set_pdeathsig, set_mp_process_titl\n             process_initializer(app, 'awesome.worker.com')\n         _set_pdeathsig.assert_called_once_with('SIGKILL')\n \n+    @patch('celery.platforms.set_pdeathsig')\n+    @patch('celery.concurrency.prefork.get_start_method', return_value='fork')\n+    def test_worker_process_init_sender_is_hostname(self, _get_start_method, _set_pdeathsig,\n+                                                    set_mp_process_title, restore_logging):\n+        from celery import signals\n+        from celery.concurrency.prefork import process_initializer\n+        on_db_worker = Mock()\n+        signals.worker_process_init.connect(on_db_worker, sender='db@example.com')\n+        try:\n+            with self.Celery(loader=self.Loader) as app:\n+                app.conf = AttributeDict(DEFAULTS)\n+                process_initializer(app, 'web@example.com')\n+                on_db_worker.assert_not_called()\n+                process_initializer(app, 'db@example.com')\n+                on_db_worker.assert_called_once()\n+                assert mp._process_hostname == 'db@example.com'\n+        finally:\n+            signals.worker_process_init.disconnect(on_db_worker, sender='db@example.com')\n+\n \n class test_process_destructor:\n \n+    @patch('celery.concurrency.prefork._process_hostname', None)\n     @patch('celery.concurrency.prefork.signals')\n     def test_process_destructor(self, signals):\n         mp.process_destructor(13, -3)\n@@ -240,6 +261,14 @@ def test_process_destructor(self, signals):\n             sender=None, pid=13, exitcode=-3,\n         )\n \n+    @patch('celery.concurrency.prefork._process_hostname', 'db@example.com')\n+    @patch('celery.concurrency.prefork.signals')\n+    def test_process_destructor_sender_is_hostname(self, signals):\n+        mp.process_destructor(13, 0)\n+        signals.worker_process_shutdown.send.assert_called_with(\n+            sender='db@example.com', pid=13, exitcode=0,\n+        )\n+\n \n class MockPool:\n     started = False\ndiff --git a/t/unit/concurrency/test_solo.py b/t/unit/concurrency/test_solo.py\nindex c26f839a5e5..6fe593e84c0 100644\n--- a/t/unit/concurrency/test_solo.py\n+++ b/t/unit/concurrency/test_solo.py\n@@ -29,3 +29,21 @@ def test_on_worker_process_init_called(self):\n         signals.worker_process_init.connect(on_worker_process_init)\n         solo.TaskPool()\n         assert on_worker_process_init.call_count == 1\n+\n+    def test_on_worker_process_init_sender_is_hostname(self):\n+        on_worker_process_init = Mock()\n+        signals.worker_process_init.connect(on_worker_process_init)\n+        try:\n+            solo.TaskPool(initargs=(Mock(), 'solo@example.com'))\n+            assert on_worker_process_init.call_args.kwargs['sender'] == 'solo@example.com'\n+        finally:\n+            signals.worker_process_init.disconnect(on_worker_process_init)\n+\n+    def test_on_worker_process_init_sender_without_initargs(self):\n+        on_worker_process_init = Mock()\n+        signals.worker_process_init.connect(on_worker_process_init)\n+        try:\n+            solo.TaskPool()\n+            assert on_worker_process_init.call_args.kwargs['sender'] is None\n+        finally:\n+            signals.worker_process_init.disconnect(on_worker_process_init)\n", "problem_statement": "signal \"worker_process_init\" not providing sender-name \nHi,\n\nreferring to  issue #1972 --> i am facing a usecase where knowing the sender (or worker_pool_name) from which a process is started is needed to decide how the worker_process is initialized. Some workers are responsible for database I/O and need to know a global db_connection variable while others are only working with local information. Right now it is only possible to setup one prozess-initialization for all worker pools...\n\nor am I missing a feature/function?\n\nbest regards\n\nFabian\n\n\n", "hints_text": "could you verify this issue on top of master?\nI think this sitll happens in 4.2.1. \r\nI use worker_init to get that into a variable before the fork.", "created_at": "2026-10-12T08:10:53Z"}
{"repo": "celery/celery", "pull_number": 10844, "instance_id": "celery__celery-10844", "issue_numbers": ["9442"], "base_commit": "a5ac9c42c66c7c6d7eddca7dffd5f37712d8e1c2", "patch": "diff --git a/celery/app/base.py b/celery/app/base.py\nindex e3b5c235279..158f7a5175d 100644\n--- a/celery/app/base.py\n+++ b/celery/app/base.py\n@@ -9,6 +9,7 @@\n import typing\n import warnings\n from collections import UserDict, defaultdict, deque\n+from collections.abc import Mapping\n from datetime import datetime\n from datetime import timezone as datetime_timezone\n from operator import attrgetter\n@@ -183,9 +184,8 @@ def pydantic_wrapper(\n \n     if context is None:\n         context = {}\n-    if dump_kwargs is None:\n-        dump_kwargs = {}\n-    dump_kwargs.setdefault('mode', 'json')\n+    # A new dict, so the caller's dict isn't changed.\n+    dump_kwargs = {'mode': 'json', **(dump_kwargs or {})}\n \n     # If a file uses `from __future__ import annotations`, all annotations will\n     # be strings. `typing.get_type_hints()` can turn these back into real\n@@ -199,26 +199,36 @@ def pydantic_wrapper(\n         # Fall back to raw annotations from inspect if get_type_hints fails\n         type_hints = None\n \n+    def get_argument_model(arg_name, arg_value):\n+        \"\"\"Return the model the wrapper validates this argument with, or ``None``.\"\"\"\n+        if type_hints and arg_name in type_hints:\n+            arg_annotation = type_hints[arg_name]\n+        else:\n+            arg_annotation = task_signature.parameters[arg_name].annotation\n+\n+        optional_arg = get_optional_arg(arg_annotation)\n+        if optional_arg is not None and arg_value is not None:\n+            arg_annotation = optional_arg\n+\n+        if annotation_issubclass(arg_annotation, BaseModel):\n+            return arg_annotation\n+        return None\n+\n+    def validate_argument(arg_model, arg_value):\n+        return arg_model.model_validate(\n+            arg_value,\n+            strict=strict,\n+            context={**context, 'celery_app': app, 'celery_task_name': task_name},\n+        )\n+\n     @functools.wraps(task_fun)\n     def wrapper(*task_args, **task_kwargs):\n         # Validate task parameters if type hinted as BaseModel\n         bound_args = task_signature.bind(*task_args, **task_kwargs)\n         for arg_name, arg_value in bound_args.arguments.items():\n-            if type_hints and arg_name in type_hints:\n-                arg_annotation = type_hints[arg_name]\n-            else:\n-                arg_annotation = task_signature.parameters[arg_name].annotation\n-\n-            optional_arg = get_optional_arg(arg_annotation)\n-            if optional_arg is not None and arg_value is not None:\n-                arg_annotation = optional_arg\n-\n-            if annotation_issubclass(arg_annotation, BaseModel):\n-                bound_args.arguments[arg_name] = arg_annotation.model_validate(\n-                    arg_value,\n-                    strict=strict,\n-                    context={**context, 'celery_app': app, 'celery_task_name': task_name},\n-                )\n+            arg_model = get_argument_model(arg_name, arg_value)\n+            if arg_model is not None:\n+                bound_args.arguments[arg_name] = validate_argument(arg_model, arg_value)\n \n         # Call the task with (potentially) converted arguments\n         returned_value = task_fun(*bound_args.args, **bound_args.kwargs)\n@@ -243,6 +253,79 @@ def wrapper(*task_args, **task_kwargs):\n \n         return returned_value\n \n+    # Parameters that positional and keyword arguments bind to. Other arguments\n+    # go to *args or **kwargs, which the wrapper doesn't validate one by one.\n+    positional_names = [\n+        name for name, param in task_signature.parameters.items()\n+        if param.kind in (param.POSITIONAL_ONLY, param.POSITIONAL_OR_KEYWORD)\n+    ]\n+    keyword_names = {\n+        name for name, param in task_signature.parameters.items()\n+        if param.kind in (param.POSITIONAL_OR_KEYWORD, param.KEYWORD_ONLY)\n+    }\n+\n+    def dump_argument(arg_name, arg_value):\n+        # Only dump a model if the wrapper gets an equal model back from the\n+        # dump. Other models are left as they are, so the serializer handles\n+        # them (usually with EncodeError) like it does for tasks without pydantic=True.\n+        if (\n+            arg_name is None\n+            or not isinstance(arg_value, BaseModel)\n+            # A model with kombu's __json__ hook already knows how to be sent.\n+            or hasattr(type(arg_value), '__json__')\n+        ):\n+            return arg_value\n+        arg_model = get_argument_model(arg_name, arg_value)\n+        if arg_model is None:\n+            return arg_value\n+        try:\n+            dumped = arg_value.model_dump(**dump_kwargs)\n+            rebuilt = validate_argument(arg_model, dumped)\n+            # Model equality includes the class, so a subclass passed for its base class is left too.\n+            if rebuilt == arg_value:\n+                return dumped\n+        except Exception:\n+            # The model's serializers and validators run here on the caller, and some\n+            # may only work on a worker. If anything fails, the model is left as it is.\n+            pass\n+        return arg_value\n+\n+    def dump_arguments(args, kwargs, bound=False):\n+        \"\"\"Dump the models passed by a caller that the wrapper rebuilds as equal models.\n+\n+        Used by :meth:`celery.app.task.Task.apply_async`. ``args`` and ``kwargs``\n+        are returned as they are when no model is dumped.\n+        \"\"\"\n+        task_args = () if args is None else args\n+        task_kwargs = {} if kwargs is None else kwargs\n+        if not isinstance(task_args, (list, tuple)) or not isinstance(task_kwargs, Mapping):\n+            # Left for send_task() to reject, as for other tasks.\n+            return args, kwargs\n+        if not any(isinstance(value, BaseModel) for value in (*task_args, *task_kwargs.values())):\n+            return args, kwargs\n+        try:\n+            # A bound task gets the task itself as its first argument.\n+            task_signature.bind(*([None] if bound else []), *task_args, **task_kwargs)\n+        except TypeError:\n+            # The wrapper can't bind these arguments either.\n+            return args, kwargs\n+\n+        names = positional_names[1:] if bound else positional_names\n+        dumped_args = [\n+            dump_argument(names[index] if index < len(names) else None, value)\n+            for index, value in enumerate(task_args)\n+        ]\n+        dumped_kwargs = {\n+            key: dump_argument(key if key in keyword_names else None, value)\n+            for key, value in task_kwargs.items()\n+        }\n+        if any(new is not old for new, old in zip(dumped_args, task_args)):\n+            args = dumped_args if isinstance(task_args, list) else tuple(dumped_args)\n+        if any(dumped_kwargs[key] is not value for key, value in task_kwargs.items()):\n+            kwargs = dumped_kwargs\n+        return args, kwargs\n+\n+    wrapper.dump_arguments = dump_arguments\n     return wrapper\n \n \n@@ -659,6 +742,10 @@ def _task_from_fun(\n         if name not in self._tasks:\n             if pydantic is True:\n                 fun = pydantic_wrapper(self, fun, name, pydantic_strict, pydantic_context, pydantic_dump_kwargs)\n+                # Used by apply_async() for models passed by the caller.\n+                dump_pydantic_arguments = staticmethod(functools.partial(fun.dump_arguments, bound=bind))\n+            else:\n+                dump_pydantic_arguments = None\n \n             run = fun if bind else staticmethod(fun)\n             task = type(fun.__name__, (base,), dict({\n@@ -666,6 +753,7 @@ def _task_from_fun(\n                 'name': name,\n                 'run': run,\n                 '_decorated': True,\n+                '_dump_pydantic_arguments': dump_pydantic_arguments,\n                 '__doc__': fun.__doc__,\n                 '__module__': fun.__module__,\n                 '__annotations__': _get_annotations(fun),\ndiff --git a/celery/app/task.py b/celery/app/task.py\nindex 5afb75c499d..3e17ede25ad 100644\n--- a/celery/app/task.py\n+++ b/celery/app/task.py\n@@ -402,6 +402,10 @@ class Task:\n \n     _exec_options = None\n \n+    #: Set for tasks created with ``pydantic=True``: dumps the models passed\n+    #: by the caller that the task gets back as equal models.\n+    _dump_pydantic_arguments = None\n+\n     __bound__ = False\n \n     from_config = (\n@@ -698,6 +702,10 @@ def apply_async(self, args=None, kwargs=None, task_id=None, producer=None,\n         preopts = self._get_exec_options()\n         options = dict(preopts, **options)\n \n+        # Pickle can send models as they are, so only dump them for other serializers.\n+        if self._dump_pydantic_arguments is not None and options.get('serializer') != 'pickle':\n+            args, kwargs = self._dump_pydantic_arguments(args, kwargs)\n+\n         options.setdefault('ignore_result', self.ignore_result)\n         if self.priority:\n             options.setdefault('priority', self.priority)\ndiff --git a/docs/userguide/tasks.rst b/docs/userguide/tasks.rst\nindex 3d86b83836b..c00f42448d4 100644\n--- a/docs/userguide/tasks.rst\n+++ b/docs/userguide/tasks.rst\n@@ -823,8 +823,52 @@ results based on typehints by passing ``pydantic=True``.\n \n .. NOTE::\n \n-   Argument validation only covers arguments/return values on the task side. You still have\n-   serialize arguments yourself when invoking a task with ``delay()`` or ``apply_async()``.\n+   Argument validation only covers arguments/return values on the task side. You still have to\n+   serialize arguments yourself in the cases listed below.\n+\n+.. versionchanged:: 5.7\n+\n+   Models can be passed directly to ``delay()`` and ``apply_async()``.\n+\n+A model passed when calling a task is sent dumped with ``pydantic_dump_kwargs``\n+only if the task gets an equal model back: before sending, the caller validates\n+the dump the way the worker will, running the model's validators with the task's\n+context, and compares the result with ``==``. Otherwise, or if dumping or\n+validating raises an error on the caller, the model is left as it is, so the\n+serializer raises ``EncodeError`` as it did before. Models are sent as they are\n+for tasks using the ``pickle`` serializer and for models that define kombu's\n+``__json__()`` hook.\n+\n+In these cases you still have to pass a dict, as passing a model raises ``EncodeError``:\n+\n+* models inside lists or dicts\n+* models in ``link`` and ``link_error`` signatures\n+* models for a later task of a chain, the body of a chord, or ``map()``,\n+  ``starmap()`` and ``chunks()``\n+* tasks called by name with ``send_task()``\n+* models the task wouldn't get back as an equal model, for example:\n+\n+  * a model with a ``SecretStr`` field, which is dumped as ``'**********'``\n+  * a model whose private attributes differ from those a new model gets, as\n+    private attributes aren't sent\n+  * a model with a computed field and ``extra='forbid'``\n+  * a subclass passed for a parameter annotated with its base class\n+  * a model for a parameter that isn't annotated with a model\n+  * with ``pydantic_strict=True``, a model with fields like ``datetime`` or\n+    ``UUID`` that ``mode='json'`` turns into strings\n+\n+An equal model can still differ in a few ways:\n+\n+* The task's model counts every dumped field as set. If the task uses\n+  ``model_fields_set`` or ``model_dump(exclude_unset=True)``, add\n+  ``exclude_unset=True`` to ``pydantic_dump_kwargs``.\n+* Aware datetimes arrive with a fixed UTC offset instead of their time zone,\n+  such as a ``ZoneInfo``.\n+* The check uses the model's own ``__eq__``. If a model defines one, anything\n+  it ignores can still change, such as a ``SecretStr`` field.\n+* With the default ``mode='json'``, the dump is what the worker gets. With\n+  ``mode='python'``, the serializer can still change it, for example tuples\n+  arrive as lists, which ``pydantic_strict=True`` rejects on the worker.\n \n For example:\n \n@@ -846,11 +890,14 @@ For example:\n         # The returned model will be converted to a dict automatically\n         return ReturnModel(value=f\"example: {arg.value}\")\n \n-The task can then be called using a dict matching the model, and you'll receive\n+The task can then be called using a model or a dict matching the model, and you'll receive\n the returned model \"dumped\" (serialized using ``BaseModel.model_dump()``):\n \n .. code-block:: python\n \n+   >>> result = x.delay(ArgModel(value=1))\n+   >>> result.get(timeout=1)\n+   {'value': 'example: 1'}\n    >>> result = x.delay({'value': 1})\n    >>> result.get(timeout=1)\n    {'value': 'example: 1'}\n@@ -920,8 +967,12 @@ There are a few more options influencing Pydantic behavior:\n \n .. attribute:: Task.pydantic_dump_kwargs\n \n-   When serializing a result, pass these additional arguments to ``dump_kwargs()``.\n-   By default, only ``mode='json'`` is passed.\n+   When serializing a result or a model passed as an argument, pass these additional\n+   arguments to ``model_dump()``. By default, only ``mode='json'`` is passed. If your\n+   models use field aliases, add ``by_alias=True`` so models passed as arguments can be sent.\n+   With ``pydantic_strict=True``, fields that ``mode='json'`` turns into strings, such as\n+   ``datetime`` or ``UUID``, don't validate, so call those tasks with ``model.model_dump()``\n+   instead of the model.\n \n \n .. _task-options:\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/t/integration/test_tasks.py b/t/integration/test_tasks.py\nindex ad6277b3f15..e807135bb27 100644\n--- a/t/integration/test_tasks.py\n+++ b/t/integration/test_tasks.py\n@@ -16,11 +16,12 @@\n from celery.worker import state as worker_state\n \n from .conftest import TEST_BACKEND, get_active_redis_channels, get_redis_connection\n-from .tasks import (ClassBasedAutoRetryTask, ExpectedException, add, add_ignore_result, add_not_typed, add_pydantic,\n-                    add_pydantic_string_annotations, fail, fail_unpickleable, print_unicode, reject_then_succeed,\n-                    reject_without_requeue, retry, retry_once, retry_once_headers, retry_once_priority,\n-                    retry_unpickleable, return_properties, return_request_time_limits, second_order_replace1,\n-                    sleeping, soft_time_limit_must_exceed_time_limit, task_with_declared_time_limits)\n+from .tasks import (AddParameterModel, ClassBasedAutoRetryTask, ExpectedException, add, add_ignore_result,\n+                    add_not_typed, add_pydantic, add_pydantic_string_annotations, fail, fail_unpickleable,\n+                    print_unicode, reject_then_succeed, reject_without_requeue, retry, retry_once,\n+                    retry_once_headers, retry_once_priority, retry_unpickleable, return_properties,\n+                    return_request_time_limits, second_order_replace1, sleeping,\n+                    soft_time_limit_must_exceed_time_limit, task_with_declared_time_limits)\n \n TIMEOUT = 10\n \n@@ -205,6 +206,22 @@ def test_pydantic_string_annotations(self, manager):\n             assert result.ready() is True\n             assert result.successful() is True\n \n+    @flaky\n+    def test_pydantic_model_arguments(self, manager):\n+        \"\"\"Tests task call with Pydantic models passed by the caller.\"\"\"\n+        results = []\n+        for i in range(10):\n+            results.append([i + i, add_pydantic.delay(AddParameterModel(x=i, y=i))])\n+        results.append([4, add_pydantic.apply_async(kwargs={'data': AddParameterModel(x=2, y=2)})])\n+        results.append([6, add_pydantic.s(AddParameterModel(x=3, y=3)).delay()])\n+        results.append([8, add_pydantic_string_annotations.delay(AddParameterModel(x=4, y=4))])\n+        for expected, result in results:\n+            value = result.get(timeout=10)\n+            assert value == {'result': expected}\n+            assert result.status == 'SUCCESS'\n+            assert result.ready() is True\n+            assert result.successful() is True\n+\n     @flaky\n     def test_timeout(self, manager):\n         \"\"\"Testing timeout of getting results from tasks.\"\"\"\ndiff --git a/t/unit/app/test_app.py b/t/unit/app/test_app.py\nindex 5624a0458a0..39cbb850b5f 100644\n--- a/t/unit/app/test_app.py\n+++ b/t/unit/app/test_app.py\n@@ -18,10 +18,10 @@\n \n import pytest\n from kombu import Exchange, Queue\n-from kombu.exceptions import LimitExceeded\n+from kombu.exceptions import EncodeError, LimitExceeded\n from pydantic import BaseModel\n from pydantic import BaseModel as PydanticBaseModel\n-from pydantic import ValidationInfo, model_validator\n+from pydantic import ConfigDict, Field, SecretStr, ValidationInfo, model_validator\n from vine import promise\n \n from celery import Celery, _state\n@@ -79,6 +79,16 @@ def __reduce_args__(self):\n         return super().__reduce_args__()\n \n \n+class StringAnnotationModel(BaseModel):\n+    \"\"\"Model used in string annotations.\n+\n+    Declared at module level because ``typing.get_type_hints()`` can only\n+    resolve string annotations from the module's globals.\n+    \"\"\"\n+\n+    value: int\n+\n+\n class test_module:\n \n     def test_default_app(self):\n@@ -1038,6 +1048,427 @@ def task(arg: ArgModel) -> RetModel:\n             assert task({'value': 1}) == {'value': datetime(2024, 5, 14, tzinfo=timezone.utc)}\n             check.assert_called_once_with(ArgModel(value=1))\n \n+    def test_task_with_pydantic_with_models_passed_by_caller(self):\n+        \"\"\"Test that models passed when calling a task are sent as dicts.\"\"\"\n+\n+        class ArgModel(BaseModel):\n+            value: int\n+\n+        class KwargModel(BaseModel):\n+            value: datetime\n+\n+        with self.Celery() as app:\n+            @app.task(pydantic=True)\n+            def task(arg: ArgModel, kwarg: Optional[KwargModel] = None):\n+                pass\n+\n+            with patch.object(app, 'send_task') as send_task:\n+                task.delay(ArgModel(value=1), kwarg=KwargModel(value=datetime(2024, 5, 14, tzinfo=timezone.utc)))\n+                task.apply_async((ArgModel(value=2),), {'kwarg': None})\n+                task.s(ArgModel(value=3)).delay()\n+\n+            sent = [call.args[1:3] for call in send_task.call_args_list]\n+            assert sent == [\n+                (({'value': 1},), {'kwarg': {'value': '2024-05-14T00:00:00Z'}}),\n+                (({'value': 2},), {'kwarg': None}),\n+                (({'value': 3},), {}),\n+            ]\n+\n+    def test_task_with_pydantic_with_models_passed_by_caller_eager(self):\n+        \"\"\"Test that models passed by the caller survive serialization in eager mode.\"\"\"\n+\n+        class Model(BaseModel):\n+            value: timedelta\n+\n+        with self.Celery() as app:\n+            app.conf.task_always_eager = True\n+            check = Mock()\n+\n+            @app.task(pydantic=True)\n+            def task(arg: Model) -> Model:\n+                check(arg)\n+                return Model(value=arg.value * 2)\n+\n+            assert task.delay(Model(value=timedelta(days=1))).get() == {'value': 'P2D'}\n+            check.assert_called_once_with(Model(value=timedelta(days=1)))\n+\n+    def test_task_with_pydantic_with_models_passed_by_caller_dump_kwargs(self):\n+        \"\"\"Test that pydantic_dump_kwargs is also used for models passed by the caller.\"\"\"\n+\n+        class ArgModel(BaseModel):\n+            value: int = Field(alias='Value')\n+\n+        with self.Celery() as app:\n+            app.conf.task_always_eager = True\n+            check = Mock()\n+\n+            # The model is only valid by alias, so the round trip needs by_alias=True\n+            @app.task(pydantic=True, pydantic_dump_kwargs={'by_alias': True})\n+            def task(arg: ArgModel):\n+                check(arg)\n+\n+            task.delay(ArgModel(Value=1)).get()\n+            check.assert_called_once_with(ArgModel(Value=1))\n+\n+    def test_task_without_pydantic_does_not_dump_models(self):\n+        \"\"\"Test that models passed to a task without pydantic=True are left alone.\"\"\"\n+\n+        class ArgModel(BaseModel):\n+            value: int\n+\n+        with self.Celery() as app:\n+            @app.task\n+            def task(arg):\n+                pass\n+\n+            arg = ArgModel(value=1)\n+            with patch.object(app, 'send_task') as send_task:\n+                task.delay(arg)\n+\n+            assert send_task.call_args.args[1] == (arg,)\n+\n+    def test_task_with_pydantic_with_pickle_does_not_dump_models(self):\n+        \"\"\"Test that models are sent as they are when the pickle serializer is used.\"\"\"\n+\n+        class ArgModel(BaseModel):\n+            value: int\n+\n+        with self.Celery() as app:\n+            @app.task(pydantic=True, serializer='pickle')\n+            def pickle_task(arg: ArgModel):\n+                pass\n+\n+            @app.task(pydantic=True)\n+            def json_task(arg: ArgModel):\n+                pass\n+\n+            arg = ArgModel(value=1)\n+            with patch.object(app, 'send_task') as send_task:\n+                pickle_task.delay(arg)\n+                json_task.apply_async((arg,), serializer='pickle')\n+\n+            first, second = (call.args[1][0] for call in send_task.call_args_list)\n+            assert first is arg\n+            assert second is arg\n+\n+    def test_task_with_pydantic_with_json_hook_does_not_dump_models(self):\n+        \"\"\"Test that models defining kombu's __json__ hook are left to it.\"\"\"\n+\n+        class ArgModel(BaseModel):\n+            value: int = Field(alias='Value')\n+\n+            def __json__(self):\n+                return self.model_dump(mode='json', by_alias=True)\n+\n+        with self.Celery() as app:\n+            app.conf.task_always_eager = True\n+            check = Mock()\n+\n+            # Dumping without by_alias=True would fail validation on the worker\n+            @app.task(pydantic=True)\n+            def task(arg: ArgModel):\n+                check(arg)\n+\n+            task.delay(ArgModel(Value=1)).get()\n+            check.assert_called_once_with(ArgModel(Value=1))\n+\n+    def test_task_with_pydantic_with_unserializable_model(self):\n+        \"\"\"Test that a model model_dump() can't serialize still raises EncodeError.\"\"\"\n+\n+        class Thing:\n+            pass\n+\n+        class ArgModel(BaseModel):\n+            model_config = ConfigDict(arbitrary_types_allowed=True)\n+            thing: Thing\n+\n+        with self.Celery() as app:\n+            app.conf.task_always_eager = True\n+\n+            @app.task(pydantic=True)\n+            def task(arg: ArgModel):\n+                pass\n+\n+            with pytest.raises(EncodeError):\n+                task.delay(ArgModel(thing=Thing()))\n+\n+    def test_task_with_pydantic_with_invalid_args_type(self):\n+        \"\"\"Test that args and kwargs of the wrong type still raise TypeError.\"\"\"\n+\n+        class ArgModel(BaseModel):\n+            value: int\n+\n+        with self.Celery() as app:\n+            @app.task(pydantic=True)\n+            def task(arg: ArgModel):\n+                pass\n+\n+            @app.task(pydantic=True, typing=False)\n+            def unchecked_task(arg: ArgModel):\n+                pass\n+\n+            with pytest.raises(TypeError, match='task args must be a list or tuple'):\n+                task.apply_async({'value': 1})\n+            with pytest.raises(TypeError, match='task keyword arguments must be a mapping'):\n+                unchecked_task.apply_async((), [('arg', ArgModel(value=1))])\n+\n+    def test_task_with_pydantic_with_masked_or_excluded_fields(self):\n+        \"\"\"Test that models losing values in model_dump() still raise EncodeError.\"\"\"\n+\n+        class SecretModel(BaseModel):\n+            secret: SecretStr\n+\n+        class ExcludeModel(BaseModel):\n+            value: int = Field(0, exclude=True)\n+\n+        with self.Celery() as app:\n+            app.conf.task_always_eager = True\n+            check = Mock()\n+\n+            @app.task(pydantic=True)\n+            def task(secret: Optional[SecretModel] = None, exclude: Optional[ExcludeModel] = None):\n+                check(secret, exclude)\n+\n+            # The task would get '**********' as the secret\n+            with pytest.raises(EncodeError):\n+                task.delay(SecretModel(secret='hunter2'))\n+            # The task would get the default instead of 1\n+            with pytest.raises(EncodeError):\n+                task.delay(exclude=ExcludeModel(value=1))\n+            check.assert_not_called()\n+\n+            # Nothing is lost if the excluded field has its default\n+            task.delay(exclude=ExcludeModel()).get()\n+            check.assert_called_once_with(None, ExcludeModel())\n+\n+    def test_task_with_pydantic_with_subclass_of_annotated_model(self):\n+        \"\"\"Test that a subclass passed for its base class still raises EncodeError.\"\"\"\n+\n+        class ArgModel(BaseModel):\n+            value: int\n+\n+        class SubModel(ArgModel):\n+            extra: int = 0\n+\n+        class ForbidModel(BaseModel):\n+            model_config = ConfigDict(extra='forbid')\n+            value: int\n+\n+        class SubForbidModel(ForbidModel):\n+            extra: int = 0\n+\n+        with self.Celery() as app:\n+            app.conf.task_always_eager = True\n+            check = Mock()\n+\n+            @app.task(pydantic=True)\n+            def task(arg: Optional[ArgModel] = None, forbid: Optional[ForbidModel] = None):\n+                check(arg, forbid)\n+\n+            # The task would get an ArgModel without extra\n+            with pytest.raises(EncodeError):\n+                task.delay(SubModel(value=1, extra=5))\n+            # Validating the dump as ForbidModel would fail on the worker\n+            with pytest.raises(EncodeError):\n+                task.delay(forbid=SubForbidModel(value=1))\n+            check.assert_not_called()\n+\n+    def test_task_with_pydantic_with_strict_validation_and_models_passed_by_caller(self):\n+        \"\"\"Test that models strict validation rejects once dumped still raise EncodeError.\"\"\"\n+\n+        class DatetimeModel(BaseModel):\n+            value: datetime\n+\n+        class UUIDModel(BaseModel):\n+            value: uuid.UUID\n+\n+        class IntModel(BaseModel):\n+            value: int\n+\n+        moment = datetime(2024, 5, 14, tzinfo=timezone.utc)\n+\n+        with self.Celery() as app:\n+            app.conf.task_always_eager = True\n+            check = Mock()\n+\n+            @app.task(pydantic=True, pydantic_strict=True)\n+            def task(\n+                when: Optional[DatetimeModel] = None,\n+                ident: Optional[UUIDModel] = None,\n+                number: Optional[IntModel] = None,\n+            ):\n+                check(when, ident, number)\n+\n+            # mode='json' turns these fields into strings, which strict validation rejects\n+            with pytest.raises(EncodeError):\n+                task.delay(DatetimeModel(value=moment))\n+            with pytest.raises(EncodeError):\n+                task.delay(ident=UUIDModel(value=uuid.UUID(int=1)))\n+            check.assert_not_called()\n+\n+            task.delay(number=IntModel(value=1)).get()\n+            check.assert_called_once_with(None, None, IntModel(value=1))\n+            check.reset_mock()\n+\n+            # The workaround from the docs\n+            task.delay(DatetimeModel(value=moment).model_dump()).get()\n+            check.assert_called_once_with(DatetimeModel(value=moment), None, None)\n+\n+    def test_task_with_pydantic_with_validator_failing_on_the_caller(self):\n+        \"\"\"Test that a model whose validator fails on the caller is sent as it is.\"\"\"\n+\n+        connection = None  # only set up in worker processes\n+\n+        class ArgModel(BaseModel):\n+            value: int\n+\n+            @model_validator(mode='after')\n+            def check_on_worker(self, info: ValidationInfo):\n+                if info.context and 'celery_app' in info.context:\n+                    connection.check(self.value)\n+                return self\n+\n+        with self.Celery() as app:\n+            @app.task(pydantic=True)\n+            def task(arg: ArgModel):\n+                pass\n+\n+            arg = ArgModel(value=1)\n+            with patch.object(app, 'send_task') as send_task:\n+                task.delay(arg)\n+            # Left to the serializer, as for tasks without pydantic=True\n+            assert send_task.call_args.args[1][0] is arg\n+\n+            app.conf.task_always_eager = True\n+            with pytest.raises(EncodeError):\n+                task.delay(arg)\n+\n+    def test_task_with_pydantic_with_models_not_validated_by_the_task(self):\n+        \"\"\"Test that models for parameters the task doesn't validate as models are left alone.\"\"\"\n+\n+        class ArgModel(BaseModel):\n+            value: int\n+\n+        class OtherModel(BaseModel):\n+            value: int\n+\n+        with self.Celery() as app:\n+            @app.task(pydantic=True)\n+            def task(\n+                arg: ArgModel,\n+                unannotated=None,\n+                union: typing.Union[ArgModel, OtherModel, None] = None,\n+                *rest,\n+                **extra,\n+            ):\n+                pass\n+\n+            models = [ArgModel(value=index) for index in range(5)]\n+            with patch.object(app, 'send_task') as send_task:\n+                task.delay(models[0], models[1], union=models[2], extra=models[3])\n+                task.delay(ArgModel(value=5), None, None, models[4])\n+\n+            first, second = send_task.call_args_list\n+            assert first.args[1] == ({'value': 0}, models[1])\n+            assert first.args[1][1] is models[1]\n+            assert first.args[2] == {'union': models[2], 'extra': models[3]}\n+            assert first.args[2]['union'] is models[2]\n+            assert first.args[2]['extra'] is models[3]\n+            assert second.args[1] == ({'value': 5}, None, None, models[4])\n+            assert second.args[1][3] is models[4]\n+\n+    def test_task_with_pydantic_keeps_args_and_kwargs_without_models(self):\n+        \"\"\"Test that args and kwargs are sent as the caller passed them unless a model is dumped.\"\"\"\n+\n+        class ArgModel(BaseModel):\n+            value: int\n+\n+        with self.Celery() as app:\n+            @app.task(pydantic=True)\n+            def task(arg: Optional[ArgModel] = None, number: int = 0):\n+                pass\n+\n+            args, kwargs = [None], {'number': 1}\n+            with patch.object(app, 'send_task') as send_task:\n+                task.apply_async(args, kwargs)\n+                task.apply_async([ArgModel(value=1)], kwargs)\n+                task.apply_async((ArgModel(value=2),), kwargs)\n+\n+            first, second, third = send_task.call_args_list\n+            assert first.args[1] is args\n+            assert first.args[2] is kwargs\n+            # The type of args is kept when a model in it is dumped\n+            assert second.args[1] == [{'value': 1}]\n+            assert type(second.args[1]) is list\n+            assert second.args[2] is kwargs\n+            assert third.args[1] == ({'value': 2},)\n+            assert type(third.args[1]) is tuple\n+\n+    def test_task_with_pydantic_with_kwargs_only_and_optional_model(self):\n+        \"\"\"Test models passed in kwargs only, and None for an Optional model.\"\"\"\n+\n+        class ArgModel(BaseModel):\n+            value: int\n+\n+        with self.Celery() as app:\n+            @app.task(pydantic=True)\n+            def task(arg: Optional[ArgModel] = None):\n+                pass\n+\n+            kwargs = {'arg': None}\n+            with patch.object(app, 'send_task') as send_task:\n+                task.apply_async(kwargs={'arg': ArgModel(value=1)})\n+                task.apply_async(kwargs=kwargs)\n+                task.apply_async((None,))\n+\n+            first, second, third = send_task.call_args_list\n+            assert first.args[1:3] == (None, {'arg': {'value': 1}})\n+            assert second.args[1] is None\n+            assert second.args[2] is kwargs\n+            assert third.args[1:3] == ((None,), None)\n+\n+    def test_task_with_pydantic_with_bound_task_and_models_passed_by_caller(self):\n+        \"\"\"Test that models passed to a bound task are matched to the right parameters.\"\"\"\n+\n+        class ArgModel(BaseModel):\n+            value: int\n+\n+        with self.Celery() as app:\n+            app.conf.task_always_eager = True\n+            check = Mock()\n+\n+            @app.task(pydantic=True, bind=True)\n+            def task(self, arg: ArgModel, kwarg: Optional[ArgModel] = None):\n+                check(self.name, arg, kwarg)\n+\n+            task.delay(ArgModel(value=1), kwarg=ArgModel(value=2)).get()\n+            check.assert_called_once_with(task.name, ArgModel(value=1), ArgModel(value=2))\n+\n+    def test_task_with_pydantic_with_string_annotations_and_models_passed_by_caller(self):\n+        \"\"\"Test models passed for string annotations, as with ``from __future__ import annotations``.\"\"\"\n+\n+        class LocalModel(BaseModel):\n+            value: int\n+\n+        with self.Celery() as app:\n+            @app.task(pydantic=True)\n+            def task(arg: 'StringAnnotationModel'):\n+                pass\n+\n+            # get_type_hints() can't resolve a local class, so the task would get the dict\n+            @app.task(pydantic=True)\n+            def unresolved_task(arg: 'LocalModel'):\n+                pass\n+\n+            arg = LocalModel(value=2)\n+            with patch.object(app, 'send_task') as send_task:\n+                task.delay(StringAnnotationModel(value=1))\n+                unresolved_task.delay(arg)\n+\n+            first, second = send_task.call_args_list\n+            assert first.args[1] == ({'value': 1},)\n+            assert second.args[1][0] is arg\n+\n     def test_task_with_pydantic_with_pydantic_not_installed(self):\n         \"\"\"Test configuring a task with Pydantic when pydantic is not installed.\"\"\"\n \n", "problem_statement": "pydantic=true is not effective\nTypeError: Object of type Request is not JSON serializable\r\n\r\nDuring handling of the above exception, another exception occurred:\r\n\n", "hints_text": "```\r\nclass Request(BaseModel):\r\n    method: str = \"GET\"\r\n    url: str = \"https://api.chatfire.cn/\"\r\n\r\n    class Config:\r\n        frozen = True\r\n@shared_task(pydantic=True)\r\ndef do_pydantic_task(request: Request) -> Request:\r\n    logger.debug(request)\r\n    return request\r\n```\r\n![Uploading image.png\u2026]()\r\n\n![image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/e1eea3e1-fbe0-447f-9c23-c327c129a10d)\r\n\nIt should be a Basemodel, but it's still a dictionary inside\n@mathiasertl \nAre you sure you're using the latest beta release?\nI'm seeing a similar error with version `5.5.0rc5`.\n\n```python\nclass NotificationTypeEnum(str, Enum):\n    DANGER = \"danger\"\n    SUCCESS = \"success\"\n    WARNING = \"warning\"\n\n\nclass NotificationData(BaseModel):\n    message: str\n    title: str\n    type: NotificationTypeEnum = NotificationTypeEnum.SUCCESS\n```\n\n```python\n@shared_task(pydantic=True)\ndef send_notification(notification_data: NotificationData, user_id: int):\n   ...\n```\n\n```\u00b4\n>>> from ... import NotificationData\n>>> from ... import send_notification\n>>> nd = NotificationData(message=\"Testnachricht\", title=\"Testtitel\")\n>>> send_notification.delay(nd, 1)\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"/projectpath/.venv/lib/python3.13/site-packages/kombu/serialization.py\", line 41, in _reraise_errors\n    yield\n  File \"/projectpath/.venv/lib/python3.13/site-packages/kombu/serialization.py\", line 220, in dumps\n    payload = encoder(data)\n  File \"/projectpath/.venv/lib/python3.13/site-packages/kombu/utils/json.py\", line 63, in dumps\n    return _dumps(s, cls=cls, **dict(default_kwargs, **kwargs))\n  File \"/pythonpath/lib/python3.13/json/__init__.py\", line 238, in dumps\n    **kw).encode(obj)\n          ~~~~~~^^^^^\n  File \"/pythonpath/lib/python3.13/json/encoder.py\", line 200, in encode\n    chunks = self.iterencode(o, _one_shot=True)\n  File \"/pythonpath/lib/python3.13/json/encoder.py\", line 261, in iterencode\n    return _iterencode(o, 0)\n  File \"/projectpath/.venv/lib/python3.13/site-packages/kombu/utils/json.py\", line 47, in default\n    return super().default(o)\n           ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~^^^\n  File \"/pythonpath/lib/python3.13/json/encoder.py\", line 180, in default\n    raise TypeError(f'Object of type {o.__class__.__name__} '\n                    f'is not JSON serializable')\nTypeError: Object of type NotificationData is not JSON serializable\n\nDuring handling of the above exception, another exception occurred:\n\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"<console>\", line 1, in <module>\n  File \"/projectpath/.venv/lib/python3.13/site-packages/celery/app/task.py\", line 444, in delay\n    return self.apply_async(args, kwargs)\n           ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n  File \"/projectpath/.venv/lib/python3.13/site-packages/celery/app/task.py\", line 601, in apply_async\n    return app.send_task(\n           ~~~~~~~~~~~~~^\n        self.name, args, kwargs, task_id=task_id, producer=producer,\n        ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n    ...<2 lines>...\n        **options\n        ^^^^^^^^^\n    )\n    ^\n  File \"/projectpath/.venv/lib/python3.13/site-packages/celery/app/base.py\", line 922, in send_task\n    amqp.send_task_message(P, name, message, **options)\n    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n  File \"/projectpath/.venv/lib/python3.13/site-packages/celery/app/amqp.py\", line 523, in send_task_message\n    ret = producer.publish(\n        body,\n    ...<8 lines>...\n        **properties\n    )\n  File \"/projectpath/.venv/lib/python3.13/site-packages/kombu/messaging.py\", line 178, in publish\n    body, content_type, content_encoding = self._prepare(\n                                           ~~~~~~~~~~~~~^\n        body, serializer, content_type, content_encoding,\n        ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n        compression, headers)\n        ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n  File \"/projectpath/.venv/lib/python3.13/site-packages/kombu/messaging.py\", line 280, in _prepare\n    body) = dumps(body, serializer=serializer)\n            ~~~~~^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n  File \"/projectpath/.venv/lib/python3.13/site-packages/kombu/serialization.py\", line 219, in dumps\n    with _reraise_errors(EncodeError):\n         ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n  File \"/pythonpath/lib/python3.13/contextlib.py\", line 162, in __exit__\n    self.gen.throw(value)\n    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~^^^^^^^\n  File \"/projectpath/.venv/lib/python3.13/site-packages/kombu/serialization.py\", line 45, in _reraise_errors\n    reraise(wrapper, wrapper(exc), sys.exc_info()[2])\n    ~~~~~~~^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n  File \"/projectpath/.venv/lib/python3.13/site-packages/kombu/exceptions.py\", line 34, in reraise\n    raise value.with_traceback(tb)\n  File \"/projectpath/.venv/lib/python3.13/site-packages/kombu/serialization.py\", line 41, in _reraise_errors\n    yield\n  File \"/projectpath/.venv/lib/python3.13/site-packages/kombu/serialization.py\", line 220, in dumps\n    payload = encoder(data)\n  File \"/projectpath/.venv/lib/python3.13/site-packages/kombu/utils/json.py\", line 63, in dumps\n    return _dumps(s, cls=cls, **dict(default_kwargs, **kwargs))\n  File \"/pythonpath/lib/python3.13/json/__init__.py\", line 238, in dumps\n    **kw).encode(obj)\n          ~~~~~~^^^^^\n  File \"/pythonpath/lib/python3.13/json/encoder.py\", line 200, in encode\n    chunks = self.iterencode(o, _one_shot=True)\n  File \"/pythonpath/lib/python3.13/json/encoder.py\", line 261, in iterencode\n    return _iterencode(o, 0)\n  File \"/projectpath/.venv/lib/python3.13/site-packages/kombu/utils/json.py\", line 47, in default\n    return super().default(o)\n           ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~^^^\n  File \"/pythonpath/lib/python3.13/json/encoder.py\", line 180, in default\n    raise TypeError(f'Object of type {o.__class__.__name__} '\n                    f'is not JSON serializable')\nkombu.exceptions.EncodeError: Object of type NotificationData is not JSON serializable\n```\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nHi,\n\nAs noted in the docs, the current implementation only covers the task side. The task function will receive a model, but the caller itself still has to dump it.\n\nKr, mat\nI think it would be a nice improvement if the caller side was also handled automatically. \nYes, absolutely! I unfortunately do not have time to implement it on my own. I'm happy to help review a pull request!\n\nKr, Mat \nI'm not familiar with the celery codebase. If you could give me a hint on where this should be done, I can try to figure it out.\nwe have this documented, right?\nYes: https://github.com/celery/celery/blob/main/docs%2Fuserguide%2Ftasks.rst#L808-L811\nSame problem here, the dict input is not automatically validated into a Pydantic model:\n\n![Image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/b1a2cac0-97cb-48d0-90a8-98553b1deffb)\n\n```\nAssertionError: Argument must be of type ArgModel, but got {'source_project': 'A\n```\nUsing celery v5.5.0\n\nThis is not the same problem as above, this really looks like an actual issue. \n\nCan you post a snippet that allows me to reproduce the issue?\nchecking the codes in the issue, it seems to me that the users are not serializing all of their objects properly so the issues. but may be I could be wrong. do we have this documented that the users have to json dump / serialize the objects by themselves?\nYes, there even is a big warning!\n@auvipy Note that this is a big problem for users of [celery-types](https://github.com/sbdchd/celery-types), because the decorator is currently typed to return a Task with the same parameters as the decorated function (arguably a celery-types issue). However, IMO this breaks the expectation that a celery task can be called without changes as a regular python function (see [Calling Tasks / Basics](https://docs.celeryq.dev/en/main/userguide/calling.html#basics))\n\nUnfortunately, In python there is no machinery in place to selectively modify function parameters in type stubs. However, the stubs could special case the case when the decorated function receives just one single argument which is a subclass of `BaseModel`.\n\n<img width=\"566\" height=\"187\" alt=\"Image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/884b71fe-b395-4256-90e7-3632288d209f\" />\n\nwhat we need to do to fix the remaining part?\nWe could modify `apply_async`  as follows:\n\n```python\ndef apply_async(self, args=None, kwargs=None, ...):\n    ...\n    # After serializer resolution, before send_task\n    if getattr(self, 'pydantic', False):\n        serializer = options.get('serializer') or app.conf.task_serializer\n        # Not sure about this\n        # We may want to always serialize as json, as on the receiving side we are going\n        # to decode it anyways so it should roundtrip, regardless of the mode\n        mode = \"json\" if serializer == \"json\" else \"python\"\n        args = _dump_pydantic_args(args, mode)\n        kwargs = _dump_pydantic_kwargs(kwargs, mode)\n    \n    return app.send_task(self.name, args, kwargs, ...)\n\ndef _dump_pydantic_value(value, mode=\"json\"):\n    if isinstance(value, pydantic.BaseModel):\n        return value.model_dump(mode=mode)\n    return value\n\ndef _dump_pydantic_args(args, mode=\"json\"):\n    if not args:\n        return args\n    return tuple(_dump_pydantic_value(a, mode) for a in args)\n\ndef _dump_pydantic_kwargs(kwargs, mode=\"json\"):\n    if not kwargs:\n        return kwargs\n    return {k: _dump_pydantic_value(v, mode) for k, v in kwargs.items()}\n```\nFor anyone wanting to use this in the meantime, you can define a custom task as following\n\n```python\nfrom typing import Any\n\nfrom celery import Task\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel\n\n\ndef _dump_pydantic_args(args: tuple[Any, ...]) -> tuple[Any, ...]:\n    return tuple(\n        arg.model_dump(mode=\"json\") if isinstance(arg, BaseModel) else arg\n        for arg in args\n    )\n\n\ndef _dump_pydantic_kwargs(kwargs: dict[str, Any]) -> dict[str, Any]:\n    return {\n        key: value.model_dump(mode=\"json\") if isinstance(value, BaseModel) else value\n        for key, value in kwargs.items()\n    }\n\n\nclass PydanticTask(Task):\n    def apply_async(self, args=None, kwargs=None, **options):\n        if args:\n            args = _dump_pydantic_args(tuple(args))\n        if kwargs:\n            kwargs = _dump_pydantic_kwargs(kwargs)\n\n        return super().apply_async(args, kwargs, **options)\n```\n\nSame issue here. `pydantic=True` validates on the worker but `.delay(Model(...))` still fails at serialize time unless you `model_dump()` first, and `.get()` gives you a dict back.\n\nI put together a small workaround for our stack: [queuebridge](https://github.com/false200/queuebridge). Registers a Kombu serializer so you can pass Pydantic models on enqueue, and `typed_result()` so `.get()` returns a model again.\n\n```python\nfrom queuebridge.celery import register_queuebridge, typed_result\n\nregister_queuebridge(app)\n\n@app.task(pydantic=True)\ndef process(order: OrderCreate) -> OrderResult:\n    return OrderResult(id=order.id, status=\"ok\")\n\nar = process.delay(OrderCreate(id=1, sku=\"ABC\"))\nresult = typed_result(ar, OrderResult).get()\n```\n\nDocs: https://queuebridge.readthedocs.io\n\nI built this to work around the same problem. Sharing here in case it helps anyone else.\n", "created_at": "2026-10-11T18:23:31Z"}
{"repo": "celery/celery", "pull_number": 10843, "instance_id": "celery__celery-10843", "issue_numbers": ["9082"], "base_commit": "ccec3554aa6b0b63227b1870500c2da14080504a", "patch": "diff --git a/celery/app/defaults.py b/celery/app/defaults.py\nindex 55f100856f1..cced577a07a 100644\n--- a/celery/app/defaults.py\n+++ b/celery/app/defaults.py\n@@ -310,6 +310,7 @@ def __repr__(self):\n         queues=Option(type='dict'),\n         queue_max_priority=Option(None, type='int'),\n         reject_on_worker_lost=Option(type='bool'),\n+        retry_on_worker_lost=Option(False, type='bool'),\n         remote_tracebacks=Option(False, type='bool'),\n         repr_maxlevels=Option(3, type='int'),\n         routes=Option(type='any'),\ndiff --git a/celery/app/task.py b/celery/app/task.py\nindex bd1b6721c36..5afb75c499d 100644\n--- a/celery/app/task.py\n+++ b/celery/app/task.py\n@@ -358,6 +358,17 @@ class Task:\n     #: what you're doing.\n     reject_on_worker_lost = None\n \n+    #: When enabled, a task whose worker process exits abruptly is sent\n+    #: again the same way :meth:`retry` would, so :attr:`max_retries` and\n+    #: :attr:`default_retry_delay` apply.  Once the retries run out the\n+    #: task fails instead of being re-queued.\n+    #:\n+    #: Takes precedence over :attr:`reject_on_worker_lost`, which is still\n+    #: used if sending the retry fails.\n+    #: The default is taken from the :setting:`task_retry_on_worker_lost`\n+    #: setting.\n+    retry_on_worker_lost = None\n+\n     #: Tuple of expected exceptions.\n     #:\n     #: These are errors that are expected in normal operation\n@@ -405,6 +416,7 @@ class Task:\n         ('acks_on_failure', 'task_acks_on_failure'),\n         ('acks_on_timeout', 'task_acks_on_timeout'),\n         ('reject_on_worker_lost', 'task_reject_on_worker_lost'),\n+        ('retry_on_worker_lost', 'task_retry_on_worker_lost'),\n         ('ignore_result', 'task_ignore_result'),\n         ('store_eager_result', 'task_store_eager_result'),\n         ('store_errors_even_if_ignored', 'task_store_errors_even_if_ignored'),\ndiff --git a/celery/worker/request.py b/celery/worker/request.py\nindex 3e84353be39..ffcbb3618f3 100644\n--- a/celery/worker/request.py\n+++ b/celery/worker/request.py\n@@ -702,14 +702,26 @@ def on_failure(self, exc_info, send_failed_event=True, return_ok=False):\n         elif isinstance(exc, Retry):\n             return self.on_retry(exc_info)\n \n+        is_worker_lost = isinstance(exc, WorkerLostError)\n+        max_retries_exceeded = False\n+        if is_worker_lost and self.task.retry_on_worker_lost:\n+            max_retries = self.task.max_retries\n+            max_retries_exceeded = (\n+                max_retries is not None and self._context.retries >= max_retries\n+            )\n+            # If sending the retry fails, fall through so the message is\n+            # still rejected/requeued as it would be without this setting.\n+            if not max_retries_exceeded and self._retry_worker_lost(exc, exc_info):\n+                return\n+\n         # (acks_late) acknowledge after result stored.\n         requeue = False\n-        is_worker_lost = isinstance(exc, WorkerLostError)\n         if self.task.acks_late:\n             is_timeout = isinstance(exc, TimeLimitExceeded)\n             ack_flag = self.task.acks_on_timeout if is_timeout else self.task.acks_on_failure\n             reject = (\n-                (self.task.reject_on_worker_lost and is_worker_lost)\n+                (self.task.reject_on_worker_lost and is_worker_lost\n+                 and not max_retries_exceeded)\n                 or (is_timeout and not ack_flag)\n             )\n             if reject:\n@@ -761,6 +773,39 @@ def on_failure(self, exc_info, send_failed_event=True, return_ok=False):\n             error('Task handler raised error: %r', exc,\n                   exc_info=exc_info.exc_info)\n \n+    def _retry_worker_lost(self, exc, exc_info):\n+        \"\"\"Retry the task after its worker was lost, return False if not retried.\"\"\"\n+        task = self.task\n+        request = self._context\n+        countdown = task.default_retry_delay\n+        try:\n+            task.signature_from_request(\n+                request, countdown=countdown, retries=request.retries + 1,\n+            ).apply_async()\n+        except Exception as send_exc:\n+            error('Failed to retry task %s[%s] after worker was lost: %r',\n+                  self.task_name, self.id, send_exc, exc_info=True)\n+            return False\n+\n+        task.backend.mark_as_retry(\n+            self.id, exc, exc_info.traceback, request=request,\n+        )\n+        if task.acks_late:\n+            self.acknowledge()\n+        self.send_event('task-retried',\n+                        exception=safe_repr(exc),\n+                        traceback=safe_str(exc_info.traceback))\n+\n+        reason = Retry(exc=exc, when=countdown)\n+        task.request_stack.push(request)\n+        try:\n+            task.on_retry(exc, self.id, self.args, self.kwargs, exc_info)\n+            send_retry(task, request=request, reason=reason, einfo=exc_info)\n+        finally:\n+            task.request_stack.pop()\n+        info('Task %s[%s] retry: %s', self.task_name, self.id, reason)\n+        return True\n+\n     def _worker_lost_info(self, exc):\n         if isinstance(exc, SystemExit):\n             code = exc.code\ndiff --git a/docs/userguide/configuration.rst b/docs/userguide/configuration.rst\nindex c187a889a2a..49a66656294 100644\n--- a/docs/userguide/configuration.rst\n+++ b/docs/userguide/configuration.rst\n@@ -729,7 +729,43 @@ worker.\n .. warning::\n \n     Enabling this can cause message loops; make sure you know\n-    what you're doing.\n+    what you're doing. See :setting:`task_retry_on_worker_lost` for a way\n+    to limit how many times a task is sent again.\n+\n+.. setting:: task_retry_on_worker_lost\n+\n+``task_retry_on_worker_lost``\n+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n+\n+.. versionadded:: 5.7\n+\n+Default: Disabled.\n+\n+When the worker process executing a task exits abruptly (e.g. it's killed\n+by the OOM killer), send the task again the same way\n+:meth:`Task.retry() <celery.app.task.Task.retry>` would, instead of\n+re-queuing the original message. This means\n+:attr:`~celery.app.task.Task.max_retries` and\n+:attr:`~celery.app.task.Task.default_retry_delay` apply, and once the\n+retries run out the task is marked as failed.\n+\n+This takes precedence over :setting:`task_reject_on_worker_lost`. If\n+sending the retry fails, the task falls back to the normal\n+:setting:`task_reject_on_worker_lost` behaviour.\n+\n+A few things to keep in mind:\n+\n+- The task is sent again after\n+  :attr:`~celery.app.task.Task.default_retry_delay` (180 seconds by\n+  default), not right away.\n+- These retries count towards the same\n+  :attr:`~celery.app.task.Task.max_retries` as calls to\n+  :meth:`Task.retry() <celery.app.task.Task.retry>` made by the task itself.\n+- It also applies when :setting:`task_acks_late` is disabled.\n+- It only covers a pool child process dying while the main worker process\n+  keeps running. If the whole worker is killed (for example the container\n+  is OOM-killed), nothing is left to retry the task, so this setting has\n+  no effect.\n \n .. setting:: task_default_rate_limit\n \ndiff --git a/t/smoke/tasks.py b/t/smoke/tasks.py\nindex 93a7aae10cf..c2f240c6131 100644\n--- a/t/smoke/tasks.py\n+++ b/t/smoke/tasks.py\n@@ -66,6 +66,12 @@ def self_termination_sigkill():\n     os.kill(os.getpid(), SIGKILL)\n \n \n+@shared_task(default_retry_delay=0, max_retries=2)\n+def self_termination_sigkill_with_retries():\n+    \"\"\"Forceful termination, with a limited number of retries.\"\"\"\n+    os.kill(os.getpid(), SIGKILL)\n+\n+\n @shared_task\n def self_termination_system_exit():\n     \"\"\"Triggers a system exit to simulate a critical stop of the Celery worker.\"\"\"\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/t/smoke/tests/test_tasks.py b/t/smoke/tests/test_tasks.py\nindex 86e3a44f019..08bedfe89cc 100644\n--- a/t/smoke/tests/test_tasks.py\n+++ b/t/smoke/tests/test_tasks.py\n@@ -9,7 +9,8 @@\n from t.integration.tasks import add, add_ignore_result, identity\n from t.smoke.conftest import SuiteOperations, TaskTermination\n from t.smoke.tasks import (noop, replace_with_task, self_termination_delay_timeout,\n-                           soft_time_limit_lower_than_time_limit, soft_time_limit_must_exceed_time_limit)\n+                           self_termination_sigkill_with_retries, soft_time_limit_lower_than_time_limit,\n+                           soft_time_limit_must_exceed_time_limit)\n \n \n class test_apply_async:\n@@ -123,6 +124,29 @@ def test_terminated_task_logs_correct_error(\n         celery_setup.worker.assert_log_exists(expected_log)\n \n \n+class test_retry_on_worker_lost:\n+    @pytest.fixture\n+    def default_worker_app(self, default_worker_app: Celery) -> Celery:\n+        app = default_worker_app\n+        app.conf.worker_prefetch_multiplier = 1\n+        app.conf.worker_concurrency = 1\n+        app.conf.task_acks_late = True\n+        app.conf.task_reject_on_worker_lost = True\n+        app.conf.task_retry_on_worker_lost = True\n+        return app\n+\n+    def test_task_fails_when_retries_run_out(self, celery_setup: CeleryTestSetup):\n+        worker = celery_setup.worker\n+        sig = self_termination_sigkill_with_retries.si().set(queue=worker.worker_queue)\n+        res = sig.apply_async()\n+\n+        with pytest.raises(WorkerLostError):\n+            res.get(timeout=RESULT_TIMEOUT)\n+\n+        assert worker.logs().count(f\"{sig.name}[{res.id}] received\") == 3\n+        assert worker.logs().count(f\"{sig.name}[{res.id}] retry:\") == 2\n+\n+\n class test_replace:\n     @pytest.fixture\n     def celery_worker_cluster(\ndiff --git a/t/unit/worker/test_request.py b/t/unit/worker/test_request.py\nindex 304df0a52aa..c7465e64f7a 100644\n--- a/t/unit/worker/test_request.py\n+++ b/t/unit/worker/test_request.py\n@@ -431,6 +431,107 @@ def test_on_failure_WorkerLostError_rejects_with_requeue(self):\n             req_logger, req.connection_errors, True)\n         req.task.backend.mark_as_failure.assert_not_called()\n \n+    def _worker_lost_request(self, retries=0):\n+        try:\n+            raise WorkerLostError()\n+        except WorkerLostError:\n+            einfo = ExceptionInfo(internal=True)\n+\n+        req = self.get_request(self.add.s(2, 2).set(retries=retries))\n+        req.task.acks_late = True\n+        req.task.reject_on_worker_lost = True\n+        req.task.retry_on_worker_lost = True\n+        req.task.max_retries = 3\n+        req.task.backend = Mock()\n+        return req, einfo\n+\n+    def test_on_failure_WorkerLostError_retry_on_worker_lost(self):\n+        req, einfo = self._worker_lost_request(retries=1)\n+\n+        retry_handler = Mock()\n+        task_retry.connect(retry_handler)\n+        try:\n+            with patch('celery.canvas.Signature.apply_async') as apply_async, \\\n+                    patch.object(req.task, 'on_retry') as on_retry:\n+                req.on_failure(einfo)\n+        finally:\n+            task_retry.disconnect(retry_handler)\n+\n+        apply_async.assert_called_once()\n+        req.on_ack.assert_called_once()\n+        req.on_reject.assert_not_called()\n+        req.task.backend.mark_as_retry.assert_called_once()\n+        req.task.backend.mark_as_failure.assert_not_called()\n+        assert any(\n+            c.args and c.args[0] == 'task-retried'\n+            for c in req.eventer.send.call_args_list\n+        )\n+        on_retry.assert_called_once()\n+        assert isinstance(on_retry.call_args.args[0], WorkerLostError)\n+        assert on_retry.call_args.args[1] == req.id\n+        retry_handler.assert_called_once()\n+        assert isinstance(retry_handler.call_args.kwargs['reason'], Retry)\n+\n+    def test_on_failure_WorkerLostError_retry_on_worker_lost_options(self):\n+        req, einfo = self._worker_lost_request(retries=1)\n+        req.task.default_retry_delay = 30\n+\n+        with patch.object(req.task, 'signature_from_request') as sfr:\n+            req.on_failure(einfo)\n+\n+        _, kwargs = sfr.call_args\n+        assert kwargs['retries'] == 2\n+        assert kwargs['countdown'] == 30\n+        sfr.return_value.apply_async.assert_called_once_with()\n+\n+    def test_on_failure_WorkerLostError_retry_on_worker_lost_exhausted(self):\n+        req, einfo = self._worker_lost_request(retries=3)\n+\n+        with patch('celery.canvas.Signature.apply_async') as apply_async:\n+            req.on_failure(einfo)\n+\n+        # No retries left: the task fails instead of being re-queued.\n+        apply_async.assert_not_called()\n+        req.on_reject.assert_not_called()\n+        req.on_ack.assert_called_once()\n+        req.task.backend.mark_as_failure.assert_called_once()\n+\n+    def test_on_failure_WorkerLostError_retry_on_worker_lost_send_fails(self):\n+        req, einfo = self._worker_lost_request()\n+\n+        with patch('celery.canvas.Signature.apply_async',\n+                   side_effect=ConnectionError('broker down')):\n+            req.on_failure(einfo)\n+\n+        # Falls back to reject_on_worker_lost, so the task isn't lost.\n+        req.on_reject.assert_called_with(\n+            req_logger, req.connection_errors, True)\n+        req.task.backend.mark_as_retry.assert_not_called()\n+        req.task.backend.mark_as_failure.assert_not_called()\n+\n+    def test_on_failure_WorkerLostError_retry_on_worker_lost_send_fails_no_reject(self):\n+        req, einfo = self._worker_lost_request()\n+        req.task.reject_on_worker_lost = False\n+\n+        with patch('celery.canvas.Signature.apply_async',\n+                   side_effect=ConnectionError('broker down')):\n+            req.on_failure(einfo)\n+\n+        req.on_reject.assert_not_called()\n+        req.task.backend.mark_as_retry.assert_not_called()\n+        req.task.backend.mark_as_failure.assert_called_once()\n+\n+    def test_on_failure_WorkerLostError_retry_on_worker_lost_no_acks_late(self):\n+        req, einfo = self._worker_lost_request()\n+        req.task.acks_late = False\n+\n+        with patch('celery.canvas.Signature.apply_async') as apply_async:\n+            req.on_failure(einfo)\n+\n+        apply_async.assert_called_once()\n+        req.on_ack.assert_not_called()\n+        req.task.backend.mark_as_failure.assert_not_called()\n+\n     def test_on_failure_WorkerLostError_redelivered_None(self):\n         try:\n             raise WorkerLostError()\n", "problem_statement": "Avoid infinite retries for reject_on_worker_lost\n<!--\r\nPlease fill this template entirely and do not erase parts of it.\r\nWe reserve the right to close without a response\r\nfeature requests which are incomplete.\r\n-->\r\n# Checklist\r\n<!--\r\nTo check an item on the list replace [ ] with [x].\r\n-->\r\n\r\n- [x] I have checked the [issues list](https://github.com/celery/celery/issues?utf8=%E2%9C%93&q=is%3Aissue+label%3A%22Issue+Type%3A+Feature+Request%22+)\r\n  for similar or identical feature requests.\r\n- [x] I have checked the [pull requests list](https://github.com/celery/celery/pulls?utf8=%E2%9C%93&q=is%3Apr+label%3A%22PR+Type%3A+Feature%22+)\r\n  for existing proposed implementations of this feature.\r\n- [x] I have checked the [commit log](https://github.com/celery/celery/commits/main)\r\n  to find out if the same feature was already implemented in the\r\n  main branch.\r\n- [x] I have included all related issues and possible duplicate issues\r\n  in this issue (If there are none, check this box anyway).\r\n\r\n## Related Issues and Possible Duplicates\r\n<!--\r\nPlease make sure to search and mention any related issues\r\nor possible duplicates to this issue as requested by the checklist above.\r\n\r\nThis may or may not include issues in other repositories that the Celery project\r\nmaintains or other repositories that are dependencies of Celery.\r\n\r\nIf you don't know how to mention issues, please refer to Github's documentation\r\non the subject: https://help.github.com/en/articles/autolinked-references-and-urls#issues-and-pull-requests\r\n-->\r\n\r\n#### Related Issues\r\n\r\n- https://github.com/celery/celery/discussions/8969#discussion-6516045\r\n\r\n#### Possible Duplicates\r\n\r\n- None\r\n\r\n# Brief Summary\r\n<!--\r\nPlease include a brief summary of what the feature does\r\nand why it is needed.\r\n-->\r\n\r\nEnabling reject_on_worker_lost may cause infinite retry because it requeues the task to message broker, and it doesn't honor the max retries. This is extremely risky and may cause significant loss. Is there a way to somehow avoid infinite retries?\r\n\r\n# Design\r\n\r\n## Architectural Considerations\r\n<!--\r\nIf more components other than Celery are involved,\r\ndescribe them here and the effect it would have on Celery.\r\n-->\r\nNone\r\n\r\n## Proposed Behavior\r\n<!--\r\nPlease describe in detail how this feature is going to behave.\r\nDescribe what happens in case of failures as well if applicable.\r\n-->\r\nAdd a param like max_requeue?\r\n\r\n## Proposed UI/UX\r\n<!--\r\nPlease provide your ideas for the API, CLI options,\r\nconfiguration key names etc. that will be introduced for this feature.\r\n-->\r\n\r\n## Diagrams\r\n<!--\r\nPlease include any diagrams that might be relevant\r\nto the implementation of this feature such as:\r\n* Class Diagrams\r\n* Sequence Diagrams\r\n* Activity Diagrams\r\nYou can drag and drop images into the text box to attach them to this issue.\r\n-->\r\nN/A\r\n\r\n## Alternatives\r\n<!--\r\nIf you have considered any alternative implementations\r\ndescribe them in detail below.\r\n-->\r\nNone\r\n\n", "hints_text": "Hi, I'd like to work on this it's the same problem as discussion #8969 where @auvipy said \n\n> a draft PR would be welcome\n\n#9720 fixed the missing request context in `task_failure` but tasks that hit a lost worker still get requeued with no limit.\n\nMy plan is option A from that discussion made opt-in: add a new setting (something like `task_retry_on_worker_lost` off by default). When it's on a lost worker goes through `retry()` instead of `reject(requeue=True)`, so `max_retries` applies. When it's off, nothing changes.\n\nI'll open a draft PR for this.\n", "created_at": "2026-10-11T11:38:07Z"}
{"repo": "celery/celery", "pull_number": 10842, "instance_id": "celery__celery-10842", "issue_numbers": ["10841"], "base_commit": "a49e5092aacc7de8cf6fd94a6525bf2cfce49d09", "patch": "diff --git a/celery/concurrency/asynpool.py b/celery/concurrency/asynpool.py\nindex 9961a46f5cc..8c1454d859c 100644\n--- a/celery/concurrency/asynpool.py\n+++ b/celery/concurrency/asynpool.py\n@@ -993,7 +993,7 @@ def _write_job(proc, fd, job):\n                         errors += 1\n                         if errors > 100:\n                             on_not_recovering(proc, fd, job, exc)\n-                            raise StopIteration()\n+                            return\n                         yield\n                     else:\n                         errors = 0\n@@ -1009,7 +1009,7 @@ def _write_job(proc, fd, job):\n                         errors += 1\n                         if errors > 100:\n                             on_not_recovering(proc, fd, job, exc)\n-                            raise StopIteration()\n+                            return\n                         yield\n                     else:\n                         errors = 0\n@@ -1043,7 +1043,7 @@ def _write_ack(fd, ack, callback=None):\n                 except KeyError:\n                     # process died, we can safely discard the ack at this\n                     # point.\n-                    raise StopIteration()\n+                    return\n                 send = proc.send_syn_offset\n \n                 Hw = Bw = 0\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/t/unit/concurrency/test_prefork.py b/t/unit/concurrency/test_prefork.py\nindex e9e147c00ef..d8924ec8396 100644\n--- a/t/unit/concurrency/test_prefork.py\n+++ b/t/unit/concurrency/test_prefork.py\n@@ -598,6 +598,83 @@ def test_schedule_writes_hub_remove_fd_only_from_writers_when_write_job_is_done(\n         assert 2 not in hub.writers\n         assert 2 in hub.readers\n \n+    @t.skip.if_pypy\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize('header_written', [False, True])\n+    @patch('billiard.pool.Pool._create_worker_process')\n+    def test_write_job_stops_when_inqueue_not_recovering(\n+            self, _create_worker_process, header_written):\n+        \"\"\"_write_job must end cleanly when the inqueue stays unwritable.\n+\n+        After more than 100 consecutive EAGAIN errors writing the header or\n+        the body, on_not_recovering() terminates the process and puts the\n+        job back.  The generator must then end with StopIteration, which the\n+        event loop expects: under PEP 479 a ``raise StopIteration()`` in the\n+        generator becomes RuntimeError and stops the worker.\n+        \"\"\"\n+        pool = asynpool.AsynPool(processes=1, threads=False)\n+        hub = self._get_hub()\n+\n+        fd = 2\n+        written = [4] if header_written else []\n+\n+        def send_job_offset(buf, offset):\n+            if written:\n+                return written.pop()\n+            raise OSError(errno.EAGAIN, 'Resource temporarily unavailable')\n+\n+        proc = Mock(name='proc', index=0, exitcode=None)\n+        proc._is_alive.return_value = True\n+        proc.send_job_offset.side_effect = send_job_offset\n+        pool._all_inqueues = {fd}\n+        pool._fileno_to_inq[fd] = proc\n+        pool._create_write_handlers(hub)\n+\n+        job = ApplyResult({}, lambda x: True)\n+        job._payload = (memoryview(b'\\0\\0\\0\\x04'), memoryview(b'body'), 4)\n+        pool.outbound_buffer.append(job)\n+\n+        with patch.object(asynpool, 'logger') as logger:\n+            # schedule_writes() takes the first step and registers the\n+            # generator as the writer for fd; the event loop drives the rest.\n+            hub.consolidate_callback(ready_fds=[fd])\n+            cor = job._writer()\n+            assert hub.writers[fd] == (cor, ())\n+            with pytest.raises(StopIteration):\n+                for _ in range(200):\n+                    next(cor)\n+\n+        logger.exception.assert_called_once()\n+        assert logger.exception.call_args[0][0].startswith(\n+            'Process inqueue damaged')\n+        proc.terminate.assert_called_once_with()\n+        assert list(pool.outbound_buffer) == [job]\n+        assert fd not in hub.writers\n+        assert fd not in pool._active_writes\n+        assert cor not in pool._active_writers\n+\n+    @t.skip.if_pypy\n+    @patch('billiard.pool.Pool._create_worker_process')\n+    def test_write_ack_stops_when_process_gone(self, _create_worker_process):\n+        \"\"\"_write_ack must end cleanly when the synq has no process anymore.\n+\n+        The ack is discarded and the generator must end with StopIteration,\n+        not RuntimeError (PEP 479).\n+        \"\"\"\n+        pool = asynpool.AsynPool(processes=1, synack=True, threads=False)\n+        hub = self._get_hub()\n+        pool._create_write_handlers(hub)\n+\n+        fd = 3\n+        pool.send_ack(asynpool.ACK, 1234, Mock(name='job'), fd)\n+        cor, _ = hub.writers[fd]\n+        assert cor in pool._active_writers\n+\n+        with pytest.raises(StopIteration):\n+            next(cor)\n+\n+        assert fd not in pool._active_writes\n+        assert cor not in pool._active_writers\n+\n     @t.skip.if_pypy\n     def test_register_with_event_loop__no_on_tick_dupes(self):\n         \"\"\"Ensure AsynPool's register_with_event_loop only registers\n", "problem_statement": "Prefork worker exits with \"RuntimeError: generator raised StopIteration\" after \"Process inqueue damaged\"\n## Summary\n\nWhen the prefork pool gives up writing a task to a child process's inqueue\n(more than 100 consecutive EAGAIN errors), the worker does not recover. Instead\nof terminating that child and rescheduling the task, it logs\n`Unrecoverable error: RuntimeError('generator raised StopIteration')`, shuts\ndown and exits with code 1.\n\n## Environment\n\n- celery 5.6.3, kombu 5.6.2, billiard 4.2.4, vine 5.1.0\n- prefork pool (default concurrency), worker started with embedded beat (`--beat`), Redis broker\n- Python 3.14.7, macOS 26.4 (arm64)\n- Still present on `main` at ed354d9100dbdaf7b867f103593b4dea4148670a\n\n## Actual behavior\n\nThe worker log shows:\n\n```\nERROR Process inqueue damaged: <ForkProcess(ForkPoolWorker-17\u2026)> BlockingIOError(35)\nCRITICAL Unrecoverable error: RuntimeError('generator raised StopIteration')\n```\n\nafter which the worker (and its embedded beat) shuts down and the process exits\nwith code 1. This happened 4 times in two days, in two separate deployments on\nthe same machine; each time the damaged child was the same pool slot\n(`ForkPoolWorker-17`).\n\nWhy the writes kept failing with EAGAIN is a separate question that this issue\ndoes not try to answer; it is about what happens after the pool gives up on the\nchild.\n\n## Expected behavior\n\nThe pool terminates the damaged child, puts the job back so it is written to\nanother child, and the worker keeps running. That is what `on_not_recovering()`\nalready does; only the way the write generator ends afterwards is broken.\n\n## Root cause\n\n`AsynPool._create_write_handlers()` defines `_write_job()`, a generator that the\nevent loop advances each time the child's inqueue is writable. When writing the\nheader or the body fails with an errno in `UNAVAIL` more than 100 times in a\nrow, it calls `on_not_recovering()` and then does `raise StopIteration()`:\n\n- https://github.com/celery/celery/blob/ed354d9100dbdaf7b867f103593b4dea4148670a/celery/concurrency/asynpool.py#L994-L996 (header)\n- https://github.com/celery/celery/blob/ed354d9100dbdaf7b867f103593b4dea4148670a/celery/concurrency/asynpool.py#L1010-L1012 (body)\n\nSince PEP 479 (always on from Python 3.7), a `StopIteration` raised inside a\ngenerator body is converted to `RuntimeError('generator raised StopIteration')`\nwhen it leaves the generator. The callers that drive the generator\n(`Hub.create_loop()` in kombu, `schedule_writes()`, `AsynPool._flush_writer()`)\ncatch `StopIteration` as \"write finished\"; they do not catch `RuntimeError`.\n`Hub.create_loop()` removes the fd and re-raises it, it propagates out of\n`asynloop()` and the consumer, and `WorkController.start()` logs it as\n`Unrecoverable error` and stops the worker with exit code 1.\n\n`_write_ack()` in the same function has the same pattern in its\n\"process died, discard the ack\" branch (synack mode):\n\n- https://github.com/celery/celery/blob/ed354d9100dbdaf7b867f103593b4dea4148670a/celery/concurrency/asynpool.py#L1043-L1046\n\nThe fix is to `return` from the generator at these sites, which ends it with a\nplain `StopIteration` from `next()`, as the callers expect.\n\n## Impact\n\nA transient condition in one child process takes down the whole worker. Under\na supervisor without automatic restart, all background processing stops (tasks\nand, with `-B`, periodic tasks) until someone restarts the worker by hand.\n\n", "hints_text": "", "created_at": "2026-10-11T09:00:12Z"}
{"repo": "celery/celery", "pull_number": 10839, "instance_id": "celery__celery-10839", "issue_numbers": ["8943"], "base_commit": "756e79f13508280cb796088a2f97faefabc2a87c", "patch": "diff --git a/celery/worker/autoscale.py b/celery/worker/autoscale.py\nindex e5b9024cade..13321b61d07 100644\n--- a/celery/worker/autoscale.py\n+++ b/celery/worker/autoscale.py\n@@ -47,6 +47,13 @@ def create(self, w):\n         )\n         return scaler if not w.use_eventloop else None\n \n+    def start(self, w):\n+        if not w.use_eventloop:\n+            on_task_received = getattr(w.autoscaler, 'on_task_received', None)\n+            if on_task_received is not None:\n+                w.consumer.on_task_message.add(on_task_received)\n+        super().start(w)\n+\n     def register_with_event_loop(self, w, hub):\n         w.consumer.on_task_message.add(w.autoscaler.maybe_scale)\n         hub.call_repeatedly(\n@@ -71,6 +78,7 @@ def __init__(self, pool, max_concurrency,\n         self.min_concurrency = min_concurrency\n         self.keepalive = keepalive\n         self._last_scale_up = None\n+        self._last_task_received = None\n         self.worker = worker\n \n         assert self.keepalive, 'cannot scale down too fast.'\n@@ -92,9 +100,14 @@ def _maybe_scale(self, req=None):\n             return True\n \n     def maybe_scale(self, req=None):\n+        if req is not None:\n+            self.on_task_received(req)\n         if self._maybe_scale(req):\n             self.pool.maintain_pool()\n \n+    def on_task_received(self, req):\n+        self._last_task_received = monotonic()\n+\n     def update(self, max=None, min=None):\n         with self.mutex:\n             if max is not None:\n@@ -113,8 +126,13 @@ def scale_up(self, n):\n         return self._grow(n)\n \n     def scale_down(self, n):\n-        if self._last_scale_up and (\n-                monotonic() - self._last_scale_up > self.keepalive):\n+        last_active = self._last_scale_up\n+        last_received = self._last_task_received\n+        if last_received is not None and (\n+                last_active is None or last_received > last_active):\n+            last_active = last_received\n+        if last_active is not None and (\n+                monotonic() - last_active > self.keepalive):\n             return self._shrink(n)\n \n     def _grow(self, n):\ndiff --git a/docs/userguide/workers.rst b/docs/userguide/workers.rst\nindex 4b0cc92bc59..058261b5433 100644\n--- a/docs/userguide/workers.rst\n+++ b/docs/userguide/workers.rst\n@@ -919,6 +919,12 @@ based on load:\n - The autoscaler adds more pool processes when there is work to do,\n     - and starts removing processes when the workload is low.\n \n+Before removing processes, it waits for the keepalive period (30 seconds\n+by default) to pass since the last task was accepted for immediate execution\n+or the pool was grown. Tasks routed through ETA/countdown or rate-limit\n+scheduling do not reset this timer.\n+Set the ``AUTOSCALE_KEEPALIVE`` environment variable to change this period.\n+\n It's enabled by the :option:`--autoscale <celery worker --autoscale>` option,\n which needs two numbers: the maximum and minimum number of pool processes:\n \n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/t/smoke/tests/test_autoscale.py b/t/smoke/tests/test_autoscale.py\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 00000000000..4782dbfa134\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/t/smoke/tests/test_autoscale.py\n@@ -0,0 +1,42 @@\n+from __future__ import annotations\n+\n+import pytest\n+from pytest_celery import CeleryTestSetup\n+\n+from celery import group\n+from t.smoke.tasks import long_running_task\n+\n+RESULT_TIMEOUT = 30\n+\n+\n+class test_autoscale_keepalive:\n+    @pytest.fixture\n+    def default_worker_command(self, default_worker_container_cls):\n+        return default_worker_container_cls.command(\"--pool=prefork\", \"--autoscale=2,1\")\n+\n+    @pytest.fixture\n+    def default_worker_env(self, default_worker_env: dict) -> dict:\n+        default_worker_env[\"AUTOSCALE_KEEPALIVE\"] = \"5\"\n+        return default_worker_env\n+\n+    def test_short_gaps_do_not_replace_pool_processes(self, celery_setup: CeleryTestSetup):\n+        worker = celery_setup.worker\n+        queue = worker.worker_queue\n+        inspector = celery_setup.app.control.inspect(\n+            destination=[worker.hostname()], timeout=RESULT_TIMEOUT,\n+        )\n+\n+        burst = group(long_running_task.si(2).set(queue=queue) for _ in range(2))\n+        assert burst.delay().get(timeout=RESULT_TIMEOUT) == [True, True]\n+        stats = inspector.stats()\n+        assert stats is not None\n+        processes = set(stats[worker.hostname()][\"pool\"][\"processes\"])\n+        assert len(processes) == 2\n+\n+        # Receipts continue beyond the keepalive measured from the initial growth.\n+        # Each gap is short enough that the same processes should handle the next burst.\n+        for _ in range(3):\n+            assert burst.delay().get(timeout=RESULT_TIMEOUT) == [True, True]\n+            stats = inspector.stats()\n+            assert stats is not None\n+            assert set(stats[worker.hostname()][\"pool\"][\"processes\"]) == processes\ndiff --git a/t/unit/worker/test_autoscale.py b/t/unit/worker/test_autoscale.py\nindex c5f459b5ff0..077537f7c4e 100644\n--- a/t/unit/worker/test_autoscale.py\n+++ b/t/unit/worker/test_autoscale.py\n@@ -1,4 +1,5 @@\n import sys\n+from threading import Event, Thread\n from time import monotonic\n from unittest.mock import Mock, patch\n \n@@ -35,6 +36,33 @@ def num_processes(self):\n \n class test_WorkerComponent:\n \n+    def test_start_without_event_loop(self):\n+        parent = Mock(name='parent')\n+        parent.autoscale = True\n+        parent.use_eventloop = False\n+        parent.consumer.on_task_message = set()\n+        parent.steps = []\n+        step = autoscale.WorkerComponent(parent)\n+        step.include(parent)\n+        step.start(parent)\n+        assert (parent.autoscaler.on_task_received in\n+                parent.consumer.on_task_message)\n+        parent.autoscaler.start.assert_called_once_with()\n+\n+    def test_start_custom_autoscaler_without_task_callback(self):\n+        parent = Mock(name='parent')\n+        parent.autoscale = True\n+        parent.use_eventloop = False\n+        parent.consumer.on_task_message = set()\n+        parent.steps = []\n+        scaler = Mock(spec=['start'])\n+        parent.autoscaler_cls = Mock(return_value=scaler)\n+        step = autoscale.WorkerComponent(parent)\n+        step.include(parent)\n+        step.start(parent)\n+        assert not parent.consumer.on_task_message\n+        scaler.start.assert_called_once_with()\n+\n     def test_register_with_event_loop(self):\n         parent = Mock(name='parent')\n         parent.autoscale = True\n@@ -76,6 +104,57 @@ class test_Autoscaler:\n     def setup_method(self):\n         self.pool = MockPool(3)\n \n+    def test_task_receipt_does_not_wait_for_pool_resize(self):\n+        scaler = autoscale.Autoscaler(self.pool, 10, 3)\n+        received = Event()\n+\n+        def receive_task():\n+            scaler.on_task_received(Mock(name='request'))\n+            received.set()\n+\n+        receiver = Thread(target=receive_task)\n+        with scaler.mutex:\n+            receiver.start()\n+            received_while_locked = received.wait(timeout=5)\n+        receiver.join(timeout=5)\n+        assert received_while_locked\n+\n+    def test_last_scale_up_in_custom_subclass(self):\n+        class Scaler(autoscale.Autoscaler):\n+            def scale_up(self, n):\n+                self._last_scale_up = autoscale.monotonic()\n+                return self._grow(n)\n+\n+        scaler = Scaler(self.pool, 10, 3, keepalive=30)\n+        with patch('celery.worker.autoscale.monotonic', return_value=100):\n+            scaler.scale_up(7)\n+        with patch('celery.worker.autoscale.monotonic', return_value=131):\n+            scaler.scale_down(7)\n+        assert self.pool.num_processes == 3\n+\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize('use_eventloop', [True, False])\n+    def test_keepalive_after_task_received(self, use_eventloop):\n+        scaler = autoscale.Autoscaler(self.pool, 10, 3, keepalive=30)\n+        req = Mock(name='request')\n+        with patch('celery.worker.autoscale.monotonic', return_value=100):\n+            scaler.scale_up(7)\n+        with patch('celery.worker.autoscale.monotonic', return_value=200):\n+            with patch.object(state, 'reserved_requests', [req] * 10):\n+                if use_eventloop:\n+                    scaler.maybe_scale(req)\n+                else:\n+                    scaler.on_task_received(req)\n+\n+        with patch('celery.worker.autoscale.monotonic', return_value=201):\n+            scaler.maybe_scale()\n+        assert self.pool.num_processes == 10\n+        with patch('celery.worker.autoscale.monotonic', return_value=230):\n+            scaler.maybe_scale()\n+        assert self.pool.num_processes == 10\n+        with patch('celery.worker.autoscale.monotonic', return_value=231):\n+            scaler.maybe_scale()\n+        assert self.pool.num_processes == 3\n+\n     def test_stop(self):\n \n         class Scaler(autoscale.Autoscaler):\n", "problem_statement": "Autoscale should only scale down if no tasks were accepted during keepalive period\n<!--\r\nPlease fill this template entirely and do not erase parts of it.\r\nWe reserve the right to close without a response\r\nenhancement requests which are incomplete.\r\n-->\r\n# Checklist\r\n<!--\r\nTo check an item on the list replace [ ] with [x].\r\n-->\r\n\r\n- [x] I have checked the [issues list](https://github.com/celery/celery/issues?q=is%3Aissue+label%3A%22Issue+Type%3A+Enhancement%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)\r\n  for similar or identical enhancement to an existing feature.\r\n- [x] I have checked the [pull requests list](https://github.com/celery/celery/pulls?q=is%3Apr+label%3A%22Issue+Type%3A+Enhancement%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)\r\n  for existing proposed enhancements.\r\n- [x] I have checked the [commit log](https://github.com/celery/celery/commits/main)\r\n  to find out if the same enhancement was already implemented in the\r\n  main branch.\r\n- [x] I have included all related issues and possible duplicate issues in this issue\r\n      (If there are none, check this box anyway).\r\n\r\n## Related Issues and Possible Duplicates\r\n<!--\r\nPlease make sure to search and mention any related issues\r\nor possible duplicates to this issue as requested by the checklist above.\r\n\r\nThis may or may not include issues in other repositories that the Celery project\r\nmaintains or other repositories that are dependencies of Celery.\r\n\r\nIf you don't know how to mention issues, please refer to Github's documentation\r\non the subject: https://help.github.com/en/articles/autolinked-references-and-urls#issues-and-pull-requests\r\n-->\r\n\r\n#### Related Issues\r\n\r\n- None\r\n\r\n#### Possible Duplicates\r\n\r\n- None\r\n\r\n# Brief Summary\r\nCurrently it scales down if there was no scale up in the keepalive period. However, I propose it should not scale down if any tasks has been received in the keepalive period.\r\n\r\nI'll illustrate this with an example. Let's say we've just finished processing 100 tasks (so there's a relatively high load for an extended period of time). Let's say the last scaling up happened an hour ago.\r\n\r\nCurrently there's a `maybe_scale` periodic check set up to run once every `keepalive` seconds so it happens sometimes that it runs just after a task finished running. So let's say `maybe_scale` runs after our 100th task just finished. It sees that last scale up happened an hour ago and there are no tasks queued up right now (which it may just be a couple of seconds without tasks) and it scales down immediately.\r\n\r\nNow a second later another burst of tasks comes in and we have to scale up again. So basically we just had a few seconds without any tasks in a relatively high load period and it already scaled down, so now we have to initialize the worker again.\r\n\r\nIt should not have scaled down just because of a second or two of inactivity. Rather, it should wait for at least `keepalive` seconds of inactivity before scaling down.\r\n\r\n# Design\r\n\r\n## Architectural Considerations\r\n<!--\r\nIf more components other than Celery are involved,\r\ndescribe them here and the effect it would have on Celery.\r\n-->\r\nNone\r\n\r\n## Proposed Behavior\r\n`Autoscaler.scale_down` should also check if `monotonic() - self._last_task_received > self.keepalive`. There's no `self._last_task_received` at the moment, but we can register another `on_task_message` which memorizes the latest task message time with `self._last_task_received = monotonic()`.\r\n\r\nIf this change makes sense, I can produce a PR.\r\n\r\n## Proposed UI/UX\r\n<!--\r\nPlease provide your ideas for the API, CLI options,\r\nconfiguration key names etc. that will be adjusted for this enhancement.\r\n-->\r\n\r\n## Diagrams\r\n<!--\r\nPlease include any diagrams that might be relevant\r\nto the implementation of this enhancement such as:\r\n* Class Diagrams\r\n* Sequence Diagrams\r\n* Activity Diagrams\r\nYou can drag and drop images into the text box to attach them to this issue.\r\n-->\r\nN/A\r\n\r\n## Alternatives\r\n<!--\r\nIf you have considered any alternative implementations\r\ndescribe them in detail below.\r\n-->\r\nNone\r\n\n", "hints_text": "This makes sense. Your contribution will be welcome.", "created_at": "2026-10-11T06:45:24Z"}
{"repo": "celery/celery", "pull_number": 10838, "instance_id": "celery__celery-10838", "issue_numbers": ["6430"], "base_commit": "155a8f720b01ebd7999b34e525c121b8f824ac73", "patch": "diff --git a/celery/backends/base.py b/celery/backends/base.py\nindex 19837ca74a9..fd9843f5a6b 100644\n--- a/celery/backends/base.py\n+++ b/celery/backends/base.py\n@@ -339,6 +339,20 @@ def _fail_chain(self, chain, exc, traceback=None,\n                 if chord_body is not None:\n                     chain_elems.append(chord_body)\n \n+    def _errback_arity_greater(self, errback, n):\n+        header = errback.type.__header__\n+        if not errback.immutable and (errback.args or errback.kwargs):\n+            try:\n+                # Only count remaining arguments; task_id precedes stored args\n+                # at invocation, although partial binds them from the left.\n+                return arity_greater(\n+                    partial(header, *errback.args, **errback.kwargs), n,\n+                )\n+            except (TypeError, ValueError):\n+                # Keep invalid calls isolated by the original classification.\n+                return arity_greater(header, n)\n+        return arity_greater(header, n)\n+\n     def _call_task_errbacks(self, request, exc, traceback):\n         old_signature = []\n         for errback in request.errbacks:\n@@ -358,7 +372,9 @@ def _call_task_errbacks(self, request, exc, traceback):\n                         # workaround to support tasks with bind=True executed as\n                         # link errors. Otherwise, retries can't be used\n                         not isinstance(errback.type.__header__, partial) and\n-                        arity_greater(errback.type.__header__, 1)\n+                        # Classify the arguments still supplied by the failure,\n+                        # not arguments already stored on a mutable signature.\n+                        self._errback_arity_greater(errback, 1)\n                 ):\n                     try:\n                         errback(request, exc, traceback)\ndiff --git a/t/integration/tasks.py b/t/integration/tasks.py\nindex bd70116cb7a..ac6263c30b3 100644\n--- a/t/integration/tasks.py\n+++ b/t/integration/tasks.py\n@@ -506,6 +506,16 @@ def errback_new_style(request, exc, tb):\n     return request.id\n \n \n+@shared_task\n+def errback_old_style_with_args(request_id, label):\n+    redis_echo(label, redis_key=request_id)\n+\n+\n+@shared_task\n+def errback_new_style_with_args(request, exc, tb, label):\n+    redis_echo(label, redis_key=request.id)\n+\n+\n @shared_task\n def replaced_with_me():\n     return True\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/t/integration/test_canvas.py b/t/integration/test_canvas.py\nindex e97c03683fb..62cae8364a0 100644\n--- a/t/integration/test_canvas.py\n+++ b/t/integration/test_canvas.py\n@@ -118,6 +118,22 @@ def compare_group_ids_in_redis(redis_key='redis-group-ids'):\n \n \n class test_link_error:\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize('make_errback', [\n+        lambda: tasks.errback_old_style_with_args.s('stored'),\n+        lambda: tasks.errback_old_style_with_args.s(label='stored'),\n+        lambda: tasks.errback_new_style_with_args.s('stored'),\n+    ], ids=['old-style-args', 'old-style-kwargs', 'new-style-args'])\n+    def test_mutable_errback_with_stored_arguments(self, make_errback, manager):\n+        if not manager.app.conf.result_backend.startswith('redis'):\n+            raise pytest.skip('Requires redis result backend.')\n+        fail_sig = fail.s()\n+        fail_sig_id = fail_sig.freeze().id\n+        get_redis_connection().delete(fail_sig_id)\n+        fail_sig.link_error(make_errback())\n+        with pytest.raises(ExpectedException):\n+            fail_sig.delay().get(timeout=TIMEOUT)\n+        await_redis_echo({'stored'}, redis_key=fail_sig_id)\n+\n     @flaky\n     def test_link_error_eager(self):\n         exception = ExpectedException(\"Task expected to fail\", \"test\")\ndiff --git a/t/unit/backends/test_base.py b/t/unit/backends/test_base.py\nindex 4d2b1f37ef3..a752ff0384a 100644\n--- a/t/unit/backends/test_base.py\n+++ b/t/unit/backends/test_base.py\n@@ -656,6 +656,72 @@ def test_mark_as_failure__errback(self):\n         b.mark_as_failure('id', exc, request=request)\n         assert self.errback.last_result == 5\n \n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize('use_kwargs', [False, True])\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize('eager', [False, True])\n+    def test_old_style_errback_with_partial_arguments(self, use_kwargs, eager):\n+        calls = []\n+\n+        @self.app.task(shared=False)\n+        def old_errback(task_id, foo, bar, baz):\n+            calls.append((task_id, foo, bar, baz))\n+\n+        self.app.conf.task_always_eager = eager\n+        errback = (old_errback.s(foo=1, bar=2, baz=3) if use_kwargs\n+                   else old_errback.s(1, 2, 3))\n+        request = Context(id='failed-id', root_id='root-id',\n+                          errbacks=[errback], delivery_info={})\n+        if eager:\n+            self.b._call_task_errbacks(request, ValueError(), None)\n+            assert calls == [('failed-id', 1, 2, 3)]\n+        else:\n+            with patch('celery.backends.base.group') as mock_group:\n+                self.b._call_task_errbacks(request, ValueError(), None)\n+            mock_group.assert_called_once_with([errback], app=self.app)\n+            mock_group.return_value.apply_async.assert_called_once_with(\n+                ('failed-id',), parent_id='failed-id', root_id='root-id')\n+            assert calls == []\n+\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize('args,kwargs', [\n+        ((1, 2, 3), {}),\n+        ((), {'nope': 1}),\n+        ((1,), {'task_id': 2}),\n+    ])\n+    def test_invalid_partial_errback_does_not_halt_other_errbacks(self, args, kwargs):\n+        calls = []\n+\n+        @self.app.task(shared=False)\n+        def bad_errback(task_id, foo):\n+            raise AssertionError('invalid errback must not execute')\n+\n+        @self.app.task(shared=False)\n+        def other_errback(task_id):\n+            calls.append(task_id)\n+\n+        self.b._store_result = Mock()\n+        bad = bad_errback.s(*args, **kwargs)\n+        other = other_errback.s()\n+        request = Context(id='failed-id', root_id='root-id',\n+                          errbacks=[bad, other], delivery_info={})\n+        with patch('celery.backends.base.group') as mock_group:\n+            self.b.mark_as_failure('failed-id', ValueError(), request=request)\n+        # The original header is new-style: a bad call is logged in isolation.\n+        mock_group.assert_called_once_with([other], app=self.app)\n+        mock_group.return_value.apply_async.assert_called_once_with(\n+            ('failed-id',), parent_id='failed-id', root_id='root-id')\n+\n+    def test_new_style_errback_with_partial_arguments(self):\n+        calls = []\n+\n+        @self.app.task(shared=False)\n+        def new_errback(request, exc, traceback, label):\n+            calls.append((request, exc, traceback, label))\n+\n+        request = Context(id='failed-id', errbacks=[new_errback.s('label')],\n+                          delivery_info={})\n+        exc = ValueError('failed')\n+        self.b._call_task_errbacks(request, exc, 'traceback')\n+        assert calls == [(request, exc, 'traceback', 'label')]\n+\n     @patch('celery.backends.base.group')\n     def test_class_based_task_can_be_used_as_error_callback(self, mock_group):\n         b = BaseBackend(app=self.app)\n", "problem_statement": "errback arity check does not account for args in the errback signature\n# Checklist\n* [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `master` branch of Celery.\n* [ ] This has already been asked to the [discussion group](https://groups.google.com/forum/#!forum/celery-users) first.\n* [x] I have read the relevant section in the\n  [contribution guide](http://docs.celeryproject.org/en/latest/contributing.html#other-bugs)\n  on reporting bugs.\n* [x] I have checked the [issues list](https://github.com/celery/celery/issues?q=is%3Aissue+label%3A%22Issue+Type%3A+Bug+Report%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)\n  for similar or identical bug reports.\n* [x] I have checked the [pull requests list](https://github.com/celery/celery/pulls?q=is%3Apr+label%3A%22PR+Type%3A+Bugfix%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)\n  for existing proposed fixes.\n* [x] I have checked the [commit log](https://github.com/celery/celery/commits/master)\n  to find out if the bug was already fixed in the master branch.\n* [x] I have included all related issues and possible duplicate issues\n  in this issue (If there are none, check this box anyway).\n\n## Mandatory Debugging Information\n* [x] I have included the output of ``celery -A proj report`` in the issue.\n    (if you are not able to do this, then at least specify the Celery\n     version affected).1\n* [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `master` branch of Celery.\n* [ ] I have included the contents of ``pip freeze`` in the issue.\n* [ ] I have included all the versions of all the external dependencies required\n  to reproduce this bug.\n\n## Optional Debugging Information\n<!--\nTry some of the below if you think they are relevant.\nIt will help us figure out the scope of the bug and how many users it affects.\n-->\n\n\n* [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one Python version\n  and/or implementation.\n* [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one message broker and/or\n  result backend.\n* [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one version of the message\n  broker and/or result backend.\n* [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one operating system.\n* [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one workers pool.\n* [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue with autoscaling, retries,\n  ETA/Countdown & rate limits disabled.\n* [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue after downgrading\n  and/or upgrading Celery and its dependencies.\n\n## Related Issues and Possible Duplicates\n<!--\nPlease make sure to search and mention any related issues\nor possible duplicates to this issue as requested by the checklist above.\n\nThis may or may not include issues in other repositories that the Celery project\nmaintains or other repositories that are dependencies of Celery.\n\nIf you don't know how to mention issues, please refer to Github's documentation\non the subject: https://help.github.com/en/articles/autolinked-references-and-urls#issues-and-pull-requests\n-->\n\n#### Related Issues\n* None\n\n#### Possible Duplicates\n* None\n\n## Environment & Settings\n<!-- Include the contents of celery --version below -->\n**Celery version**: 5.0.1\n<!-- Include the output of celery -A proj report below -->\n<details>\n<summary><b><code>celery report</code> Output:</b></summary>\n<p>\n\n```\n\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n# Steps to Reproduce\n## Required Dependencies\n<!-- Please fill the required dependencies to reproduce this issue -->\n\n\n* **Minimal Python Version**: N/A or Unknown\n* **Minimal Celery Version**: N/A or Unknown\n* **Minimal Kombu Version**: N/A or Unknown\n* **Minimal Broker Version**: N/A or Unknown\n* **Minimal Result Backend Version**: N/A or Unknown\n* **Minimal OS and/or Kernel Version**: N/A or Unknown\n* **Minimal Broker Client Version**: N/A or Unknown\n* **Minimal Result Backend Client Version**: N/A or Unknown\n\n### Python Packages\n<!-- Please fill the contents of pip freeze below -->\n<details>\n<summary><b><code>pip freeze</code> Output:</b></summary>\n<p>\n\n```\n\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n### Other Dependencies\n<!--\nPlease provide system dependencies, configuration files\nand other dependency information if applicable\n-->\n<details>\n<p>\nN/A\n</p>\n</details>\n\n## Minimally Reproducible Test Case\n<!--\nPlease provide a reproducible test case.\nRefer to the Reporting Bugs section in our contribution guide.\n\nWe prefer submitting test cases in the form of a PR to our integration test suite.\nIf you can provide one, please mention the PR number below.\nIf not, please attach the most minimal code example required to reproduce the issue below.\nIf the test case is too large, please include a link to a gist or a repository below.\n-->\n\n<details>\n<p>\n\n```python\nimport celery\n\napp = celery.Celery(broker=\"redis://\", backend=\"redis://\")\n\n@app.task\ndef handle_error(parent_uuid, foo, bar, baz):\n    print(\"Called errback\", parent_uuid, foo, bar, baz)\n\n@app.task\ndef fail():\n    raise Exception\n\ndef run():\n    fail.apply_async(link_error=handle_error.s(1, 2, 3))\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n# Expected Behavior\nerrback should be called with the 3 args provided in the signature and old style errback calling rules should apply (ie, get sparent task UUID).\n\n# Actual Behavior\nNew style errback calling (ie. request, exc, traceback) is applied to the errback resulting in a TypeError:\n\n\n```\n\n  File \"celery/celery/app/trace.py\", line 362, in on_error\n    R = I.handle_error_state(\n  File \"celery/celery/app/trace.py\", line 165, in handle_error_state\n    return {\n  File \"celery/celery/app/trace.py\", line 212, in handle_failure\n    task.backend.mark_as_failure(\n  File \"celery/celery/backends/base.py\", line 170, in mark_as_failure\n    self._call_task_errbacks(request, exc, traceback)\n  File \"celery/celery/backends/base.py\", line 194, in _call_task_errbacks\n    errback(request, exc, traceback)\n  File \"celery/celery/canvas.py\", line 174, in __call__\n    return self.type(*args, **kwargs)\n  File \"celery/celery/app/trace.py\", line 702, in __protected_call__\n    return orig(self, *args, **kwargs)\n  File \"celery/celery/app/task.py\", line 388, in __call__\n    return self.run(*args, **kwargs)\nTypeError: handle_error() takes 4 positional arguments but 6 were given\n```\n\n\n", "hints_text": "This is a naive fix:\r\n```diff\r\ndiff --git a/celery/backends/base.py b/celery/backends/base.py\r\nindex 74fce23c3..d4afe9320 100644\r\n--- a/celery/backends/base.py\r\n+++ b/celery/backends/base.py\r\n@@ -188,7 +188,9 @@ class Backend:\r\n                         # workaround to support tasks with bind=True executed as\r\n                         # link errors. Otherwise retries can't be used\r\n                         not isinstance(errback.type.__header__, partial) and\r\n-                        arity_greater(errback.type.__header__, 1)\r\n+                        arity_greater(\r\n+                            errback.type.__header__, len(errback.args) + 1\r\n+                        )\r\n                 ):\r\n                     errback(request, exc, traceback)\r\n                 else:\r\n ```\r\n\r\nbut I suspect the logic may need to be a bit more complicated to catch edge cases. The arity check already handles situations where the errback takes varargs, so I think it's fine to assume new-style calling in that case.\nAlso worth noting that the new-style errback calling convention doesn't appear to be stated in the doco (AFAICT). Documenting it and removing the problematic synchronous get() in an old-style errback which is currently shown as an example should allow us to close #4099", "created_at": "2026-10-11T03:53:48Z"}
{"repo": "celery/celery", "pull_number": 10837, "instance_id": "celery__celery-10837", "issue_numbers": ["7894"], "base_commit": "2fa4aa287865e0d7c79a783d82050a3f6744f589", "patch": "diff --git a/celery/bin/base.py b/celery/bin/base.py\nindex be3999236bc..4250de14d80 100644\n--- a/celery/bin/base.py\n+++ b/celery/bin/base.py\n@@ -1,4 +1,5 @@\n \"\"\"Click customizations for Celery.\"\"\"\n+import inspect\n import json\n import numbers\n from collections import OrderedDict\n@@ -140,24 +141,91 @@ def handle_remote_command_error(command: str, exc: Exception) -> None:\n     ) from exc\n \n \n+def _preload_option_names(app):\n+    return [option.name for option in app.user_options.get('preload', ())]\n+\n+\n+def _callback_accepts_keyword(fun, name):\n+    \"\"\"Whether ``fun`` can take ``name`` without raising ``TypeError``.\"\"\"\n+    try:\n+        parameters = inspect.signature(fun).parameters.values()\n+    except (TypeError, ValueError):\n+        return False\n+    for parameter in parameters:\n+        if parameter.kind is inspect.Parameter.VAR_KEYWORD:\n+            return True\n+        if parameter.kind is inspect.Parameter.POSITIONAL_ONLY:\n+            continue\n+        if parameter.name == name:\n+            return True\n+    return False\n+\n+\n+def _take_preload_options(app, kwargs, fun):\n+    \"\"\"Copy preload values and drop only those ``fun`` cannot accept.\"\"\"\n+    taken = {}\n+    for name in _preload_option_names(app):\n+        if name not in kwargs:\n+            continue\n+        taken[name] = kwargs[name]\n+        if not _callback_accepts_keyword(fun, name):\n+            kwargs.pop(name)\n+    return taken\n+\n+\n+def _handles_preload_options(fun):\n+    seen = set()\n+    while fun is not None and id(fun) not in seen:\n+        seen.add(id(fun))\n+        if getattr(fun, '_handles_preload_options', False):\n+            return True\n+        fun = getattr(fun, '__wrapped__', None)\n+    return False\n+\n+\n+def _mark_preload_handler(wrapper, wrapped):\n+    wrapper = update_wrapper(wrapper, wrapped)\n+    wrapper._handles_preload_options = True\n+    return wrapper\n+\n+\n def handle_preload_options(f):\n     \"\"\"Extract preload options and return a wrapped callable.\"\"\"\n+    if _handles_preload_options(f):\n+        return f\n+\n     def caller(ctx, *args, **kwargs):\n         app = ctx.obj.app\n+        options = _take_preload_options(app, kwargs, f)\n+        if options:\n+            user_preload_options.send(sender=f, app=app, options=options)\n+        return f(ctx, *args, **kwargs)\n \n-        preload_options = [o.name for o in app.user_options.get('preload', [])]\n+    return _mark_preload_handler(caller, f)\n \n-        if preload_options:\n-            user_options = {\n-                preload_option: kwargs[preload_option]\n-                for preload_option in preload_options\n-            }\n \n-            user_preload_options.send(sender=f, app=app, options=user_options)\n+def consume_preload_options(fun):\n+    \"\"\"Deliver preload options and drop only values the callback cannot accept.\n \n-        return f(ctx, *args, **kwargs)\n+    Preload options are added to every sub-command, including commands\n+    installed through the ``celery.commands`` entry-point group. Click\n+    forwards them as keyword arguments. A callback with no ``**kwargs``\n+    and no matching parameter then raises ``TypeError``. Those values are\n+    delivered through ``user_preload_options`` instead. A callback that can\n+    accept the name, including ``worker``, ``beat``, and ``events``, keeps it.\n+    \"\"\"\n+    if fun is None or _handles_preload_options(fun):\n+        return fun\n+\n+    def caller(*args, **kwargs):\n+        ctx = click.get_current_context()\n+        app = ctx.obj.app\n+        options = _take_preload_options(app, kwargs, fun)\n+        if options:\n+            user_preload_options.send(sender=fun, app=app, options=options)\n+        return fun(*args, **kwargs)\n \n-    return update_wrapper(caller, f)\n+    return _mark_preload_handler(caller, fun)\n \n \n class CeleryOption(click.Option):\ndiff --git a/celery/bin/celery.py b/celery/bin/celery.py\nindex e1fae1a7761..77033d8a394 100644\n--- a/celery/bin/celery.py\n+++ b/celery/bin/celery.py\n@@ -13,7 +13,7 @@\n from celery import VERSION_BANNER\n from celery.app.utils import find_app\n from celery.bin.amqp import amqp\n-from celery.bin.base import CeleryCommand, CeleryOption, CLIContext\n+from celery.bin.base import CeleryCommand, CeleryOption, CLIContext, consume_preload_options\n from celery.bin.beat import beat\n from celery.bin.call import call\n from celery.bin.control import control, inspect, status\n@@ -159,8 +159,13 @@ def celery(ctx, app, broker, result_backend, loader, config, workdir,\n     beat.params.extend(ctx.obj.app.user_options.get('beat', []))\n     events.params.extend(ctx.obj.app.user_options.get('events', []))\n \n+    # Plugin callbacks such as Flower do not declare these options. Take\n+    # them off the keyword arguments before Click calls the callback (#7894).\n+    preload_options = ctx.obj.app.user_options.get('preload', ())\n     for command in celery.commands.values():\n-        command.params.extend(ctx.obj.app.user_options.get('preload', []))\n+        command.params.extend(preload_options)\n+        if preload_options:\n+            command.callback = consume_preload_options(command.callback)\n \n \n @celery.command(cls=CeleryCommand)\ndiff --git a/docs/userguide/extending.rst b/docs/userguide/extending.rst\nindex beb1caec88f..5061622cfa2 100644\n--- a/docs/userguide/extending.rst\n+++ b/docs/userguide/extending.rst\n@@ -782,6 +782,16 @@ Preload options\n The :program:`celery` umbrella command supports the concept of 'preload\n options'.  These are special options passed to all sub-commands.\n \n+.. versionchanged:: 5.7\n+\n+    Preload options now work with commands installed through the\n+    ``celery.commands`` entry point (e.g. Flower). A callback that cannot\n+    accept an option receives it only through ``user_preload_options``.\n+    The signal is now sent for every sub-command. Built-in commands that\n+    never used ``handle_preload_options``, such as :program:`report`, send\n+    ``user_preload_options`` too, because ``consume_preload_options`` wraps\n+    every command. That is the intended behavior.\n+\n You can add new preload options, for example to specify a configuration\n template:\n \n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/t/unit/app/test_preload_cli.py b/t/unit/app/test_preload_cli.py\nindex 1b83073ec74..adf9be238d9 100644\n--- a/t/unit/app/test_preload_cli.py\n+++ b/t/unit/app/test_preload_cli.py\n@@ -1,11 +1,15 @@\n import contextlib\n+from types import SimpleNamespace\n from typing import Tuple\n from unittest.mock import patch\n \n+import click\n import pytest\n from click.testing import CliRunner\n \n+from celery.bin.base import handle_preload_options\n from celery.bin.celery import celery\n+from celery.signals import user_preload_options\n \n \n @pytest.fixture(autouse=True)\n@@ -55,3 +59,399 @@ def test_preload_options(subcommand_with_params: Tuple[str, ...], isolated_cli_r\n     )\n \n     assert res_with_preload.exit_code == 0, res_with_preload.output\n+\n+\n+@contextlib.contextmanager\n+def _temporary_command(command):\n+    \"\"\"Register a Click command for one CLI invoke, then put the group back.\"\"\"\n+    original_callbacks = {\n+        name: existing.callback for name, existing in celery.commands.items()\n+    }\n+    celery.add_command(command)\n+    try:\n+        yield\n+    finally:\n+        for name, callback in original_callbacks.items():\n+            existing = celery.commands.get(name)\n+            if existing is not None:\n+                existing.callback = callback\n+        celery.commands.pop(command.name, None)\n+\n+\n+def _on_preload(received):\n+    def _receive(sender=None, app=None, options=None, **kwargs):\n+        received.append(dict(options))\n+\n+    return _receive\n+\n+\n+def test_plugin_command_accepts_preload_options():\n+    \"\"\"Preload options reach plugin commands that have no ``**kwargs`` (#7894).\n+\n+    ``celery.commands`` entry points such as Flower are not defined here.\n+    Celery still adds every preload option to those commands, and Click then\n+    passes the values into the callback. The documented receiver is\n+    ``user_preload_options``.\n+    \"\"\"\n+    entered = []\n+    received = []\n+\n+    @click.command(\"plugin-without-kwargs\")\n+    @click.pass_context\n+    def plugin_without_kwargs(ctx):\n+        entered.append(True)\n+\n+    receiver = _on_preload(received)\n+    user_preload_options.connect(receiver)\n+    try:\n+        with _temporary_command(plugin_without_kwargs):\n+            result = CliRunner().invoke(\n+                celery,\n+                [\n+                    \"-A\",\n+                    \"t.unit.bin.proj.pyramid_celery_app\",\n+                    \"plugin-without-kwargs\",\n+                    \"--ini\",\n+                    \"some_ini.ini\",\n+                ],\n+                catch_exceptions=False,\n+            )\n+    finally:\n+        user_preload_options.disconnect(receiver)\n+\n+    assert result.exit_code == 0, result.output\n+    assert entered == [True]\n+    assert len(received) == 1\n+    assert received[0][\"ini\"] == \"some_ini.ini\"\n+\n+\n+def test_decorated_command_accepts_preload_options_without_var_keyword():\n+    \"\"\"In-tree commands using ``handle_preload_options`` must not require ``**kwargs``.\"\"\"\n+    entered = []\n+    received = []\n+\n+    @click.command(\"decorated-without-kwargs\")\n+    @click.pass_context\n+    @handle_preload_options\n+    def decorated_without_kwargs(ctx):\n+        entered.append(True)\n+\n+    receiver = _on_preload(received)\n+    user_preload_options.connect(receiver)\n+    try:\n+        with _temporary_command(decorated_without_kwargs):\n+            result = CliRunner().invoke(\n+                celery,\n+                [\n+                    \"-A\",\n+                    \"t.unit.bin.proj.pyramid_celery_app\",\n+                    \"decorated-without-kwargs\",\n+                    \"--ini\",\n+                    \"some_ini.ini\",\n+                ],\n+                catch_exceptions=False,\n+            )\n+    finally:\n+        user_preload_options.disconnect(receiver)\n+\n+    assert result.exit_code == 0, result.output\n+    assert entered == [True]\n+    assert len(received) == 1\n+    assert received[0][\"ini\"] == \"some_ini.ini\"\n+\n+\n+def test_plugin_command_without_preload_options_still_runs():\n+    entered = []\n+\n+    @click.command(\"plugin-without-kwargs\")\n+    @click.pass_context\n+    def plugin_without_kwargs(ctx):\n+        entered.append(True)\n+\n+    with _temporary_command(plugin_without_kwargs):\n+        result = CliRunner().invoke(\n+            celery,\n+            [\"-A\", \"t.unit.bin.proj.app\", \"plugin-without-kwargs\"],\n+            catch_exceptions=False,\n+        )\n+\n+    assert result.exit_code == 0, result.output\n+    assert entered == [True]\n+\n+\n+def test_command_option_survives_preload_option():\n+    \"\"\"Pop only preload names; leave the command's own options in place.\"\"\"\n+    entered = []\n+    received = []\n+\n+    @click.command(\"decorated-with-own-option\")\n+    @click.option(\"--force\", is_flag=True)\n+    @click.pass_context\n+    @handle_preload_options\n+    def decorated_with_own_option(ctx, force):\n+        entered.append(force)\n+\n+    receiver = _on_preload(received)\n+    user_preload_options.connect(receiver)\n+    try:\n+        with _temporary_command(decorated_with_own_option):\n+            result = CliRunner().invoke(\n+                celery,\n+                [\n+                    \"-A\",\n+                    \"t.unit.bin.proj.pyramid_celery_app\",\n+                    \"decorated-with-own-option\",\n+                    \"--force\",\n+                    \"--ini\",\n+                    \"some_ini.ini\",\n+                ],\n+                catch_exceptions=False,\n+            )\n+    finally:\n+        user_preload_options.disconnect(receiver)\n+\n+    assert result.exit_code == 0, result.output\n+    assert entered == [True]\n+    assert len(received) == 1\n+    assert received[0][\"ini\"] == \"some_ini.ini\"\n+\n+\n+def test_preload_wrapper_is_applied_once():\n+    \"\"\"A second CLI invoke must not deliver the same preload options twice.\"\"\"\n+    received = []\n+\n+    @click.command(\"plugin-without-kwargs\")\n+    @click.pass_context\n+    def plugin_without_kwargs(ctx):\n+        return None\n+\n+    receiver = _on_preload(received)\n+    user_preload_options.connect(receiver)\n+    try:\n+        with _temporary_command(plugin_without_kwargs):\n+            original_params = {\n+                name: list(command.params)\n+                for name, command in celery.commands.items()\n+            }\n+            args = [\n+                \"-A\",\n+                \"t.unit.bin.proj.pyramid_celery_app\",\n+                \"plugin-without-kwargs\",\n+                \"--ini\",\n+                \"some_ini.ini\",\n+            ]\n+            first = CliRunner().invoke(celery, args, catch_exceptions=False)\n+            for name, params in original_params.items():\n+                celery.commands[name].params[:] = params\n+            second = CliRunner().invoke(celery, args, catch_exceptions=False)\n+            depth = _preload_wrapper_depth(\n+                celery.commands[\"plugin-without-kwargs\"].callback\n+            )\n+    finally:\n+        user_preload_options.disconnect(receiver)\n+\n+    assert first.exit_code == 0, first.output\n+    assert second.exit_code == 0, second.output\n+    assert len(received) == 2\n+    assert depth == 1\n+\n+\n+def test_handle_preload_options_ignores_missing_values():\n+    \"\"\"A direct call that did not receive a preload kwarg must not KeyError.\"\"\"\n+    from t.unit.bin.proj.pyramid_celery_app import app as pyramid_app\n+\n+    called = []\n+    received = []\n+\n+    @handle_preload_options\n+    def command(ctx):\n+        called.append(True)\n+\n+    ctx = SimpleNamespace(obj=SimpleNamespace(app=pyramid_app))\n+    receiver = _on_preload(received)\n+    user_preload_options.connect(receiver)\n+    try:\n+        command(ctx)\n+    finally:\n+        user_preload_options.disconnect(receiver)\n+\n+    assert called == [True]\n+    assert received == []\n+\n+\n+def _preload_wrapper_depth(fun):\n+    depth = 0\n+    seen = set()\n+    while fun is not None and id(fun) not in seen:\n+        seen.add(id(fun))\n+        if getattr(fun, \"_handles_preload_options\", False):\n+            depth += 1\n+        fun = getattr(fun, \"__wrapped__\", None)\n+    return depth\n+\n+\n+def test_var_keyword_callback_keeps_preload_options_it_can_accept():\n+    \"\"\"A callback with ``**kwargs`` still receives the preload values.\n+\n+    ``worker``, ``beat``, and ``events`` take ``**kwargs``. Dropping every\n+    preload name removed values those commands used to see. Only a callback\n+    that cannot accept the name loses it.\n+    \"\"\"\n+    seen = {}\n+    received = []\n+\n+    @click.command(\"plugin-with-kwargs\")\n+    @click.pass_context\n+    def plugin_with_kwargs(ctx, **kwargs):\n+        seen.update(kwargs)\n+\n+    receiver = _on_preload(received)\n+    user_preload_options.connect(receiver)\n+    try:\n+        with _temporary_command(plugin_with_kwargs):\n+            result = CliRunner().invoke(\n+                celery,\n+                [\n+                    \"-A\",\n+                    \"t.unit.bin.proj.pyramid_celery_app\",\n+                    \"plugin-with-kwargs\",\n+                    \"--ini\",\n+                    \"some_ini.ini\",\n+                    \"--ini-var\",\n+                    \"a=b\",\n+                ],\n+                catch_exceptions=False,\n+            )\n+    finally:\n+        user_preload_options.disconnect(receiver)\n+\n+    assert result.exit_code == 0, result.output\n+    assert seen[\"ini\"] == \"some_ini.ini\"\n+    assert seen[\"ini_var\"] == \"a=b\"\n+    assert len(received) == 1\n+    assert received[0][\"ini\"] == \"some_ini.ini\"\n+    assert received[0][\"ini_var\"] == \"a=b\"\n+\n+\n+def test_explicit_parameter_is_kept_and_unknown_preload_is_dropped():\n+    seen = {}\n+    received = []\n+\n+    @click.command(\"plugin-with-one-preload-name\")\n+    @click.pass_context\n+    def plugin_with_one_preload_name(ctx, ini):\n+        seen[\"ini\"] = ini\n+\n+    receiver = _on_preload(received)\n+    user_preload_options.connect(receiver)\n+    try:\n+        with _temporary_command(plugin_with_one_preload_name):\n+            result = CliRunner().invoke(\n+                celery,\n+                [\n+                    \"-A\",\n+                    \"t.unit.bin.proj.pyramid_celery_app\",\n+                    \"plugin-with-one-preload-name\",\n+                    \"--ini\",\n+                    \"some_ini.ini\",\n+                    \"--ini-var\",\n+                    \"a=b\",\n+                ],\n+                catch_exceptions=False,\n+            )\n+    finally:\n+        user_preload_options.disconnect(receiver)\n+\n+    assert result.exit_code == 0, result.output\n+    assert seen[\"ini\"] == \"some_ini.ini\"\n+    assert len(received) == 1\n+    assert received[0][\"ini\"] == \"some_ini.ini\"\n+    assert received[0][\"ini_var\"] == \"a=b\"\n+\n+\n+def test_report_accepts_preload_option():\n+    result = CliRunner().invoke(\n+        celery,\n+        [\n+            \"-A\",\n+            \"t.unit.bin.proj.pyramid_celery_app\",\n+            \"report\",\n+            \"--ini\",\n+            \"some_ini.ini\",\n+        ],\n+        catch_exceptions=False,\n+    )\n+\n+    assert result.exit_code == 0, result.output\n+\n+\n+def test_handle_preload_options_applied_twice_sends_once():\n+    from t.unit.bin.proj.pyramid_celery_app import app as pyramid_app\n+\n+    called = []\n+    received = []\n+\n+    @handle_preload_options\n+    @handle_preload_options\n+    def command(ctx):\n+        called.append(True)\n+\n+    ctx = SimpleNamespace(obj=SimpleNamespace(app=pyramid_app))\n+    receiver = _on_preload(received)\n+    user_preload_options.connect(receiver)\n+    try:\n+        command(ctx, ini=\"some_ini.ini\", ini_var=None)\n+    finally:\n+        user_preload_options.disconnect(receiver)\n+\n+    assert called == [True]\n+    assert len(received) == 1\n+    assert received[0][\"ini\"] == \"some_ini.ini\"\n+    assert _preload_wrapper_depth(command) == 1\n+\n+\n+def test_preload_defaults_flags_and_empty_values_reach_the_signal():\n+    from t.unit.bin.proj.pyramid_celery_app import app as pyramid_app\n+\n+    extra = (\n+        click.Option((\"--template\",), default=\"default\"),\n+        click.Option((\"--debug-preload\",), is_flag=True),\n+    )\n+    for option in extra:\n+        pyramid_app.user_options[\"preload\"].add(option)\n+\n+    entered = []\n+    received = []\n+\n+    @click.command(\"plugin-without-kwargs\")\n+    @click.pass_context\n+    def plugin_without_kwargs(ctx):\n+        entered.append(True)\n+\n+    receiver = _on_preload(received)\n+    user_preload_options.connect(receiver)\n+    try:\n+        with _temporary_command(plugin_without_kwargs):\n+            result = CliRunner().invoke(\n+                celery,\n+                [\n+                    \"-A\",\n+                    \"t.unit.bin.proj.pyramid_celery_app\",\n+                    \"plugin-without-kwargs\",\n+                    \"--ini\",\n+                    \"\",\n+                    \"--debug-preload\",\n+                ],\n+                catch_exceptions=False,\n+            )\n+    finally:\n+        user_preload_options.disconnect(receiver)\n+        for option in extra:\n+            pyramid_app.user_options[\"preload\"].discard(option)\n+\n+    assert result.exit_code == 0, result.output\n+    assert entered == [True]\n+    assert len(received) == 1\n+    assert received[0][\"ini\"] == \"\"\n+    assert received[0][\"template\"] == \"default\"\n+    assert received[0][\"debug_preload\"] is True\n", "problem_statement": "Celery not configuring flower subcommand for user-defined preload CLI options\nPer the [documentation][1], users may add a command-line option that is passed to all sub-commands by adding a Click Option instance to `user_options['preload']`. \r\n\r\nWith this option, I've been able to pass an `--ini` cli option to the celery cli and configure my celery app from an ini file containing my application config.\r\n\r\nHowever, because this new option is also passed to all sub-commands, `flower` fails to start as it does not recognize the new argument. Since new user-added options appear to work for other sub-commands like worker, beat, and events, I'm wondering if this is a bug in that celery is not passing this new command option down to flower?\r\n\r\nThis works for the `worker`, `beat` and `events` subcommands:\r\n\r\n    $ celery -A example worker --ini app.ini\r\n    app.ini\r\n\r\nBut it fails for the `flower` subcommand:\r\n\r\n    $ celery -A example flower --ini app.ini\r\n\r\nThe traceback:\r\n\r\n    Traceback (most recent call last):\r\n      File \"/usr/local/bin/celery\", line 8, in <module>\r\n        sys.exit(main())\r\n      File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.8/dist-packages/celery/__main__.py\", line 15, in main\r\n        sys.exit(_main())\r\n      File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.8/dist-packages/celery/bin/celery.py\", line 217, in main\r\n        return celery(auto_envvar_prefix=\"CELERY\")\r\n      File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.8/dist-packages/click/core.py\", line 1130, in __call__\r\n        return self.main(*args, **kwargs)\r\n      File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.8/dist-packages/click/core.py\", line 1055, in main\r\n        rv = self.invoke(ctx)\r\n      File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.8/dist-packages/click/core.py\", line 1657, in invoke\r\n        return _process_result(sub_ctx.command.invoke(sub_ctx))\r\n      File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.8/dist-packages/click/core.py\", line 1404, in invoke\r\n        return ctx.invoke(self.callback, **ctx.params)\r\n      File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.8/dist-packages/click/core.py\", line 760, in invoke\r\n        return __callback(*args, **kwargs)\r\n      File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.8/dist-packages/click/decorators.py\", line 26, in new_func\r\n        return f(get_current_context(), *args, **kwargs)\r\n    TypeError: flower() got an unexpected keyword argument 'ini'\r\n\r\n\r\n**dependencies:**\r\n* celery==5.2.7\r\n* flower==1.2.0\r\n\r\n\r\n**example.py**\r\n\r\n    from celery import Celery\r\n    from celery import signals\r\n    from click import Option\r\n    \r\n    app = Celery()\r\n    \r\n    ini_option = Option(('-i', '--ini'), help='ini file path to use.')\r\n    \r\n    app.user_options['preload'].add(ini_option)\r\n    \r\n    @signals.user_preload_options.connect\r\n    def on_preload_parsed(options, **kwargs):\r\n        print(options['ini'])\r\n        exit(-1)\r\n\r\n\r\n  [1]: https://docs.celeryq.dev/en/latest/userguide/extending.html#preload-options\r\n\n", "hints_text": "Hey @joshuamcginnis :wave:,\nThank you for opening an issue. We will get back to you as soon as we can.\nAlso, check out our [Open Collective](https://opencollective.com/celery) and consider backing us - every little helps!\n\nWe also offer priority support for our sponsors.\nIf you require immediate assistance please consider sponsoring us.\n", "created_at": "2026-10-10T18:15:30Z"}
{"repo": "celery/celery", "pull_number": 10836, "instance_id": "celery__celery-10836", "issue_numbers": ["10835"], "base_commit": "77529b9c4a2a02f3188df134f8249fc7985942b6", "patch": "diff --git a/celery/backends/base.py b/celery/backends/base.py\nindex 61c066b87ce..19837ca74a9 100644\n--- a/celery/backends/base.py\n+++ b/celery/backends/base.py\n@@ -298,7 +298,11 @@ def _fail_chain(self, chain, exc, traceback=None,\n             chain_elem_ctx.update(chain_elem_ctx.options)\n             chain_elem_ctx.id = chain_elem_ctx.options.get('task_id')\n             chain_elem_ctx.group = chain_elem_ctx.options.get('group_id')\n+            # Only check state for chord members to avoid requiring a result backend\n+            # for ordinary chains. The already_failed check is only used when\n+            # calling on_chord_part_return() for chord members below.\n             already_failed = (\n+                'chord' in chain_elem_ctx.options and\n                 chain_elem_ctx.id is not None and\n                 self.get_state(chain_elem_ctx.id) in states.PROPAGATE_STATES\n             )\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/t/smoke/tests/test_disabled_backend.py b/t/smoke/tests/test_disabled_backend.py\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 00000000000..256398b60f0\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/t/smoke/tests/test_disabled_backend.py\n@@ -0,0 +1,85 @@\n+\"\"\"Smoke test for DisabledBackend chain failure handling.\n+\n+Regression test for issue where _fail_chain() called get_state() for plain chain\n+elements, causing NotImplementedError with DisabledBackend.\n+\"\"\"\n+\n+from __future__ import annotations\n+\n+import uuid\n+from time import monotonic, sleep\n+\n+import pytest\n+from pytest_celery import RESULT_TIMEOUT, CeleryTestSetup\n+\n+from celery.canvas import chain\n+from t.integration.conftest import get_redis_connection\n+from t.integration.tasks import fail, identity, redis_echo\n+\n+\n+@pytest.fixture\n+def celery_backend_cluster() -> None:\n+    \"\"\"No backend cluster needed for DisabledBackend.\"\"\"\n+    return None\n+\n+\n+@pytest.fixture\n+def celery_setup_config(celery_setup_config: dict) -> dict:\n+    \"\"\"Disable result backend for testing DisabledBackend behavior.\n+\n+    Configures the client-side app to use DisabledBackend.\n+    \"\"\"\n+    celery_setup_config[\"result_backend\"] = \"disabled://\"\n+    return celery_setup_config\n+\n+\n+@pytest.fixture\n+def default_worker_app(default_worker_app) -> object:\n+    \"\"\"Configure worker to use DisabledBackend.\n+\n+    Direct attribute assignment lands in conf.changes, which is what\n+    pytest-celery serializes into the worker container config; the\n+    celery_setup_config dict alone only configures the client side.\n+    \"\"\"\n+    default_worker_app.conf.result_backend = \"disabled://\"\n+    return default_worker_app\n+\n+\n+class test_disabled_backend:\n+    def test_chain_failure_with_disabled_backend(self, celery_setup: CeleryTestSetup):\n+        \"\"\"Regression test: plain chain failure with DisabledBackend can execute link_error callback.\n+\n+        Verifies that when a plain chain fails with DisabledBackend, the link_error\n+        callback is still called, proving _fail_chain() doesn't raise NotImplementedError.\n+        \"\"\"\n+        redis_key = str(uuid.uuid4())\n+        queue = celery_setup.worker.worker_queue\n+\n+        # Create a chain where the first (non-final) task fails\n+        sig = chain(\n+            fail.si().set(queue=queue),\n+            identity.si(\"should_not_run\").set(queue=queue),\n+        )\n+\n+        # Add a link_error callback that writes to Redis\n+        errback_msg = \"failure_handled\"\n+        sig.link_error(redis_echo.si(errback_msg, redis_key).set(queue=queue))\n+\n+        # Apply the chain (res.get() is not used because DisabledBackend raises NotImplementedError)\n+        sig.apply_async()\n+\n+        # Wait for the worker to process and the callback to execute\n+        redis_connection = get_redis_connection()\n+        deadline = monotonic() + RESULT_TIMEOUT\n+        actual = []\n+\n+        try:\n+            while monotonic() < deadline:\n+                actual = redis_connection.lrange(redis_key, 0, -1)\n+                if actual:\n+                    break\n+                sleep(0.1)\n+\n+            assert actual == [b\"failure_handled\"]\n+        finally:\n+            redis_connection.delete(redis_key)\ndiff --git a/t/unit/backends/test_base.py b/t/unit/backends/test_base.py\nindex 7c39b67c608..4d2b1f37ef3 100644\n--- a/t/unit/backends/test_base.py\n+++ b/t/unit/backends/test_base.py\n@@ -832,6 +832,33 @@ def test_fail_chain_skips_a_chord_without_a_body(self):\n \n         store_result.assert_not_called()\n \n+    def test_fail_chain_does_not_call_get_state_for_plain_chain(self):\n+        \"\"\"Regression test for issue where _fail_chain called get_state()\n+        for ordinary chain elements, causing NotImplementedError with DisabledBackend.\"\"\"\n+        # Plain chain element without chord option\n+        after = signature('after', options={'task_id': 'after-id'})\n+\n+        with patch.object(self.b, 'store_result'), \\\n+                patch.object(self.b, 'get_state') as get_state:\n+            self.b._fail_chain([after], ValueError('boom failed'))\n+\n+        # get_state should not be called for plain chain elements\n+        get_state.assert_not_called()\n+\n+    def test_fail_chain_calls_get_state_for_chord_member(self):\n+        \"\"\"Ensure chord members still perform state lookup for duplicate failure protection.\"\"\"\n+        # Chord member has 'chord' in options\n+        options = {'task_id': 'member-id', 'chord': signature('outer'), 'group_id': 'gid'}\n+        member = signature('member', options=options)\n+\n+        with patch.object(self.b, 'store_result'), \\\n+                patch.object(self.b, 'get_state', return_value=states.PENDING) as get_state, \\\n+                patch.object(self.b, 'on_chord_part_return'):\n+            self.b._fail_chain([member], ValueError('header failed'))\n+\n+        # get_state should be called for chord members\n+        get_state.assert_called_once_with('member-id')\n+\n     def test_chord_error_from_stack_resolves_the_current_exception_for_the_chain(self):\n         after = signature('after', options={'task_id': 'after-id'})\n         callback = signature('body', options={'task_id': 'body-id', 'chain': [after]})\n@@ -1988,6 +2015,26 @@ def test_chain_with_chord_raises_error(self):\n              group(self.add.s(2, 2),\n                    self.add.s(5, 6)) | self.add.s()).delay()\n \n+    def test_fail_chain_with_plain_chain(self):\n+        \"\"\"Regression test: _fail_chain should work with DisabledBackend for plain chains.\"\"\"\n+        backend = DisabledBackend(self.app)\n+        # Plain chain element without chord option\n+        after = signature('after', options={'task_id': 'after-id'})\n+\n+        # Should not raise NotImplementedError\n+        backend._fail_chain([after], ValueError('boom failed'))\n+\n+    def test_fail_chain_with_chord_member_raises(self):\n+        \"\"\"Chord members still require a result backend.\"\"\"\n+        backend = DisabledBackend(self.app)\n+        # Chord member has 'chord' in options\n+        options = {'task_id': 'member-id', 'chord': signature('outer'), 'group_id': 'gid'}\n+        member = signature('member', options=options)\n+\n+        # Should raise NotImplementedError for chord members\n+        with pytest.raises(NotImplementedError):\n+            backend._fail_chain([member], ValueError('header failed'))\n+\n \n class test_as_uri:\n \n", "problem_statement": "Regression: task failure handling is interrupted for chains without a result backend\n<!--\nPlease fill this template entirely and do not erase parts of it.\nWe reserve the right to close without a response\nbug reports which are incomplete.\n-->\n\n# Checklist\n- [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `main` branch of Celery.\n- [ ] This has already been asked to the [discussions forum](https://github.com/celery/celery/discussions) first.\n- [x] I have read the relevant section in the\n  [contribution guide](https://docs.celeryq.dev/en/main/contributing.html#other-bugs)\n  on reporting bugs.\n- [x] I have checked the [issues list](https://github.com/celery/celery/issues?q=is%3Aissue+label%3A%22Issue+Type%3A+Bug+Report%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)\n  for similar or identical bug reports.\n- [x] I have checked the [pull requests list](https://github.com/celery/celery/pulls?q=is%3Apr+label%3A%22PR+Type%3A+Bugfix%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)\n  for existing proposed fixes.\n- [x] I have checked the [commit log](https://github.com/celery/celery/commits/main)\n  to find out if the bug was already fixed in the main branch.\n- [x] I have included all related issues and possible duplicate issues\n  in this issue (If there are none, check this box anyway).\n- [ ] I have tried to reproduce the issue with [pytest-celery](https://docs.celeryq.dev/projects/pytest-celery/en/latest/userguide/celery-bug-report.html) and added the reproduction script below.\n\n## Mandatory Debugging Information\n\n- [x] I have included the output of ``celery -A proj report`` in the issue.\n- [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `main` branch of Celery.\n- [x] I have included the contents of ``pip freeze`` in the issue (relevant packages).\n- [x] I have included all the versions of all the external dependencies required\n  to reproduce this bug.\n\n## Optional Debugging Information\n\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one Python version\n  and/or implementation.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one message broker and/or\n  result backend.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one version of the message\n  broker and/or result backend.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one operating system.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one workers pool.\n- [x] I have tried reproducing the issue with autoscaling, retries,\n  ETA/Countdown & rate limits disabled.\n- [x] I have tried reproducing the issue after downgrading\n  and/or upgrading Celery and its dependencies (works on 5.7.0a1, broken on 5.7.0b1).\n\n## Related Issues and Possible Duplicates\n\n#### Related Issues\n\n- #10767 \u2014 \"Fail the chords after a chord whose header failed\". It is the PR that moved\n  the chain walk out of `mark_as_failure()` into `_fail_chain()`, and it is the most likely\n  origin of this regression (see \"Regression\" below).\n\n#### Possible Duplicates\n\n- None found. I searched issues and PRs for `_fail_chain`, `DisabledBackend` + chain,\n  `No result backend is configured` + chain, `get_state` + chain, and\n  chain + `on_failure` / errback / `task_failure` created after 2026-10-02.\n  #10783, #10784 and #10778 concern chord errbacks and are a different problem.\n\n## Environment & Settings\n\n**Celery version**: 5.7.0b1 (`celery/backends/base.py` is identical to upstream `main` as of 2026-10-10)\n\n**Python version**: 3.12.3 (CPython, Linux)\n\n**Broker**: `memory://`\n\n**Result backend**: none configured (`DisabledBackend`)\n\n<details>\n<summary><b><code>celery report</code> Output:</b></summary>\n<p>\n\n```\nsoftware -> celery:5.7.0b1 (collider) kombu:5.7.0b1 py:3.12.3\n            billiard:4.3.1 memory:N/A\nplatform -> system:Linux arch:64bit, ELF\n            kernel version:6.18.44-fc-v114 imp:CPython\nloader   -> celery.loaders.app.AppLoader\nsettings -> transport:memory results:disabled\n\nbroker_url: 'memory://:********@localhost//'\ndeprecated_settings: None\nworker_hijack_root_logger: False\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n# Steps to Reproduce\n\n## Required Dependencies\n\n* **Minimal Python Version**: 3.12.3 (not bisected lower)\n* **Minimal Celery Version**: 5.7.0b1 (regression; 5.7.0a1 is fine)\n* **Minimal Kombu Version**: 5.7.0b1\n* **Minimal Broker Version**: N/A (in-memory broker)\n* **Minimal Result Backend Version**: N/A (no result backend; this is the trigger)\n* **Minimal OS and/or Kernel Version**: Linux 6.18 (other OSes not tested)\n* **Minimal Broker Client Version**: N/A\n* **Minimal Result Backend Client Version**: N/A\n\n### Python Packages\n\n<details>\n<summary><b><code>pip freeze</code> Output (relevant packages):</b></summary>\n<p>\n\n```\namqp==5.4.0\nbilliard==4.3.1\ncelery==5.7.0b1\nclick==8.5.0\nkombu==5.7.0b1\npython-dateutil==2.9.0.post0\ntzdata==2026.4\nvine==5.1.0\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n### Other Dependencies\n\nNone. No external broker or result backend is needed.\n\n## Minimally Reproducible Test Case\n\nSelf-contained: it starts an in-process worker, so it runs with a single `python repro.py`.\n\n```python\nimport time\n\nfrom celery import Celery, Task, chain, signals\nfrom celery.contrib.testing.worker import start_worker\n\n# Broker only: no result backend is configured (DisabledBackend).\napp = Celery(\"proj\", broker=\"memory://\")\napp.conf.worker_hijack_root_logger = False\nseen = []\n\n\nclass Base(Task):\n    def on_failure(self, exc, task_id, args, kwargs, einfo):\n        seen.append(f\"Task.on_failure() called: {exc!r}\")\n\n\n@app.task(name=\"proj.boom\", base=Base)\ndef boom():\n    raise ValueError(\"boom\")\n\n\n@app.task(name=\"proj.after\")\ndef after(x=None):\n    seen.append(\"after ran\")\n\n\n@app.task(name=\"proj.alert\")\ndef alert(*args, **kwargs):\n    seen.append(\"errback (link_error) ran\")\n\n\n@signals.task_failure.connect\ndef on_task_failure(sender=None, exception=None, **kwargs):\n    seen.append(f\"task_failure signal fired: {exception!r}\")\n\n\n@signals.task_internal_error.connect\ndef on_internal_error(sender=None, exception=None, **kwargs):\n    seen.append(f\"task_internal_error signal: {exception!r}\")\n\n\nwith start_worker(app, pool=\"solo\", perform_ping_check=False,\n                  loglevel=\"CRITICAL\", shutdown_timeout=10):\n    chain(boom.s(), after.s()).on_error(alert.si()).apply_async()\n    time.sleep(3)\n\nprint(\"\\n\".join(seen) or \"(nothing happened: no log, no signal, no on_failure, no errback)\")\n```\n\n**Output on 5.7.0a1**\n\n```\nerrback (link_error) ran\nTask.on_failure() called: ValueError('boom')\ntask_failure signal fired: ValueError('boom')\n```\n\n**Output on 5.7.0b1 / `main`**\n\n```\ntask_internal_error signal: NotImplementedError('No result backend is configured.\\nPlease see the documentation for more information.')\ntask_internal_error signal: NotImplementedError('No result backend is configured.\\nPlease see the documentation for more information.')\n```\n\n## Expected Behavior\n\nA chain does not need a result backend, so its failure handling must not need one either.\nWhen a task in a chain fails, Celery should:\n\n* call `Task.on_failure()`;\n* send the `task_failure` signal;\n* run the `link_error` errbacks;\n* log `Task ... raised unexpected: ...`.\n\n## Actual Behavior\n\nWhen a non-last task of a chain fails and no result backend is configured, none of the above happens.\nThe worker only emits `task_internal_error` (twice) with `NotImplementedError: No result backend is configured.`\nNo error is logged for the failed task, and no errback, `on_failure` or `task_failure` handler runs.\n\n| Failure handling              | 5.7.0a1 | 5.7.0b1 / `main`  |\n| ----------------------------- | ------- | ----------------- |\n| `link_error` errback          | runs    | **never runs**    |\n| `Task.on_failure()`           | called  | **never called**  |\n| `task_failure` signal         | fired   | **never fired**   |\n| \"raised unexpected\" error log | logged  | **not logged**    |\n\nAnything that relies on `task_failure` (for example error-tracking integrations) or on `link_error`\nsees nothing. In a worker this is a silent loss of failure handling.\n\n## Root Cause\n\n`BaseBackend._fail_chain()` (`celery/backends/base.py`) now reads the state of every chain element\nbefore propagating the failure:\n\n```python\nalready_failed = (\n    chain_elem_ctx.id is not None and\n    self.get_state(chain_elem_ctx.id) in states.PROPAGATE_STATES\n)\n```\n\nWithout a result backend, `self` is a `DisabledBackend`, whose `get_state` is `_is_disabled` and\nraises `NotImplementedError`. `mark_as_failure()` calls `_fail_chain()` before it fires the task's\nerrbacks, so the exception escapes `handle_failure()` and everything after it is skipped.\n\n`already_failed` is only used for chord members (`'chord' in chain_elem_ctx.options`). A chord requires\na result backend, so a disabled backend never needs this read. On a real backend it is also a needless\nround trip per remaining chain element on every failure.\n\n## Regression\n\nWorks on 5.7.0a1, broken on 5.7.0b1. `_fail_chain()` was introduced in #10767, which is the likely\norigin. I could not confirm which commit added the `get_state` line.\n\n## Possible Fix\n\nOnly read the state for chord members. It must still be read before the failure is stored:\n\n```diff\n+            # Only a chord member needs this: it is what keeps its part from\n+            # being returned twice.  Reading the state is also left to chord\n+            # members because a chord needs a result backend, whereas a plain\n+            # chain does not, and a disabled backend refuses to be read.\n+            # It must be read before the failure is stored below.\n             already_failed = (\n+                'chord' in chain_elem_ctx.options and\n                 chain_elem_ctx.id is not None and\n                 self.get_state(chain_elem_ctx.id) in states.PROPAGATE_STATES\n             )\n```\n\nI have a patch with regression tests (backend-level and an end-to-end tracer test) and will open a PR\nlinked to this issue.\n", "hints_text": "@Namraa310806 was this done by bot agent? may be this might fix this https://github.com/celery/celery/pull/10824 ?\nInvestigating on it little more\nOnce I am sure I will reopen it\nNo it is not sure in #10824\n@auvipy Here we go now it is confirmed and working on its minimal fix\n", "created_at": "2026-10-10T15:20:00Z"}
{"repo": "celery/celery", "pull_number": 10830, "instance_id": "celery__celery-10830", "issue_numbers": ["4207"], "base_commit": "f8d52588ec347613ba20c044e63669cfe738b7a1", "patch": "diff --git a/celery/backends/base.py b/celery/backends/base.py\nindex 1327c48216e..5b332588032 100644\n--- a/celery/backends/base.py\n+++ b/celery/backends/base.py\n@@ -1155,11 +1155,27 @@ def __init__(self, *args, **kwargs):\n         if hasattr(self.key_t, '__func__'):  # pragma: no cover\n             self.key_t = self.key_t.__func__  # remove binding\n         super().__init__(*args, **kwargs)\n+        self._chord_expires = self.app.conf.get('result_chord_expires')\n         self._add_global_keyprefix()\n         self._encode_prefixes()\n         if self.implements_incr:\n             self.apply_chord = self._apply_chord_incr\n \n+    @property\n+    def chord_expires(self):\n+        if self._chord_expires is None:\n+            return self.expires\n+        return self.prepare_expires(self._chord_expires, type=int)\n+\n+    @property\n+    def _chord_result_expires(self):\n+        # Header results and a saved GroupResult are also read by users, so\n+        # never keep them for less than result_expires (0 means no expiry).\n+        expires = self.chord_expires\n+        if not expires or not self.expires:\n+            return 0\n+        return max(expires, self.expires)\n+\n     def _add_global_keyprefix(self):\n         \"\"\"\n         This method prepends the global keyprefix to the existing keyprefixes.\n@@ -1381,6 +1397,8 @@ def _apply_chord_incr(self, header_result_args, body, **kwargs):\n         self.ensure_chords_allowed()\n         header_result = self.app.GroupResult(*header_result_args)\n         header_result.save(backend=self)\n+        self.expire(self.get_key_for_group(header_result.id),\n+                    self._chord_result_expires)\n \n     def on_chord_part_return(self, request, state, result, **kwargs):\n         if not self.implements_incr:\n@@ -1449,8 +1467,16 @@ def on_chord_part_return(self, request, state, result, **kwargs):\n             finally:\n                 deps.delete()\n                 self.delete(key)\n+                # The chord is done: give the header results back their\n+                # normal lifetime.\n+                for dep in deps.results:\n+                    self.expire(self.get_key_for_task(dep.id), self.expires)\n         else:\n-            self.expire(key, self.expires)\n+            self.expire(key, self.chord_expires)\n+            # The last part joins every header result, so keep this one too.\n+            self.expire(self.get_key_for_group(gid), self._chord_result_expires)\n+            self.expire(self.get_key_for_task(request.id),\n+                        self._chord_result_expires)\n \n \n class KeyValueStoreBackend(BaseKeyValueStoreBackend, SyncBackendMixin):\ndiff --git a/celery/backends/cache.py b/celery/backends/cache.py\nindex 488270ee4de..25337d1afcb 100644\n--- a/celery/backends/cache.py\n+++ b/celery/backends/cache.py\n@@ -5,11 +5,22 @@\n from celery.exceptions import ImproperlyConfigured\n from celery.utils import deprecated\n from celery.utils.functional import LRUCache\n+from celery.utils.log import get_logger\n \n from .base import KeyValueStoreBackend\n \n __all__ = ('CacheBackend',)\n \n+logger = get_logger(__name__)\n+\n+# Memcached reads a longer expiry as an absolute Unix time.\n+MAX_RELATIVE_EXPIRES = 30 * 24 * 60 * 60\n+\n+W_CHORD_EXPIRES_TOO_LONG = \"\"\"\\\n+result_chord_expires is over 30 days, which memcached reads as an absolute \\\n+time, so chord keys will expire at once.\\\n+\"\"\"\n+\n REQUIRES_BACKEND = \"\"\"\\\n The Memcached backend requires the 'pymemcache' library. \\\n The 'pylibmc' and 'python-memcached' libraries are no longer used.\\\n@@ -208,6 +219,9 @@ def __init__(self, app, expires=None, backend=None,\n             raise ImproperlyConfigured(UNKNOWN_BACKEND.format(\n                 self.backend, ', '.join(backends)))\n         self._encode_prefixes()  # rencode the keyprefixes\n+        if (self._chord_expires is not None\n+                and self.chord_expires > MAX_RELATIVE_EXPIRES):\n+            logger.warning(W_CHORD_EXPIRES_TOO_LONG)\n \n     def get(self, key):\n         return self.client.get(key)\n@@ -223,7 +237,7 @@ def delete(self, key):\n \n     def _apply_chord_incr(self, header_result_args, body, **kwargs):\n         chord_key = self.get_key_for_chord(header_result_args[0])\n-        self.client.set(chord_key, 0, self.expires or 0)\n+        self.client.set(chord_key, 0, self.chord_expires or 0)\n         return super()._apply_chord_incr(\n             header_result_args, body, **kwargs)\n \ndiff --git a/celery/backends/redis.py b/celery/backends/redis.py\nindex 71f560a2f14..db529b60b96 100644\n--- a/celery/backends/redis.py\n+++ b/celery/backends/redis.py\n@@ -400,7 +400,6 @@ def __init__(self, host=None, port=None, db=None, password=None,\n                  connection_pool=None, **kwargs):\n         super().__init__(expires_type=int, **kwargs)\n         _get = self.app.conf.get\n-        self._chord_expires = _get('result_chord_expires')\n         failure_expires = _get('result_failure_expires')\n         self.failure_expires = (\n             self.expires if failure_expires is None\n@@ -754,12 +753,6 @@ def _unpack_chord_result(self, tup, decode,\n             raise chord_error\n         return retval\n \n-    @property\n-    def chord_expires(self):\n-        if self._chord_expires is None:\n-            return self.expires\n-        return self.prepare_expires(self._chord_expires, type=int)\n-\n     def _set_chord_key(self, key, value):\n         if isinstance(value, str) and len(value) > self._MAX_STR_VALUE_SIZE:\n             raise BackendStoreError('value too large for Redis backend')\ndiff --git a/docs/userguide/configuration.rst b/docs/userguide/configuration.rst\nindex 49a66656294..3a75eac28ee 100644\n--- a/docs/userguide/configuration.rst\n+++ b/docs/userguide/configuration.rst\n@@ -1091,8 +1091,11 @@ Default: Same as :setting:`result_expires`.\n \n Time (in seconds, or a :class:`~datetime.timedelta` object) a chord's\n bookkeeping is kept after the chord starts or after one of its header tasks\n-finishes. If it runs out before the next header task finishes, the chord's\n-body never runs, so set it longer than the slowest header task.\n+finishes. With the cache backend, each header task's result is also kept that\n+long after the task finishes, and never for less than :setting:`result_expires`.\n+When the chord finishes, the header results get :setting:`result_expires` back.\n+If it runs out before the chord's last header task finishes, the chord's body\n+never runs, so set it longer than the chord's total run time.\n A value of 0 means the chord's bookkeeping never expires while the chord is\n open, and the saved header of a chord with nested groups never expires.\n The bookkeeping of a chord that never completes, for example after a lost\n@@ -1100,7 +1103,10 @@ header task, is then never removed.\n \n .. note::\n \n-    For the moment this only works with the Redis backend.\n+    For the moment this only works with the cache and Redis backends.\n+\n+    Memcached reads an expiry over 30 days as an absolute time, so keep this\n+    setting at 30 days or less with the cache backend.\n \n .. setting:: result_failure_expires\n \n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/t/integration/test_cache_backend.py b/t/integration/test_cache_backend.py\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 00000000000..706bffe72bf\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/t/integration/test_cache_backend.py\n@@ -0,0 +1,30 @@\n+import time\n+from types import SimpleNamespace\n+\n+import pytest\n+\n+from celery import states, uuid\n+from celery.result import AsyncResult, GroupResult\n+\n+\n+@pytest.mark.celery(result_expires=1, result_chord_expires=60)\n+def test_chord_expires(app):\n+    if not app.conf.result_backend.startswith('cache+memcached'):\n+        pytest.skip('Requires memcached cache result backend.')\n+\n+    backend = app.backend\n+    group_id, task_id = uuid(), uuid()\n+    header = GroupResult(group_id, [AsyncResult(task_id), AsyncResult(uuid())])\n+    request = SimpleNamespace(id=task_id, group=group_id, chord={})\n+    try:\n+        backend.apply_chord((group_id, header.results), None)\n+        backend.store_result(task_id, 1, states.SUCCESS)\n+        backend.on_chord_part_return(request, states.SUCCESS, 1)\n+        time.sleep(2)\n+\n+        assert backend.get_task_meta(task_id, cache=False)['result'] == 1\n+        assert GroupResult.restore(group_id, backend=backend) == header\n+    finally:\n+        backend.forget(task_id)\n+        backend.delete_group(group_id)\n+        backend.delete(backend.get_key_for_chord(group_id))\ndiff --git a/t/smoke/tests/test_memcached_backend.py b/t/smoke/tests/test_memcached_backend.py\nindex a3cdbac0493..7251f101581 100644\n--- a/t/smoke/tests/test_memcached_backend.py\n+++ b/t/smoke/tests/test_memcached_backend.py\n@@ -10,9 +10,9 @@\n import pytest\n from pytest_celery import RESULT_TIMEOUT, CeleryBackendCluster, CeleryTestSetup, MemcachedTestBackend\n \n-from celery import states\n+from celery import Celery, states\n from celery.canvas import chord, group\n-from t.integration.tasks import add, identity, tsum\n+from t.integration.tasks import add, identity, sleeping, tsum\n \n # Larger than the default memcached item size limit (1 MB).\n TOO_LARGE_RESULT_SIZE = 2 * 1024 * 1024\n@@ -45,3 +45,19 @@ def test_too_large_result_is_not_silently_dropped(self, celery_setup: CeleryTest\n         with pytest.raises(Exception):\n             res.get(timeout=RESULT_TIMEOUT)\n         assert res.state == states.FAILURE\n+\n+\n+class test_memcached_backend_chord_expires:\n+    @pytest.fixture\n+    def default_worker_app(self, default_worker_app: Celery) -> Celery:\n+        app = default_worker_app\n+        app.conf.result_expires = 2\n+        app.conf.result_chord_expires = 60\n+        return app\n+\n+    def test_chord_outlives_result_expires(self, celery_setup: CeleryTestSetup):\n+        queue = celery_setup.worker.worker_queue\n+        header = group(sleeping.si(4).set(queue=queue) for _ in range(3))\n+        sig = chord(header, identity.s().set(queue=queue))\n+        res = sig.apply_async(queue=queue)\n+        assert res.get(timeout=RESULT_TIMEOUT) == [None, None, None]\ndiff --git a/t/unit/backends/test_base.py b/t/unit/backends/test_base.py\nindex 936ba24654b..8c2d0e89928 100644\n--- a/t/unit/backends/test_base.py\n+++ b/t/unit/backends/test_base.py\n@@ -1757,6 +1757,7 @@ def callback(result):\n             deps = GR.restore.return_value = Mock(name='DEPS')\n             deps.__len__ = Mock()\n             deps.__len__.return_value = 10\n+            deps.results = []\n             b.incr = Mock()\n             b.incr.return_value = 10\n             b.expire = Mock()\ndiff --git a/t/unit/backends/test_cache.py b/t/unit/backends/test_cache.py\nindex b85132ef553..3e3961046bb 100644\n--- a/t/unit/backends/test_cache.py\n+++ b/t/unit/backends/test_cache.py\n@@ -1,7 +1,8 @@\n import sys\n import types\n from contextlib import contextmanager\n-from unittest.mock import Mock, patch\n+from datetime import timedelta\n+from unittest.mock import Mock, call, patch\n \n import pytest\n from kombu.utils.encoding import bytes_to_str, ensure_bytes, str_to_bytes\n@@ -85,6 +86,7 @@ def test_on_chord_part_return(self, restore):\n         deps = Mock()\n         deps.__len__ = Mock()\n         deps.__len__.return_value = 2\n+        deps.results = []\n         restore.return_value = deps\n         task = Mock()\n         task.name = 'foobarbaz'\n@@ -95,6 +97,7 @@ def test_on_chord_part_return(self, restore):\n             uuid(),\n             [self.app.AsyncResult(uuid()) for _ in range(3)],\n         )\n+        task.request.id = uuid()\n         task.request.group = result_args[0]\n         tb.apply_chord(result_args, None)\n \n@@ -106,6 +109,74 @@ def test_on_chord_part_return(self, restore):\n         deps.join_native.assert_called_with(propagate=True, timeout=3.0)\n         deps.delete.assert_called_with()\n \n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize(\n+        'result_expires,chord_expires,expected_chord,expected_result', [\n+            (100, 600, 600, 600),\n+            (86400, 600, 600, 86400),\n+            (100, None, 100, 100),\n+            (100, 0, 0, 0),\n+            (None, 600, 600, 0),\n+        ])\n+    @patch('celery.result.GroupResult.restore')\n+    def test_chord_uses_chord_expires(self, restore, result_expires,\n+                                      chord_expires, expected_chord,\n+                                      expected_result):\n+        self.app.conf.result_expires = result_expires\n+        self.app.conf.result_chord_expires = chord_expires\n+        tb = CacheBackend(backend='memory://', app=self.app)\n+        restore.return_value.__len__ = Mock(return_value=2)\n+        task = Mock()\n+        task.request.id = uuid()\n+        task.request.group = gid = uuid()\n+        task.request.chord = {}\n+        with patch.object(tb.client, 'set') as client_set, \\\n+                patch.object(tb, 'expire') as expire:\n+            tb.apply_chord((gid, []), None)\n+        client_set.assert_any_call(\n+            tb.get_key_for_chord(gid), 0, expected_chord or 0)\n+        expire.assert_called_once_with(\n+            tb.get_key_for_group(gid), expected_result)\n+        tb.client.set(tb.get_key_for_chord(gid), 0)\n+        with patch.object(tb, 'expire') as expire:\n+            tb.on_chord_part_return(task.request, states.SUCCESS, 1)\n+        expire.assert_has_calls([\n+            call(tb.get_key_for_chord(gid), expected_chord),\n+            call(tb.get_key_for_group(gid), expected_result),\n+            call(tb.get_key_for_task(task.request.id), expected_result),\n+        ])\n+\n+    @patch('celery.result.GroupResult.restore')\n+    def test_on_chord_part_return_resets_header_results(self, restore):\n+        self.app.conf.result_expires = 100\n+        self.app.conf.result_chord_expires = 0\n+        tb = CacheBackend(backend='memory://', app=self.app)\n+        task = Mock()\n+        task.name = 'foobarbaz'\n+        self.app.tasks['foobarbaz'] = task\n+        task.request.id = uuid()\n+        task.request.group = gid = uuid()\n+        task.request.chord = signature(task)\n+        header = [self.app.AsyncResult(uuid()) for _ in range(2)]\n+        restore.return_value.__len__ = Mock(return_value=1)\n+        restore.return_value.results = header\n+        tb.client.set(tb.get_key_for_chord(gid), 0)\n+        with patch.object(tb, 'expire') as expire:\n+            tb.on_chord_part_return(task.request, states.SUCCESS, 1)\n+        assert expire.call_args_list == [\n+            call(tb.get_key_for_task(result.id), 100) for result in header]\n+\n+    def test_warns_on_chord_expires_over_30_days(self):\n+        self.app.conf.result_chord_expires = timedelta(days=31)\n+        with patch('celery.backends.cache.logger') as logger:\n+            CacheBackend(backend='memory://', app=self.app)\n+        logger.warning.assert_called_once()\n+\n+    def test_no_warning_on_chord_expires_within_30_days(self):\n+        self.app.conf.result_chord_expires = timedelta(days=30)\n+        with patch('celery.backends.cache.logger') as logger:\n+            CacheBackend(backend='memory://', app=self.app)\n+        logger.warning.assert_not_called()\n+\n     def test_mget(self):\n         self.tb._set_with_state('foo', 1, states.SUCCESS)\n         self.tb._set_with_state('bar', 2, states.SUCCESS)\n@@ -497,6 +568,7 @@ def test_chord_with_pymemcache_signatures(self, restore):\n \n                 deps = Mock()\n                 deps.__len__ = Mock(return_value=2)\n+                deps.results = []\n                 restore.return_value = deps\n                 task = Mock()\n                 task.name = 'foobarbaz'\n@@ -507,6 +579,7 @@ def test_chord_with_pymemcache_signatures(self, restore):\n                     uuid(),\n                     [self.app.AsyncResult(uuid()) for _ in range(2)],\n                 )\n+                task.request.id = uuid()\n                 task.request.group = result_args[0]\n                 b.apply_chord(result_args, None)\n \n", "problem_statement": "Chord body will not be called if result_expires is too low\n## Checklist\n* [x] I have included the output of ``celery -A proj report`` in the issue.\n      (if you are not able to do this, then at least specify the Celery\n       version affected).\n\nsoftware -> celery:4.0.2 (latentcall) kombu:4.0.2 py:2.7.12\n            billiard:3.5.0.2 redis:2.10.5\nplatform -> system:Linux arch:64bit, ELF imp:CPython\nloader   -> celery.loaders.app.AppLoader\nsettings -> transport:redis results:redis://localhost:6379/\n\nBROKER_TRANSPORT_OPTIONS: {\n    'fanout_patterns': True, 'fanout_prefix': True}\nCELERY_TASK_COMPRESSION: 'gzip'\nCELERY_RESULT_SERIALIZER: 'json'\nCELERY_BROKER_URL: u'redis://localhost:6379//'\nDEBUG_PROPAGATE_EXCEPTIONS: False\nCELERY_ACCEPT_CONTENT: ['application/json']\nCELERY_TIMEZONE: 'UTC'\nCELERY_MESSAGE_COMPRESSION: 'gzip'\nUSE_TZ: True\nCELERY_RESULT_BACKEND: u'redis://localhost:6379/'\nCELERY_FLOWER_URL: 'http://localhost:5556'\n\n\n* [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `master` branch of Celery.\nI do not have the time resource to test different versions of celery while at work. This project is at work and I have other things to do while worrying about callbacks not being queued/called. If I do get time to test on master, I will.\n\n## Steps to reproduce\nRun about 100 task header of a chord, with a callback. The chord is started through `.delay()`.\nUsage is similar to what the docs have:\n`chord((import_contact.s(c) for c in contacts), notify_complete.si(import_id)).apply_async()`. In my case, I do not use an immutable signature, and i pass many `kwargs` into the callback/body.\n\n\n## Expected behavior\nI expect that once my header has completed, that the callback will run.\n\n\n## Actual behavior\nDepending on how long the header tasks run for, the callback is not called. I'm still trying to figure out if it is a time or memory or other sort of issue. But, I wanted to get the issue created while I'm looking through it further.\n\nIt's also possible that it doesn't have to do with how long it takes at all. I'll try updating further.\n\n\n", "hints_text": "If you could verify this again on top of latest master plz ping me to reopen\nMy new leading hypothesis is that this is related to my project's use of `result_expires`. We used to have this set at 60 seconds, and now we have it at 10 seconds.\r\n\r\nTonight I decided to revisit this (we're using celery `4.2.0`). There are a few workflows that would benefit us from having this work _all_ of the time; we've been hesitant to utilize the `chord` because we were having issues with it running the body all the time.\r\n\r\nNow, if we have, say, 1 task, and we have a header of 2 of them being called (say `add` each), and then let the body also be an `add`, if we lower the result expire to 1 second, and also include a `time.sleep(2)` in the `add` implementation, I've never seen the `body` task run.\r\n\r\nPlaying with the result expires and the \"task time\" (fake long task with a time.sleep) seems like the culprit.\r\n\r\nThere is documentation around result expires, however, if this is the issue for the `redis` backend, then the docs should warn against setting a low task expire results time, in the case of a `chord`.\r\n\r\nAt this point, I'm fairly confident that this is the bug.\r\nBelow is the section of code which introduces the notion of the adjusted completed task count.\r\nWhen I add more logs around the readycount vs total (chord_size), and when I see the issue rear its head, its because it never reaches the `total`. You'll note the `self.expires`\r\n```        with client.pipeline() as pipe:\r\n            _, readycount, totaldiff, _, _ = pipe \\\r\n                .rpush(jkey, self.encode([1, tid, state, result])) \\\r\n                .llen(jkey) \\\r\n                .get(tkey) \\\r\n                .expire(jkey, self.expires) \\\r\n                .expire(tkey, self.expires) \\\r\n                .execute()\r\n```\r\n\r\nAnd `self.expires` gets its value from:\r\n```    def prepare_expires(self, value, type=None):\r\n        if value is None:\r\n            value = self.app.conf.result_expires\r\n        if isinstance(value, timedelta):\r\n            value = value.total_seconds()\r\n        if value is not None and type:\r\n            return type(value)\r\n        return value\r\n```\r\n\r\n\r\nSo, I wonder, should the tkey and jkey be honoring the result expires (or at least, the one set in the config, as opposed to the `day` default)? Is there any harm in having it be no less than 1 day, or perhaps, making it its own setting?\r\n\r\nOr, is this really just a documentation update to help users?\n@auvipy please see above.\nWould it be possible to have a per-task (possibly even on `chord`, too) result expires setting? My team would be very interested in that usage.\r\n\r\nWe don't care about most of the results of our task runs (in practice), but we don't necessarily wish to always use `ignore_result`, especially if we need the task result around at least briefly but we forget about the global setting.\n> Would it be possible to have a per-task (possibly even on `chord`, too) result expires setting? My team would be very interested in that usage.\r\n> \r\n> We don't care about most of the results of our task runs (in practice), but we don't necessarily wish to always use `ignore_result`, especially if we need the task result around at least briefly but we forget about the global setting.\r\n\r\nwhen you have time can you revisit the current codes & share your current consensus on that? we can dig further based on your finding. I know it so old & sorry for taking so long to reply.\nThis still reproduces on 5.6.3 with the Redis backend. A chord of three tasks that each sleep 3 seconds, on a single worker with `result_expires=1`, never runs its body. With `result_expires=60` it runs.\n\nThe cause looks like `on_chord_part_return`: each finished header task resets the chord's `.j`, `.t` and `.s` keys to expire after `result_expires`. If the gap between two header tasks finishing is longer than that, the keys are gone and the body never fires. `main` has the same code.\n\nOne narrow fix would be to keep those keys until the body is sent. They're already deleted at that point, so no expiry is needed while the chord is open. Another option is a separate expiry for chord keys. Would either be welcome? Happy to send a PR with tests.\ncan you reproduce with v5.7.0b1?\nYes, it still reproduces on 5.7.0b1 with the same chord setup. I can send a PR.", "created_at": "2026-10-09T14:10:38Z"}
{"repo": "celery/celery", "pull_number": 10829, "instance_id": "celery__celery-10829", "issue_numbers": ["8890", "9608"], "base_commit": "b69c634f9b3fa3bc2494cbbcefe1ac30e632804c", "patch": "diff --git a/celery/canvas.py b/celery/canvas.py\nindex 0b62c22954b..420b8ee8765 100644\n--- a/celery/canvas.py\n+++ b/celery/canvas.py\n@@ -1420,7 +1420,7 @@ def prepare_steps(self, args, kwargs, tasks,\n                 # for chords we freeze by pretending it's a normal\n                 # signature instead of a group.\n                 tasks.pop()\n-                results.pop()\n+                body_res = results.pop()\n                 try:\n                     task = chord(\n                         task, body=prev_task,\n@@ -1435,10 +1435,20 @@ def prepare_steps(self, args, kwargs, tasks,\n                         task, body=prev_task,\n                         root_id=root_id, app=app,\n                     )\n-                # Do not overwrite prev_res here; it may intentionally be a GroupResult (see #8903).\n-                # But we must reset prev_task after the pop so we don't link a chord to its own body\n-                # when use_link/task_protocol==1.\n+                # Reset prev_task after the pop so we don't link a chord to\n+                # its own body when use_link/task_protocol==1.\n                 prev_task = tasks[-1] if tasks else None\n+                # The rest of the chain is linked to the body's result,\n+                # directly or through the GroupResult of a following chord.\n+                # Unlink it so it is linked to the chord's result below;\n+                # otherwise the body's id is in the result tree twice\n+                # (#9608), which chord.freeze() rejects (#8890).\n+                node = results[-1] if results else None\n+                while node is not None and node.parent is not body_res:\n+                    node = node.parent\n+                if node is not None:\n+                    node.parent = None\n+                    prev_res = node\n \n             if is_last_task:\n                 # chain(task_id=id) means task id is set for the last task\ndiff --git a/t/canvas_helpers.py b/t/canvas_helpers.py\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 00000000000..f18d4f82fa0\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/t/canvas_helpers.py\n@@ -0,0 +1,16 @@\n+def ids_in_as_tuple(tuple_repr):\n+    \"\"\"Return the id of every node in an as_tuple() tree.\"\"\"\n+    ids = []\n+\n+    def walk(tup):\n+        if tup is None:\n+            return\n+        (res, nodes) = tup\n+        (node_id, parent) = res\n+        ids.append(node_id)\n+        for child in nodes or ():\n+            walk(child)\n+        walk(parent)\n+\n+    walk(tuple_repr)\n+    return ids\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/t/integration/test_canvas.py b/t/integration/test_canvas.py\nindex e58674271a5..e97c03683fb 100644\n--- a/t/integration/test_canvas.py\n+++ b/t/integration/test_canvas.py\n@@ -14,6 +14,7 @@\n from celery.exceptions import ChordError, ImproperlyConfigured, TimeoutError\n from celery.result import AsyncResult, GroupResult, ResultSet\n from celery.signals import before_task_publish, task_received\n+from t.canvas_helpers import ids_in_as_tuple\n \n from . import tasks\n from .conftest import TEST_BACKEND, check_for_logs, flaky, get_active_redis_channels, get_redis_connection\n@@ -1304,6 +1305,63 @@ def test_chain_forwards_args_past_leading_empty_steps(self, manager):\n         c = _chain(_chain(group(), add.s(10)), add.s(20), app=manager.app)\n         assert c.apply_async((5,)).get(timeout=TIMEOUT) == 35\n \n+    @flaky\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize('with_task_id', [False, True])\n+    def test_chain_of_two_groups_as_tuple_holds_each_task_once(self, manager, with_task_id):\n+        \"\"\"Regression test for https://github.com/celery/celery/issues/9608.\n+\n+        The task after a group becomes the body of a chord, and the rest of\n+        the chain must be linked to the chord's result only. as_tuple() must\n+        list every node once, with or without a task_id.\n+        \"\"\"\n+        try:\n+            manager.app.backend.ensure_chords_allowed()\n+        except NotImplementedError as e:\n+            raise pytest.skip(e.args[0])\n+\n+        options = {'task_id': str(uuid.uuid4())} if with_task_id else {}\n+        c = chain(\n+            add.si(1, 0),\n+            group(add.s(1), add.s(2)),\n+            group(tsum.s(), tsum.s()),\n+            tsum.s(),\n+            add.s(1),\n+            **options,\n+        )\n+        res = c.apply_async()\n+        ids = ids_in_as_tuple(res.as_tuple())\n+\n+        # The first task, two tasks and a GroupResult per group, two last tasks.\n+        assert len(ids) == len(set(ids)) == 9, ids\n+        assert res.get(timeout=TIMEOUT) == 11\n+\n+    @flaky\n+    def test_chord_with_chain_body_with_task_id(self, manager):\n+        \"\"\"Regression test for https://github.com/celery/celery/issues/8890.\n+\n+        A chain with a task_id and a group inside used to make the chord\n+        raise RuntimeError('Recursive result parents') when it was frozen\n+        before it was sent.\n+        \"\"\"\n+        try:\n+            manager.app.backend.ensure_chords_allowed()\n+        except NotImplementedError as e:\n+            raise pytest.skip(e.args[0])\n+\n+        body = chain(\n+            add.si(1, 0),\n+            group(add.si(1, 1), add.si(1, 2)),\n+            add.si(2, 2) | add.si(3, 3),\n+            task_id=str(uuid.uuid4()),\n+        )\n+        c = chord([add.si(1, 1), add.si(1, 2)], body)\n+        c.freeze()\n+        res = c.apply_async()\n+        ids = ids_in_as_tuple(res.as_tuple())\n+\n+        assert len(ids) == len(set(ids)), ids\n+        assert res.get(timeout=TIMEOUT) == 6\n+\n \n class test_result_set:\n \ndiff --git a/t/smoke/tests/test_canvas.py b/t/smoke/tests/test_canvas.py\nindex 447c0b088cb..c9c77030f06 100644\n--- a/t/smoke/tests/test_canvas.py\n+++ b/t/smoke/tests/test_canvas.py\n@@ -4,8 +4,9 @@\n from pytest_celery import RESULT_TIMEOUT, CeleryTestSetup\n \n from celery.canvas import chain, chord, group, signature\n+from t.canvas_helpers import ids_in_as_tuple\n from t.integration.conftest import get_redis_connection\n-from t.integration.tasks import ExpectedException, add, fail, identity, redis_echo\n+from t.integration.tasks import ExpectedException, add, fail, identity, redis_echo, tsum\n \n \n class test_signature:\n@@ -102,6 +103,28 @@ def test_chain_skips_empty_group_on_worker(self, celery_setup: CeleryTestSetup):\n         res = sig.apply_async()\n         assert res.get(timeout=RESULT_TIMEOUT) == 6\n \n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize(\"with_task_id\", [False, True])\n+    def test_chain_of_two_groups_as_tuple_holds_each_task_once(\n+        self, celery_setup: CeleryTestSetup, with_task_id: bool\n+    ):\n+        \"\"\"https://github.com/celery/celery/issues/9608\"\"\"\n+        queue = celery_setup.worker.worker_queue\n+        options = {\"task_id\": str(uuid.uuid4())} if with_task_id else {}\n+        sig = chain(\n+            add.si(1, 0).set(queue=queue),\n+            group(add.s(1).set(queue=queue), add.s(2).set(queue=queue)),\n+            group(tsum.s().set(queue=queue), tsum.s().set(queue=queue)),\n+            tsum.s().set(queue=queue),\n+            add.s(1).set(queue=queue),\n+            **options,\n+        )\n+        res = sig.apply_async(queue=queue)\n+        ids = ids_in_as_tuple(res.as_tuple())\n+\n+        # The first task, two tasks and a GroupResult per group, two last tasks.\n+        assert len(ids) == len(set(ids)) == 9, ids\n+        assert res.get(timeout=RESULT_TIMEOUT) == 11\n+\n \n class test_chord:\n     def test_sanity(self, celery_setup: CeleryTestSetup):\ndiff --git a/t/unit/tasks/test_canvas.py b/t/unit/tasks/test_canvas.py\nindex 9ecdb43c839..c645fc09dc1 100644\n--- a/t/unit/tasks/test_canvas.py\n+++ b/t/unit/tasks/test_canvas.py\n@@ -16,6 +16,7 @@\n                            xstarmap)\n from celery.exceptions import Ignore, Reject\n from celery.result import AsyncResult, EagerResult, GroupResult, result_from_tuple\n+from t.canvas_helpers import ids_in_as_tuple\n \n SIG = Signature({\n     'task': 'TASK',\n@@ -1427,9 +1428,12 @@ def link_chain(sig):\n         assert signature(flat_chain.tasks[1].options['link'][0]) == signature('link_b')\n         assert signature(flat_chain.tasks[1].options['link_error'][0]) == signature('link_ab')\n \n-    def test_group_in_center_of_chain(self):\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize('options', [{}, {'task_id': 'last-task-id'}])\n+    def test_group_in_center_of_chain(self, options):\n+        # With an option, chain() keeps its steps and the group is upgraded\n+        # in prepare_steps, which must not link the body's result twice.\n         t1 = chain(self.add.si(1, 1), group(self.add.si(1, 1), self.add.si(1, 1)),\n-                   self.add.si(1, 1) | self.add.si(1, 1))\n+                   self.add.si(1, 1) | self.add.si(1, 1), **options)\n         t2 = chord([self.add.si(1, 1), self.add.si(1, 1)], t1)\n         t2.freeze()  # should not raise\n \n@@ -1466,6 +1470,48 @@ def test_consecutive_groups_in_chain_preserve_group_results_in_as_tuple(self, ta\n         ):\n             assert sizes.count(n) >= 2, sizes\n \n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize('task_protocol', [2, 1])\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize('method', ['apply_async', 'freeze'])\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize('task_id', [None, 'last-task-id'])\n+    def test_groups_in_chain_as_tuple_holds_each_task_once(self, task_id, method, task_protocol):\n+        # Regression for #9608: as_tuple() must hold every task of the chain\n+        # once, with or without a task_id. The task after a group becomes the\n+        # body of a chord, and the rest of the chain must be linked to the\n+        # chord's result only, not to the body's result as well.\n+        self.app.conf.task_protocol = task_protocol\n+        n = 3\n+        options = {'task_id': task_id} if task_id else {}\n+        canvas = chain(\n+            self.add.si(0, 0),\n+            group([chain(self.add.si(i, 0), self.add.si(0, i)) for i in range(n)]),\n+            group([chain(self.add.si(i, 1), self.add.si(1, i)) for i in range(n)]),\n+            self.add.si(2, 0),\n+            self.add.s(3),\n+            **options,\n+        )\n+        ids = ids_in_as_tuple(getattr(canvas, method)().as_tuple())\n+\n+        # The first task, 2 * n tasks and a GroupResult per group, two last tasks.\n+        assert len(ids) == len(set(ids)) == 1 + 2 * (2 * n + 1) + 2, ids\n+\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize('task_protocol', [2, 1])\n+    def test_group_before_chord_in_chain_as_tuple_holds_each_task_once(self, task_protocol):\n+        # Regression for #9608: here the rest of the chain is linked to the\n+        # task after the group through the GroupResult of the next chord.\n+        self.app.conf.task_protocol = task_protocol\n+        canvas = chain(\n+            self.add.si(0, 0),\n+            group(self.add.si(1, 1), self.add.si(1, 2)),\n+            self.add.si(2, 2),\n+            chord([self.add.si(3, 3), self.add.si(3, 4)], self.add.si(4, 4)),\n+            self.add.si(5, 5),\n+            task_id='last-task-id',\n+        )\n+        ids = ids_in_as_tuple(canvas.apply_async().as_tuple())\n+\n+        # Eight tasks and two GroupResults.\n+        assert len(ids) == len(set(ids)) == 10, ids\n+\n     def test_upgrade_to_chord_on_chain(self):\n         group1 = group(self.add.si(10, 10), self.add.si(10, 10))\n         group2 = group(self.xsum.s(), self.xsum.s())\n", "problem_statement": "Error \"Recursive result parents\" on some tasks trees\n# Checklist\r\n<!--\r\nTo check an item on the list replace [ ] with [x].\r\n-->\r\n- [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `main` branch of Celery.\r\n- [ ] This has already been asked to the [discussions forum](https://github.com/celery/celery/discussions) first.\r\n- [x] I have read the relevant section in the\r\n  [contribution guide](https://docs.celeryq.dev/en/main/contributing.html#other-bugs)\r\n  on reporting bugs.\r\n- [x] I have checked the [issues list](https://github.com/celery/celery/issues?q=is%3Aissue+label%3A%22Issue+Type%3A+Bug+Report%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)\r\n  for similar or identical bug reports.\r\n- [x] I have checked the [pull requests list](https://github.com/celery/celery/pulls?q=is%3Apr+label%3A%22PR+Type%3A+Bugfix%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)\r\n  for existing proposed fixes.\r\n- [x] I have checked the [commit log](https://github.com/celery/celery/commits/main)\r\n  to find out if the bug was already fixed in the main branch.\r\n- [x] I have included all related issues and possible duplicate issues\r\n  in this issue (If there are none, check this box anyway).\r\n\r\n## Mandatory Debugging Information\r\n\r\n- [x] I have included the output of ``celery -A proj report`` in the issue.\r\n    (if you are not able to do this, then at least specify the Celery\r\n     version affected).\r\n- [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `main` branch of Celer\r\ny.\r\n- [x] I have included the contents of ``pip freeze`` in the issue.\r\n- [x] I have included all the versions of all the external dependencies required\r\n  to reproduce this bug.\r\n\r\n## Optional Debugging Information\r\n<!--\r\nTry some of the below if you think they are relevant.\r\nIt will help us figure out the scope of the bug and how many users it affects.\r\n-->\r\n- [x] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one Python version\r\n  and/or implementation.\r\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one message broker and/or\r\n  result backend.\r\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one version of the message\r\n  broker and/or result backend.\r\n- [x] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one operating system.\r\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one workers pool.\r\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue with autoscaling, retries,\r\n  ETA/Countdown & rate limits disabled.\r\n- [x] I have tried reproducing the issue after downgrading\r\n  and/or upgrading Celery and its dependencies.\r\n\r\n## Related Issues and Possible Duplicates\r\n<!--\r\nPlease make sure to search and mention any related issues\r\nor possible duplicates to this issue as requested by the checklist above.\r\n\r\nThis may or may not include issues in other repositories that the Celery project\r\nmaintains or other repositories that are dependencies of Celery.\r\n\r\nIf you don't know how to mention issues, please refer to Github's documentation\r\non the subject: https://help.github.com/en/articles/autolinked-references-and-urls#issues-and-pull-requests\r\n-->\r\n\r\n#### Related Issues\r\n\r\n- None\r\n\r\n#### Possible Duplicates\r\n\r\n- None\r\n\r\n## Environment & Settings\r\n<!-- Include the contents of celery --version below -->\r\n**Celery version**: `5.4.0rc1`\r\n\r\n<!-- Include the output of celery -A proj report below -->\r\n<details>\r\n<summary><b><code>celery report</code> Output:</b></summary>\r\n<p>\r\n\r\n```\r\nsoftware -> celery:5.4.0rc1 (opalescent) kombu:5.3.5 py:3.11.8\r\n            billiard:4.2.0 memory:N/A\r\nplatform -> system:Windows arch:64bit, WindowsPE\r\n            kernel version:10 imp:CPython\r\nloader   -> celery.loaders.app.AppLoader\r\nsettings -> transport:memory results:cache+memory:///\r\n\r\nbroker_url: 'memory://localhost//'\r\nbroker_backend: 'memory:///'\r\nresult_backend: 'cache+memory:///'\r\ndeprecated_settings: None\r\n```\r\n\r\n</p>\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n# Steps to Reproduce\r\n```python\r\nimport celery\r\n\r\ncelery_app = celery.Celery()\r\ncelery_app.config_from_object({\r\n    'broker_url': 'memory://',\r\n    'broker_backend': 'memory://',\r\n    'result_backend': 'cache+memory://'\r\n})\r\n\r\n\r\n@celery.shared_task\r\ndef test_task(x: int):\r\n    print(x)\r\n\r\n\r\ndef main():\r\n    t1 = celery.chain(test_task.s(0), celery.group(test_task.s(1), test_task.s(2)), test_task.s(3) | test_task.s(4))\r\n    t2 = celery.chord([test_task.s(5), test_task.s(6)], t1)\r\n    t3 = celery.chord([test_task.s(7)], t2)\r\n    t3.apply_async()\r\n\r\n\r\nif __name__ == '__main__':\r\n    main()\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Required Dependencies\r\n<!-- Please fill the required dependencies to reproduce this issue -->\r\n- **Minimal Python Version**: `3.9 / 3.11`\r\n- **Minimal Celery Version**: `5.3.1 / 5.3.4 / 5.3.6 / 5.4.0rc1`\r\n- **Minimal Kombu Version**: N/A or Unknown\r\n- **Minimal Broker Version**: N/A or Unknown\r\n- **Minimal Result Backend Version**: N/A or Unknown\r\n- **Minimal OS and/or Kernel Version**: `Windows 11 / MacOS / Linux`\r\n- **Minimal Broker Client Version**: N/A or Unknown\r\n- **Minimal Result Backend Client Version**: N/A or Unknown\r\n\r\n### Python Packages\r\n<!-- Please fill the contents of pip freeze below -->\r\n<details>\r\n<summary><b><code>pip freeze</code> Output:</b></summary>\r\n<p>\r\n\r\n```\r\namqp==5.2.0\r\nbilliard==4.2.0\r\n-e git+https://github.com/celery/celery.git@9edeab6b4526a59bf699df10f1b48ac65809eaea#egg=celery\r\nclick==8.1.7\r\nclick-didyoumean==0.3.0\r\nclick-plugins==1.1.1\r\nclick-repl==0.3.0\r\ncolorama==0.4.6\r\nkombu==5.3.5\r\nprompt-toolkit==3.0.43\r\npython-dateutil==2.9.0.post0\r\nsix==1.16.0\r\ntzdata==2024.1\r\nvine==5.1.0\r\nwcwidth==0.2.13\r\n```\r\n\r\n</p>\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### Other Dependencies\r\n<!--\r\nPlease provide system dependencies, configuration files\r\nand other dependency information if applicable\r\n-->\r\n<details>\r\n<p>\r\nN/A\r\n</p>\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n## Minimally Reproducible Test Case\r\n<!--\r\nPlease provide a reproducible test case.\r\nRefer to the Reporting Bugs section in our contribution guide.\r\n\r\nWe prefer submitting test cases in the form of a PR to our integration test suite.\r\nIf you can provide one, please mention the PR number below.\r\nIf not, please attach the most minimal code example required to reproduce the issue below.\r\nIf the test case is too large, please include a link to a gist or a repository below.\r\n-->\r\n\r\nSame as \"Steps to Reproduce\"\r\n\r\n# Expected Behavior\r\nThe code runs successfully\r\n\r\n# Actual Behavior\r\n```\r\nTraceback (most recent call last):\r\n  File \"scratch.py\", line 20, in <module>\r\n    t3.apply_async()\r\n  File \"C:\\git\\celery\\celery\\canvas.py\", line 2152, in apply_async\r\n    return self.run(tasks, body, args, task_id=task_id, kwargs=kwargs, **merged_options)\r\n           ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\r\n  File \"C:\\git\\celery\\celery\\canvas.py\", line 2233, in run\r\n    bodyres = body.freeze(task_id, root_id=root_id)\r\n              ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\r\n  File \"C:\\git\\celery\\celery\\canvas.py\", line 2093, in freeze\r\n    raise RuntimeError('Recursive result parents')\r\nRuntimeError: Recursive result parents\r\n```\nUsing a custom task_id on a chain results in an incomplete as_tuple graph.\n<!--\nPlease fill this template entirely and do not erase parts of it.\nWe reserve the right to close without a response\nbug reports which are incomplete.\n-->\n# Checklist\n<!--\nTo check an item on the list replace [ ] with [x].\n-->\n- [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `main` branch of Celery.\n- [ ] This has already been asked to the [discussions forum](https://github.com/celery/celery/discussions) first.\n- [x] I have read the relevant section in the\n  [contribution guide](https://docs.celeryq.dev/en/main/contributing.html#other-bugs)\n  on reporting bugs.\n- [x] I have checked the [issues list](https://github.com/celery/celery/issues?q=is%3Aissue+label%3A%22Issue+Type%3A+Bug+Report%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)\n  for similar or identical bug reports.\n- [x] I have checked the [pull requests list](https://github.com/celery/celery/pulls?q=is%3Apr+label%3A%22PR+Type%3A+Bugfix%22+-label%3A%